\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WORLD NEWS TONIGHT WITH DAVID MUIR\b0\par\par\uc2 511 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, breaking news, as we come on the air, a major \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  derailment, the train from LA to Chicago, reports of multiple fatalities and dozens of injuries.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMTRAK TRAIN DERAILMENT\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The images coming in at this hour, the train hitting a dump truck in Missouri. Multiple cars derailing. Video showing passengers inside trying to force open a window. Passengers on top of the wreckage, waiting for help. The train with 243 passengers onboard. Alex Perez, with late reporting.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE STATES BANNING ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the list of states immediately banning abortion after the Supreme Court overturns Roe versus Wade. Mississippi, one of several states with trigger laws, certifying it today, soon making it a felony to perform an abortion. Anti-abortion rights groups now taking aim at abortion pills and the growing concern that women who ordered those pills could be tracked with their internet IP address. Rachel Scott, traveling the country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT RULES IN PRAYER CASE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And tonight, the new ruling, now, from the Supreme Court involving religion and public schools. In a 6-3 ruling, conservative justices siding with a high school football coach praying at the 50-yard line following games, often with players by his side. Some parents concerned, what about players who might not want to pray? The pressure put on them. Some asking, what happened to the notion of separation of church and state? Terry Moran, standing by at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUSSIAN MISSILES STRIKE SHOPPING MALL\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The war in Ukraine and the horrific scene still unfolding at this hour after Russian missiles slam into a shopping mall. The concern at this hour about the people who were inside. And did air raid sirens save many others? James Longman is live on the scene for us.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GRINER'S DETENTION EXTENDED\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 To Russia and news, tonight, on \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner, handcuffed, in court in Moscow, today. Russia extending her detention now, and what we learned, today.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HUNDREDS OF FLIGHTS CANCELED\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Back here at home, hundreds of flights canceled across the US today. More than a thousand flights canceled over the weekend. What's driving this?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JANUARY 6TH SURPRISE HEARING\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 News coming in from Capitol Hill, tonight, the January 6th committee calling a surprise hearing with just 24 hours notice. 1:00 PM tomorrow, new evidence and a key witness.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRUCK BY LIGHTNING\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We're learning more about that dramatic rescue at sea. Seven people on a boat off Florida hit by lightning, one man struck, and then the rescue. And "America Strong," tonight, the retired kindergarten teacher and the surprise years later. She must have taught them well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT WITH DAVID MUIR\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From ABC News world headquarters in New York, this is "World News Tonight with David Muir."\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220628ei6r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BIDEN UNDER PRESSURE PRESIDENT URGED TO TAKE ACTION TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS\b0\par\par\uc2 580 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AMTRAK\uc2 , COLLISION, RESCUE, NIGHTINGALE, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, LUCAS, MCNICHOLAS, COLEMAN, EVERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE STATES BANNING ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, G7 SUMMIT, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN UNDER PRESSURE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Biden is in Europe, tonight, for meetings with G7 and \uc2 NATO\uc2  leaders.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The leaders not talking publicly as a group there on the major headline back in the US involving the overturning of Roe, but many of those leaders already speaking out before they gathered about what would not happen in their own countries, weighing in on a woman's right to decide.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our chief White House correspondent, Cecilia Vega, traveling with the President. And, Cecilia, President Biden overseas, obviously, but the political pressure here at home from many in his own party to take action wherever he can?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT URGED TO TAKE ACTION TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, David. Exactly. That pressure is coming from within his own party, right now. In fact, 34 Senate Democrats sent a letter to him demanding that he take immediate action. They say he has to take this action now, and they are calling on him to use the full force of the federal government.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Look, the White House, at this point, they say the DOJ will protect the right for women seeking an abortion to cross state lines to get that procedure.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHITE HOUSE ACTION\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) They say that they will protect the right to obtain the abortion pill. But Democrats and advocates want the President to do more, and they want him to do more right now. They're calling on him, when it comes to the abortion pill, to loosen restrictions on that, to be able for women to access it in a pharmacy, to make it easier to import from other countries.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They want him to declare a public health emergency. That would make it easier for the federal government to deploy resources and funds. But look, the reality, David, we've been talking about this, there is no executive order that the President can sign that would restore Roe's protections. At this point, Democrats, though, are calling on him to do more, now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Cecilia, in the meantime, we know many of those leaders did make public statements even before their official meetings there, reacting to the US rolling back women's reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD LEADERS REACT TO HISTORIC SUPREME COURT RULING\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, David. In fact, President Biden told our team that this issue has not come up in his one-on-one meetings, but you're exactly right. These leaders are not holding back in their criticism. They've been very public with it. France's \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 , for example, said that abortion is a fundamental right for all women. The UK's \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  calls on, called the ruling a step backward. And this, David, from Canada's \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2 , he said his country will pay for an abortion for American women if they travel to Canada to get one.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Cecilia Vega, reporting live, tonight, traveling with the President. Cecilia, thank you with the global reaction there to overturning Roe versus Wade here in the US.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220628ei6r00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 31 MILLION AMERICANS NOW IMPACTED BY SCOTUS RULING DIVIDED NATION TAKE TO THE STREETS AS BANS GO INTO EFFECT\b0\par\par\uc2 756 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, MANCINI, ABORTION, TRUMP, PROTESTS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But we do start here at home with the new protests following the Supreme Court's overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION NOW ILLEGAL\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As of this morning, more than 31 million Americans are living in states where abortion bans are in effect. And those states are likely to be joined in the coming weeks by a more than dozen others in outlawing abortion.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 31 MILLION AMERICANS NOW IMPACTED BY SCOTUS RULING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DIVIDED NATION TAKE TO THE STREETS AS BANS GO INTO EFFECT IN AT LEAST 8 STATES\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We start our coverage with ABC's Devin Dwyer at the Supreme Court. Devin, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Janai. It's quiet here at the Supreme Court today, but a scramble is underway nationwide to shore-up abortion access as a growing number of states are now enforcing those strict new abortion bans with few exceptions.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Hey, ho, ho. Abortionists have got to go.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, a divided America...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 My body...\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \u8230\'20\'26sounding off with the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I think that it's very important for women and anyone who can have kids to have control over when they have their kids.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At a rally in Illinois, former President Trump celebrating the justices he appointed to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 This breakthrough is the answer to prayers of millions and millions of people.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While in Mississippi, which led the case that took down Roe, deep sadness setting in at the state's only abortion clinic left, which would close in eight days. Jackson Women's Health has been a refuge for predominantly low-income and minority women like Nicole, who we met last year when she came for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (ABORTION RECIPIENT)\par \par \uc2 You don't want to bring it, you're scared. And it's not just something my husband and I are ready for. We're just not ready yet.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Eight states this morning now have abortion bans enforced with 18 more likely ban or severely restrict abortion in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI (PRESIDENT MARCH FOR LIFE)\par \par \uc2 And that abortion has, you know, significant consequences. Adoption is an option. It's a beautiful and noble decision.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) President Biden vowing to ensure those states don't violate federal law.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 ...like deciding to not allow people to cross state lines to get public health services.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democratic governors signing measures to shore-up protection for abortion care.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 We will stand for every single person to the fullest extent of the law.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Stand up and (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some grassroots groups are gearing up to help people defy abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 PAROS HATCHER (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR BLACK FEMINIST FUTURE)\par \par \uc2 We're going to make sure any person who needs an abortion is going to get one. Did you hear me? We're going to make sure any person that needs an abortion will have what they need.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Others pointing them to online providers, including foreign doctors who prescribe the abortion pill to women in the US even if their state law prohibits it.\par \par \uc2 DR REBECCA GOMPERTS (FOUNDER & DIRECTOR WOMEN ON WAVES/AID ACCESS)\par \par \uc2 I will review her case and send my prescription to her and into a pharmacy in India that can fill my prescription and then it sent from India. I am acting according to the medical, ethical guidelines and the laws of the countries where I'm based.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, Canada's Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  is inviting American women to seek abortions north of the border, where it's legal nationwide. Sixteen states plus DC have laws explicitly protecting abortion here in the United States. Clinics this morning, Whit, in those states are reporting a surge in calls from women seeking appointments, willing to travel. Meanwhile, here at Supreme Court, more major decisions expected tomorrow. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Devin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220728ei6q0008e\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HIGH COURT OVERTURNS ROE V. WADE 31 MILLION AMERICANS NOW IMPACTED BY BAN\b0\par\par\uc2 812 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, MANCINI, ABORTION, TRUMP, PROTESTS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 G7 SUMMIT, BIDEN, ABORTION, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATIC SENATORS DEMAND BIDEN TO TAKE "BOLD ACTION"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 34 LEADERS URGING WHITE HOUSE TO DO MORE AMID SCOTUS RULING\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, NEUMANN, SALAS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, turning now to a look at what could be the next battleground, the abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH COURT OVERTURNS ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 31 MILLION AMERICANS NO IMPACTED BY BAN\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The US attorney general saying, states cannot ban the drugs while some states are already trying to limit access top them. Joining us now is ABC News contributor Kate Shaw, professor of constitutional law at Cardoza Law School. Kate, thanks so much for being with us. After Friday's ruling, Attorney General Garland made a point of saying that states can't ban the abortion pill based on disagreement on with the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's judgment and its safety and efficacy, that sounds like a qualified statement. Do states have legal ground to ban the abortion pill?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Janai, I think it's clear that the abortion pills, and that's a two-pill sequence that's approved for terminating early pregnancies up to about ten weeks, is going to be a very important part of the post-Roe landscape. So, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has approved these pills for terminating early pregnancies and it has been approved and being delivered by mail. But, of course, some states are going to seek to prevent their citizens from obtaining those pills and using those pills by mail. In a word, it's unclear sort of what the legal status of the federal or the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's approval and its interaction with states' attempts to ban these pills will look like in the courts.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The idea is something called preemption. If there's a direct conflict between state and federal law, federal law is supposed to preempt the state law. And I presume the Federal Department of Justice will sue states if they seek to prohibit access to the abortion pills claiming that the federal law basically supersedes the state law.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But the Supreme Court is going to have to weigh in on this. It's a complex body of law and the Supreme Court is pretty hostile to abortion rights. And so, it's anybody's guess how that legal dispute will play out in the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. And Planned Parenthood said that it's going to challenge the court, and going to court to challenge Utah's trigger law. How likely is that to succeed?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  decided in the Dobbs decision on Friday when it overturned Roe was that the federal Constitution doesn't protect the right to abortion, but it didn't say anything about what state Constitution say. And, of course, every state has its own Constitution. And so, what Planned Parenthood is saying is, the Utah Constitution protects a right to abortion, even if the federal Constitution doesn't. And the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  doesn't get to control what the Utah Constitution means, that's for Utah judges to decide. And this is a strategy abortion advocates have used in other states, even states like in Kansas. Those courts have found state constitution protects the right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Again, whatever the federal Constitution is understood to do. And so, that's I think where a lot of these legal fights are going to shift to, state courts and state constitutions.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But it is certain that the fight is not over.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Kate, thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thank you, Janai.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And just a reminder to tune in to a special edition of "This Week" later this morning following that landmark Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROE V WADE OVERTURNED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: A SPECIAL EDITION OF THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP), SOUTH DAKOTA\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Martha Raddatz goes one-on-one with South Dakota Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  and Senator Elizabeth Warren, leading voices on both sides of the issue on the future of reproductive rights in America.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM), MASSACHUSETTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REP. JAMIE RASKIN (DEM), MARYLAND MEMBER JAN. 6 COMMITTEE\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Plus, January 6th committee member, Representative \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 , on the latest from the hearings and the breakthrough deal on guns. Whit?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220728ei6q0008h\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 RECORD NUMBER OF AMERICANS EXPECTED TO TRAVEL MORE THAN 11 MILLION TO FLY AND 42 MILLION ON THE ROADS\b0\par\par\uc2 713 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, MANCINI, ABORTION, TRUMP, PROTESTS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 G7 SUMMIT, BIDEN, ABORTION, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATIC SENATORS DEMAND BIDEN TO TAKE "BOLD ACTION"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 34 LEADERS URGING WHITE HOUSE TO DO MORE AMID SCOTUS RULING\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, NEUMANN, SALAS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, INVASION, RUSSIA, KLITSCHKO, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KYIV ROCKED BY RUSSIAN MISSILES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST ATTACK ON THE CAPITAL CITY SINCE EARLY JUNE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, UVALDE, GARZA, GARCIA\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, FOURTH OF JULY, GROSS, FAUL, GODLIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD NUMBER OF AMERICANS EXPECTED TO TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE THAN 11 MILLION TO FLY AND 42 MILLION ON THE ROADS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Turning now to travel outlook for the 4th of July weekend. If this weekend's airport crowds are any indication, it's going to be a very busy holiday.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIRPORT SCREENINGS FRIDAY\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The TSA saying, its screeners handled 2.5 million travelers on Friday alone, the most since before the pandemic. ABC's transportation correspondent Gio Benitez has more on how you can beat the travel crush.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The so-called summer of revenge travel is underway. More than 11 million people will take to the skies over the July 4th holiday and a record 42 million will hit the roads.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW GROSS (AAA SPOKESPERSON)\par \par \uc2 Far more people are going to go this year by car than ever before. And that's surprising given how expensive it is right now with these high gas prices.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the holiday comes after weeks of travel chaos at airports, bad weather and staffing issues at US airlines leading to thousands of cancellations. And it's a risk Matt Fall isn't willing to take, he' driving from Cincinnati to Orlando to avoid any travel headaches.\par \par \uc2 MATT FAUL (TRAVELER)\par \par \uc2 I would rather have a guaranteed vacation and spend a little more time getting there than risk not having a vacation because my flight gets cancelled.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With gas averaging $5 a gallon for regular nationwide, what might a road trip cost? Well, just for gas, a road trip from New York City to Miami could cost just over $270 round trip. Now, that doesn't include tolls and any overnight stays. Round trip airfare on that same route could cost about a hundred dollars more.\par \par \uc2 MATT FAUL (TRAVELER)\par \par \uc2 Even with our increased gas prices right now, when you figure out how much you're spending on an airline ticket, compared to the amount of gas it takes to travel to that location, it might be break even.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAN AHEAD BEFORE TRAVELING\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Experts say it's important to plan ahead, you can avoid heavy traffic by leaving early in day and the first flight out is the most likely to leave on time. But if you're worried your plans could change, travel insurance can provide some peace of mind. Beth Godlin runs a travel insurance company.\par \par \uc2 BETH GODLIN (PRESIDENT AON AFFINITY TRAVEL PRACTICE)\par \par \uc2 Travel insurance is designed to protect against the unforeseen and the unexpected. Take a look at the benefits you're most interested in and take a look at what's excluded.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And remember, if your plans change, the airlines still aren't charging for a changed fee. So, take advantage of that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIPS TO HELP IMPROVE YOUR SUMMER TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In most cases, you can either rebook, you can get a voucher, and you'll only have to pay the difference in fare for the new flight. Guys?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Gio, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS BAN ALTERNATIVES"\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Well, alternatives to abortion following the historic Supreme Court decision, we'll look at the services available for women who carry their babies to term.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RISING DEBT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And growing debt starting to be a major problem for many Americans. The advice for your finances coming up. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 STORY SYNOPSES\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220728ei6q0008l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ALTERNATIVES FOLLOWING ROE V. WADE DECISION WHAT SOME PROGRAMS & INSTITUTIONS ARE OFFERING\b0\par\par\uc2 843 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, MANCINI, ABORTION, TRUMP, PROTESTS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 G7 SUMMIT, BIDEN, ABORTION, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATIC SENATORS DEMAND BIDEN TO TAKE "BOLD ACTION"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 34 LEADERS URGING WHITE HOUSE TO DO MORE AMID SCOTUS RULING\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, NEUMANN, SALAS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, INVASION, RUSSIA, KLITSCHKO, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KYIV ROCKED BY RUSSIAN MISSILES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST ATTACK ON THE CAPITAL CITY SINCE EARLY JUNE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, UVALDE, GARZA, GARCIA\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, FOURTH OF JULY, GROSS, FAUL, GODLIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD NUMBER OF AMERICANS EXPECTED TO TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE THAN 11 MILLION TO FLY AND 42 MILLION ON THE ROADS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "TOP GUN: MAVERICK"\par \par \uc2 GOMPERTS, ABORTION, LORI, WHITLEY, MANSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALTERNATIVES FOLLOWING ROE V. WADE DECISION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT SOME PROGRAMS & INSTITUTIONS ARE OFFERING AMID CONTROVERSIAL RULING\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This morning, we're following the Supreme Court's landmark decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. And we're taking a closer look at the services available for women with unwanted pregnancies who decide to carry their babies to term. ABC's Devin Dwyer joins us now from outside the Supreme Court with more on that. Devin, good morning again.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning again, Whit. Pregnancy support has been a key promise of abortion rights opponents. Now, maternity homes and resource centers are bracing for a surge in demand.\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP WILLIAM E LORI (US CONFERENCE OF CATHOLIC BISHOPS' CMTE)\par \par \uc2 This is not a moment to celebrate. I'm not celebrating.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, Baltimore Archbishop William Lori, the cleric leading the Catholic Church's campaign against abortion, says the real work is just beginning.\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP WILLIAM E LORI (US CONFERENCE OF CATHOLIC BISHOPS' CMTE)\par \par \uc2 The church is not just about bans, not only do we provide services, we are robust advocates for the poor and needy and vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Religious institutions like the Catholic Church have long pledged unconditional emotional and financial aid to women in crisis pregnancies. Now comes a big test.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are the resources there to adequately meet those needs?\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP WILLIAM E LORI (US CONFERENCE OF CATHOLIC BISHOPS' CMTE)\par \par \uc2 I don't think we should underestimate the generosity either of the charities and services we provide or of God's people.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Texas, after the SPA ban of nearly all abortions, a wave of women have been seeking out financial support, 84,000 signing up so far this year with a state program for women with unwanted pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 KASEY WHITLEY (DIR SOCIAL MINISTRY CATHOLIC CHARITIES FORT WORTH TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 This is our community baby closet.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Texas Catholic aid programs are seeing an impact, too.\par \par \uc2 KASEY WHITLEY (DIR SOCIAL MINISTRY CATHOLIC CHARITIES FORT WORTH TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Having a waitlist now, we're already trying to gear up and make sure that we can meet the current need in addition to any increase that we might see.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kasey Whitley oversees the Gabriel Project in Fort Worth that provides women with diapers and formula and a volunteer angel to give moral support. Critics say it's far from enough.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE MANSON (PRESIDENT \uc2 CATHOLICS FOR CHOICE\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 The amount of care and social work and life skill training that these women need is massive.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Many health care providers worry desperate women will still take desperate measures to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 DR REBECCA GOMPERTS (FOUNDER & DIRECTOR WOMEN ON WAVES/AID ACCESS)\par \par \uc2 What happens is what happens everywhere in the world. And that is, that there will be a huge underground market.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 American Public Health Association\uc2  saying, abortion bans will lead to elevated risk of maternal mortality and infant mortality.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you worry that this transition to less abortion access in America could end up harming some women?\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP WILLIAM E LORI (US CONFERENCE OF CATHOLIC BISHOPS' CMTE)\par \par \uc2 Since Roe v. Wade, it's been 63 million abortions. That's a lot, a lot of loss of life.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Critics say abortion opponents must now step up calls for social programs that support life, like a higher minimum wage, nutrition assistance and paid family leave. States with the most restrictive abortion laws have invested the least in policies and programs for women and children.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE MANSON (PRESIDENT \uc2 CATHOLICS FOR CHOICE\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 To deny someone what is for them essential care is wrong. I think it's a sin.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Archbishop Lori told me the Catholic Church, one of the most influential religious institutions in America, is fully behind expansion of those social safety net programs. But now, guys, after the court's decision, many watching closely to see whether abortion opponents can deliver. Janai?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And they will be watching. Devin, we appreciate you pulling double duty for us.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220728ei6q0008n\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MIXED EMOTIONS OVER ABORTION RULING AUTHORITIES WARN OF POSSIBLE VIOLENCE\b0\par\par\uc2 517 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, MANCINI, ABORTION, TRUMP, PROTESTS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 G7 SUMMIT, BIDEN, ABORTION, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATIC SENATORS DEMAND BIDEN TO TAKE "BOLD ACTION"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 34 LEADERS URGING WHITE HOUSE TO DO MORE AMID SCOTUS RULING\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, NEUMANN, SALAS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the protests across the country in response to the Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIXED EMOTIONS OVER ABORTION RULING ACROSS THE COUNTRY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTHORITIES WARN OF POSSIBLE VIOLENCE FOR WEEKS AMID RULING\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ABC's Aaron Katersky joins us with the latest. Good morning to you, Aaron.\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Eva, good morning to you. Police have already been worried about extremist threats to pride marches like the one today here in New York City, now they fear Friday's abortion ruling is only adding fuel to the fire.\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Many protests across the country this weekend following the Supreme Court's Roe v. Wade reversal were largely peaceful, but it's incidents like these that have law enforcement officials on high alert. An off-duty Providence, Rhode Island, police officer who's running for office as a Republican seen here punching his Democratic opponent at an abortion protest. That officer is now suspended and dropping out of his race. In Arizona, police used tear gas to dispersed abortion rights protesters who breached the state Capitol. And in Iowa, a pickup truck driver allegedly struck an abortion rights demonstrator after an argument. The concern from federal law enforcement officials is that extremists on the far left and the far right are ceasing this divisive moment to incite violence.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH NEUMANN (FORMER ASSISTANT SECRETARY DHS)\par \par \uc2 We're seeing in chatter online in domestic violence extremist pages that indicate both white nationalists, anti-government extremists view this as a target of opportunity.\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In a bulletin obtained by ABC News, the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  said the abortion ruling could spur violence for weeks with abortion providers, churches and judges most at risk.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE ESTHER SALAS (US DISTRICT COURT NEW JERSEY)\par \par \uc2 One of my biggest fears is that somebody else would lose their life or their loved one.\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Judge Esther Salas, whose son was murdered by a man targeting her, has been urging Congress to take action to protect judges.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE ESTHER SALAS (US DISTRICT COURT NEW JERSEY)\par \par \uc2 We know that my son would expect nothing more than for us to not only survive in this world, but he wants us to thrive.\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Congress has passed legislation to protect Supreme Court justices and their families, but no amount of security can compensate for the hostility that's now coursing through the country. And, Janai, it's not just the abortion ruling. The Supreme Court's concealed carry decision has led for calls on the far right for protesters to arrive armed. Janai?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you said it right, Aaron, there's so much hostility.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220728ei6q0008g\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INCREASED DEBT AMID RISING RATES AMERICANS STRETCHING THEIR DOLLARS\b0\par\par\uc2 704 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, MANCINI, ABORTION, TRUMP, PROTESTS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 G7 SUMMIT, BIDEN, ABORTION, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATIC SENATORS DEMAND BIDEN TO TAKE "BOLD ACTION"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 34 LEADERS URGING WHITE HOUSE TO DO MORE AMID SCOTUS RULING\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, NEUMANN, SALAS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, INVASION, RUSSIA, KLITSCHKO, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KYIV ROCKED BY RUSSIAN MISSILES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST ATTACK ON THE CAPITAL CITY SINCE EARLY JUNE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, UVALDE, GARZA, GARCIA\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, FOURTH OF JULY, GROSS, FAUL, GODLIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD NUMBER OF AMERICANS EXPECTED TO TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE THAN 11 MILLION TO FLY AND 42 MILLION ON THE ROADS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "TOP GUN: MAVERICK"\par \par \uc2 GOMPERTS, ABORTION, LORI, WHITLEY, MANSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALTERNATIVES FOLLOWING ROE V. WADE DECISION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT SOME PROGRAMS & INSTITUTIONS ARE OFFERING AMID CONTROVERSIAL RULING\par \par \uc2 CREDIT CARDS, DEBT, MCBRIDE, TIPS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Welcome back to "GMA." And how Americans are running up the debt on their credit cards as inflation climbs.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INCREASED DEBT AMID RISING RATES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICANS STRETCHING THEIR DOLLARS, CHARGING MORE ON CREDIT CARDS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It can be tempting to rely on plastic, but you will pay dearly in the end. Here's ABC's Deirdre Bolton.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, soaring inflation forcing millions of households to stretch their dollars further by putting charges on their credit cards. Americans carry an average of $6,194 in credit card debt. In May of this year, Americans had a collective balance of $856 billion, up from $757 billion in May 2021.\par \par \uc2 GREG MCBRIDE (CHIEF FINANCIAL ANALYST BANKRATE.COM)\par \par \uc2 We're starting to see credit card debt move up. We're also starting to see that savings that have been accumulated over the past couple of years during the pandemic start to run down.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) According to Bank Rate, more than 1 in 3 Americans have less emergency savings now than one year ago. And with \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics calculating that American households are spending an additional $341 month to purchase the same goods and services compared to 2021, the rising prices are upending the budgets of millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 LILY RN BUDGETS (TIKTOK INFLUENCER)\par \par \uc2 It's really scary because it doesn't seem to get better any time soon.\par \par \uc2 GREG MCBRIDE (CHIEF FINANCIAL ANALYST BANKRATE.COM)\par \par \uc2 Inflation is hitting low to moderate income households the hardest. And that credit card debt is going up at a time that interest rates are also going up.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CREDIT CARD DELINQUENCIES\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Experts are also tracking credit card delinquencies. And while they are up, they're still near record lows. But carrying over that existing balance is getting more expensive. The average credit card interest rate is currently just shy of 17% and only expected to climb. In this economy, there is little room for error. And for many, getting out of debt can seem overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 LILY RN BUDGETS (TIKTOK INFLUENCER)\par \par \uc2 We are stuffing my cash envelopes.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Viral TikToker, Lily, RN Budgets, says she racked up over $20,000 in credit card debt while attending nursing school, but managed to pay it all off by using a technique called cash stuffing.\par \par \uc2 LILY RN BUDGETS (TIKTOK INFLUENCER)\par \par \uc2 Cash stuffing is essentially taking all of your spending that you would do like on a credit card or debit card, for example, and using strictly cash. And basically it's just a way to keep yourself more disciplined and stay within a budget rather than just swiping a card recklessly.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) If you do have to use a credit card, remember to call the company and negotiate a lower rate. If you have good credit, they will want to work with you.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, that's good to know. Deirdre, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STONEWALL'S LEGACY\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Still coming up here on "Good Morning America," Eva sits down with the owners of New York City's historic Stonewall Inn on the importance of the landmark to the LGBTQ+ community.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220728ei6q0008p\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WEATHER\b0\par\par\uc2 558 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, MANCINI, ABORTION, TRUMP, PROTESTS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 G7 SUMMIT, BIDEN, ABORTION, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATIC SENATORS DEMAND BIDEN TO TAKE "BOLD ACTION"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 34 LEADERS URGING WHITE HOUSE TO DO MORE AMID SCOTUS RULING\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, NEUMANN, SALAS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, INVASION, RUSSIA, KLITSCHKO, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KYIV ROCKED BY RUSSIAN MISSILES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST ATTACK ON THE CAPITAL CITY SINCE EARLY JUNE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, UVALDE, GARZA, GARCIA\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, FOURTH OF JULY, GROSS, FAUL, GODLIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD NUMBER OF AMERICANS EXPECTED TO TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE THAN 11 MILLION TO FLY AND 42 MILLION ON THE ROADS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "TOP GUN: MAVERICK"\par \par \uc2 GOMPERTS, ABORTION, LORI, WHITLEY, MANSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALTERNATIVES FOLLOWING ROE V. WADE DECISION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT SOME PROGRAMS & INSTITUTIONS ARE OFFERING AMID CONTROVERSIAL RULING\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Time now, though, for a check of the weather. And Greg Dutra from WLS, our ABC station in Chicago, is back with more. How are you, Greg?\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm doing pretty well. And it's a day a lot of parents are maybe heading to T-ball games or soccer. Imagine you're sitting at the game and then this happens.\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This is a dust devil, that's just east of Nashville and that things are ripping pretty good. Everybody's are just watching it, like, oh, that's kind of neat. And it gets the trash can and everybody's like, oh, you know what, maybe it's time to get out of here. And those can get pretty serious despite not being actual tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MONSOON STORMS\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Monsoon moisture, boy, do they need the moisture out there in Tennessee. But it happens in the four corners. Two to three inches of rain on the western slope of the Rocky Mountains and then into the northern New Mexico. Check out this picture from my friend Jennifer Broome in Denver. Nice, beautiful rainbow, which transitions well to our pride forecast.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRIDE WEEKEND\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A great day for many of us across the nation, especially across the Midwest and to Denver. 72 degrees there, hot in the Pacific Northwest. And let's take a look at your forecast now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONAL MAP\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back in Chicago, we just had a cold front move through, the clouds are finally getting out of here. It's going to be a great day for pride again not only in Chicago but all the way up through New England and into the west. I'll send it back to you, guys.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I see your sleeves rolled up because it's warming up out there. Greg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It's humid. It's humid, yeah.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Watch out for the hair.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RISING DEBT\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Well, coming up on "Good Morning America" credit card debt rising in this country, some advice that could save you some money.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LUPITA'S FASHION SHOW\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And then, Lupita Nyong'o turns a bad day at the airport into a fashion show. That's coming up in "Pop News."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220728ei6q0008o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FINAL FUNERAL FOLLOWING UVALDE MASSACRE HEARINGS INTO POLICE RESPONSE TO RESUME TOMORROW\b0\par\par\uc2 555 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, MANCINI, ABORTION, TRUMP, PROTESTS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 G7 SUMMIT, BIDEN, ABORTION, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATIC SENATORS DEMAND BIDEN TO TAKE "BOLD ACTION"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 34 LEADERS URGING WHITE HOUSE TO DO MORE AMID SCOTUS RULING\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, NEUMANN, SALAS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, INVASION, RUSSIA, KLITSCHKO, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KYIV ROCKED BY RUSSIAN MISSILES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST ATTACK ON THE CAPITAL CITY SINCE EARLY JUNE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, UVALDE, GARZA, GARCIA\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, now to Texas and the final school massacre victim laid to rest in Uvalde.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FINAL FUNERAL FOLLOWING UVALDE MASSACRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEARINGS INTO POLICE RESPONSE TO RESUME TOMORROW\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The funeral coming as more parents are speaking out about the police response to the shooting and as hearings resume tomorrow. ABC's Marcus Moore has more from Dallas.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MOORE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A month later, Kimberly and Angel Garcia are still yearning for answers.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY GARCIA (PARENT OF AMERIE GARZA)\par \par \uc2 I just miss her. I just start talking about her and it's just so hard just to know that I don't have her anymore.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MOORE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Ten-year-old Amerie Jo Garza was one of the 19 children killed in Uvalde, May 24th, along with two of their beloved teachers. Like so many other desperate parents, her stepfather, Angel, a medic, quickly responding to calls at work that day at Robb Elementary.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARCIA (PARENT OF AMERIE GARZA)\par \par \uc2 I don't get how you can listen to those kids and not go save them.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MOORE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) There are also calls for accountability over the law enforcement response to the massacre. And since the shooting much of the focus of the public has been place squarely on the school district police chief, Pete Arredondo. Arredondo has defended his actions and said in an interview that he didn't consider himself the commanding officer on the scene that day.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY GARCIA (PARENT OF AMERIE GARZA)\par \par \uc2 There are so many officers at that school and they're so busy trying to keep us away.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MOORE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do you want us to know about Amerie?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY GARCIA (PARENT OF AMERIE GARZA)\par \par \uc2 She always made sure that people knew that they had a friend.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MOORE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Amerie's family now cherishing the art that she crafted with her innocent hands that now bring comfort to their bleeding hearts. Angel writing, this open letter to Amerie after her death.\par \par \uc2 ANGEL GARCIA (PARENT OF AMERIE GARZA)\par \par \uc2 I know with the talent you had, my love, you could have gone anywhere. It hurts me the most I won't get to see who you would become.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MOORE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Family members describe Amerie Jo this morning as a brave child and a natural protector. In fact, they say she was trying to call 911 before she was shot and killed. And so, for this 10-year-old girl, even in her final moments, she was thinking of others, trying to help her friends and her teachers. Guys?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Incredible. And our hearts are so with all of those families in that community impacted. Marcus, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220728ei6q0008j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DEMOCRATIC SENATORS DEMAND BIDEN TO TAKE "BOLD ACTION" 34 LEADERS URGING WHITE HOUSE TO DO MORE\b0\par\par\uc2 655 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, MANCINI, ABORTION, TRUMP, PROTESTS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 G7 SUMMIT, BIDEN, ABORTION, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATIC SENATORS DEMAND BIDEN TO TAKE "BOLD ACTION"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 34 LEADERS URGING WHITE HOUSE TO DO MORE AMID SCOTUS RULING\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And turning to President Biden at the \uc2 G7\uc2  summit in Germany, but still facing questions about the abortion battle back home. A group of Senate Democrats now urging him to take action. ABC's chief White House correspondent Cecilia Vega joins us from Austria with more. Cecilia, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Whit, good morning to you. It's day one of these meetings here with allies in the Alps. But I got to tell you, it really is that history unfolding back at home that is hanging over this entire summit.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) You heard the President say just before he left the United States that he thinks that the Supreme Court has made some terrible decisions lately. He says, this is going to be the issue for the midterms. But this morning, he is taking some heat from some members from his own party. Take a look at this, we've got 34 Senate Democrats, you just mentioned, writing this letter to the President calling on him to take immediate action to use the full force of the federal government to protect access to abortion in the United States. They go on to say, there is no time to waste, writing, "You have the power to fight back and lead a national response to this devastating decision, so we call on you to take every step available to your administration."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STEPS BIDEN ADMINISTRATION IS TAKING\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) You know, so far, the administration has said this, take a look, that it will protect the right to cross state lines. It will protect the right to obtain the abortion pill. That it will protect federal agencies and employees who carry out reproductive health services from any criminal or civil liability.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit, we have been talking about this, though, there's nothing the President can do in terms of an executive order that would restore the rights that Roe had in place. But certainly, right now, this morning here, that call for him to do more is hanging over this summit as it kicks off. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, this battle far from over. And, Cecilia, while we have you here, of course, President Biden is there for the \uc2 G7\uc2  summit. We know Russia's invasion of Ukraine also topping the agenda as he sits down with allies.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES MEETING IN GERMANY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: U.S. ANNOUNCES BAN ON RUSSIAN GOLD IMPORTS\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. In fact, he just wrapped not too long ago, his very first meeting of the morning with the German chancellor, \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And a big theme that the President talked about in that meeting is keeping allies united. But here, we could already be starting to see some cracks. The United States announced today that it, along with some other allies, is going to ban Russian gold imports. That's just another attempt to further exclude Russia from the global economy, gold being Russia's second largest export.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But I got to tell you, Whit, so far, a number of allies have not signed on to that. Eva, the President right now hoping that others do, but they are working this week to send this theme out, this message out that they want these allies to stay united, especially when it comes to sanctions and military aid, Eva.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Cecilia Vega for us there in Austria. Thank you, Cecilia.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220728ei6q0008f\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM SD TRIGGER LAWS BAN NEARLY ALL ABORT. AFTER RULING\b0\par\par\uc2 1,598 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , ROE, ABORTION, BIDEN, ABC NEWS/WAPO, POLL\par \par \uc2 NOEM, SOUTH DAKOTA, ABORTION, BAN, VOTE, PILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROE V. WADE OVERTURNED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOUTH DAKOTA TRIGGER LAWS BAN NEARLY ALL ABORTIONS AFTER RULING\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Joining me now is \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , the Governor of South Dakota, one of the 13 states with trigger laws, and as of Friday, where abortion is no longer legal. Thanks for joining us this morning, Governor. You say this ruling is the answer to your prayers. So, abortion is now illegal in your state, unless the mother's life is in danger. There is no exception for rape, no exception for incest. What should the punishment be for women who receive abortions or doctors and anyone who assists them?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BAN DOES NOT INCLUDE EXCEPTIONS FOR RAPE OR INCEST\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 Well, Martha, I don't believe there should be any punishment for women, ever, that are in a crisis situation or have an unplanned pregnancy, and South Dakota has been strong on, on that argument. What I would say is this was wonderful news in the defense of life. Every life is precious. South Dakota had a statute on the books that said that abortion would be illegal, except to save the life of a mother, should Roe v. Wade be overturned. And so, that is how it stands here today, but I anticipate there'll be more debate and discussion.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 What was interesting about the Supreme Court decision is that it gave the authority back to the states to make these decisions. So, now that this decision's been made, it'll be up to each of the states and the state legislators and the people there to talk to their elected representatives about what their laws look like closer to home.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  calls the Supreme Court decision a brazen violation of patients' rights, saying that states that end legal abortion will not end abortion, they will end safe abortion, risking devastating consequences, including patient's lives. You just heard Dr. Ashton say the decision will affect women of color to an even greater degree.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 Well, what's interesting to me, Martha, is that I've talked to many doctors who say when they do procedures on babies in the womb, or when they work on babies that have not been born yet, that those babies are patients. So, they define them as patients, and if they defend patients' rights, they should be defining and defending that life that's in the womb, as well. And it is an individual, and every life has value. So, in South Dakota, we're working...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But we're talking about the mothers, as well.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 ...that is life...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're talking about the mothers, as well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW IS SOUTH DAKOTA PLANNING TO HELP EXPECTANT MOTHERS AND FAMILIES?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 Yes, we are, and that's what I'm, that's what I'm getting to, is this website that we have connects those mothers that are in this situation with all the resources that they may need during that pregnancy to support them financially, get them the health care that they would need, make sure they're connecting with pregnancy centers, and also making sure that they can connect with adoptive families, should they choose to give that baby up for adoption. But we want to help support these mothers. I think we can do better, in this country, making sure that we're walking alongside them in these situations and making sure that they do have the type of healthcare and the support, financially, that they need.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, look, let's look at that support. The nonpartisan \uc2 Commonwealth Fund\uc2 , the social policy think tank, says the 14 states that have the most restrictive abortion laws, including South Dakota, invest the least in policies and programs for women and children. So, what, what do you mean when you say these mothers will never be alone?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 Well, I would say that the facts on the ground are that South Dakota's doing a lot to coordinate with nonprofits, with churches, and then, also the state, in a new way, by launching this website and committing to in-legislative session to support these mothers is incredibly powerful. You know, this is a conversation that we have a change now at the federal level. The states will be each evaluating how they approach this situation. Getting health care to mothers, helping them during a time of crisis is incredibly important. And these states, remember, Martha, are overwhelmed, right now, with other situations that are going on, with what Joe Biden is doing to the energy crisis in this country, what inflation is doing in this country, our national security...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I - want to stick with abortion. I want to go back to abortion, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 ...there's a lot of things that need to be addressed in this country today.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You, you say every abortion always has two victims, the unborn child and the mother. What would you say to adult women in this country who do not feel they are in any way victims, in fact they consider choices they make with their own bodies no one's business but theirs and their doctor's?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 I would encouraged them to continue to follow the science, to continue to follow what we know to be true today with the technology that's been advanced and to really look the Supreme Court decision for what it is. Take the sensational of pundits' commentary out of it and look at the fact that what the Supreme Court did was fix a wrong decision that was made many years ago and now give the power back to the states. What they said is that the Constitution does not guarantee a right to an abortion. That means that each state will make the decision for themselves on what their laws look like, and that those women have an opportunity to speak to their elected officials there closer to home to make sure that their laws reflect what they value.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOUTH DAKOTA PREVIOUSLY VOTED DOWN BILL TO COMPLETELY BAN ABORTION\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In, in 2006, and again in 2008, South Dakota voters voted down ballot initiatives for a complete ban on abortions. Would you be willing to let the voters of the state decide the issue again?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 They decide the issue every single year when we come to legislative session. They, they vote for their representatives to come to session. We have passed many pro-life bills since I've been governor of the state of South Dakota. We've had heated debates and good discussions on the policy of what really is something that make sure every life is precious in the state of South Dakota, that we're making sure that we are providing those types of resources and doing more, even, in the future to help those mothers and those families, make sure that they're raising their babies or connecting them with families who would want to, should they choose adoption.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and what about medication abortions, the so-called abortion pill? How will you know if women receive those in the mail, and should those women be prosecuted?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IS A BAN ON FDA-APPROVED MEDICATION AND ABORTION PILLS NEXT?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 I don't believe women should ever be prosecuted. I don't believe that, that mothers in this situation never be prosecuted. Now, doctors who knowingly violate the law, they should be prosecuted, definitely. And we've addressed (inaudible) abortion...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But the pill is okay? You're saying the pill's okay?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 Again, Martha, you're interrupting, but I, I am answering your question. I don't believe that the telemedicine abortions are safe for individuals, for women to conduct at home. Many times they're doing it unsupervised. It's a medical procedure, and so I do believe that there should be a physician supervision in place when that is being conducted by any individual.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and just one more question. What if your constituents travel to another state to get an abortion?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOUTH DAKOTA CLOSES ALL ABORTION CLINICS STATEWIDE\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 You know, that certainly isn't addressed in our statute today, and so I think that's things that they'll be debate about. But also, we're having lots of debates in South Dakota. How fundamentally our life has changed the last couple of years, just with increase costs and inflation and what this administration is doing to every single family's budget and life right now going on in this country.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thanks very much for joining us this morning, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Martha. Have a wonderful day.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220629ei6q00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 3,267 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , ROE, ABORTION, BIDEN, ABC NEWS/WAPO, POLL\par \par \uc2 NOEM, SOUTH DAKOTA, ABORTION, BAN, VOTE, PILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROE V. WADE OVERTURNED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOUTH DAKOTA TRIGGER LAWS BAN NEARLY ALL ABORTIONS AFTER RULING\par \par \uc2 WARREN, ROE V WADE, SCOTUS, BIDEN, CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 RASKIN, JAN 6, TRUMP, MOSS, CLARK, POLL, BOWERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, SCOTUS, MIDTERMS, GUNS, JAN 6\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Millions of women in America will go to bed tonight without access to the health care and reproductive care that they had this morning, without access to the same health care, or reproductive health care that their mothers and grandmothers had for 50 years.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Vice President Harris reacting to Roe's reversal Friday. Joining us now for more on that landmark decision, former DNC chair and ABC contributor Donna Brazile, ABC News contributor and "Dispatch" staff writer Sarah Isgur, "USA Today" chief Washington, Washington bureau chief Susan Page, and chief Washington correspondent and "This Week" co-anchor, \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2 . Welcome to all of you. And Donna, I want to start with you and your reaction to the ruling.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's hard. I was, I was devastated. I mean, yes, we knew for over 58 days, eight weeks, that Justice Alito's leaked draft may become the actual document, but still, it was - it was horrible. Horrible. When have rights that have ever been given been taken away so abruptly? In my home state of Louisiana, a trigger state, a woman today who may be faced with something she can't handle or deal with, she has to find the resources to drive 600 miles. The closest place is in Illinois, or if she heads east, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) If one of my nieces, God forbid, or cousins is raped, what recourse will they have? This is a personal decision. And what worries all of us, and women should be worried, men too. There should be no second-class citizens in the United States of America. And today, I feel as though we have gone back in time, not just 50 years, but we've gone even further. Because for poor women, for Black women, for women of color, where it, it has been hard to have reproductive health care, to have access to affordable, and now we have, we've lost this right, and it's a sad day for America.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sarah, how do you see this?\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Constitution is a contract, and most of it was meant to be majoritarian. We were supposed to put most things to elections, with a few things reserved, counter-majoritarian, free speech, the freedom of religion, and the question here was, which one was abortion? Was it a counter-majoritarian issue, or was it something to be put to a vote? You know, for the, for the protests that you showed earlier in the show, those were in states were people have voted on it, and they voted to protect that right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We've been told for years how popular Roe v. Wade is, how popular abortion is. Well, then, great. Women make up the majority of voters in this country. And yet, in so many of these states, the vote has been to restrict it to something much closer to the rest of Europe, to the rest of Western countries, 12 weeks, 15 weeks. That's what Mississippi now has. I think that, legally, even most liberal scholars, \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , Akhil Amar, these are pro-choice people, believed that Roe v. Wade was wrongly decided. And then the question is, do you keep a decision on the books that's wrong, that's just incorrect about what the Constitution says?\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We didn't do it in Plessy. We didn't do it when it came to Obergefell, Griswold. All these other cases, we overturned precedent. Every justice on the Court has voted to overturn precedent, and they did this week.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2 , coming off of Sarah there, this, this has obviously been something that has rallied Republicans. What do Democrats do now, and can they keep the passion of Donna going?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, you know, for nearly half a century, Roe v. Wade kept a lid on this issue, politically. You had activist groups on each side, but it wasn't a central issue in campaigns. You had Republicans who weren't necessarily true believers on this issue who could appeal to anti-abortion groups by declaring themselves pro-life without having to worry about the consequences of what that means, because Roe v. Wade meant it was impossible to ban abortion. So, now you have Republican governors out there defending, explaining why there shouldn't be an exception for rape.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT EFFECTS WILL ABORTION AND GUN RULINGS HAVE ON MIDTERMS?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And this is - a story that's going to continue to play out. I mean, will there be prosecutions of women who cross state lines to get abortion, or even investigations of, of miscarriages? Democratic strategists who are working on the key midterm races tell me that they see this issue breaking through now, and actually starting to see it breaking through when the, when the draft opinion leaked in a way that it has never before. And you know who agrees with that, Martha? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had been privately telling people once that draft opinion leaked, that this would be bad for Republicans in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Susan, you wrote back in May, when the draft came out, that there are Democrats who hope the Supreme Court decision would shake the midterms and Republicans who feared it would. How do you see it, now?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 I think, I think it's an open question. You know, there is enormous energy among Democrats against this ruling. There is a big risk for Republicans who are at odds with American public opinion about whether abortion rights, whether access to abortion is a right for Americans. But Americans have a lot of things on their mind this year. And when we talked to them in a poll that we put out this week, by two-to-one, Americans who support Roe v. Wade said the economy is a bigger issue for them in deciding how they're going to vote in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Now, maybe the response to the particular ruling and some of the stories that we are going to see coming out of these states that, as of today, have restricted abortion in a serious way, maybe that will change that dynamic. But some, some key Democrats do worry that this is less a galvanizing issue for November than they hoped.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and that's exactly right, Donna. People have so much more on their minds. I, I was struck by the picture from the Supreme Court this morning. There weren't any protesters out there. They disbursed pretty quickly, there. Does this resonate over all issues?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS GUARANTEES RIGHT TO OPENLY CARRY GUNS IN PUBLIC\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, look, the American people have a lot on their mind, and I should say a lot on their plate, rising gas prices, of course, going to the grocery store, but yes, this is a defining issue. It's is a defining issue for those who believe that we should have the freedom to choose. I mean, look at the other Supreme Court decision this week on guns. I mean, you call it a contract? I call it a failure of moral responsibility of the Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And what \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said, Justice Thomas said that they could go further. Hell, yeah. It's a defining issue, but it's like every other issue in American politics. For the moment, it's hot. It might cool off, but in the back of every woman's mind, every woman's mind, and probably a bunch of men, this is going to be a defining issue this fall.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sarah, do you agree? And how do Republicans handle this, because of the polling?\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, I think we've seen the, the limits of issue polling. For instance, let's take guns. Background checks will poll at 83, 90 percent, but when it's on the ballot, and I don't mean candidates, I mean ballot measures in liberal states, California, Oregon, Nevada, they lose. They got fewer votes than \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  did in 2016. Voters are saying one thing to pollsters and a very different thing. Take the Texas special election, sorry, the, the runoff in the Democratic primary in Texas three weeks ago, you had the last pro-life House Democrat in a challenge against a pro-choice Democrat.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) If elections are decided whether people show up who otherwise are going to stay home or they change their vote based on an issue, Democrats had the opportunity in the wake of the draft opinion, when this was getting so much attention. The pro-life Democrat won. I do think Republicans have an issue, going too far, you know, this cosplay that's been going on, because of Roe v. Wade, like Jon said, but the idea that this somehow a winning issue for Democrats, there's just no data to support that, right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and I do, I, I want to turn to guns, in the, in the short amount of time we have. They passed a gun rights bill on the Hill, bipartisan support, first time in decades and decades that they've done anything. Is it enough, Jon?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN SIGNS BREAKTHROUGH BIPARTISAN GUN CONTROL BILL INTO LAW\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, it's, it's not enough, but it's big deal, Martha, and, and the way it was done, is, it was, was fascinating. You saw Chris Murphy, who has, you know, some, some views on, on gun rights that are, that are pretty far out, you know, too, but Republicans have very little agreement with him. He put aside virtually everything that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  called for in his speech after Uvalde. There was no, nothing in this to, to ban assault weapons, to raise the age for the purchase of, of rifles, no bans on ammunition, none of that stuff.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He wanted to actually get something done. And, actually, Martha, they brought the \uc2 NRA\uc2  in on this. The \uc2 NRA\uc2  opposed this, but not vocally, because they were actually involved in the drafting, as was every town, you know, the, the pro-gun safety group. So, this, this was a, this was a victory for bipartisanship and - and a victory, it will have an impact, I mean, you, you know - increasing the length of time for background checks for people under the age of 21, it's a big deal. The, the extra resources for red flag laws, this is a big deal. It's no where near as far as somebody like Chris Murphy wanted to go, but he got something done.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Susan, but back to guns and the Supreme Court. A lot of people are saying, kind of a mixed message there, because it'll now be easier to carry a gun, and the court talked about states' rights. Let me, let me read what the former solicitor, General \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , tweeted right before the decision was announced. He says, "It's going to be very weird if Supreme Court ends a constitutional right to obtain an abortion," saying it should be left to the states to decide right after it just imposed a constitutional right to concealed carry of firearms, saying it cannot be left to the states to decide. So, so what happened? How do you read that?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 So...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, obviously you have an amendment, the Second Amendment.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Look at these two issues together, and it's usually one conclusion you draw is, there is no way you can argue that the Supreme Court is now not just another partisan player in national politics, the idea that it's a dispassionate group of nine people who are going to all just look at the law. It seems to me that that has been shredded, and what we're left with is a sense that here's just another place, another polarized place where politics is what matters.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 The President who appoints you is what matters. You get to the (inaudible) the confirmation process by saying as little as possible about the issues that matter, and then you are on that court. One difference from this and the other two branches of government, lifetime appointment. And the justices who made this decision are mostly in their 50s. They're going to be there for a long time.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, they will be. And we're going to be right back. We have lots more to talk about, especially January 6th. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we are back with the Roundtable. And Jon, we got to talk about the January 6th Committee. Powerful and emotional testimony, this week. You heard Congressman Raskin. Did they accomplish what they needed to accomplish?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Way too early to tell, Martha, but, look, they have given se a series of powerful presentations that made it clear that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was ultimately responsible for what happened on January 6th, and that it wasn't just the attack on the Capitol. They went through in methodically using Republicans, using people who worked for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who were appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to make the case to show that what he was saying about the election was not true, that when the violence broke out on January 6th, he did absolutely nothing to stop it, despite the fact that he was urged and pleaded to by the people around him to do something. This has to have an impact over time.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DID JANUARY 6 COMMITTEE MAKE THE CASE TO AMERICAN PUBLIC?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, but Susan, can you really imagine legal action against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Yes, I think it's possible. It would be a big step. Obviously, the Justice Department and the Attorney General has been very cautious on this point. But yes, I think it's possible that a series of people will be held individually responsible and in, we don't have a history in our country of going after former presidents legally, but we haven't quite had a situation like this in our history before, either.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and what would that do to the country, do you think?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE ("\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. That's one reason we have been cautious about pursuing this path in the past and why there's concern about doing it now, because as divided and polarized and angry as our country is now, that would be one more - one more fuel on that fire.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Donna, I, I have to go back to Rusty Bowers, again, because that was such incredible...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Powerful.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...powerful testimony, and he says if, if Trump runs against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , he would vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  again. So...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...so what is the real impact, in your view of this?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOWERS: TRUMP REQUESTED ALTERNATIVE, FAVORABLE ELECTORS\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...it's, it's tragic that, that the, the former president's big lie has left us so divided, but also left the country, part of the country that believes that the only way to resolve this is through threats, through intimidation. It's, it's sad to, to see that he would still vote for the President. He believed in one candidate. That's fine. But this is about our system of government. It's about the electoral count. And the fact that people are being threatened, as we speak, simply because they're not embracing the Big Lie, he should pay some, I, when I say he, the President, the former president should pay a consequence for his lies.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sarah, do you think any minds have been changed or might be changed because of these hearings?\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, but I also don't think it was the purpose of them. I think these hearings really are for prosperity. The committee is at its best when it's not talking about January 6th, as I think we use it colloquially, which is an attack on the Capitol, violence at the Capitol. The committee was at its best when it was talking about a United States president who did not want to stop being president regardless of the outcome of an election.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He called the Department of Justice. He called his vice president. He called Georgia election officials to say, "I'm staying here. Even though I know I don't have legal arguments that any of the current Supreme Court justices will agree with, I'm going to stay, anyway." I think the committee could've been better if they had had a more adversarial process, Republicans who disagreed with the overall narrative. But, again, I think this is more for prosperity. It's not about the midterm elections. It's not about changing minds, right now.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And they've been remarkably - bipartisan even though the, even though the makeup of it was a partisan...\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Trump officials...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...and...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and we've got one or one, two more, actually...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, two more hearings...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...coming up with, with, we've got about five seconds here, Jon. But what's, what's expected?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The, the most dramatic one will be the final hearing on the 187 minutes that the Capitol was under attack, and what Trump was doing and what he wasn't doing.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, we will all stand by to see that. Thanks for, so much for joining us, this morning.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's all for us today. ABC will have continuous coverage of the fallout from Supreme Court's abortion ruling on ABC News live, ABCNews.com, and of course later tonight on "World News." Thanks for - thanks for sharing part of your Sunday with us. Good day.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220629ei6q00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMIE RASKIN TRUMP TOLD DOJ TO CALL ELECTION CORRUPT\b0\par\par\uc2 1,525 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , ROE, ABORTION, BIDEN, ABC NEWS/WAPO, POLL\par \par \uc2 NOEM, SOUTH DAKOTA, ABORTION, BAN, VOTE, PILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROE V. WADE OVERTURNED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOUTH DAKOTA TRIGGER LAWS BAN NEARLY ALL ABORTIONS AFTER RULING\par \par \uc2 WARREN, ROE V WADE, SCOTUS, BIDEN, CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 RASKIN, JAN 6, TRUMP, MOSS, CLARK, POLL, BOWERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 SHAYE MOSS (GEORGIA ELECTION WORKER)\par \par \uc2 It's turned my life upside-down. Um, I no longer give out my business card. I don't transfer calls. I, um, don't want anyone knowing my name. I don't want to go anywhere with my mom, 'cause she might yell my name out over the grocery aisle or something. I don't go to the grocery store, at all. I haven't been anywhere, at all.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JAN 6 TESTIMONY: TRUMP TOLD DOJ TO CALL ELECTION UPT\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Former Georgia election worker Shaye Moss recounting the effects of former President Trump's personal attacks on her and her mother, as he worked to reverse the 2020 election. It comes as the January 6th Committee says new evidence and a flurry of tips of necessitate additional hearings. Congressman Jamie Raskin is a member of the Select Committee and joins me this morning. Good to see you this morning, sir. You had detailed testimony this week with a Trump Department of Justice official saying Trump directly told the DOJ to just say the election was corrupt and leave the rest to me and the Republican congressmen. Pretty clear what direction you're going, here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC THIS WEEK: EXCLUSIVE\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMIE RASKIN (DEM-MD) (JAN 6 COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, we saw a series of successive shakedowns of the election officials, of secretaries of state like \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  in Georgia, state legislative officials, and we saw lot of heroes, people who hung tough like Shaye Moss and were not willing to be deterred from doing their public duties. We saw the same thing at the Department of Justice. I mean, those were Trump's own appointees who were telling him they could not do what he was asking them to do. They could not claim that there was fraud and corruption in the election when there wasn't.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMIE RASKIN (DEM-MD) (JAN 6 COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 But all of it is zeroing in on Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , to get him to betray his constitutional oath, to step outside of his role, and then to unilaterally reject Electoral College votes so, the, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's friends in the House could simply vote him in through a contingent election.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Your, your committee also looked at the role of Jeffrey Clark, a former Justice Department official, in trying to overturn the election. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  conducted an early morning search of his home this week. Why now? And is that one of the reasons another hearing is coming up and may be delayed?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FBI SEARCH HOME OF FORMER DOJ OFFICIAL JEFFREY CLARK\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMIE RASKIN (DEM-MD) (JAN 6 COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, Jeffrey Clark was a, a Trump loyalist, a flunky, if you will, who was willing to be appointed to go in and do what the real attorney general and his legal staff would, would refuse to do, which was to pretend as if there had been fraud in the election, and then to create this counterfeit subterfuge for Pence to reject the Electoral College votes. So, the, you know, the Department of Justice is apparently interested in him because of crimes committed, potentially, and were interested because of the entire plot against the election.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Given this week's testimony, will you be, all the testimony, really, will you be disappointed if the Justice Department does not file charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHOULD THE DOJ PURSUE CRIMINAL CHARGES AGAINST TRUMP?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMIE RASKIN (DEM-MD) (JAN 6 COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 You know, the, I, I have to tell you, speaking for myself, that is not my principle interest. I mean, our democracy's on the line, here. Our Constitution is at stake. Are we going to have violent assaults against our elections? Are we going to have politicians who, disappointed with the results, try to overthrow the election and just seize power? Is that what American democracy's going to look in the 21st century? So, for me, I'm principally interested in telling the American people the truth, so we can fortify our institutions against coups and insurrections, going forward.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMIE RASKIN (DEM-MD) (JAN 6 COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 But I know that there's a great public hunger for individual criminal accountability. And I've got confidence in the Department of Justice, in Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to do the right thing in terms of making all the difficult decisions about particular cases. And they've brought more than 850 indictments, already.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARE JANUARY 6 HEARINGS CHANGING AMERICAN'S OPINIONS?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What - what do you think the real impact is, here? And as much as some people are very riveted by, by these hearings, a recent ABC News/Ipsos showed that only 34 percent of Americans are following the hearings somewhat or very closely.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMIE RASKIN (DEM-MD) (JAN 6 COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, people are busy, and so we know a lot of people, especially younger people, will learn about the hearings through snippets that go out on TV or online, and people now are able to process information in different ways. It's not like the Watergate hearings, where everybody had to be watching at the same moment because of the relatively primitive state of technology, then. People are going to be able to absorb this over time, and I certainly have met a lot of people who are saying, "I missed one of the hearings, but I'm going to watch it this weekend."\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And - I assume you, you, you want to change minds. There was powerful testimony from Arizona House Speaker \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2 , earlier this week. He described being asked to replace Arizona's Biden electors with those who would support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AZ SPEAKER BOWERS SAYS TRUMP ASKED HIM TO REPLACE ELECTORS\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RUSTY BOWERS\uc2  (REP) (ARIZONA HOUSE SPEAKER)\par \par \uc2 It is a tenet of my faith that the Constitution is divinely inspired, of my most basic foundational beliefs. And so, for me to do that, because somebody just asked me to is foreign to my very being. I will not do it.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He talked about being harassed and threatened while his adult daughter was gravely ill. She later died. And yet, when asked by "The Associated Press" if he would support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if he ran in 2024, he said, "If he is the nominee, if he was up against Biden, I'd vote for him again simply because what he did the first time, before COVID, was so good for the country. In my view, it was great." So, if January 6th and what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did to him hasn't changed \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2 ' mind, how's anybody else's mind going to be changed?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMIE RASKIN (DEM-MD) (JAN 6 COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 I was very moved by \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2 ' testimony and his constitutional faith and patriotism. When he said that, I thought to myself, well, if you want to get \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  back in office and that were actually to materialize, you've got to be prepared to do the exact same thing next time, because Trump has proven himself to be absolutely disrespectful of the rule of law and completely ungovernable by the Constitution. So, I think, I took Mr. Bowers to be saying that's the nature of the party system.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMIE RASKIN (DEM-MD) (JAN 6 COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 I know he's running for state Senate, now, in his state, I believe, and you know, I take that that's a statement of political necessity for him. That's what he thinks. But the, the reality is that I think these hearings are unifying Republicans, Democrats, independents, Liberaltarians, Greens, people across the spectrum in a determination that America constitutional democracy has got to work the way we know it has to work, which is, we can't have election officials being corrupted to fix elections. We can't have politicians who mobilize mobs to overturn our processes.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, and I know we have one more hearing after the July 4th holiday. Thanks for joining us, today. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMIE RASKIN (DEM-MD) (JAN 6 COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 You bet.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Up next, the other major ruling from the Court this week making it easier to carry guns in public. States scrambling to react amid concerns of an uptick in gun violence. We'll discuss that, and much more, on the Roundtable, next.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Roundtable's here, ready to go. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220629ei6q00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THIS WEEK WITH GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS\b0\par\par\uc2 761 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 "This week with George Stephanopoulos" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 My body, my choice.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Roe overturned.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MAXINE WATERS (DEM) (CALIFORNIA)\par \par \uc2 To hell with the Supreme Court. We will defy them. Women will be in control of their bodies.\par \par \uc2 CATHY MCMORRIS RODGERS (REP) (WASHINGTON)\par \par \uc2 The court affirmed today that every life is worth living.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court reverses Roe v. Wade, ending 50 years of precedent.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Thanks to the courage found within the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , this long, divisive issue will be decided by the states.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ELISE STEFANIK (REP) (NEW YORK)\par \par \uc2 It will save countless innocent children.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Let's be very clear, the health and life of women in this nation are now at risk.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (DEM-CA) (SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE)\par \par \uc2 It's a slap in the face to women about using their own judgment.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 This morning, we cover every angle about the seismic shift in American life. Cecilia Vega traveling with the President, Dan Abrams on the legal repercussions, Terry Moran on the political fallout, and Dr. Jennifer Ashton on the medical questions facing so many women.\par \par \uc2 ABORTION CARE PROVIDER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Within minutes of the decision, we had to stop providing abortion care.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOV. KRISTI NOEM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 In the last 48 hours, eight states have outlawed abortion, more than two dozen bans expected in the weeks to come. We're tracking the very latest, and we'll hear from both sides. Republican Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  of South Dakota, where abortions have ended, and Democratic Senator Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts, a safe haven for those seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REP. JAMIE RASKIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Plus, Congressman Jamie Raskin on the latest, powerful testimony from the January 6th hearings and the breakthrough deal on guns. Our Powerhouse Roundtable covers it all.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From ABC News, this is a special edition of "This Week." Here, now, co-anchor Martha Raddatz.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FALLOUT AFTER ROE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, and welcome to "This Week." On Friday morning, women in this country, like they have for nearly 50 years, woke up with a constitutional right to abortion, a right enshrined by the Supreme Court's 1973 decision in Roe v. Wade and reaffirmed again and again.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS UPHOLDS MISSISSIPPI'S 15-WEEK BAN 6-3\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS OVERTURNS ROE V. WADE 5-4\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But just after 10:00 am on Friday, a legal earthquake, the Court stripping women of that fundamental right. In a 6-3 decision, the Conservative majority upheld Mississippi's ban on abortion after 15 weeks of pregnancy, with five of those justices voting to go even further, overturning Roe v. Wade, the first time an individual right of this magnitude set in decades of precedent has been taken away. Since the announcement, abortion rights activists have swarmed the court and launched protests across the country, alongside anti-abortion rights groups celebrating a landmark legal and political victory decades in the making.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Abortion is now a matter for the states and Congress, a decision for voters and their elected leaders rather than between a woman and her doctor. The decision had an immediate effect.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8 STATES WHERE ABORTION IS ILLEGAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXPECTED TO RESTRICT ABORTION ACCESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROTECTING ACCESS TO ABORTION\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As of this morning, abortion is now illegal in eight states. Seven additional states had passed so-called trigger laws that automatically went into effect once Roe was overturned. And in the coming weeks and months, a total of 26 states are expected to ban or severely restrict abortion. Just 16 states, plus Washington, DC, have laws that explicitly protect access to abortion care. President Biden called Friday's decision a sad day for the Court and for the country, and talked about the steps the administration will take in the wake of the ruling. Amid so much uncertainty, what does seem clear is the emergence of a new era in which the Supreme Court, like so much of the rest of the country, is mired by partisan divide.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's all certain to keep the Court at the center of a political battle to come this fall in the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220629ei6q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN WHAT ACTIONS CAN CONGRESS AND THE ADM TAKE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,194 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , ROE, ABORTION, BIDEN, ABC NEWS/WAPO, POLL\par \par \uc2 NOEM, SOUTH DAKOTA, ABORTION, BAN, VOTE, PILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROE V. WADE OVERTURNED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOUTH DAKOTA TRIGGER LAWS BAN NEARLY ALL ABORTIONS AFTER RULING\par \par \uc2 WARREN, ROE V WADE, SCOTUS, BIDEN, CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And joining us now is Senator Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts, where abortion remains legal, and the commonwealth's governor issued an executive order Friday to further protect women's reproductive health services. Good morning to you, Senator. You heard Governor Noem, there. She and millions of others believe this will save the lives of unborn children. Is that a position you can respect in any way?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 Look, what she's really saying is that when this decision is made, it should be made by the government, that the government should move in and the government should determine whether or not a pregnancy is forced to continue or whether or not a pregnancy can be terminated. I believe, and it has been the constitutional right of women across this nation for nearly half a century, for the woman to be able to make that decision with her doctor, with her religious adviser, with her family, but not something that the government should be in the middle of.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT ACTIONS CAN CONGRESS AND THE ADMINISTRATION TAKE?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, you, you and President Biden are saying that Roe is now on the ballot. Indeed, it is. Go to the polls, you say. President Biden says, go to the polls. But look at the states outlawing abortions. Those are largely conservative states. Governor Noem had a point, there, people go to the polls. They went to the polls, just like your constituents in Massachusetts where abortion is legal. So, why not leave it to the states?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXPECTED TO FURTHER RESTRICT ABORTION ACCESS\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 Look, we have never left individual rights to the states. The whole idea is that women are not second-class citizens, and the government is not the one that will decide about the continuation of a pregnancy. You know, there's no equivalent there for men. So, what we believe is that access to abortion, like other medical procedures, should be available across the board to all people in this country. Now, we've got a lot of things on our agenda, right now. First of all, we need to help the women who are pregnant right now and need help, and that means sending resources to the states like New Mexico, you know, that border other states that are going to try to help out.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 It means getting involved by volunteering, by sending money. It also means asking the President of the United States to make abortion as available as possible with the tools he has, including medication abortion, including using federal lands as a place where abortions can occur. But it's also focused like a laser on the election in November, and we get two more senators on the Democratic side, two senators who are willing to protect access to abortion and get rid of the filibuster, so that we can pass it. And yes, John Fetterman, I'm looking at you in Pennsylvania. Mandela Barnes, I'm looking at you in Wisconsin. We bring them in, then we've got the votes, and we can protect every woman, no matter where she lives.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, you saw Justice Thomas' concurring opinion, I know, calling on the Court to reconsider rulings, striking down state restrictions, contraception, gay marriage. Are you concerned about that? You also heard what the rest of the Court said about that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP: ATTACK ON THE CAPITOL INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REP JAMIE RASKIN ON JAN 6 HEARING TESTIMONY, ADDITIONAL HEARINGS, AND THE POTENTIAL OF A TRUMP CRIMINAL REFERRAL\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 I am deeply concerned about that. I understand that the rest of the Court said, "No, no, we're not going there." But remember how we got to where we are. When Roe versus Wade first came down, there was a tiny minority that really put a lot of energy into, in effect, for themselves and for Republicans, putting Roe on the ballot over and over. But on the ballot didn't mean try to get it through the Congress, because they knew they couldn't do it. They're not even close to having national support for that. So, instead, it was about getting extremist judges into the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . And let's see how much credibility those justices have, now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let, let, let me...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 Replay the tapes of one after another saying that Roe versus Wade is settled law and no one disturbs settled law.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and I was going to ask you about that, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 And yes, as soon as they count the numbers, they toss it out.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , who voted for \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , as well as Joe Manchin, have said they were misled. Do you think the process should change, now?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 Which process do you mean? The process of having of hearings of Supreme Court justice...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Of confirming - of confirming justices.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 You know, look, I understand that Justice Kavanaugh, for example, I don't know what he said to Senator Collins. I wasn't in the room. But I do know this, that the Republicans have been very overt about trying to get people through the Court who didn't have a published record on Roe, but who they knew, wink-wink, nod-nod, were going to be extremist on the issue of Roe versus Wade. And that is exactly what we have ended up with. This Court has lost legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 They have burned whatever legitimacy they may still have had after their gun decision, after their voting decision, after their union decision. They just took the last of it and set a torch to it with the Roe versus Wade opinion. I believe we need to get some confidence back in our Court, and that means we need more justices on the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I know that's something you support. Thank you...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 It's happened before. We've, we've done it before. We need to do it again.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thanks very much for joining us this morning, Senator. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Up next, major revelations from the January 6th hearings, including new details on President Trump's relentless effort to overturn the 2020 election. Committee member Jamie Raskin joins us exclusively here in studio. We're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220629ei6q00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SCOTUS OVERTURNS ROE, REMOVING FED RIGHT TO ABORT. ABORTION NOW OUTLAWED IN 8 STS, TRIGGER LAWS IN 7\b0\par\par\uc2 1,499 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , ROE, ABORTION, BIDEN, ABC NEWS/WAPO, POLL\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We will cover all of the fallout from the landmark ruling and the state of play for reproductive rights across the country, including interviews with South Dakota Governor Kristi Noem and Massachusetts Senator Elizabeth Warren.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT OVERTURNS ROE, REMOVING FEDERAL RIGHT TO ABORTION\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But we begin with our ABC team covering the very latest. And Terry Moran, you've covered the Court for so many years. The shock of this may have been tempered by the leak of the draft opinion in May, but you cannot overstate how important and what an impact this will have.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION NOW OUTLAWED IN 8 STATES, TRIGGER LAWS TAKE EFFECT IN 7\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Martha, this is the most consequential Supreme Court decision in decades. It changes the status of American women as citizens of the United States and as citizens of their states. That's the big picture, but let's not mince words. Women will die because of this ruling. We already have a disgraceful rate of maternal mortality. But, from now on, doctors in many states will have to ask themselves in pregnancies where there are serious complications, a split placento or placenta in a dangerous position, will the local prosecutor, will the local jury think my patient is in enough danger for me to perform what I otherwise would consider a medically necessary abortion?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 26 STATES CERTAIN OR LIKELY TO BAN ABORTION AFTER SCOTUS RULING\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And in 11 states, already, including Texas and Florida, state governments can seize control of the bodies of women who have been raped or who are victims of incest and compel them to carry the baby, the child of their rapist to term. This is a different world for women in America.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It, it certainly is. And I want to turn to you, Dan Abrams, our chief legal analyst. At the heart of this decision is precedent. So, what other existing rights could now be in jeopardy? Justice Thomas, of course, his concurring opinion said the Court should reconsider other past precedent cases like decisions on contraception, same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MOUNTING CONCERN OTHER RIGHTS COULD BE ROLLED BACK BY COURT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOMAS INVITES CHALLENGES TO SAME-SEX MARRIAGE, CONTRACEPTION RULINGS\par \par \uc2 DAN ABRAMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Court went to great lengths to suggest that it doesn't apply to any of those other areas. But when you read Justice Thomas' concurrence, it's difficult not to ask the question, what could be next? He puts it all out there on the table, Justice Thomas. And while, as a practical manner, it seems the court doesn't have the votes to overturn in any of those other areas, analytically, if you read the reasoning of the Court, it's hard not to say that Justice Thomas' assessment is more intellectually consistent. I mean, if the whole reason that this opinion is applying is because abortion, for example, isn't enshrined in our history, you look at these other rights and you say, well, they're not really enshrined in our history, either.\par \par \uc2 DAN ABRAMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, while as a practical matter, I don't think you're going to see any of these other areas changed, constitutionally. As an analytical matter, it's tough to say, doesn't Justice Thomas' reasoning apply?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Cecilia Vega, our chief White House correspondent, you're overseas with President Biden as he heads to the G7. The White House has known about the likelihood that Roe would be overturned for months. But were they really ready for this? And, and what kind of difference will those steps he outlined on Friday really make?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WH: ONLY CONGRESS CAN FULLY RESTORE ABORTION PROTECTION, NOT BIDEN\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Martha, and I got to tell you, it's this history that's happening back home that is really starting to overshadow at least the first day here of this summit with allies the President is embarking on, today. We've been talking about this. There had been all of these emergency meetings behind closed doors at the White House with advocacy groups, with lawyers of the White House brainstorming on things that they could do when this decision came down. They basically started doing this as soon as that draft document leaked.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the White House sources are telling me that President Biden had that speech written, the response speech that he gave on Friday, that it was already written, so when draft, when the opinion actually came down on Friday, he went out there and gave it. He just had to give some minor tweaks. Look, they're certainly bristling at the suggestion that they were not acting fast enough, that they weren't prepared, but when it came time to give that speech, you heard it, there wasn't really very many bold actions, responses in it. Look, there isn't an executive order that the President can sign that's going to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 34 DEM SENATORS CALL ON BIDEN TO TAKE 'BOLD ACTION'\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, what he did at this point, he is saying the administration is going to protect access to the abortion pill, that the DOJ will certainly challenge any state that tries to restrict any woman from traveling to get the procedure. But you've got a lot of advocates right now who are looking to this White House as the face of this movement going forward, asking is there more you can and should be doing? Where is the urgency, today?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN ASKS DOJ TO CHALLENGE LAWS RESTRICTING TRAVEL TO SEEK ABORTION\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and I want to turn to Dr. Jennifer Ashton. Dr. Jen, you're a board certified OB/GYN, so let's talk about the real life impact here. Terry mentioned it, but abortion will now be largely outlawed in more than half the states. And you have made the very important point this week that it is not just women who will be affected by this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ADVOCATES SAY WOMEN OF COLOR AND LOW-INCOME MOST AFFECTED BY RULING\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely, Martha. And I think, you know, first of all, there are so many different ways to look at this politically, socially, legally, but medically, and in the principles of biomedical ethics, you know, I think we have to remember that it's not just life or death, as Terry mentioned. Of course, there are real medical issues and the life of the mother at stake, but it's also morbidity, and it's who will be affected by this. We know that 49 percent of the abortions done in this country are performed for women who live below the poverty level. This will disproportionately affect women of color, poor women who are already in jeopardy in terms of our healthcare system.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We also have the worst maternal mortality rates of industrialized countries. This will make it worse in the field of OB/GYN. Any healthcare professional who takes care of women knows that this will have an immediate impact not on a life or death level, but on a long-term morbidity level, as well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICANS' VIEWS ON ABORTION\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Terry, I want to end with you. This is a deeply polarizing and political issue, but Americans are actually pretty united in public opinion. In our latest ABC News/\uc2 Washington Post\uc2  poll, 70 percent said the decision should be up to a woman and her doctor. Fifty-eight percent said abortion should be legal in all or most cases.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJORITY OF AMERICANS BELIEVE RIGHT TO AN ABORTION IN ALL OR MOST CASES\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, in the courts and in social media and in our politics, this is a very polarizing issue, but the country has always been, polls show, in the, the kind of vast muddled middle. People believe that abortion should be legal. They don't like it in, in a lot of situations. Bill Clinton, with his unerring sense of where the votes were, put it best, capturing the public mind this way, saying abortion in America should be safe, legal and rare. Today, no Democrat could say that and no Republican could say that. So, where the vast muddled middle is, is unrepresented in our law and in our politics.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thanks very much to you, Terry. Thanks to all of you, this morning.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220629ei6q00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 A NATION DIVIDED PROTESTERS TAKE TO STREETS, EIGHT STATES HAVE ABORTION BANS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,163 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: A NATION DIVIDED\par \par \uc2 ROE, SUPREME COURT, PATIENTS, MILLER, WALES, ZENTNER, LORI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: A NATION DIVIDED\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening, everyone. Thanks so much for joining us on this Sunday. I'm Linsey Davis. As we come on the air, tonight, it is clear an already divided nation has only become further incensed by the rapidly changing landscape of abortion rights after the Supreme Court struck down Roe v. Wade. Across the country this weekend, people on both sides are taking to the streets. Most of the marches have remained peaceful.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But we've also seen moments of tension, a truck slamming into protesters here in Iowa. A Christian pregnancy center vandalized in Colorado. Today, some churches celebrated it, considering it a victory after fighting against abortion for nearly five decades. The issue followed President Biden all the way to Europe, threatening to overshadow his meeting with G-7 allies, which was intended to shore up support for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back home, South Dakota Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  telling our Martha Raddatz that she plans to bar mail-order abortion pills. Her state is one of eight where abortion is now banned, in nearly all cases. Another 18 are likely to ban or limit access.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Today, New York's pride parade was led by Planned Parenthood. Many fear other rights, including same-sex marriage, could soon be threatened in the wake of Friday's Supreme Court decision. We have team coverage, tonight. ABC's Devin Dwyer leads us off from the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: A NATION DIVIDED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROTESTERS TAKE TO STREETS, EIGHT STATES HAVE ABORTION BANS\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today, a nation divided and on edge.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Our bodies. Our choice.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Peaceful protests over abortion rights punctuated by isolated incidents of vandalism and violence. Windows smashed at the Vermont statehouse. In Colorado, a Christian pregnancy center threatened and burned. In Providence, an off-duty officer charged with assault, allegedly seen in this video punching an abortion rights demonstrator, who is also his Democratic opponent in a State Senate race.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tensions running high as a growing number of states start enforcing bans on abortion, with few exceptions. Eight states tonight have near total bans in force. Eleven states have no clinics performing abortions. Former President Trump, who put three justices on the court to overturn Roe v. Wade, taking a victory lap in Illinois.\par \par \uc2 FMR PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The court handed down a victory for the constitution, a victory for the rule of law, and above all, a victory for life.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Trump stumping for Republican Congresswoman Mary Miller, who said this.\par \par \uc2 REP MARY MILLER (REP) (ILLINOIS)\par \par \uc2 I want to thank you for the historic victory for white life in the Supreme Court yesterday.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Miller's spokesman refuting claims of racism, calling it a mix-up of words. Both sides in the debate on knife's edge. When former President Obama tweeted criticism of the court's reversal of 50 years of precedent, Republican Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  of Texas fired back, "Now do Plessy v. Ferguson/Brown v. Board of Education," the decision upholding segregation and the one that ended it.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Cornyn batting down cries of racism, tweeting "Thank Goodness some precedents are overturned." Largely unseen and unheard in the firestorm. The low-income and minority women who are most likely to seek abortion care. Like Nicole, who we met at an abortion clinic in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (PATIENT JACKSON WOMEN'S HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 You don't want to bring it. You're scared. But it's just not something that my husband and I are ready for. We're just not ready, yet.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Providers, who abruptly halted abortions Friday, described stunned patients scrambling for options.\par \par \uc2 EMILY WALES (PRES & CEO PLANNED PARENTHOOD GREAT PLAINS)\par \par \uc2 A lot of questions about why care was not available in Arkansas, but it would be available potentially in Illinois. We are pivoting and saying we used to be a haven, but you've got to get out of this region to get health care.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Alabama clinic escort Kathy Zentner says even giving advice could now expose abortion rights advocates to potential prosecution.\par \par \uc2 KATHY ZENTNER (CLINIC ESCORT ALABAMA WOMEN'S CENTER)\par \par \uc2 If it's a felony in Alabama, if you tell anybody how to do something, even if they're going out of state, you're guilty of a felony, because you're aiding and abetting something that's against the law in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She says medication for abortion delivered by mail could be critical.\par \par \uc2 KATHY ZENTNER (CLINIC ESCORT ALABAMA WOMEN'S CENTER)\par \par \uc2 It's going to be survival for a lot of women. There's just no other place to get it.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the pills also a top target in anti-abortion rights states.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How will you know if women receive those in the mail, and should those women be prosecuted?\par \par \uc2 GOV KRISTI NOEM (REP) (SOUTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 I don't believe women should ever be prosecuted. Now, doctors who knowingly violate the law, they should be prosecuted, definitely.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And abortion opponents say there are resources for women who are forced or choose to keep their babies.\par \par \uc2 MATERNITY HOME WORKER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 This is our community baby closet.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Maternity homes and pregnancy centers like we saw in Ft. Worth, Texas, gearing up for a surge in demand. The Catholic Archbishop leading the church's campaign for abortion alternatives, says financial resources are there.\par \par \uc2 ARCHBISHOP WILLIAM LORI (CHMN US CONFERENCE CATHOLIC BISHOP'S CMTE PRO LIFE ) ACTIVITIES\par \par \uc2 I don't think we should underestimate the generosity either of the charities and services we provide, or of God's people.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But studies show states with the most restrictive abortion laws have invested the least in policies and programs for women and children.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's get right to Devin Dwyer, at the Supreme Court for us. Devin, how quickly will we see more states begin enforcing abortion bans? And will there be any exceptions?\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Linsey, in the eight states, tonight, where near total bans on abortion are being enforced, all provide an exception when the life of the woman is at risk. But only one, Utah, allows abortions in cases of rape or incest. As for the other states, six states have trigger laws that are in motion, set to take effect very soon. Meantime, more major decisions coming down here from the Supreme Court tomorrow. Linsey.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020220627ei6q00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WHAT COMES NEXT? WILL WOMEN BE PROSECUTED OVER ABORTION PILL USE?\b0\par\par\uc2 431 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: A NATION DIVIDED\par \par \uc2 ROE, SUPREME COURT, PATIENTS, MILLER, WALES, ZENTNER, LORI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: A NATION DIVIDED\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, G-7, TRUDEAU, JOHNSON, NATO, DEMOCRATS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GLOBAL TEST FOR BIDEN\par \par \uc2 ROE, ABORTION, SUPREME COURT, CHURCHES, THOMAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REACTION TO HISTORIC DECISION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PILL, PROSECUTED, STATES, CONSTITUTIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT COMES NEXT?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Zohreen, thank you. So many still reacting with such mixed emotions to the ruling. Now let's bring in our Supreme Court contributor, Kate Shaw. And, Kate, some women are considering abortion pills as an alternative. And we know that state statutes vary. Give us the bottom line, here. Will women be criminally prosecuted for seeking and taking the abortion pill?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT COMES NEXT?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILL WOMEN BE PROSECUTED OVER ABORTION PILL USE?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, the state statutes that have either gone into effect or will go into effect prohibiting abortion in the wake of overturning Roe versus Wade are worded in a range of different ways. Some of them make reference to criminal penalties for anyone who administers an abortion.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And even if those laws intend to target doctors, it seems pretty clear that a DA could prosecute a woman for self-administering abortion pills, because the language of the statute allows that. So, a lot is going to rest with the discretion of prosecutors in states and cities.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It certainly seems that's the case. And, Kate, we also know that Planned Parenthood is suing the state of Utah for its trigger law, which has already taken effect. So, explain what that lawsuit is about and how focusing on the state's constitution, at least in Utah, could be the new legal frontier?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT COMES NEXT?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILL STATE CONSTITUTIONS BE THE NEW LEGAL FRONTIER?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , in the Dobbs decision, held that federal Constitution does not protect the right to an abortion. But, of course, every state has its own constitution, and some of those documents have rights provisions that go further than the US Constitution.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, here, Planned Parenthood is arguing that the Utah constitution and its right to family formation and sex equality, protects a right to abortion regardless of what the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  says about the federal constitution. And when it comes to this suit, it's the Utah courts, not the Supreme Court, that will have the final word.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020220627ei6q00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 REACTION TO HISTORIC RULING POLL: 58% SAY ABORTION SHOULD BE LEGAL IN ALL OR MOST CASES\b0\par\par\uc2 469 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: A NATION DIVIDED\par \par \uc2 ROE, SUPREME COURT, PATIENTS, MILLER, WALES, ZENTNER, LORI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: A NATION DIVIDED\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, G-7, TRUDEAU, JOHNSON, \uc2 NATO\uc2 , DEMOCRATS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GLOBAL TEST FOR BIDEN\par \par \uc2 ROE, ABORTION, SUPREME COURT, CHURCHES, THOMAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REACTION TO HISTORIC DECISION\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A lot of eyes on what the Biden administration will do. Cecelia, thank you. And while thousands have taken to the streets, both in support, as well as to protest the Supreme Court ruling, there was a different take today at many churches in America. Here's ABC's Zohreen Shah.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REACTION TO HISTORIC RULING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLL: 58% SAY ABORTION SHOULD BE LEGAL IN ALL OR MOST CASES\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) On this the first Sunday after the high court's ruling, some are calling the decision answered prayer.\par \par \uc2 CHURCHGOER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm actually very thrilled, after many, many rosaries, and a lot of praying.\par \par \uc2 CHURCHGOER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Obviously, as a Catholic, we believe that, you know, abortion is not right.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But outside many churches, there is also reflection.\par \par \uc2 CHURCHGOER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I am anti-abortion. But if you were raped or the mother's life is in jeopardy if she was to have the child, then I understand that, and I think there should be some, some leeway, there.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The majority of Americans in our ABC News/"Washington Post" poll, 58% said abortion should be legal in all or most cases. And as Americans grapple with a post-Roe reality...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 My body.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm listening to both sides. Everyone has a story for everything that they do, and everyone's lives is (sic) different. So, what I'm going through is not the same thing that you're going through.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This first Sunday also coincides with pride marches across the country.\par \par \uc2 PARADE SPEAKER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 The attack on abortion is connected to the entire attack on bodily autonomy and equal rights.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And after Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ' concurring opinion that the decision opens the right to contraception and the rights of same-sex couples to review, today's marches taking on a new urgency.\par \par \uc2 PARADE GOER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 And I'm sure what'll come next with the other rights that are under attack. They'll come, but we're ready for the fight. We've been fighting for our whole lives just like women have been, and we're ready for it.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The differing opinion we've seen in this region alone shows the abortion debate is happening everywhere, in every city, in every household, and across the country. Linsey.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020220627ei6q00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WORLD NEWS SUNDAY\b0\par\par\uc2 428 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: A NATION DIVIDED\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a nation divided after that monumental Supreme Court decision, overturning Roe versus Wade. Tempers running high. A Christian pregnancy center vandalized in Colorado. A truck plowing through a demonstration in Iowa. An off-duty officer accused of punching a protester in Providence. Eight states are enforcing nearly total bans on abortion. And tonight, new reaction from providers who abruptly halted abortions, stunning patients. Our Devin Dwyer is live, at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GLOBAL TEST FOR BIDEN\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The abortion battle proves to be a global test for President Biden, overshadowing his European trip. Tonight, world leaders weigh in on the issue. And Senate Democrats put new pressure on him to take action. Our Cecelia Vega is standing by.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REACTION TO HISTORIC DECISION\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And we're on the ground, tonight. This first Sunday after the high court's ruling, reaction is pouring in, as Americans grapple with a post-Roe reality. Some groups voice concerns that other rights, including same-sex marriage, could soon be threatened. And why the next legal frontier could focus on the abortion pill and state constitutions.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHILD RESCUED FROM DEADLY STRIKE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Developing overseas. The child rescued after Russian missiles slam residential buildings in Ukraine's capital. The desperate efforts to save a young girl and her mother. Our team is on the scene of the deadly attack.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXTREME WEATHER DANGERS\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Here at home, the extreme weather dangers. Scorching heat and a relentless drought turns the South and West into a tinderbox, stoking wildfires. What it means for the holiday week ahead, as we track a new system in the tropics.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DIRECT HIT\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The direct hit. Dramatic new video shows the moment a boat full of passengers is hit by lightning near Tampa Bay.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HORRIFIC STAND COLLAPSE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Deadly collapse. Several killed and dozens hurt when the stands give way at a bullfight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MYSTERIOUS TAVERN DEATHS\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The mysterious deaths inside a tavern. More than 20 young people found dead in a bar with no obvious signs of injury. Tonight, the search for clues.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROCK LEGENDS BACK ON STAGE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And the legends are still rocking. The Rolling Stones and Paul McCartney mark new milestones in their storied careers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From ABC News world headquarters in New York, this is "World News Tonight."\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020220627ei6q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BINGE THIS\b0\par\par\uc2 873 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, BILL\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, OSLO, PRIDE\par \par \uc2 G7, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, SHELLING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE RETREATING FROM SIEVIERODONETSK\par \par \uc2 NHL, LIGHTNING, AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, COMMONWEALTH\par \par \uc2 FAMU, MARCHING 100, VUITTON, NEASMAN, ROBERTS, JARRELL\par \par \uc2 CAR, CAKE, OVEN, BROWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, SHEPHERD\par \par \uc2 CAMP, LGBTQ+, BERKOWITZ, BOGARD, GRABEL, GOLDSCHMIDT\par \par \uc2 SWAEBE, SPONGELLE, PERRICONE, FEELGOODZ, THEOS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 ADELE, ROBERTS, "THOR: LOVE AND THUNDER", "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 DURAN, FANCY FOOD SHOW\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, LEGISLATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN SIGNS GUN SAFETY BILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: $13.2 BILLION PACKAGE PASSED ONE MONTH AFTER UVALDE SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 ODOM, CRIBB, SUMMER, HOST\par \par \uc2 OUTKAST, "ANCHORMAN", "THE MANDALORIAN"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BINGE THIS\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Welcome back to "GMA." It is time for a special edition of "Binge This."\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are celebrating our guy's, Whit Johnson's, 40th birthday.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Forty.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Forty, I know.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, you have your top picks for us for music, movies and TV.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And these are like your real picks.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, yeah, yeah. They definitely, they'd asked for some picks. I gave them a range because, you know, I've got a range here. So, but first, what are we talking about first here?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tunes. Music.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Music. Okay. Music here. I felt like everything from Bob Marley to Bob Dylan. But we went with Outkast here because I love Outkast. One of the greatest hip hop bands of all time. Andres 3000, Big Boy together known as Outkast. Six-time Grammy-winning duo.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHIT'S MUSIC PICK: OUTKAST\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Did you know this? They initially wanted to be called Two Shades Deep or The Misfits, but those names were already taken, so they ended up with Outkast.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And we call you, DJ Quick Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) DJ Quick Whit. Yeah. I do love my hip hop.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, we also have multiplex movies. And you put a lot of them.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, there are a lot of movies. Again, a lot of range here, but we've got to go with "Anchorman," right?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You've already quoted "Anchorman" this morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHIT'S MOVIE PICK: "ANCHORMAN"\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And finally, we already did this morning, you know, I'm an anchor man from California, so it must be "Anchorman" starring Will Farrell. Dear to my heart here, San Diego's top newsman Ron Burgundy. Your ubulus connects to your (inaudible). I don't know if you heard me counting back there. But I did well over a thousand. Tuesdays, arms and back. This timeless piece of cinema hitting theaters, this is back in 2004. And, of course, everyone has been quoting it ever since. Not just me.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You quote it at least once a week.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, now to TV because we're hopping to light speed for this one.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) "The Mandalorian"?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, we're going with "The Mandalorian"? Was that like supposed to be some kind of a Mandalorian voice?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Just get on with it, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The force is strong with this one. Of course, "The Mandalorian." One of our favorites. We just finished watching "Obi Wan Kenobi."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHIT'S TV PICK: "THE MANDALORIAN"\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But this one can't be beat. Baby Yoda was a big part of my family. And in our household, we all have Baby Yoda t-shirts, also known as the Child and Grogu. There's a new season coming up pretty soon.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, you know, you can't have a birthday without a cake.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Bring out the cake.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Big old cake. Happy birthday, Whit Johnson.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Happy birthday to Whit Johnson.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thank you so much. Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So funny.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 A big happy birthday to Whit.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yes. And we are so happy to celebrate with you. And I hope you have a good day at work all day.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 All day.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Oh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p0000u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HOSTING IN THE HEAT HOT TIPS TO THROWING A SIZZLING SUMMER SOIREE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,361 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, BILL\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, OSLO, PRIDE\par \par \uc2 G7, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, SHELLING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE RETREATING FROM SIEVIERODONETSK\par \par \uc2 NHL, LIGHTNING, AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, COMMONWEALTH\par \par \uc2 FAMU, MARCHING 100, VUITTON, NEASMAN, ROBERTS, JARRELL\par \par \uc2 CAR, CAKE, OVEN, BROWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, SHEPHERD\par \par \uc2 CAMP, LGBTQ+, BERKOWITZ, BOGARD, GRABEL, GOLDSCHMIDT\par \par \uc2 SWAEBE, SPONGELLE, PERRICONE, FEELGOODZ, THEOS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 ADELE, ROBERTS, "THOR: LOVE AND THUNDER", "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 DURAN, FANCY FOOD SHOW\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, LEGISLATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN SIGNS GUN SAFETY BILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: $13.2 BILLION PACKAGE PASSED ONE MONTH AFTER UVALDE SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 ODOM, CRIBB, SUMMER, HOST\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. It is party time with the hot tips you need for hosting in the heat.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOSTING IN THE HEAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT TIPS TO THROWING A SIZZLING SUMMER SOIREE\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Joining us now are Betsy Cribb and Ivy Odom, both editors with "Southern Living" magazine. Thank you, ladies, for being here with us. This table looks fantastic.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Betsy, we're going to kick things off with you and the setting for this table. You say that this is where we can save a few bucks and the environment.\par \par \uc2 BETSY CRIBB (EDITOR "SOUTHERN LIVING" MAGAZINE)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely. Reusable is the name of the game for summer entertaining.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REUSABLE D\u201\'00\'C9COR IS KEY MINE THRIFT STORES FOR TIMELESS FINDS\par \par \uc2 BETSY CRIBB (EDITOR "SOUTHERN LIVING" MAGAZINE)\par \par \uc2 It's better for your wallet and better for the environment. So, here are a couple ways to do that. First up, I love these scallop melamine plates. Melamine, is a really sturdy plastic, so they're, and they're much prettier than your run of the mill disposable paper or plastic plates. And best of all, they're reusable. So, you can put these in the dishwasher after the meal is over and bring them out time and time again. Love those. Another thing I love, cloth napkins. So, these are from Sweet Grass Home in North Carolina. Love shopping small where we can. And the great thing about these is they're an inexpensive way to change up your table.\par \par \uc2 BETSY CRIBB (EDITOR "SOUTHERN LIVING" MAGAZINE)\par \par \uc2 So, these are great for this weekend. But if you want to swap them out for something red for the 4th of July, you've got an easy festive table ready to go. And like the melamine, these are also reusable. You just throw them in the washing machine at the end of the meal and break them out again.\par \par \uc2 IVY ODOM (EDITOR "SOUTHERN LIVING" MAGAZINE)\par \par \uc2 And they don't stain, Betsy.\par \par \uc2 BETSY CRIBB (EDITOR "SOUTHERN LIVING" MAGAZINE)\par \par \uc2 Oh, and they don't stain. So, these busy patterns are really good for camouflaging little messes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Especially if you have children in your house. Then you've got a mess.\par \par \uc2 BETSY CRIBB (EDITOR "SOUTHERN LIVING" MAGAZINE)\par \par \uc2 Exactly.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What is on trend this summer when it comes to party decor?\par \par \uc2 BETSY CRIBB (EDITOR "SOUTHERN LIVING" MAGAZINE)\par \par \uc2 Okay. So, it sounds kinds of crazy, but hear me out. The coastal grandmother aesthetic is all over social media.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "COASTAL GRANDMOTHER" TREND IS IN USE FRUIT FOR POPS OF COLOR\par \par \uc2 BETSY CRIBB (EDITOR "SOUTHERN LIVING" MAGAZINE)\par \par \uc2 Basically, if you've seen a Nancy Myers movie set at the beach, that's what we're talking about. So, think breezy linens, blue and white, Ina Garten whipping up the perfect roast chicken. That's what we're talking about. So, bring that look home with hydrangeas. Blue and white is a great way to bring that into your table. I love hydrangeas because you get a lot of bang for your buck. And they're so voluminous, so you really only need a few stems to fill a vase. Plus, a lot of people have them growing in their yards this time of year or you might have a friendly neighbor who's willing to share. The vases, too. I love thrifting vases.\par \par \uc2 BETSY CRIBB (EDITOR "SOUTHERN LIVING" MAGAZINE)\par \par \uc2 So, go to your local shift store, find some new to you stuff that's inexpensive. And I like the mismatch look, honestly, because it looks like it's more collected over time. Last, but not the least, I love decorating with citrus. Big pops of color bring a lot of fun and energy to the table. And best of all, you can use them to garnish your drinks.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, Ivy, let's move on to you now because, of course, it's not a party without the food. So, what have you made for us here today?\par \par \uc2 IVY ODOM (EDITOR "SOUTHERN LIVING" MAGAZINE)\par \par \uc2 Well, speaking of garnishing our drinks with our lemons, I have some sweet tea because it is not a southern soir\u233\'00\'E9e without it. But speaking of food, y'all, you do not have to only grill your meat. I have some grilled peaches. These are in season right now, and grills are for free.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PICK YOUR PERFECT PEACH, SOFT PEACHES BETTER FOR PIES, FIRM ONES BETTER FOR GRILLING\par \par \uc2 IVY ODOM (EDITOR "SOUTHERN LIVING" MAGAZINE)\par \par \uc2 You can grill watermelons, nectarines, peaches. I'm a Georgia grill. So, for me, this is what I love. You really just put them straight on the grill to get grill marks.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PEACH GRILLING TIPS, BRUSH WITH OIL, GRILL FOR FOUR MINUTES\par \par \uc2 IVY ODOM (EDITOR "SOUTHERN LIVING" MAGAZINE)\par \par \uc2 You can serve them with meat. You can serve it here with a salad. I think y'all have that to taste with me. So, this is salad with feta, arugula, pistachios, olive oil, the grilled peaches. The peach flavor is awesome with this. And you can serve it, like is I said, over meats but I also have it over vanilla ice cream and whipped cream. So, you can go sweet or savory. Yeah, exactly. A drizzle of honey would be so good. I also have a grilled tomato and corn salad. Y'all, this is so good and it looks so impressive. What I love about this in the summer is that it's served as room temperature, which is perfect whenever you're thinking about outdoor grilling to serve things like at room temperature because you don't have to worry about keeping them hot or cold.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOLDEN TOMATO AND CORN SALAD MAKE YOGURT SAUCE IN ADVANCE\par \par \uc2 IVY ODOM (EDITOR "SOUTHERN LIVING" MAGAZINE)\par \par \uc2 But this tomato salad is a perfect example. It looks really impressive. It has yogurt sauce with turmeric. I love the plates of yellow in it. We have a nice citrus vinaigrette. I charred the lemons. So, another example of some great grilling over the summer. I use that charred lemon juice to sort of make this vinaigrette. And then I'm going to top it with some grilled corn. Oh, my goodness. Look at how pretty it is. This will impress all of your friends, and it is so, so delicious.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So bright and colorful. And also, a really good rule of thumb when it comes to food and hosting in the heat. Thank you, ladies, so much for being with us, Betsy and Ivy. And you at home can scan the QR code on the screen to get these recipes on our website. And the latest issue of "Southern Living" magazine is on newsstands now.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It is peach season, July 4th. Lexington County Peach Festival. That's my whole life.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But coming up, it's all about Whit and his birthday today. His picks for "Binge This" when we come back.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible)\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p0000t\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HIGH COURT OVERTURNS ROE V. WADE WHAT THE DECISION MEANS FOR WOMEN & HEALTHCARE\b0\par\par\uc2 830 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, BILL\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, OSLO, PRIDE\par \par \uc2 G7, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, SHELLING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE RETREATING FROM SIEVIERODONETSK\par \par \uc2 NHL, LIGHTNING, AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, COMMONWEALTH\par \par \uc2 FAMU, MARCHING 100, VUITTON, NEASMAN, ROBERTS, JARRELL\par \par \uc2 CAR, CAKE, OVEN, BROWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, SHEPHERD\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And as Stephanie mentioned, abortion bans already going into effect in seven states. And that number is likely to rise as other states act on so-called trigger laws designed to go into effect at some point after the High Court's ruling.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH COURT OVERTURNS ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT THE DECISION MEANS FOR WOMEN & HEALTHCARE\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, here with what you need to know about how this affects your health care is OB-GYN Dr. Jessica Shepherd. Dr. Shepherd, thank you so much for being with us this morning. For the average person, what does the Supreme Court overturning this law mean for their health care?\par \par \uc2 DR JESSICA SHEPHERD (CHIEF MEDICAL OFFICER VERYWELL HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Now, you know, as am OB-GYN and a women's health physician, this is devastating when we think of women who now have this stripped from them. You know, this is potentially causing, can cause severe health, financial, emotional consequences. So, this is going to impact people on every level and their families, their loved ones in different states. Now, we were talking earlier about trigger states. And these are basically states that have some sort of law that basically will wait for this overturning. And from that, they're going to be able to design to take effect automatically or very quickly. And so, this is what we're very worried about is that, again, people may not understand exactly what states, what law applies to their state and how this is going to impact their access and their care when we think about abortions.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, Dr. Shepherd, for those who are in need of access to an abortion right now, what do they need to know about traveling out of state?\par \par \uc2 DR JESSICA SHEPHERD (CHIEF MEDICAL OFFICER VERYWELL HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. You know, this is where it really comes down to health care providers and organizations and resources. Being able to really give the information that's helpful because many times when people maybe make that decision or if it is something that is medically necessary for the baby's safety or for the mom's safety, sometimes time is not a factor. And so, we really have to be careful in how we give information because these are so different. What you will notice between these trigger laws is, again, there's going to be differences in penalties and exceptions. Now that, again, can make a big difference, even if you do travel to a state that may be able to give you abortion care, is knowing what the parameters are.\par \par \uc2 DR JESSICA SHEPHERD (CHIEF MEDICAL OFFICER VERYWELL HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 And so, that's why I think hi this point there is a lot of confusion. There is a lot of chaos. But we really have to dive deep into what are those specificities and what are those specifics that people need to know when it comes to abortion care and where they may be able to get that.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Dr. Shepherd, thank you so much. A lot of confusion out there, but thank you for helping us sort through it this morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 4 MINS, SUMMER FUN FOR ALL\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Well, coming up in our "Morning Menu," "GMA" out loud. The summer camp setting itself apart and making a difference for LGBTQ+ kids.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 8 MINS, SOAK UP THE SAVINGS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And soak up the savings with "Deals & Steals" all about summer fun.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 16 MINUTES, ADELE ANNOUNCEMENT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Plus, Adele's announcement, who will be joining her on staged in London? But what about that postponed Las Vegas residency? All the details ahead in "Pop News."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Welcome back to "GMA." Coming up, hosting in the heat.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOSTING IN THE HEAT, COMING UP\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The hot tips to avoid a major meltdown at your summer soir\u233\'00\'E9e. We're talking table-scapes. We'll have much more on that in our next half hour. They were actually really pretty. I saw them.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, we're going. We're going to show it to you. That's coming up.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPREME COURT OVERTURNS ROE V. WADE SEVERAL STATES BEGAN IMPOSING BANS\b0\par\par\uc2 778 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In the hours since the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade, abortion bans have gone into effect in seven states, and that number is likely to rise as other states act on trigger laws designed to go into effect at some point after the high court's ruling.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BANS IN U.S.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And overnight, supporters of abortion rights filled the streets in many cities. This video shows police clashing with protesters in Los Angeles. Others gathered outside the home of Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . While anti-abortion right backers also demonstrated in celebration.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The ruling is at odds with the opinions of most Americans. At least according to an ABC News/\uc2 Washington Post\uc2  released just before the leak of the draft Supreme Court ruling. The survey suggesting that most Americans favored upholding Roe, keeping abortion legal in all or most cases, and that the decision should be left up to a woman and her doctor. We start our coverage with ABC's Terry Moran at the Supreme Court. Good morning to you, Terry.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION SURVEY\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Eva. It is a new day for women in America who have lost a constitutional right first recognized 50 years ago. For the millions of Americans who fought to end that right, and many of those now feel their prayers have been answered, and for the Supreme Court and its newly emboldened activist conservative majority.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, shockwaves across the country. In a sweeping ruling that overturned a half a century of precedence, five justices ended the right of American women to choose abortion under the Constitution. The monumental decision now leaves the issue up to the 50 states. The much anticipated ruling upheld Mississippi's ban on abortions after 15 weeks by a vote of 6-3 with five of the conservative justices also voting to overturn Roe. Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  writing in the majority opinion that "the Constitution makes no reference to abortion and no such right is implicitly protected by any constitutional provision." The decision handed down more than eight weeks after that unprecedented leak of a draft version of Justice Alito's opinion. For those opposed to abortion rights, it was a day of victory and vindication.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALITO ON ROE V. WADE VERDICT\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS (PRESIDENT OF NATL RIGHT TO LIFE COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 We have been working on this for more than 50 years, before Roe v. Wade, working to protect unborn children. We have certainly in the pro-life movement a lot of support and programs, centers, pregnancy centers that will help women.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In dissent, Justice \uc2 Steven Breyer\uc2  joined by Justices Elena Kagan and \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , sounded a somber warning. "With sorrow for this court, but more, for the many millions of American women who have today lost a fundamental Constitutional protection, we dissent."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREYER ON ROE V. WADE VERDICT\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) President Biden addressing the nation, saying it is a sad day for the court and for the country.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The court has done what it has never done before, expressly take away a constitutional right that is so fundamental to so many Americans, that had already been recognized.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Former president Trump taking credit for recent conservative Supreme Court decisions. "Because I delivered everything as promised, including nominating and getting three highly respected and constitutionalists confirmed to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 ." Now with this ruling, 13 states could enact their trigger laws, outlawing abortion after Roe is gone. So far seven states have put their bans in effect. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  calling the ruling cruel and heart wrenching.\par \par \uc2 REP NANCY PELOSI (DEM) (SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE)\par \par \uc2 Is deadly serious, but we are not going to let this pass. A woman's right to choose, reproductive freedom is on the ballot in November.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In his opinion, Justice Alito insisted that this ruling applies only to abortion, but the reasoning that the court used to overturn Roe versus Wade could be used to go after other rights and Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said it should. He wrote his own opinion in which he called on the court to reconsider the cases that established a right to purchase contraceptives, a right for gay people to get married, even a right to private sexual conduct.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 POP NEWS\b0\par\par\uc2 773 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, BILL\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, OSLO, PRIDE\par \par \uc2 G7, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, SHELLING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE RETREATING FROM SIEVIERODONETSK\par \par \uc2 NHL, LIGHTNING, AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, COMMONWEALTH\par \par \uc2 FAMU, MARCHING 100, VUITTON, NEASMAN, ROBERTS, JARRELL\par \par \uc2 CAR, CAKE, OVEN, BROWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, SHEPHERD\par \par \uc2 CAMP, LGBTQ+, BERKOWITZ, BOGARD, GRABEL, GOLDSCHMIDT\par \par \uc2 SWAEBE, SPONGELLE, PERRICONE, FEELGOODZ, THEOS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 ADELE, ROBERTS, "THOR: LOVE AND THUNDER", "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It is time now for "Pop News." Our friend Megan Ryte is in the house. What's happening?\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hello. Good morning. We begin this morning with a big congratulations to our very own Robin Roberts.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROBIN ROBERTS WINS EMMY\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Her \uc2 Disney\uc2  Plus show, "Turning the Tables" picking up the award for outstanding informative talk show at last night's Daytime Emmys. Cheers to Robin and her teammates. Way to go. That is amazing.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, also this morning, hello, it's Adele.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ADELE REVEALS HYDE PARK LINEUP\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The singer is revealing she's sharing the stage for our highly anticipated London shows. Adele writing on her \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , quote, "The lineup for Hyde Park shows next week will blow your socks off." Kasey Musgrave, Gabrielle, Mahalia and more. Now, Adele will take the stage on July 1st and 2nd as a part of the British Summertime Music Festival that kicked off last night with a performance from Elton John. Now, still no word from the "Easy on Me" singer on her postponed Vegas residency, which we all need to go to, by the way. But it will be great to see Adele back on the stage in London.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, road trip to Vegas.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Yes. Yes. Let's do it. I'm still working on these tickets.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It's so dangerous for this crowd.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good point, yes.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And news from the MCU and it's out of this world.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST REACTIONS ROLL IN FOR 'THOR: LOVE AND THUNDER'\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The stars of "Thor: Love and Thunder" hit the red carpet for the world premiere. And the early reactions from critics are electric. Now, one describes the movie as, quote, "a vivid and vibrant blast that delivers." Hemsworth's Thor remains a jewel in Marvel's crown. And Hemsworth isn't the only star bringing the thunder. Natalie Portman making her return to Marvel, and she can flex. Portman telling "Variety," quote, "I was asked to get as big as possible. That's an amazing challenge and also state of mind as a woman." "Thor: Love and Thunder" hits theaters July 8th.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, finally, our hearts will go on.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 'TITANIC' REMASTERED SET FOR 2023 RELEASE\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) "Titanic," the Oscar-winner setting sail back to the cinema in celebration of its 25th anniversary. The Oscar darling better than ever, fully remastered for its return to the big screen. You can experience it all over again just before Valentine's Day. "Titanic" remastered hits theaters February 2023. When that movie first came out, I ran out of the theater and my mom had to run after me. So, I might try to recreate that. I was so upset. I was crying, screaming out of the theater. I mean, what?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Make room on that door?\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They're going to start that debate over again.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She should have made room.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, no spoilers. We can't let people know what happens at the end.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I thought...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible)\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That's a true statement, too.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I hope they have bathroom breaks this time.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Coming up, the tastiest trends taking a bite out of the hottest summer eats. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DEALS & STEALS SUMMERTIME ESSENTIALS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,367 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, BILL\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, OSLO, PRIDE\par \par \uc2 G7, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, SHELLING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE RETREATING FROM SIEVIERODONETSK\par \par \uc2 NHL, LIGHTNING, 