\uc2 ) So, they're just built for comfort. They're designed for comfort. We've got have a huge assortment for men and women. There's a kind of like a spongy softness that molds to your feet.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I was going to say. They're basic, but they feel really good.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. But they're elevated in the terms of the comfort that they provide. Price is phenomenal. They range from $6 to $30.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEELGOODZ, 50%-75% OFF\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Ooh.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, then to go with our background that you described a little Miami, I took two pairs here. These are Rheos. These are floating sunglasses.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Floating?\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Did I get something, did I mess up that one on the front of it?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That might have been from my fingertip.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That might have been from you, yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll try another one. So cool.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. They both look cool on you, though. Oh, yeah. They do. So, they float. So, do you see how lightweight they are?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VC) They're designed to float. So, for people who are near water, these are phenomenal. They're also, they've got a very clear lens with them. So, these are extremely well made. They're going to last. A huge assortment hat you will find on line, $32.50 and free shipping.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RHEOS, 50% OFF, FREE SHIPPING FOR GMA\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, amazing.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And then to go with your sunglasses, we've got a big seafood summer feast from City Pier Seafood. They have kind of assembled all of their best sellers. Salmon hot, salmon hot dogs.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Salmon hot dogs?\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yep, bacon-wrapped scallops, gouda, smoked gouda mac and cheese, shrimp skewers, their seafood medley. You get all of this in their bundle. It is $93 and free shipping.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CITY PIER SEAFOOD, 50% OFF\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And actually, it's not free shipping. I'm sorry. It's free shipping on Perricone, Spongelle. It's a really...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Still a good deal. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A phenomenal deal on this. It is a phenomenal deal. You get everything and you spend more time having fun. Less time prepping in the kitchen.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm not that excited about salmon hot dogs, I swear. Thank you, Tory. We have partnered with all of these incredible companies. Find each deal on our website, goodmorningamerica.com.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE COUNTRY SPLIT OVER ROE V. WADE DECISION THOUSANDS TAKE TO THE STREETS\b0\par\par\uc2 590 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, BILL\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, OSLO, PRIDE\par \par \uc2 G7, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, SHELLING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE RETREATING FROM SIEVIERODONETSK\par \par \uc2 NHL, LIGHTNING, AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, COMMONWEALTH\par \par \uc2 FAMU, MARCHING 100, VUITTON, NEASMAN, ROBERTS, JARRELL\par \par \uc2 CAR, CAKE, OVEN, BROWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, it is a busy Saturday morning here. Of course, reaction overnight to the Supreme Court overturning Roe v. Wade, ending 50 years of federal rights.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And supporters of abortion rights filling the streets across the country, some gathering outside the home of Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  while backers of anti-abortion rights celebrated the ruling.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION SURVEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHOULD THE U.S. SUPREME COURT UPHOLD OR OVERTURN ROE V. WADE?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The decision at odds with the opinions of most Americans at least according to an ABC News/Washington Post poll released just before that leak of the draft's ruling. The survey suggesting that most Americans favored upholding Roe, keeping abortion legal in all or most cases and that the decision should be left to a woman and her doctor.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION SHOULD BE...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DECISION WHETHER A WOMAN CAN HAVE AN ABORTION SHOULD BE...\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For more on the reaction overnight, let's go back to ABC's Stephanie Ramos. Good morning again, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hi there, Eva. We're outside the only abortion clinic in the state of Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE COUNTRY SPLIT OVER ROE V. WADE DECISION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS TAKE TO THE STREETS PROTESTING, SOME TURNING VIOLENT\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They tell us they'll be able to stay open for about another week and a half, and then they will be forced to shut down. You can see, it's calm here now, but soon after that decision was made, it was contentious out here between clinic workers and anti-abortion rights activists. Take a look at what we saw in other parts of the country overnight.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Thousands taking to the streets across the country in protest of the Supreme Court's decision overturning of Roe v. Wade. From New York to Los Angeles, many still in shock following the decision even after the leaked draft opinion indicated the historic reversal. Some with emotions of joy, others angered. And at this abortion rights rally in Iowa, a truck charged into a crowd, injuring one protester. In Arizona authorities using tear gas to disperse protesters near the state capitol building in Phoenix. The Supreme Court ruling in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization clears the way for states to reshape abortion law in this country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BAN TRIGGER LAWS\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, with Roe overturned, a dozen Republican-controlled states with so-called trigger laws would make abortion illegal almost immediately and more could follow quickly. This here is the only abortion clinic in the state of Mississippi. And it is at the heart of the Supreme Court decision. Volunteers here are worried about the ruling and how it will affect women seeking its services. Janai?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Stephanie, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WEATHER\b0\par\par\uc2 365 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, BILL\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, OSLO, PRIDE\par \par \uc2 G7, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, SHELLING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE RETREATING FROM SIEVIERODONETSK\par \par \uc2 NHL, LIGHTNING, AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, COMMONWEALTH\par \par \uc2 FAMU, MARCHING 100, VUITTON, NEASMAN, ROBERTS, JARRELL\par \par \uc2 CAR, CAKE, OVEN, BROWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, SHEPHERD\par \par \uc2 CAMP, LGBTQ+, BERKOWITZ, BOGARD, GRABEL, GOLDSCHMIDT\par \par \uc2 SWAEBE, SPONGELLE, PERRICONE, FEELGOODZ, THEOS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 ADELE, ROBERTS, "THOR: LOVE AND THUNDER", "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 DURAN, FANCY FOOD SHOW\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, LEGISLATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN SIGNS GUN SAFETY BILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: $13.2 BILLION PACKAGE PASSED ONE MONTH AFTER UVALDE SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) For now, though, we want to turn, get a check of the weather. Greg Dutra is here from our station in Chicago, WLS. Greg, good morning once again.\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Whit. And taking a live view from where I'm from early this morning, Chicago.\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're looking out on Lake Michigan, as it's a fairly dry and cool morning, but are going to change up. You can see those clouds moving in those are from showers and thunderstorms that are off from the west.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRIDE WEEKEND\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We'll see some showers in the Midwest through the mid-morning. Iowa had a ton of rain. But look at this, Pride forecast for the weekend, moving into Sunday, some really good weather including back in Chicago and Tulsa and in San Antonio and back to New York City, too, really looking fantastic through the second half of the weekend. Some of this forecast, Love, Texas, 98.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRIDE CITIES\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our graphics team outdid themselves on this one. 95 on Pride, Alabama, and Rainbow, California hitting 89 degrees.\par \par \uc2 GREG DUTRA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll take a look at your local forecast now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONAL MAP\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p0000s\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TASTIEST TRENDS TAKE A BITE OUT OF THE LATEST SUMMER EATS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,036 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, BILL\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, OSLO, PRIDE\par \par \uc2 G7, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, SHELLING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE RETREATING FROM SIEVIERODONETSK\par \par \uc2 NHL, LIGHTNING, AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, COMMONWEALTH\par \par \uc2 FAMU, MARCHING 100, VUITTON, NEASMAN, ROBERTS, JARRELL\par \par \uc2 CAR, CAKE, OVEN, BROWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, SHEPHERD\par \par \uc2 CAMP, LGBTQ+, BERKOWITZ, BOGARD, GRABEL, GOLDSCHMIDT\par \par \uc2 SWAEBE, SPONGELLE, PERRICONE, FEELGOODZ, THEOS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 ADELE, ROBERTS, "THOR: LOVE AND THUNDER", "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 DURAN, FANCY FOOD SHOW\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, we do want to turn now to some tasty trends. Chef George Duran is here with the coolest summer eats that will have the whole family digging in. George, good morning. It is always great to have you. So, we've got you in studio, you're going to walk us through this thing here. So, this is the biggest food show in the US. Tell us what this is all about.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE DURAN (CHEF)\par \par \uc2 It is the Fancy Food Show. It is every year. It is where the world comes to kind of showcase their innovation with food. It is a foodie's Disneyland. That's what I call it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TASTIEST TRENDS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TAKE A BITE OUT OF THE LATEST SUMMER EATS\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, okay. So, you brought us some of the incentive new food products from the show. And the biggest trend was plant-based food. I see a lot of plants on this plate.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE DURAN (CHEF)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Plant-based was like the name of the game here with different kinds of meats. But this goes above and beyond. These guys are called carrot cheese. That's right, a cheese made out of carrots. It's by a company called Rind and they won the prize for best new product, actually.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLANT BASED FOODS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "CHEESE" MADE WITH CARROT PULP INSTEAD OF DAIRY\par \par \uc2 GEORGE DURAN (CHEF)\par \par \uc2 It melts and it tastes like the real thing, actually. And I was just floored by when I tasted their grilled cheese sandwich how good it is. And then you also have things like jerky, which is plant-based and it's made with actually the stems of the mushroom by Vegky. They actually use the end stems. Instead of throwing it away, they use it to make jerky.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JERKY MADE WITH "UP-CYCLED" MUSHROOM STEMS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE DURAN (CHEF)\par \par \uc2 And then if you want a crispier version, well, these guys from Mush Gardens have a shiitake mushroom that, they taste so good. They're just made out of real mushrooms, shiitake mushrooms but they're super crispy.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow. And there are also some new creative milk substitutes?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE DURAN (CHEF)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Beyond going the nut milks and the oat milks, well, the seeds are a big thing, too, chia milk by So. And then this one is incredible. Open sesame, it's a sesame milk.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW MILK SUBSTITUTES: CHIA & SESAME MILK NUTRITIOUS & SUSTAINABLE OPTIONS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE DURAN (CHEF)\par \par \uc2 And you guys have the chocolate milk over there. It froths really nicely for cappuccinos. And honestly, it's also very planet healthy, let's put it that way. It's not bad, right?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I like it. Although, on a hot day milk is a bad choice.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But this is delicious. Little "Anchor Man" impression there. In case you miss it. For the people cutting carbs, there's a new is a fun substitute here. Tell us about the cheese wraps we have (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 GEORGE DURAN (CHEF)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, it's incredible. It is a cheese wrap. It's called the Folio's cheese wrap by (inaudible). And essentially, instead of tortilla, the Keto people are going to go nuts over this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CARB SUBSTITUTES: CHEESE WRAPS SUPER THIN & CAN BE USED IN PLACE OF TORTILLAS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE DURAN (CHEF)\par \par \uc2 You pull out this cheese slice with this and you fold it into a sandwich wrap. But the best part is that when you microwave it for a minute or two, it crisps up into like salad bowls or chips or even a tortilla shell, but it's made out of cheese, 100% cheese. Cheddar, Jarlsberg, Parmesan, mozzarella, whatever you want it. These guys at Folios they know what they're making. It was like amazing.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, and for those parents, you know, who are trying to get their kids to get more vegetables, always sneaking the vegetables in, you've got some new products to help us.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE DURAN (CHEF)\par \par \uc2 Yes. Something like this by Kicker Feast. It's a beet pancake mix. So, you get the sweetness of the beets naturally. This one was pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EAT YOUR VEGETABLES! BEET PANCAKES, SWEET POTATO, CROUTONS, CAULIFLOWER PUFFS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE DURAN (CHEF)\par \par \uc2 It's called Roots Farm and Fresh. So, it's, essentially it's a crouton made out of sweet potato. So, all the ugly sweet potatoes that people don't want in the market, they take it and they make it into a frozen product into a sweet potato crouton. And finally, Vegan Rob's, well, if you like chips and puffs and give them to your kids, this is a cauliflower puff. And my kids swear that it's better than the bright orange ones that you get at the market.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, that is a strong statement.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It is, it's good. Nice and firm. Yeah, this is really good actually.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm going to that very shortly here. Well, coming up, thank you so much for being with us. Summer soir\u233\'00\'E9e season is here. The hottest tips to planning your next party. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 09:00 HALF-HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 486 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, BILL\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, OSLO, PRIDE\par \par \uc2 G7, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, SHELLING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE RETREATING FROM SIEVIERODONETSK\par \par \uc2 NHL, LIGHTNING, AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, COMMONWEALTH\par \par \uc2 FAMU, MARCHING 100, VUITTON, NEASMAN, ROBERTS, JARRELL\par \par \uc2 CAR, CAKE, OVEN, BROWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. It's our second hour.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LATEST THIS MORNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OVERTURNING ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Overnight, reaction on both sides to the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade, ending 50 years of federal rights. What you need to know about the trigger laws already in effect.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA OUT LOUD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUMMER FUN FOR ALL\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also, this morning, "GMA" out loud.\par \par \uc2 SHIRA BERKOWITZ (CO-FOUNDER CAMP INDIGO POINT)\par \par \uc2 I think it's everything.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Summer fun for all. How Camp Indigo Point is making a difference for LGBTQ+ kids.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LINEUP FOR LONDON SHOWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ADELE ROLLS DEEP\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Adele rolls deep, announcing a powerhouse lineup. Who's joining the Grammy winner on stage for our highly anticipated Hyde Park shows? This as fans await the fate of that postponed Las Vegas residency.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OUR HEARTS WILL GO ON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNSINKABLE "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Our hearts will go on. Twenty-five years later, "Titanic" still proving unsinkable.\par \par \uc2 LEONARDO DICAPRIO (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 I'm king of the world.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 How the blockbuster plans to set sail for box office domination once more.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT TIPS & TASTY TRENDS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAKE YOUR SUMMER SIZZLE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Make your summer sizzle from the tastiest trends to soaking up savings. Plus, hot tips for hosting a summertime soir\u233\'00\'E9e.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PARTY TIME!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHIT'S 40TH BIRTHDAY\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And celebrating our birthday boy.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Check out my force.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Whoa.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Whit's turning 40, ready to kick off his big B-day bash as we say, "Good Morning America."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 It's Saturday morning. This is "GMA."\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. And happy birthday to Whit today. Today is the day.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Today.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 I know, yeah. And there's no place I'd rather be than right here in the studio all day long. I don't feel a day older than 39, though. So...\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Really?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 No, but it's fun.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Let's see how you (inaudible) end of this long day.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good point. Good point.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible) go back.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 09:30 HALF-HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 564 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, BILL\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, OSLO, PRIDE\par \par \uc2 G7, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, SHELLING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE RETREATING FROM SIEVIERODONETSK\par \par \uc2 NHL, LIGHTNING, AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, COMMONWEALTH\par \par \uc2 FAMU, MARCHING 100, VUITTON, NEASMAN, ROBERTS, JARRELL\par \par \uc2 CAR, CAKE, OVEN, BROWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, SHEPHERD\par \par \uc2 CAMP, LGBTQ+, BERKOWITZ, BOGARD, GRABEL, GOLDSCHMIDT\par \par \uc2 SWAEBE, SPONGELLE, PERRICONE, FEELGOODZ, THEOS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 ADELE, ROBERTS, "THOR: LOVE AND THUNDER", "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We should get a midi-chlorian count on this child because it's high as a Jedi. Check out my force.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Whoa.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Welcome back. It's "GMA." Whit doing his best Baby Yoda. Coming up, in "Binge This," we are celebrating his big 40th birthday with his favorite movie, music and TV from a galaxy far, far away.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also, just for the record, none of that was scripted. You came up with that on your own. That is true Whit Johnson for the audience.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That is clever.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 I love me some Baby Yoda, also known as Grogu or the child. So, that was fun, though. It looked a little more ridiculous watching it back. In the moment it seemed, it was better when it happened.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 It's still our favorite.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Our favorite Baby Yoda Dan Harris.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That's right. Exactly, yep.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That is all ahead. We are really looking forward to that. Here are some of the headlines we're following right now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUUL BAN BLOCKED\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 A Federal Appeals Court has temporary blocked an FDA ban on \uc2 Juul\uc2  electronic cigarettes. That means \uc2 Juul\uc2  can continue selling its goods while it appeals that ban. On Thursday, the FDA said \uc2 Juul\uc2  has to stop selling their vaping devices in flavored cartridges after the company failed to prove their products benefit public health.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NOTEBOOK RELEASED\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 A lawyer has released pages from a notebook found near the body of Brian Laundrie where he admits to killing Gabby Petito. In the notebook, Laundrie wrote that Petito was injured and he tried to help her. He wrote, quote, "I ended her life. I thought it was merciful, that it is what she wanted. But I see now all the mistakes I made. I was panicked. I was in shock." Petito's cause of death was ruled to be strangulation.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALVAREZ CHEERS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And artistic swimmer Anita Alvarez was in the stands cheering on the Team USA at the World Aquatic Championship on Friday. This was after she fainted in the water and was seen sinking to the bottom of the pool earlier this week as her coach dove in to save her. Alvarez has written on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  that she is okay and healthy after a frightening scare.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Those scary moments.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p0000p\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUMMER FUN FOR ALL HOW CAMP INDIGO POINT IS MAKING A DIFFERENCE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,028 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, BILL\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, OSLO, PRIDE\par \par \uc2 G7, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, SHELLING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE RETREATING FROM SIEVIERODONETSK\par \par \uc2 NHL, LIGHTNING, AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, COMMONWEALTH\par \par \uc2 FAMU, MARCHING 100, VUITTON, NEASMAN, ROBERTS, JARRELL\par \par \uc2 CAR, CAKE, OVEN, BROWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, SHEPHERD\par \par \uc2 CAMP, LGBTQ+, BERKOWITZ, BOGARD, GRABEL, GOLDSCHMIDT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right now, though, our "GMA Cover Story." And the summer camp in Illinois making a big difference this Pride Month.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Our friend Will Ganss has that story and all the details. Will, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUMMER FUN FOR ALL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW CAMP INDIGO POINT IS MAKING A DIFFERENCE\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning. And happy birthday to you, Whit Johnson. Okay, so many of us have warm memories from summer camp as kids, including my new friend, Shira Berkowitz. They went to camp as a child and became a counselor as a young adult. But when they came out, they were asked not to return to work. So, Shira, along with some other change makers, decided to open their own camp.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At first glance, it looks like any ordinary summer camp. From fireside sing-alongs, to canoeing on the lake, to gaga ball. But Camp Indigo Point is anything but ordinary.\par \par \uc2 SHIRA BERKOWITZ (CO-FOUNDER CAMP INDIGO POINT)\par \par \uc2 We started as gender expansive and trans camp out of LGBTQ+, so that we were like as inclusive as possible.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the camp's inaugural week, 97 kids from 29 states across the country filling each available spot. And a waitlist of 60 plus proving there was a need for a place like this.\par \par \uc2 SHIRA BERKOWITZ (CO-FOUNDER CAMP INDIGO POINT)\par \par \uc2 It's just this little like oasis for LGBTQ youth.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A spot where these kids are able to just be kids.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) As a parent, what does that mean to you?\par \par \uc2 DANIEL BOGARD (CO-FOUNDER CAMP INDIGO POINT)\par \par \uc2 It just made my kid confident in who they are and who he is. I don't know how to overstate, yeah. No, I mean it. I don't know how to overstate how important that has been.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For the founders of Camp Indigo Point, a mission of inclusion is personal.\par \par \uc2 SHIRA BERKOWITZ (CO-FOUNDER CAMP INDIGO POINT)\par \par \uc2 I was working at a summer camp, a new summer camp to me. And when I came out to my co-counselor, there was all of this talk about how that was not appropriate, and I was asked not to come back to the camp.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, to be here now, you know, with these 97 kiddos on their way here to celebrate their identities, your identity.\par \par \uc2 SHIRA BERKOWITZ (CO-FOUNDER CAMP INDIGO POINT)\par \par \uc2 I think it's everything. I just needed to see other peers were like me, and that could have been enough.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dan Grabel has spent more than 20 years running summer camps and recognized the need for a camp like this six years ago.\par \par \uc2 DAN GRABEL (CO-FOUNDER CAMP INDIGO PLANT)\par \par \uc2 We got a call from a family whose kid was not allowed to go to a camp that they had originally chosen. He's a transgender kid, and they did not want him coming to camp. So, they called us and we said, sure.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) LGBTQ+ campers supported by a fully LGBTQ+ staff of counselors like music therapist Daniel Goldschmidt.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL GOLDSCHMIDT (COUNSELOR CAMP INDIGO POINT)\par \par \uc2 You know, here is a place that people is going to understand how pronouns work. We're all kind of creating this space that hasn't existed for most of us.\par \par \uc2 SHIRA BERKOWITZ (CO-FOUNDER CAMP INDIGO POINT)\par \par \uc2 Being able to give them a space where they not only have to think about it or to know that they're not valuable, that every single person around here is like them, so they don't have to talk about it if they don't want to is a beautiful thing.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What are you most looking forward to this week? Like on just like a summer camp level?\par \par \uc2 SHIRA BERKOWITZ (CO-FOUNDER CAMP INDIGO POINT)\par \par \uc2 Oh, my God, being at camp. This lake is the cleanest lake. I just want to run and jump in it and swim.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You knew that was going to happen.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Cannonball.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thousands of dollars in scholarships were handed out to help welcome as many kiddos as possible there, too. And the future of Camp Indigo Point is bright. Shira and the other founders coming back next year with more weeks to host more campers. And they're launching a nonprofit to provide programming for campers all year long.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What a great story.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It is really.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I also appreciated your singing, too, there. Is that Pocahontas there on the riverbend?\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It was Pocahontas, yeah. My wig did not fit in my carry-on, so that's the only thing that was missing.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You looked like you had so much fun.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WILL GANSS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It was so fun, but also so important to be there to share that story.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. Thank you, Will. Let's head over to Janai. Janai?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why aren't you complimenting my singing voice, Whit Johnson?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, yeah.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BIDEN SIGNS GUN SAFETY BILL $13.2 BILLION PACKAGE PASSED ONE MONTH AFTER UVALDE SHOOTING\b0\par\par\uc2 632 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, BILL\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, OSLO, PRIDE\par \par \uc2 G7, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, SHELLING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE RETREATING FROM SIEVIERODONETSK\par \par \uc2 NHL, LIGHTNING, AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, COMMONWEALTH\par \par \uc2 FAMU, MARCHING 100, VUITTON, NEASMAN, ROBERTS, JARRELL\par \par \uc2 CAR, CAKE, OVEN, BROWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, SHEPHERD\par \par \uc2 CAMP, LGBTQ+, BERKOWITZ, BOGARD, GRABEL, GOLDSCHMIDT\par \par \uc2 SWAEBE, SPONGELLE, PERRICONE, FEELGOODZ, THEOS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 ADELE, ROBERTS, "THOR: LOVE AND THUNDER", "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 DURAN, FANCY FOOD SHOW\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, LEGISLATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN SIGNS GUN SAFETY BILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: $13.2 BILLION PACKAGE PASSED ONE MONTH AFTER UVALDE SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're back now with some breaking news. We want to go to Washington right now where President Biden signed the new bill into law this morning. It's the first gun reform bill to get through Congress in decades. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 ...undergo enhanced background checks. It includes the first ever federal law that makes gun trafficking and straw purchases distinct federal crimes for the first time and clarifies who needs to register as a federally licensed gun dealer and run background checks before selling a single weapon. You know, this also provides historic funding to address youth mental health crisis in this country, especially, especially the trauma experienced by the survivors of this gun violence. Look, you know, we invest in anti-violence programs that work and work with the people most likely to commit these crimes and become victims of gun crimes. Today, we say more than enough. We say more than enough.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It is time when it seems impossible to do anything down in Washington, we are doing something consequential. We can reach compromise on guns, we ought to be able to reach compromise on other critical issues from veterans' health care to cutting-edge American innovation and so much more. I know there's much more work to do, and I'm never going to give up. But this is a monumental day. God bless us with the strength to continue to work to get the work that's left undone done and the lives lost that can't be saved that are obviously gone, but will be an inspiration for us to do more. I'm now going to sign this bill into law.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) President Biden there moving over to sign this landmark legislation. This bipartisan gun safety reform bill. As he signs this, we can just walk through what it includes here. It enhances background checks for gun buyers under 21 years old, prevents partners convicted of domestic abuse from purchasing a gun and provides money for mental health services and school security. The bill was passed following the recent mass shootings in Uvalde, Texas and Buffalo, New York. President Biden has acknowledged he's not getting everything he wanted, but it's still significant that Republicans and Democrats alike were able to pass this through. The President answering some questions. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Will you reconsider your (inaudible) expanding the (inaudible), sir? What about term limits for justices?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And there you have it once again, the President not answering questioning at the end there. Both related to the gun safety legislation, but also the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. The President there at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p0000r\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 COUNTRY SPLIT OVER ROE V. WADE DECISION THOUSANDS TAKE TO THE STREETS\b0\par\par\uc2 548 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Terry, thank you. We move now to the reaction to this ruling across the country. Several states immediately moving to put abortion bans into effect. ABC's Stephanie Ramos joins us from Jackson, Mississippi with more on that. Stephanie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hi there, Whit. We are outside the only abortion clinic in the state of Mississippi. They tell us they will be able to stay open for about another week and a half. And then they will be forced to shut their doors.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Overnight, thousands taking to the streets across the country protesting the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. From New York to Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 No justice.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 No peace.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Many still in shock following the decision, even after the leaked draft opinion indicated the historic reversal. Some with emotions of joy.\par \par \uc2 ADDIA WUCHNER (FORMER KENTUCKY STATE REP)\par \par \uc2 This is a monumental moment for all of us that have worked so many years on pro-life (inaudible) legislation. It is amazing.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And rage. At an abortion rights rally in Iowa, a truck charging into a crowd, injuring one protester.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I look over. I see people trying to push the truck back, and I just instantly got mad and ran over, tried to stop the truck.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Arizona, authorities using tear gas to disperse protestors near the state capitol building in Phoenix. The Supreme Court ruling in Dobbs versus Jackson Women's Health Organization clears the way for states to reshape abortion law in this country. Now with Roe overturned, about a dozen Republican controlled states with so-called trigger laws would make abortion illegal almost immediately, and more could follow quickly. This is the only abortion clinic in the state of Mississippi. And it is at the heart of the Supreme Court decision. Volunteers worried about how the ruling will affect women seeking its services.\par \par \uc2 DERENDA HANCOCK (PINK HOUSE DEFENDER)\par \par \uc2 They will either be forced to give birth to an unwanted pregnancy, or they're going to take matters into their own hands.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And we were there as antiabortion rights demonstrators turned some women away, telling them the clinic was closed, even though it isn't yet. Groups plan to prescribe abortion pills along borders of restrictive states. The group Just The Pill, and Abortion Delivered, has vowed to deploy a fleet of mobile clinics along the borders of states that impose restrictions. But for now, protests are planned across the country. DC Police ramping up staffing in anticipation of more of these demonstrations.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  says they are on alert for domestic violent extremists following the court's decision, as you can see, it is calm now outside this clinic in Jackson. But soon after that decision was made yesterday, it was contentious out here between clinic workers and antiabortion rights activists.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 JUSTICE THOMAS' CONCURRING OPINION TAKES AIM AT RIGHTS TO CONTRACEPTION & GAY RIGHTS\b0\par\par\uc2 589 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it will no doubt be an issue that everyone is talking about for the election. Joining us now is Kate Shaw, professor of constitutional law at Cardoza Law School. Thanks so much for being with us this morning. One of the things that a lot of people are talking about is this concurring opinion from Justice Thomas, which he said that the court should reconsider cases that made contraception legal, same sex relations legal, and gay marriage legal. How much danger are those things in now that Roe has been overturned?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think they're in a good deal of danger. The majority opinion by Justice Sam Alito goes out of its way to say, none of those precedents on contraception, on same sex marriage, on sexual intimacy, are at issue here because this case is about abortion, and abortion is different. But if you read the reasoning of the Alito majority opinion, it says the way to figure out if a right is actually protected by the liberty guaranteed by the Constitution, is to look at whether that right has been protected historically. What do history and tradition tell us? In this case, Alito said, the history tells us abortion hasn't been subject to protection and for that reason, we should overturn Roe because it was wrong.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When you take the same reasoning to things like access to contraception and same sex marriage, history is not gonna provide a lot of support for legal protections for those rights. Those are recent developments. So the method seems to spell I think real danger for those previous cases the court has decided. And that's just the inference you can draw from the majority opinion. The Thomas concurrence you just mentioned is explicit. It says in no uncertain terms, the court should overrule cases guaranteeing the right to access contraception, prohibiting bans on same sex sexual intimacy, and guaranteeing a right to same sex marriage. So I think all of that is now in play.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, and I think the big thing a lot of people are trying to figure out is what does this ruling tell us? We have this conservative super majority. What do we garner from all of this that tells us how they may act in the future.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think that they are emboldened, and not interested in proceeding incrementally, right? Just think about this week. We saw the court fundamentally change the law of carrying concealed weapons in major American cities, and now we saw the court overturn a 50 year old precedent. This is just the first full term that the three newest appointees, all President Trump's appointees, have been sitting on the court. So it doesn't appear that they are interested in proceeding cautiously or incrementally. But maybe we will see them in the coming years further change, really fundamental change, in kind of the face of American constitutional law. So I think that the court is in some ways just getting started.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Kate Shaw, thank you so much for being with us this morning. Whit, over to you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PLAY OF THE DAY\b0\par\par\uc2 384 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, BILL\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, OSLO, PRIDE\par \par \uc2 G7, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, SHELLING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE RETREATING FROM SIEVIERODONETSK\par \par \uc2 NHL, LIGHTNING, AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 PRINCE CHARLES, COMMONWEALTH\par \par \uc2 FAMU, MARCHING 100, VUITTON, NEASMAN, ROBERTS, JARRELL\par \par \uc2 CAR, CAKE, OVEN, BROWN\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Time now for our Play of the Day. And the guy who turned his car into an easy bake oven. So TikToker Joe Brown of Phoenix attempting to bake a cake when his car interior reached 210 degrees in the recent heat wave. After a couple of hours, he checked on it, his creation. He frosted it. Added some decorations. Look, a number two. He was ready for the feast. He rated it 10 out of 10. The best part, he says, was that his car smelled like a bakery.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I could see that, yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Maybe we should get him to bake your birthday cake, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, oh sure.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You should get him an icing spatula, so he can smooth out that icing.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I was just gonna say, I think that could be a new live shot for us. We're always looking for the hot car live shot.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yeah, you're right.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Two hours, that's all of "GMA" right.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A fun-fetti cake.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That's all it took.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And "GMA" is two hours. Just enough time to bake the cake in your car. Coming up, the states taking the next steps with the Supreme Court's overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And our "GMA" cover story, a summer camp addressing the needs of youth in the LGBTQ-plus community.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Plus, it's Deals & Steals, great items to get the most out of summer, just ahead.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PRESIDENT'S REACTION: "THIS IS NOT OVER" BIDEN SAYS RULING PUTS WOMEN'S HEALTH AT RISK\b0\par\par\uc2 367 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we will likely see that for a long time to come. Thank you so much. And from Mississippi back to Washington where President Biden is responding to the Supreme Court's historic ruling, saying the battle isn't over. ABC's Mary Bruce joins us now with more on what the White House is promising to do. Though, Mary, the steps the President can take are limited. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They are, Janai. Good morning. The President calling this decision a tragic error, saying it will put women's lives at risk. And he is vowing to do what we can here, announcing steps to defend women when they travel to other states to get an abortion, and to try and ensure that they abortion pill is still readily available. Now, there are other possible steps he could take, some more drastic than others.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OTHER STEPS PRESIDENT COULD TAKE\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He could declare a public health emergency or sue states at odds with FDA policy, or lease federal buildings to abortion providers or try and ease restrictions on importing those abortion pills. But the reality is that any steps the President takes are limited. He has acknowledged that. And anything he does is likely to be challenged and could be locked up in lengthy court battles, and the President knows this. This is why you are seeing Biden and Democrats stressing the importance of the upcoming midterm elections because of course the only way to really change this is for Congress to act, to pass a law protecting the right to an abortion. Democrats don't have the support to do that now, but they're hoping that could change in November if Americans elect candidates who back and support abortion rights. And that is why the President put it so bluntly yesterday, that Roe is on the ballot this fall.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GOOD MORNING AMERICA\b0\par\par\uc2 397 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 "Good Morning America." Night of protests. Abortion rights supporters take to the streets, all across America, following the Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe v. Wade. Police firing tear gas at demonstrators in Arizona. A black pickup truck seen plowing into people in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 We are moving.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 With officials warning, years of building passion could explode into violence.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIXED EMOTIONS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Mixed emotions. Antiabortion rights advocates hailing the ruling, while abortion supporters say their fight isn't over. The trigger laws putting abortion bans in place right now, and the time line for other states to follow.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BAN TRIGGER LAWS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 President Biden weighing in on the sweeping ruling overturning a half century of precedent.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN'S REACTION\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It just stuns me.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Is the door now open for the court to consider other landmark cases, some wondering what it could mean for things like contraception and gay marriage.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GUN SAFETY\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Gun safety following those recent mass shootings. President Biden ready to sign a historic bill this morning. The action to keep Americans safer.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORCES RETREAT\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Ukraine retreat. Forces expected to withdraw from a key besieged city, while Russian missiles strike a military facility near the Polish border. The move comes as President Biden heads to the G7 summit overseas. What's at stake.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASS SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Mass shooting. At least two killed and 10 wounded when a gunman opens fire in Norway during a Pride festival. Was it a terrorist attack?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: QUITTING THE QUEEN?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Quitting the Queen. The message from Prince Charles to commonwealth countries considering parting ways with the royal family.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE LIGHTNING STRIKES!\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And the Lightning strikes. Tampa Bay breaks through, defeating the Avalanche on Colorado's home turf. The play sending the series to a sixth game.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live from ABC News in New York, this is "Good Morning America."\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. Happy Saturday morning to you. Glad you're starting your weekend with us. Of course, a lot to follow.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CONCERNS OF VIOLENCE AMID ROE V. WADE PROTESTS AUTHORITIES ON HIGH ALERT\b0\par\par\uc2 865 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHT OF PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, PROTESTS, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ABOTION, WHITE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, RIGHTS, CONTRACEPTION, GAY MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GUN REFORM, BILL\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, OSLO, PRIDE\par \par \uc2 G7, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, SHELLING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINE RETREATING FROM SIEVIERODONETSK\par \par \uc2 NHL, LIGHTNING, AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "TITANIC"\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We start this half hour with law enforcement on alert following the Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe v. Wade. Officials seeing a rise in violent threats coming from some abortion rights supporters and some antiabortion right groups. ABC's Aaron Katersky has more. Aaron, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning to you. The Supreme Court opinion landed in a divided county, igniting years of building passion on both sides, that police now worry could explode into violence. Demonstrators are roiling American the streets. Police are warning them to stay peaceful, lawful, and nonviolent. Law enforcement agencies say there are already signs the Supreme Court opinion has motivated activists from both sides of the abortion debate to carry out vandalism, harassment and arson. Potential targets include abortion providers like this one, allegedly set on fire last month in Wyoming, and this clinic that provides alternatives to abortion, gutted by fire in western New York earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has warned locations like this they need to review security and be mindful that abortion related extremism is real and dangerous. And that message could not have been any clear when police arrested a man charged with traveling from California to attempt to assassinate Supreme Court Justice Kavanaugh. The Homeland Security bulletin obtained by ABC News said judges and government officials are most at risk for violence. The US Marshalls Service told us they are reviewing security for Supreme Court justices, and we're hearing from law enforcement sources they're seeing signs domestic extremists are trying to capitalized on the abortion debate by infiltrating protests.\par \par \uc2 AARON KATERSKY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Foreign actors are seeking to take advantage of this, too, by posting social media content inciting violence. It is a dangerous time, guys.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Aaron, thank you so much. Joining us now is ABC News deputy political director Avery Harper. Good morning. Always great to see you. Let's start with the political ramifications here of the court's decision. How is this likely to impact the midterm elections this year?\par \par \uc2 AVERY HARPER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right, we heard yesterday from Democrat after Democrat, including the President, urging Americans to vote for Democratic candidates who will support abortion access. There are hopes on the part of Democrats that this will be a galvanizing issue. When you look at recent polling including our own and you ask Americans what are the most important issues as you try to decide who you're going to vote for, abortion is not at the top of the list. It is matters of inflation or the economy. Now that we're seeing the rubber hit the road with abortion, there are hopes that that will shift.\par \par \uc2 AVERY HARPER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) At the same time, on the Republican side of things, many folks hope this, too, will be a motivating issue because you have to remember that overturning Roe is really the product and the culmination of years of organizing. When I poured over the reactions of lawmakers and Republican lawmakers, what I found is there is really a desire to go even further than overturning Roe. Only time will tell how this will impact the election but on both sides, folks are hoping it does.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and so now this issue goes back to the states. We have already seen some of the trigger laws taking effect. We have seen some governors sort of banning together. How are governors and lawmakers across the country preparing to address this?\par \par \uc2 AVERY HARPER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right, and so, yes, you mentioned the trigger bans that have gone into effect in several states. We know that there are women who are already being impacted by this ruling. There are lots of states we know are going to follow because there are lots of Republican states, again, when I looked at all these different states, they will move quickly to enforce some of these more strict restrictions on abortion care. At the same time, we're seeing Democrats do the exact opposite. They are pledging to defend access to abortion care in their states, not only for residents but also for women who might be seeking care from elsewhere. We saw in California, Washington, Oregon, those governors banding together saying they are going to provide abortion care and also try to find ways to prevent women from being punished in their home states for seeking abortion elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're expecting changes in about half the states across the country. Just shows how divided people are on this issue. Avery, thank you so much, as always.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220726ei6p0000d\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WHAT'S NEXT AFTER ROE? STATES AND GROUPS REACT QUICKLY AFTER HISTORIC DECISION\b0\par\par\uc2 586 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, PROTESTS, HAYWOOD, COLLINS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, PROTESTS, SHAW, WELLS, WALZ, O'CONNOR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT'S NEXT AFTER ROE?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And with this landmark Supreme Court decision, many are now asking, what's next, after Roe? While more states are moving to make abortions illegal, others are working to increase access. Plus, growing questions about how the Supreme Court could take on other matters, like birth control and same-sex marriage. Here's ABC's Zohreen Shah.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We are the post-Roe generation.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATES AND GROUPS REACT QUICKLY AFTER HISTORIC DECISION\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, the immediate impact of that game-changing Supreme Court decision being felt, nationwide.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Reproductive freedom requires access.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And tense standoffs.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We will vote. We will vote.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION ILLEGAL\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Eight states, tonight, have bans enforced, with 18 more likely to ban or severely restrict abortion in the coming days or weeks, including Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 JOHN O'CONNOR (OKLAHOMA ATTORNEY GENERAL)\par \par \uc2 That makes this Supreme Court decision the most life-saving decision in the history of our nation.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Sioux Falls, South Dakota, this Planned Parenthood sending patients to neighboring states.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR TIM WALZ (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 This outrage of this, we will stand for every single person to the fullest extent of the law to make sure that that never happens in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) That response from Minnesota's governor coming in the form of executive order, protecting people seeking abortions there from legal repercussions in their own state. Some women looking to telemedicine and virtual mailboxes. The online abortion site Plan C tells women how to find the abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 ELISA WELLS (CO-FOUNDER PLAN C)\par \par \uc2 And we want to make all forms of abortion as easily accessible to people as we can.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And tonight, experts weighing in after Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  argued in his concurring opinion the Supreme Court should reconsider other rulings codifying rights to contraception access and same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (CARDOZO SCHOOL OF LAW)\par \par \uc2 (VO) There's a very good chance that, probably not in the next year or two, but within a few years, issues like contraception or same-sex marriage could actually be subject to revisiting.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) People out here know the abortion decision has been made, but they tell us they are concerned for what could come next.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 These issues really affect the next generation. And if I come out here and speak up, then I can show my support for change.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  saying that, you know, basically, queer rights are next is why we all have to show up.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) California, Oregon, and Washington are joining forces to protect abortion rights. And here in California, the governor just signed a law to protect patients and providers from civil liability. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Zohreen Shah, thank you. And be sure to tune in to a special edition of "This Week" tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROE V WADE OVERTURNED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: A SPECIAL EDITION OF THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Martha Raddatz will go one-on-one with South Dakota Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  and Senator Elizabeth Warren on the future of the reproductive rights in America.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020220628ei6p00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PROTESTS ERUPT NATIONWIDE THOUSANDS TAKE TO THE STREETS AFTER SCOTUS RULING\b0\par\par\uc2 855 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, PROTESTS, HAYWOOD, COLLINS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. It's great to have you with us on this Saturday. I'm Whit Johnson.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROTESTS ERUPT NATIONWIDE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As we come on the air, tonight, protests erupting nationwide, reaction from both sides to the Supreme Court's landmark ruling overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Demonstrators gathering at the Supreme Court, those in favor of abortion rights at times facing off against many celebrating the justices' decision. Those protests spanning the entire country, today, most of them peaceful, as eight states already moved to make abortion illegal. Overnight, officers firing tear gas to break up a large crowd at outside the Arizona state capitol. And in Iowa, police launching an investigation after a truck hit protesters at an abortion rights rally in Cedar Rapids. One person taken to the hospital. Many now looking to the court after the conservative justices ended constitutional protections for abortion. And Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , in his concurring opinion, suggested the court might now rethink precedents set by other landmark rulings on gay marriage and contraception.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Those words helping to fuel some of those taking to the streets, today. We have team coverage, tonight. And ABC's congressional correspondent, Rachel Scott, leads us off from the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS TAKE TO THE STREETS AFTER SCOTUS RULING\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the Supreme Court's decision sending shockwaves across the nation.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 My body, my choice.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) From coast to coast, thousands protesting the end of the constitutional right to an abortion. In Los Angeles, demonstrators flooding the freeway. Overnight, a truck driver plowing through a crowd in Cedar Rapids, Iowa. Police now investigating. In Arizona, at the State Capitol, officers firing tear gas at the protesters. And police in Providence, Rhode Island now investigating an off-duty officer after this violent altercation at an abortion rights rally. But outside the Supreme Court, cheers and relief for those against abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I honestly cried tears of joy. It's been something that we have been waiting for for so long. And now, finally, for the lives of so many of the unborn to finally be protected and recognized that we do have the right to life, it was really moving for me.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN: SUPREME COURT HAS MADE SOME TERRIBLE DECISIONS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) President Biden warned of the dire consequences, insisting the ruling puts reproductive health at risk.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Do you think the Supreme Court is broken, in your view?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I think the Supreme Court has made some terrible decisions.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The landmark decision backed by the conservative majority, stripping away the right to an abortion after nearly 50 years. Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  writing, "Roe was egregiously wrong and deeply damaging. Roe was on a collision course with the Constitution from the day it was decided." And joining Alito were Justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , and Amy Coney Barrett. Three of them Trump appointees. Republican Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  was critical to putting Kavanaugh and Gorsuch on the bench, saying this about Kavanaugh at the time.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR SUSAN COLLINS (REP) (MAINE)\par \par \uc2 He noted that Roe had been reaffirmed 19 years later by Planned Parenthood versus Casey, and that it was precedent on precedent. He said it should be extremely rare that it be overturned.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, she says she was misled. In notes obtained by the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," Kavanaugh apparently gave Collins private assurances, claiming he understood the real-world consequences, and stating, "I am a don't-rock-the-boat kind of judge." But with Roe overturned, the impact was felt within minutes. Abortion now illegal for 31 million Americans in at least eight states, including Louisiana, where reproductive rights advocates say they are worried.\par \par \uc2 DEON HAYWOOD (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR WOMEN WITH A VISION)\par \par \uc2 People will have to carry a child to term when they're already living in substandard housing, when their children are not getting the best education, when they can barely feed their families.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Emotions raw on both sides. Rachel Scott, joining us, now, from the Supreme Court. And, Rachel, recent polling seems to indicate this ruling overturning Roe v. Wade is at odds with where a majority of Americans stand. So, how do supporters of abortion rights plan the carry on this fight in the future?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, Whit, I can tell you that many of the people gathered here today, along with Democrats, hope that this turns into driving issue in elections in November. On Capitol Hill, there is a push to protect access under the law, but Republicans are largely opposed to that. So, for now, this battle will be left up to the states, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rachel. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020220628ei6p00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPREME COURT RULES ON ABORTION RIGHTS CASE COURT OVERRULES ROE VERSUS WADE\b0\par\par\uc2 12,921 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Special Report\par \uc2 ABSR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS SPECIAL REPORT\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ABORTION, ROE, WADE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning. We're coming on the air with breaking news from the Supreme Court at this hour. The justices handing down the highly anticipated ruling on abortion, and the fate of the landmark Roe versus Wade decision. This is a majority opinion written by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 . Very close to what was leaked several weeks back. The justices saying we hold that Roe and Casey must be overruled. I want to bring in Terry Moran, who has covered the Supreme Court for many years. Terry, bottom line, what has the court said this morning?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Bottom line, Roe versus Wade is overruled. There is no constitutional right to an abortion in this country anymore as of today. Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  in, as you say, an opinion that tracks very, very carefully, as far as I can see at this point, with the opinion that was leaked, the draft opinion, says, without question, it is time to heed the Constitution and return the issue of abortion to the people's elected representatives. Again and again, he knocks down in this opinion, as he did in his draft opinion, the claim that there is a right to choose an abortion in the right to privacy, or the right to liberty under the Fourth Amendment.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He says that at the adoption of the 14th Amendment and the Constitution, abortion was a crime in this country, and that there was no talk about a constitutional right to an abortion until the latter part of the 20th century. He says that this court does not have to abide by the Roe versus Wade precedent because it was so wrong, because it has not settled the issue, and he says without question, it is time now to return the issue to the people, which will open the doors, obviously, to all kinds of legislation and laws that have already been passed and are now good law in those states, which passed so-called trigger laws as soon as this opinion comes down, abortion is outlawed in many states in this country.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The bottom line, once again, just as it was in Justice Alito's draft opinion, this is an opinion that from now on, there is no more constitutional right to an abortion in the United States.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, it's interesting, Terry, because initially in this opinion that had been leaked, Justice Alito had written previously that "we cannot allow our decisions to be affected by any extraneous influences such as concern about the public's reaction to our work," talking about any reaction actually to that leak or what could happen if this ruling came down, and it would seem in delivering the same opinion today, that that's exactly what they've done here. They've ignored any potential public reaction to this.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right, David. There was some question when the draft opinion was leaked. Boy, this sounds very absolute and extreme. His tone, that Roe is egregiously wrong, that any constitutional right must be located in our country's history and traditions. People were concerned about how that might impact gay marriage and other rights that are not specified in the Constitution. It looks to me like he doesn't pull back from any of that. Now there is a concurrence by Chief Justice Roberts. He concurs in the judgment. So he doesn't sign onto all that. But it looks like five justices do.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So this is not only overturning Roe, but overturning the entire approach to constitutional law, that just because something might not have been a constitutional right when the Constitution was written in 1787, perhaps the generalities in the Constitution can be read to guarantee these rights, to people marrying people of the same sex. To, constitutional right to choose abortion. Contraception isn't mentioned in the Constitution either. It does seem that this opinion opens the door not just to overruling Roe versus Wade, but to other rights that many Americans have taken for granted that are based on the same reasoning, that the Constitution can evolve.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Terry Moran, stay with us here. You're watching ABC News live coverage this morning of the Supreme Court's historic ruling this morning overruling Roe versus Wade in this country. It will have a profound impact on abortion rights in America, in this country, in fact, as you heard Terry mention moments ago, the impact will be felt almost immediately in 13 states. They have what are called trigger laws which means it would take any abortion rights off the books as they currently stand. 26 states in totality could actually change their laws almost immediately. There you can see the map on the screen there. Those are the states expected to ban or severely restrict abortion rights almost immediately. I want to bring in Rachel Scott, our congressional correspondent. She's live outside the Supreme Court where people are already demonstrating this morning. Rachel, what's the reaction down there?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, this is a seismic shift. People from all over the country gathering here today and take a look at the reaction. We have seen tears of sadness, tears of happiness, from people on both sides of the issue today, and take a look. I want to bring you in to the fencing. That has been up here around the Supreme Court since that leaked draft came out. This fence wraps around the entire Supreme Court complex, because they have been preparing for this moment. David, I have been covering this issue for over a year. I have been traveling to many states, speaking with Republicans that have been trying to restrict abortion, talking to women who have had travel hundreds of miles to get an abortion. This is the new reality for women in this country, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott, obviously reaction coming in already, and reacting on both sides of this issue in this country. We'll go back to Rachel when we can hear her a little more clearly. Obviously being drowned out by the immediate reaction at the court. I want to bring in Kate Shaw, our Supreme Court contributor, legal scholar. And Kate, what do you make of the decision? What stands out to you?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, this is one of the most important decisions the Supreme Court has handed down in the century, you know, certainly in the past half century, but it will stand with, among the most important decisions of the Supreme Court. Look, for nearly half a century, Americans, women, but all Americans have structured their lives and made their choices with the sort of understanding that if contraception should fail, they have abortion, a constitutionally protected right to terminate a pregnancy, essentially as a backstop. Justice Elena Kagan, I'm sorry. Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , who wrote the dissent for himself, plus Justice Elena Kagan and Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , basically writes that the state can control a woman's body and a woman's destiny by taking from her the choice to continue with or to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I think this will have seismic impact on women's lives and on the choices they can make and really on the course of the country. So it's an enormously consequential decision, and Justice Alito says that it doesn't call into question any of the court's other precedents on things like contraception or same sex marriage, but the dissent, I think, rightly points out that the logic of the majority opinion very much calls those cases into question, whatever the majority says in insisting that it does not call those rights into question.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Kate, let me ask you, because you point out in the dissenting opinion there, states can control a woman's body and destiny. Bottom line in those 26 states we were just looking at, obviously 13 of them have the trigger laws. Bottom line here, what does the future look like for women in those states if they were going to get an abortion?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, as of today, you know, in many states, these trigger laws will go immediately into effect, and in other states, there are laws that have been struck down by courts because they were inconsistent with Roe, and those decisions will no longer be enforceable, and so those laws will come back into effect in many instances, and in several other states, it's clear, because political leaders have said they're going to again pass laws to criminalize or prohibit abortion in all or nearly all instances. And so, you know, those women will either seek to travel to other states if they want to terminate a pregnancy, or continue with unwanted pregnancy, and some states have also indicated they're going to move to make it difficult to leave the state to obtain abortion care elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So in no way does this decision end the roiling national debate about abortion. In some ways I think it's going to kick it into higher gear as we see states begin to respond.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Kate Shaw describing a time we haven't seen in this country in 50 years, a situation where women in this country will have to travel across state lines if they're looking to have an abortion, if they're in one of these states that will have a trigger law that goes into effect immediately. In fact, there are 13 of those. 26 in total that plan to take action almost immediately. So in 26 states in this country, if a woman seeks to get an abortion, they will most likely have to travel outside that state to find the opportunity to make that decision on her own.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to bring in Devin Dwyer who covers the Supreme Court for us, and Devin, this country knows very well these Supreme Court confirmation hearings, and every time there is a justice who comes before the American people and the Senate, of course, to answer questions, they're always asked about this issue. They're very careful with their answers, but almost always we hear about precedent in this country and respect for precedent, and this law was on the books for 50 years. So what are Americans to make of the answers they heard from some of these justices before?\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) There's been a lot of attention, David, on those statements in at least the confirmation hearings of the three most recent justices appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Remember with the expressed purpose goal of overturning Roe versus Wade, which they've now accomplished, and during those confirmation hearings, they were asked, will you stand by Roe versus Wade as established precedent, stare decisis is the doctrine of the Supreme Courts, let decisions stand. And today we saw in a concurrence from Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 . He said that stare decisis is not absolute. The conservatives today, David, making clear that they have a more expansive, flexible view of stare decisis, and Justice Alito saying that in cases where decisions are egregiously wrong, the court must act.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They did that in Plessy versus Ferguson, with that separate but equal doctrine way back, and Brown versus Board, overturning segregation in schools in the '50s. Today, this conservative majority making clear they're doing just that. Correcting a wrong, in their view, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Devin Dwyer who is with us here this morning, covers the court for us. Devin, stay with us here. We'll certainly come back with you before the morning is done, our coverage here this morning. Stephanie Ramos is in Mississippi. She's at the clinic that was at the center of this case, testing the future of Roe versus Wade in this country, and Stephanie, what's the scene there?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, David, as you can hear, there are some antiabortion rights activists on a loud speaker behind me, but you're exactly right. This is the clinic at the heart of the cases. The only abortion clinic left in the state of Mississippi. It is run by the Jackson Women's Health Organization. It's also known as the Pink House. As you can see, it's a pink building here situated on the corner. Also on the corner, antiabortion rights activists who are here to persuade women not to get an abortion. Now this is where the rubber meets the road, David. Families have depended on this clinic. Women have relied on this clinic year after year. A recent estimate showed more than 2,500 women received an abortion here in a year in the state of Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, if this clinic closes, it could have severe impact on the lowest income communities. Keep in mind here, Mississippi is one of the poorest states in the nation. Now that Roe has fallen, as you mentioned, some 26 states could ban or severely restrict abortions. That's including Mississippi, and we know that women have traveled here from other states like Texas and Oklahoma. They have already set some of these bans in place, but now those women and women here in Mississippi may not have an option to visit this clinic, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Stephanie Ramos in Mississippi, at the clinic at the center of this case. I want to bring in Carrie Severino. She is the president of Judicial Crisis Network, a conservative organization. And Carrie, I know this is considered a major victory for you and the people that you work with, who have been working for 50 years to get Roe overturned.\par \par \uc2 CARRIE SEVERINO (PRESIDENT OF THE JUDICIAL CRISIS NETWORK)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely, but most importantly, because this is a return to what the Constitution simply says, and I think that's what the majority opinion is very clear about is the Constitution nowhere provides the right to abortion. That's something that some states may choose to do, but that's not something that's in the federal Constitution and the Supreme Court has to limit itself to deciding - cases based on the Constitution itself. So this will put that issue back in the hands of our elected representatives. That's where it was for most of American history, and I think that's where the Constitution leaves it, and so that's the correct result today.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) I wanted to ask you, Carrie, about the most recent polling. I know that you're aware of it, from the Pugh Research Center, also from Quinnipiac, just in the last couple of days, that 61% of Americans say abortion should be legal in all or most cases. What do you make of those numbers and the fact that this ruling would appear to go against what the majority of Americans are hoping for or asking for?\par \par \uc2 CARRIE SEVERINO (PRESIDENT OF THE JUDICIAL CRISIS NETWORK)\par \par \uc2 It doesn't in any way contradict that. I mean, what this is saying is yes, if you would like to make abortion legal in all or most circumstances, now you have the ability to do that at the legislative level. That's something that Americans, again, always until 1973, had the ability to legislate on this issue. It's simply not in the Constitution. So the Supreme Court's job is not to look at opinion polls and say, what do people want? Let's come up with laws that reflect what people want. No, their job is to simply interpret the Constitution and interpret the laws that are actually passed by Congress and passed by our elected representatives. So if most Americans want to see that, that's what we will see, be we will see it in a legislative way rather than judges usurping that legislative power.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Carrie Severino, president of Judicial Crisis Network, a conservative organization that has worked to see this day come true. She says this decision is rooted in the Constitution, that this should be at the state level where this decision is made, and that's what she believes the court has decided here today. Carrie, thank you so much for joining us. I want to bring Terry Moran back in, because Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  did weigh in on this decision. You have had more time to pore through this. What have you found, Terry?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, David, this case has five conservative justices voting to overrule Roe versus Wade. It has three liberal justices opposing that, and it has \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , in the middle. He says that he would have validated the Mississippi law at issue here. Mississippi passed a law banning almost all abortions at 15 weeks. He says that's all you have to decide. That you don't have to decide that a woman no longer has a constitutional right to an abortion, and he says, we should adhere closely to the principles of judicial restraint here instead of, quote, "repudiating a constitutional right we have not only previously recognized, but also expressly reaffirmed."\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He says that the court is too full of certainty on this issue and he would wait for the next case to come. He's lost control of this court, but it is significant that a justice as conservative as \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  is, looks at what his conservative colleagues did, and says, that's judicial activism.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Very interesting, Terry, that the Chief Justice was appearing to write that there could have been some sort of meeting in the middle here, some sort of a compromise. As Terry points out, five conservative justices, majority opinion. Three liberals and then there was the Chief Justice, \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , who said, we could validate what Mississippi is asking for, and that's all we have to do. And Terry, one more question before we move on and ask about the White House reaction, what the President might do. We have Dr. Jen Ashton standing by as well, but Terry, one more question for you because Justice Breyer, in his dissent, talks about the court, how the court will be viewed in this country.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I bring this up because you just talked about the Chief Justice losing control of the court, in so many words, what you just said. Justice Breyer writes that "one of us once said that it is not often in the law that so few have so quickly changed so much. For all of us in our time on this court, that has never been more true than today. In overruling Roe and Casey, this court betrays its guiding principles. With sorrow for this court, but more for many millions of American women who have today lost a fundamental constitutional protection, we dissent." He talks about the court betraying its guiding principles, that there has been such swift change on the court.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that, David, is what justices are always concerned about. Because the power of the Supreme Court comes from the confidence and trust of the people. That is why people respect its opinions. And if its decisions shift because one political party gets to appoint some justices, that erodes confidence. This is, this is bedrock belief of justices throughout our history that they should abide by the law and the cannons of judicial conduct in a way that doesn't seem to communicate to people that they're just another political institution, and what Justice Breyer is saying there explicitly, and what Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  is suggesting, is that this is an activist decision.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is a decision that did not have to be made, and that given the nature of the issue and how it has been brought to the court, will suggest that this is a court not of judges, but of politicians in judges' robes. That is the danger that Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  is alerting the country to.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Terry Moran, stay with us here. We'll continue our coverage here this morning. I want to bring in our chief White House correspondent Cecilia Vega, because Cecilia, this White House knew since the leaked opinion that this very well could happen, what action have they been planning behind the scenes, if any at all?\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and David, they have been holding a number of emergency meetings behind closed doors in the weeks since that leaked draft opinion came out, certainly preparing for what has happened here today. The White House has said all along that they would be ready when and if this time came, but they've played it very close to the vest in terms of what these options are. I will just tell you, we have not heard an official reaction from anyone, anybody in the higher levels of the administration, certainly not from the President. He doesn't have anything on his public schedule today. He's getting ready to head overseas for a foreign trip to Europe, but certainly this decision will impact and overshadow so much of this trip to come.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POSSIBLE EXECUTIVE ACTIONS BY BIDEN ADMINISTRATION\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The President has asked the Gender Policy Council for - advice on what to do. The White House Counsel has been weighing legal decisions and moves that the President could make. Lawmakers, advocacy groups, have been pitching in in these closed doors meetings. So you can see right here on the screen next to me some of the options that are on the table. This is a very broad range and frankly, we're doing a little tea leaf reading here because they haven't signaled exactly what they - will do. We're talking about potentially declaring a public health emergency. They could sue states for being at odds with \uc2 FDA\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They could allow abortion pills to be provided at pharmacies, they could ease restrictions on the imports of abortion pills into this country. And there's this one. They could lease federal buildings to abortion - providers, the thinking there is perhaps some of those - sites are out of jurisdiction of some of these states where the procedure is now banned. But look, you know, David, advocates have told us all along, and certainly even people inside the White House will tell you privately, this is not something the President can executive order, can executive action his way out of. No matter what the President decides to do, if he does decide to go the executive action route, that is not going to overturn this ruling.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The President has been lobbying, frankly the White House has been asking and hopeful that Congress would codify Roe into law. They do not have the votes. The Democrats do not have the votes. Republicans certainly have not wanted that to happen, and there's no expectation that they will after this, but all eyes, particularly from the left and liberals and progressives in this country, are on this White House starting today, starting this minute to see what they will do to help women in this situation.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Cecilia Vega, our thanks to you on the potential options. Though they are limited for sure, that the White House can take. The potential steps that the President might try to take, but again, the road is very limited because, again, we have just witnessed here on our coverage, the Supreme Court ruling to overturn Roe versus Wade, allowing states to ban abortions across this country. We have mentioned the numbers before, but if you're just joining us, that means 13 states with so-called trigger laws can move to ban abortions immediately. 26 states in all planning to take action very swiftly.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) We heard from the president of the Judicial Crisis Network, a conservative organization, Carrie Severino, who said this is what we have been working for for 50 years in this country, and she made the case that this should be back to the states. That's where the decision making on this particular issue should take place. We will of course, hear the other side of this as we continue our coverage here this morning. I want to bring in former senator Heidi Heitkamp, who is with us here this morning. Heidi, obviously you come in on the other side of this issue, an extraordinary morning, an historic morning. 50 years of precedent in this country overturned. What's your reaction?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (DEM) (FORMER SENATOR OF NORTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 You know, my first reaction is, who is this going to hurt the most? It's going to hurt low income women who seek abortion mainly for economic reasons, and they can't afford to get on a plane. Once again, the difference between the services that can be provided to rich people in this country and poor people in this country have taken a huge impact today. But more importantly, this is the first time in my history that the Supreme Court has taken a step backward on individual rights, and it is absolutely amazing to me that one president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , can basically override that will of literally millions of people in this country who believe that this procedure should be available and safe for women across the country.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (DEM) (FORMER SENATOR OF NORTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 This is no, this is now no, there's nothing about this decision that doesn't further divide our country, and by that I mean, now all of a sudden we're going to see huge divisions between those red states and (inaudible) state legislators have basically enacted trigger laws. (Inaudible) And blue states, making abortion available and safe for women, and once again, young women who grow up in a state like mine are going to say, if you can't recognize my right of privacy, I'm not going to live in your state. And so this has huge ramifications for the social fabric and the political fabric of our country.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (DEM) (FORMER SENATOR OF NORTH DAKOTA)\par \par \uc2 Probably what everybody is going to be analyzing is how this will affect the midterms. You know, for years, we always said, one issue voters have really driven division (inaudible) but now is this going to be a one issue vote that people on the other side take, and are we going to see implications in the midterms as a result of this decision?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Former senator Heidi Heitkamp, ABC News contributor, talking about the divisions between red states and blue states in this country, only deepening, she believes, because of this particular issue, and now this decision coming down from the Supreme Court, overturning Roe versus Wade this morning. 50 years of precedent in this country, sending it back to the states, and we already know that some 26 states plan to take action, if not immediately, in the very near future, to overturn abortion rights in their particular states. One state where that is not the case is obviously Connecticut, where the governor, \uc2 Ned Lamont\uc2 , in May, signed the first in the nation Reproductive Rights Legislation. Governor, first of all, your reaction to the court this morning, and what this means in particular for the women in your state and why you took action in recent weeks?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR NED LAMONT (DEM) (CONNECTICUT)\par \par \uc2 I'm shocked that 50 years of precedent is being overturned by this court. The idea that politicians can stand between a woman and her doctor and let them make the right choice, and I can guarantee you it's not going to happen here in Connecticut. We're going to respect Roe v. Wade. We've got by law, Roe V. Wade continues, despite what the federal government does, and if there are any women who feel like their rights are being abused in other states, they're welcome here in Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Governor, of course, you're only the governor of Connecticut. You don't have any say over surrounding states or for that matter, states across this country. What do you make of what you just heard from the former senator, Heidi Heitkamp, who said this is only going to deepen divisions across this country, when you have some 26 states planning to take action, and a return to the time when women who are seeking an abortion will very likely, if they can afford it, have to travel across state lines in this country to exercise that right?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR NED LAMONT (DEM) (CONNECTICUT)\par \par \uc2 I agree with her. I think you're going to find people voting with their feet. I think women and companies may decide, I want to go to a state that respects my rights, my right to choose, and Connecticut will be one of those states, and if you are somebody who's coming from Texas, where they have vigilante justice and they try to enforce it against the doctor and the woman if she goes out of state, we're not going to let it happen here in Connecticut. We're going to protect those women that come into our state, and we're going to prosecute right back.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Both sides reacting very quickly this morning. Governor \uc2 Ned Lamont\uc2 , echoing what we heard from former senator Heidi Heitkamp this morning, describing a country where women will have to go across state lines to exercise their right. Of course, we heard from Judicial Crisis Network on the other side this morning saying that they believe 50 years in this fight has finally brought victory for them from the Supreme Court in returning this to the states to make this decision, that state lawmakers at the local level should decide the future of these rights.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to bring in our chief medical correspondent, and of course, a board certified OB/GYN, Dr. Jen Ashton tracking this with us here this morning. And again, Jen, you know, I always ask these questions. What's the bottom line for women watching at home this morning? What are their options, and where does that stand in the country right now if this is a situation that you are facing or you fear you could face at some point in the future?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, first of all, David, I think not only board certified OB/GYNS, but certified nurse midwives and nurse practitioners and physicians' assistants, everyone in the medical profession who has dedicated their lives to taking care of women, are concerned, but we remain committed medically to providing safe reproductive health care to women, and I want to be clear. This is not just a women's health issue. This affects men in these women's lives. Their fathers, their sons, their partners, and reproductive health is not just women's health. It's human health.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So I think that what's important right now for women to know is there will always be options, medical options, surgical options. The key is protecting two lives when a woman is pregnant, not just the life of the woman and the mother carrying that fetus, but the fetus as well, and there are medical conditions that literally put both of those lives at stake. You know, obviously there are a plethora of other issues involved when we talk about abortion, but medically this is literally oftentimes a life or death issue, and I think that the health care professionals who were there yesterday will be there today and tomorrow to help these women.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) And if you are a woman in one of these 13 states with a trigger law, Dr. Jen, that goes into effect almost immediately banning abortion, what are the options medically?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAN B\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MEDICAL ABORTION PILLS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, first of all it's not one size fits all, David. It depends on the health of that woman, it depends on the gestational age, how far along she is in that pregnancy, which oftentimes is difficult to ascertain. Medication abortions are \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved up until 10 weeks. That's 70 days. Again, that's not always a fact that can be known for sure, and there are options to prevent unintended pregnancy. Most people confuse the two. That so-called Plan B which people may have heard of as the morning after pill. That does not cause a termination or an abortion. That is taken after intercourse to prevent an unintended or undesired pregnancy. It's not 100% effective, but usually it's in the high 90s. The earlier it's taken, the better.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Then when you talk about options for medication abortions. This will terminate a pregnancy and cause the evacuation of any contents of the uterus. These are given usually orally with two medications that are \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved and again, up until about 10 weeks. So time is not always on the side of the woman from a medical standpoint. Oftentimes in cases of a fetal anomaly, that's not diagnosed until 21 or 22 weeks. So that's where you get that 1% of abortions occurring after 21 weeks. Oftentimes because it's not diagnosed before then. It is important to remember also, David, that over 90% of pregnancies, of abortions occur in the first trimester. So that's really when time matters.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Dr. Jen Ashton, we really appreciate your insight here this morning. Board certified OB/GYN and of course, our chief medical correspondent, reaction within the medical community that will have to react swiftly because of the impact this is having on what perhaps they might be able to or not be able to do in some of these states moving forward. Dr. Jen, our thanks to you. We're getting reaction on both sides of the political aisle. Democrats and Republicans. Former President Obama issuing a statement just moments ago, saying, "today the Supreme Court not only reversed nearly 50 years of precedent, it relegated the most intensely personal decision someone can make to the whims of politicians and ideologues attacking the essential freedoms of millions of Americans."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TWEET FROM BARACK OBAMA\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) That tweet sent out by former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  just moments ago. On the other side, there was a statement this morning from former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  who said, "today, life won. By overturning Roe versus Wade, the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  has given the American people a new beginning for life, and I commend the justices in the majority for having the courage of their convictions." I want to bring in our chief Washington correspondent \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2 . I loathe to talk about the politics of it all because we're talking about women's lives here, and as Jen pointed out, it's not just the woman's life, it's the ripple effect on the entire family, the unit surrounding that woman as well.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But the politics are major in this case, and given these two statements, in particular from \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  moments ago, this is something former President Trump and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  campaigned on going into that election, and then when given the chance, they did appoint. Former President Trump did appoint justices who he believed if the opportunity occurred, would overturn Roe versus Wade, and that's what we've seen here this morning.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it's a simple fact, David, that without those three Trump nominated justices, this decision would not have come down this way. What's significant in the statement by \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , because I think you'll see a lot more of this, is what he says happens next. He says, "it is incumbent on all who cherish the sanctity of life to resolved that we will take the defense of the unborn and the support of women in crisis pregnancy centers to every state in America." And then he says this, "we must not rest and must not relent until the sanctity of life is restored to the center of American law in every state on the land."\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Here's the truth about Roe v. Wade. For all of these years, Roe v. Wade has kept this issue, it's always been, you know, abortion has been a wedge issue. It's been a divisive issue, but it has been relegated to - not the center of American politics because there was a sense that no matter what politicians did, there was a constitutional right to an abortion. Now that's gone. So now you see this in every race. At the state level, at the federal level. The question will be asked, you know, where do you stand on abortion rights, and now you see, you know, one of the leading Republican figures, the former Republican Vice President, being the first to come out and say, there should be a movement across the entirety of the United States to ban abortion.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's not a position that every Republican holds by any measure, but now this issue becomes much more front and center in our politics, and will be right up there as one of the top one, two, or three issues in the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) And \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2  as you say, it's a fact. The three justices appointed by former President Trump, that decision making, allowed then this to happen, and I think in recent times, people will look back to the 2016 election no matter what side of this issue you're on, and recognizing that that was a significant turning point for the future of Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No question. No question at all, and, you know, - if you look at the polling going into the 2016 election, abortion was not one of the top issues. Abortion is an issue that people long have had strong views about, but wasn't necessarily an issue that people would vote on, certainly not a majority of people on either side of the issue. Now there's an all-out political fight not just about supporting, you know, justices who may or may not rule one way on a decision, but about whether or not abortion remains legal.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2 , our thanks to you this morning. Stay with us here. Our coverage continues, and you heard Terry Moran talking earlier about the Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  on the Supreme Court, and we have been able to take more time to read this decision today, in what the Chief Justice actually wrote, attached to this decision. Obviously this was written by \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , five of the conservative justices signing on with Alito, but then Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  wrote this separately from that. He said, "none of this, however, requires that we also take the dramatic step of altogether eliminating the abortion right first recognized in Roe. Mississippi itself previously argued as much to this court in this litigation, when the state petitioned for our review."\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) "Its basic request was straight forward, clarify whether abortion prohibitions before viability are always unconstitutional." So again, the bottom line in what I've just read to you is that the Chief Justice said, we are not required to take the dramatic step all together of eliminating the abortion right first recognized in Roe, which of - course, is what the conservative justices have gone ahead and done here this morning. An historic morning in this country, no matter what side of the issue you stand on. Roe versus Wade overturned, handing legislation back to the states, the decision making back to the states. We know this will almost immediately have impact on the women in 26 states, 13 of which have what are called trigger laws which automatically kick in and ban abortion rights in those states. Carol Tobias, the president of the National Right to Life Group with us here this morning, and Carol, your reaction here this morning?\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS (PRESIDENT OF NATIONAL RIGHT TO LIFE COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 I'm ecstatic. The Supreme Court has correctly decided that the right to kill unborn children is not in the US Constitution. This is a great day for unborn children and their mothers.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do you make of the Chief Justice, you heard me, Carol, mention just moments ago? What Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , a conservative, wrote, essentially saying, we don't need to take the, quote, "dramatic step of altogether eliminating the abortion right first recognized in Roe," suggesting that there could have been some sort of compromise? Your reaction to the Chief Justice?\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS (PRESIDENT OF NATIONAL RIGHT TO LIFE COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 I'm not sure what compromise he would have come up with that would not result in the deaths of many, many preborn babies. I think the court was correctly decided, and I'm very appreciative for the five justices who had the courage to say this court was wrong in 1973.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Carol, I know this has been a fight for many of you for 50 years since Roe versus Wade became the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS (PRESIDENT OF NATIONAL RIGHT TO LIFE COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do you make of the polling? How do you explain the polling from Quinnipiac just this week, not long ago from \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  that shows more than 60% of Americans do believe that a woman should have the right to an abortion in this country?\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS (PRESIDENT OF NATIONAL RIGHT TO LIFE COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 I think the interesting information is when you start getting down into the details. Many