 and you'll remember we covered this yesterday, that New York State could not make decisions that affect the Second Amendment, the right to bear arms, that the states can't make that decision, but then today saying the states should be the ones making the decision when it comes to a woman's choice about her reproductive future.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll get into that in a moment. I'll be asking Terry Moran and Kate Shaw if there is, in fact, a legal contradiction there. The House Speaker saying the hypocrisy is raging. But I want to get immediate reaction from what the House Speaker said about American women having less freedom today than their own mothers did. I want to bring in Dr. Janet Cathey, an OB/GYN. She's the only doctor at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  in Little Rock, there's only abortion clinic in the state of Arkansas, the Little Rock Family Planning. And Dr. Cathey, I know that doctors come in from other states actually to work at this clinic.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JANET CATHEY (OB-GYN PLANNED PARENTHOOD LITTLE ROCK)\par \par \uc2 Yes. There are other state providers at the clinic that does surgical care here at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . We have had a full schedule of patients today and tomorrow that we're contacting now to tell them that we're going to have to make other arrangements. And the closest, they're going to look at driving six to seven to eight hours one way to be able to have this care provided for them.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Dr. Cathey, you're among the first to crystallize what this means for women immediately in this moment, and again, I say this, no matter where you stand on the issue. Dr. Cathey, just underscore what you just said. Explain that for our viewers who are watching right now. I know that you work at the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  in Little Rock, Arkansas, Little Rock Family Planning, and you just said moments ago that you have now begun to call women to tell them that they're going to have to make alternate plans and for many of them, that means six, seven, eight-hour drives ahead to get to other states?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JANET CATHEY (OB-GYN PLANNED PARENTHOOD LITTLE ROCK)\par \par \uc2 Yes. Just, and that, you know, one way, and that, you know, just the expense involved and the logistics involved and I'm thinking of it on a very personal level, just what it's going to take for any one person to manage the logistics of this. It's just going to be a very complicated process that it shouldn't be.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Dr. Janet Cathey, an OB/GYN at a Planned Parenthood location in Little Rock, Arkansas. It's in fact the only abortion clinic in that state, describing how they're already calling women to say, you're going to have to find other plans. She said dealing on this at a very personal level with women this morning. It just illustrates just how quickly this has impact on real lives in our country. I want to bring in \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 . We mentioned what Alexis said. She's the president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , the Action Fund. Moments ago we read her statement on the air, and Alexis, I wanted to get your reaction to what the House Speaker just said, about women in this country have less freedom than their mothers, that this is a slap in the face and an insult to women about making their own decisions for their reproductive futures.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2  (PRESIDENT AND CEO OF PLANNED PARENTHOOD)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely. I mean, look. I have two daughters who are 13 and 10, and the idea that they will grow up with less rights than I have at the age of 49, my entire life I have had a belief that the, you know, that, in freedom and equality, and the fact that we have gone back after 50 years is absolutely devastating. It is devastating and we are furious.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Alexis, this decision was leaked several weeks back. \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  obviously authoring the decision for the majority on the Supreme Court. There was some belief that perhaps the language would be tweaked somewhat. It appears that that simply is not the case, that the majority on the court held firm on this. Did you believe that this was coming given the political dynamic in this country, that the court is now 6-3, given the recent appointments to the court, or did you think that a 50 year precedent would simply hold despite the political dynamics right now?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2  (PRESIDENT AND CEO OF PLANNED PARENTHOOD)\par \par \uc2 We have been believing that this would happen for, particularly for the last few years, and certainly anyone listening to the oral arguments in December believed that this was a very distinct possibility, but there's a difference between belief and hope, and we spent the last, you know, few weeks after this leak still trying to hold onto hope beyond measure that something be different, that the outrage that the court would see would change someone's mind. The reality is this is just going to be devastating. It is going to create a health care crisis in this country where people who need access to abortion care, and so yes, I believed it would happen. I believed that the court showed its hands in that leak, and yet I still tried to have faith and hope that something could be different.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2 . Alexis who said she's 49 years old and today she - recognizes that her own daughters have fewer rights, she says, than she did when she was their age, talking about the decision that they anticipated, but the day has come, and now \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and those who support a woman's right to choose will have to take action on their own moving forward. I want to recap for those of you watching. ABC News live coverage this morning, the Supreme Court overturning Roe versus Wade, a 50 year precedent in this country, turning that decision-making process back to the states on the state level for legislation involving abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) As you know, if you have been watching here this morning, and forgive me for repeating that number, if you are just joining us, though, 26 states plan to take action immediately or very soon. 13 of which have these so-called trigger laws in effect that would trigger a change immediately when it comes to a woman's right to make this decision on her own. The former first lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  issuing a statement moments ago. "I am heartbroken today. I am heartbroken for people around this country who just lost the fundamental right to make informed decisions about their own bodies." Again, reaction from both sides of the aisle, from former President Obama, the former first lady, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  moments ago, and then on the other side, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  who, of course, ran on this along with former President Trump, who said if elected, that they would appoint justices who would overturn Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) You can never predict how many justices a certain president will have an opportunity to appoint to the court. In this case, it was three, and it changed the makeup of the court significantly. And we are here today. I want to bring in Rachel Scott who we showed you some time ago down in front of the court, and Rachel, we have seen in these live pictures that the crowd has only grown, and I presume there are people there on both sides of this issue.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Both sides of the issue, David, and the crowd is truly growing by the minute. This is sending a seismic jolt throughout the country, and we are seeing that play out here right in front of the Supreme Court because the moment that his decision came out, it was read out here on this loud speaker, and you have this polarized divide represented, where on one side you had cheers and applause, tears of joy, on the other, you had immense frustration. I want to give you a look at what we are seeing behind us right now. On one side here, you have people saying Roe was - illegitimate. You say this decision is wrong, on the other side, you have people celebrating this. We have also seen the police presence start to grow as well.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You can see the lines of officers surrounding this fencing right out here outside of the Supreme Court. This fencing up around the Supreme Court has been up since that draft was leaked. It started and created this immense response out here. We've seen people gather here outside of the Supreme Court every single decision day, waiting for this to happen. Advocates say that although this is a seismic shift, that the ground has been shaking for so long. Because, David, as you have mentioned, 13 states have these so-called trigger bans where abortion would immediately be severely restricted or banned altogether, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) (inaudible) Rachel Scott live at the Supreme Court here this morning. Rachel, I really appreciate you reporting in and reflecting the scene there on the ground, the crowd growing, with Rachel standing by down there. And we'll go back to her should we need to momentarily. Again, the reaction coming in on both sides of this issue. Many believe this is a victory 50 years in the making in this country. We heard from Carol Tobias, the president of the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2  who told me she's ecstatic, that this was some time in the making. We asked her about the polling in this country that shows the majority of Americans believe that this is a woman's right. She said that should be determined at the state level.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that's what many on that side of the argument continue to say here this morning. What the Supreme Court did was to give this back to the states, which is what they have been fighting for all along. On the flip side, we're hearing from former Secretary of State Hillary Clinton moments ago, saying, "most Americans believe the decision to have a child is one of the most sacred decisions there is, and that such decisions should remain between patients and their doctors." Mrs. Clinton says, "today's Supreme Court opinion will live in infamy as a step backward for women's rights and for human rights here in the US." Which takes me back to Cecilia Vega, our chief White House correspondent.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) You covered Hillary Clinton's campaign going into 2016. This obviously was a major issue on both sides of that campaign, and when we think about recent history in this country, it just shows you an election can then determine obviously who gets appointed to the court. It can have swift and major impacts on the direction one way or another in this country.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think, David, what we are looking at right now on that screen of what is happening outside of the Supreme Court is the legacy of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This very much is about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did, and it's not just on the Supreme Court in terms of who he appointed. He has appointed a record number of federal judges on federal courts around the country as well, and look. I want to point out we're over an hour since this decision has been made and we have still yet to hear from President Biden or anyone at the top levels of his administration. We're waiting to find out if, in fact, he will address the nation on this country, certainly progressives around the country, not just in Washington, are now looking to him and to his administration as the face for what happens next.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He will be the one to push this fight for abortion rights as we head into the midterms in a few months. The Democrats when you talk to them in the lead up to this, having read this leaked draft opinion, had very much said that the issue of abortion would be a galvanizing one for the midterms. Certainly that is expected to happen now, and we talk about the politics of this, but we have to remember the polling on abortion has been consistent for decades in our country. By a majority of voters, a 2-1, more than a 2-1 margin believe that the court should have upheld the Roe versus Wade decision.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So certainly Democrats will use this going forward, President Biden had said and has been saying in the lead up to this decision that this is now going to be about voters at the ballot box. But what is happening at this turn of events, David, this is not just about federal elections. Certainly they matter. What we're seeing here shows the legacy of former President Trump. This is now very much going to be about state elections, and state - legislators, governors, particularly in red states, and these states with trigger laws that are now carrying this out at a state level. This is a decision that has an immediate trickle-down effect, and David, on the politics of this, I want to point out one more thing.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The White House and administration officials have been sounding the alarm about what this decision means from a broader perspective, that today's decision is about abortion. They very much feel that this could then be interpreted to apply to other civil rights issues. Perhaps same-sex marriages. Will this be interpreted as something that could impact those, and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  in his concurring opinion, I'll leave it to Terry and others to weigh in on this, says that this means the Supreme Court could reconsider substantive due process cases including same-sex marriage. They have been sounding the alarm on that, and not just that, but things like IVF, in vitro fertilization, and if states are interpreting this as life begins at conception, how many other things in our life will this decision impact today?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Cecilia Vega, who has been reporting for some weeks now on the President's position on this, the Biden administration saying that this decision if, in fact, it is the decision, and we learned today that it is, and it did not change from several weeks back when it was leaked, the Biden administration saying this decision does not happen in a vacuum, that it very well could affect many issues in this country beyond simply a woman's right to make her own decision on her reproductive future. I want to bring in Jeanne Mancini, the president for the March For Life. And Jeanne, we're looking at live pictures at the Supreme Court, obviously the crowd growing, and as Rachel Scott just reported, and you don't need me to tell you, the crowd growing on both sides of this issue. I just am curious, your reaction here this morning and what you make of the court's decision?\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI (PRESIDENT OF MARCH FOR LIFE)\par \par \uc2 Right. Well, I'm no stranger to growing crowds. So of course the March for Life just by way of explanation, we are the largest annual human rights demonstration. We started the year after Roe v. Wade, and we literally have hundreds of thousands of people cross the Supreme Court every anniversary of Roe v. Wade in January, and we have had collectively millions of Americans March For Life over the years. And so I was just thinking so much about all of the marchers over these years as today Roe has been overturned, and the question of abortion goes back to the legislative branches primarily in the states, also at the national level.\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI (PRESIDENT OF MARCH FOR LIFE)\par \par \uc2 I think it's really important not to fear monger about what's happening. I mean, some of the news reports are, I think that they can evoke a lot of things that are not reality here. So this is about specifically allowing states to be able to decide when the limits to abortion can happen. So the case that went before this (inaudible) to lead to this was about allowing a 15 week ban on abortion, and under Roe, our country could not have that 15 week ban on abortion. Let's be clear that the United States is one of only seven countries around the world that allows late term abortion, and so this puts us back in line with most mainstream countries in Europe, for example. So now states will have the right to enact limitations, will be able to protect life and I for one am very grateful.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Jeanne Mancini with the March for Life. Jeanne, I'm curious what you would say, we had a doctor on earlier who worked at the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , the only clinic in Arkansas, who this very morning is on the phone with women, saying, we're going to have to find alternate routes for you, which she said could mean six, seven, eight hour drives to other states. What would you say to those women and about those women?\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI (PRESIDENT OF MARCH FOR LIFE)\par \par \uc2 What I would say is that you've got this. That you've got so many opportunities in front of you, and that that is a growing life, an actual human being. The poorest of the poor, the most vulnerable, the unborn that is within you, and we would love to give you all sorts of resources to choose adoption, to be able to support you to make the choice for life, but there's also chemical abortion in this country. There are also other states, so what we're not talking about here is abortion being made illegal in the United States. What we are talking about is states being able to reflect what the people want through their votes. So, you know, that woman has all sorts of different options in front of her, and I would offer that adoption is a beautiful and noble and viable option as well.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Jeanne Mancini, the president of March for Life, pointing out that the marches began one year after Roe versus Wade some 50 years ago, embracing what they consider a major victory this morning from the Supreme Court. Our thanks to Jeanne for weighing in here, with us here this morning. I want to bring back in Terry Moran who has covered the Supreme Court for many years for ABC News and Terry, we heard from the House Speaker just a short time ago talking about what many are going to feel is a bit of a contradiction, a bit of whiplash between yesterday's ruling on guns and then today's ruling on abortion.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know there are many differences in both of these cases, but at the, at sort of the 30,000 feet here, the gun decision said the states couldn't make decisions on guns that would affect the Second Amendment. Today's decision says the states should make the decision on a woman and her reproductive future. Help people at home who are going to hear this, and some who will believe, yes, that does sound like a contradiction, like there is hypocrisy at work here.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, David, conservatives would say the answer is simple. Guns, arms are in the Second Amendment to the Constitution. Abortion is not. But that begs the question really. There are many rights we take for granted that are not in the Constitution, the right to travel across state lines, the right of bodily integrity. You can't be forced to take medicine or undergo medical procedures. That's a right not found in the Constitution. The right to choose contraceptives, the right to marry someone of the same sex. All of these rights are not enumerated in the Constitution, neither was abortion. And so that begs the question, as I say.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But we are in a new era in the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 , and therefore in our country. The Supreme Court is now in the hands of not only very deeply conservative justices, but activist judges or justices. What you saw yesterday and today are justices who reach out to decide on the sweeping grounds, the most sweeping grounds, the biggest issues and award victory in these very, very serious and intense national debates to one side. That is what's happening, and I just want to let you know what Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  said about it in dissent. He said that Roe versus Wade was upheld in 1991 by the votes of Justices \uc2 Sandra Day O'Connor\uc2 , \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2 , and David Souter. He said those were justices, judges of wisdom, he says.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They would not have won any contests for the kind of ideological purity that some courts watchers want the justices to deliver. But if there were awards for justices who left this court better than they found it, and who for that reason left this country better, and the rule of law stronger, sign them up. We are in a new era where the reaching for the center to keep the court's legitimacy in the eyes of the public, to keep the debate going, is over, and we have a very, very strongly activist, conservative court.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Terry Moran talking about a new era. Terry, thank you for that insight. A new era for the Supreme Court and this country. One in which Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  seemed to allude to as well in what he wrote. We had mentioned this earlier. He said that we do not need to take the dramatic step of all together eliminating the abortion right first recognized in Roe, but the Chief Justice not getting his way on that point. Five justices, conservative justices, joining the majority opinion written by \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , and very, very similar to the opinion that was leaked some weeks ago. A final thought from Kate Shaw, our legal scholar and our Supreme Court contributor. Kate, just for folks watching and digesting it here this morning, bottom line on the level of impact on lives across this country, of course, in particular on women's lives in this country, what do you make of the ruling?\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Enormous and immediate impact, David. And I think that it will affect people's access in very tangible ways to a medical procedure that they have become accustomed to having the right to access now for nearly half a century. In many, many states, over half of the states, and as the dissent points out, some states have passed bans that don't have exceptions for rape or incest, and in those states, girls or women could be forced to carry to term, their rapist's children, and as the polling tells us, those are incredibly unpopular laws, and yet several states have moved to, or actually passed those laws. And so incredibly dramatic impact. And I also think impact for other rights. What the court says is in trying to figure out what liberty means or what equality means, we have to look to history.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the dissent says, well, if we're looking at history, most of history didn't acknowledge that women had full, equal citizenship, and so history is always going to cash out in ways that aren't advantageous to women and to their full liberty and equality. And so I don't think this decision stops in any way with access to abortion, and I do think that things like contraception and also, you know, same-sex marriage are very much, you know, up for grabs based on the method that the majority opinion uses. So I think this case is about much more than just abortion.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Kate Shaw on the immediate impact on women across this country, and then the questions being asked right now about issues beyond abortion in this country that could be impacted given the decision from the Supreme Court today, and how they came to that decision. The fact that it could now impact other issues in this country, deeply personal issues in this country. One last check in over at the White House. Mary Bruce standing by, waiting for word from the President, and we expect to hear from President Biden at some point today, Mary?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, we have just learned that the President will be speaking here in about an hour, at 12:30. We will have to wait until then, it seems, to get any reaction from the President. He has though, of course, in the past said that this would be a radical decision. He has warned, as you have been discussing, of the potential trickle-down effect of this decision, how it could impact other rights in this country, and he had been bracing for this decision. Weighing his potential executive options. I asked the White House yesterday how quickly the President would move on any executive action. They said he's still weighing his options. It seems he hasn't made a decision yet, and while there certainly are plenty of things he could possibly do, the President is also well aware that an executive order can't reestablish a constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that any action he takes is likely to be caught up in protracted legal battles, and that is why I suspect you will hear the President again today saying that this comes down to voters. That it's not just about elected officials at the state level, which will be incredibly important going on, but also who represents Americans here in Washington, because of course, Congress could pass legislation codifying Roe. That is something the President has called for, but that means that Americans are going to have to get out and elect more pro-abortion rights candidates, and that's something that the President has been urging for and I think you're going to hear him make that plea again today, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mary Bruce from the White House reporting that we will be hearing from the President shortly, reacting to this historic move from the Supreme Court. When the President speaks, we will bring that to you live. In the meantime, as you have been watching our coverage this morning, the Supreme Court overturning Roe versus Wade, turning that decision-making back to the states. And we know that will have immediate impact in some 26 states in this nation, 13 of which have trigger laws, they call them, which immediately change abortion rights and access in their states. Our coverage is going to continue on ABC News Live and abcnews.com. I thank our team for their careful and thoughtful analysis on all sides of this issue. And I'll be back, of course, with the President when he addresses the nation and later for or coverage on "World News Tonight." I'm David Muir in New York. Until then, good day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS SPECIAL REPORT\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 This has been a special report from ABC News.\par \par \uc2 Document ABSR000020220629ei6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PRESIDENT BIDEN STATEMENT SUPREME COURT OVERTURNS ROE V. WADE\b0\par\par\uc2 4,237 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Special Report\par \uc2 ABSR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS SPECIAL REPORT\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, SUPREME COURT, ABORTION, ROE, WADE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good afternoon. We're coming back on the air because President Biden is about to address the nation concerning the historic Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe vs. Wade. Let's listen to the President at the White House.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 ...a right so important to so many Americans, but they did it. It's a sad day for the court and for the country. 50 years ago, Roe v. Wade was decided and has been the law of the land since then. This landmark case protected a woman's right to choose, her right to make intensely personal decisions with her doctor, free from - interference of politics. It reaffirmed basic principles of equality, that women have the power to control their own destiny. And it reinforced a fundamental right of privacy, a right of each of us to choose how to live our lives. Now with Roe gone, let's be very clear, the health and life of women in this nation are now at risk.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 As chairman and ranking member of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , as vice president and now as president of the United States, I've studied this case carefully. I've overseen more Supreme Court confirmations than anyone today, where this case was always discussed. I believe Roe v. Wade was a correct decision as a matter of constitutional law and application of the fundamental right to privacy and liberty and matters of family and personal autonomy. It was a decision on a complex matter, that drew a careful balance between a woman's right to choose early in her pregnancy and a state's ability to regulate later in her pregnancy. A decision with broad national consensus that most Americans of faith and backgrounds found acceptable, that had been the law of the land for most of the lifetime of Americans today.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN (UNITED STATES) (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 And it was a constitutional principle upheld by justices appointed by Democrat and Republican presidents alike. Roe v. Wade was a 7-2 decision written by a justice appointed by a Republican president, Richard Nixon. In the five decades that followed Roe v. Wade, justices appointed by Republican presidents from Eisenhower, Nixon, and Reagan, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , were among the justices who voted to uphold the principles set forth in Roe v. Wade. It was three justices named by one president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who were the core of today's decision to upend the scales of justice and eliminate a fundamental right for women in this country. Make no mistake, this decision is a culmination of a deliberate effort over decades to upset the balance of our law.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It's a realization of an extreme ideology and a tragic error by the Supreme Court, in my view. The court has done what it's never done before, expressly take away a constitutional right that is so fundamental to so many Americans, that had already been recognized. The court's decision to do so will have real and immediate consequences. State laws banning abortion are automatically taking effect today, jeopardizing the health of millions of women, some without exceptions. So extreme that the women could be punished for protecting their health. So extreme that women and girls who are forced to bear their rapist's child. A child of consequence. - It just stuns me. So extreme that doctors will be criminalized for fulfilling their duty to care.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Imagine having, a young woman having to carry a child of incest, as a consequence of incest, and no option. Too often the case. And poor women are gonna be hit the hardest. It's cruel. In fact, the court laid out state laws criminalizing abortion that go back to the 1800s, as a rational. The court literally taking America back 150 years. This is a sad day for the country, in my view. But it doesn't mean the fight is over. Let me be very clear and unambiguous. The only way we can secure a woman's right to choose, and the balance that existed, is for Congress to restore the protections of Roe v. Wade as federal law. No executive action from the president can do that.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And if Congress, as it appears, lacks the votes to do that now, voters need to make their voices heard. This fall, you must elect more senators and representatives who will codify a woman's right to choose in the federal law once again. Elect more state leaders to protect this right at the local level. We need to restore the protections of Roe as law of the land. We need to elect officials who will do that. This fall, Roe is on the ballot. Personal freedoms are on the ballot. The right to privacy, liberty, equality. They are all on the ballot. Until then, I will do all in my power to protect a woman's right in states where they will face the consequences of today's decision. While the court's decision casts a dark shadow over a large swath of the land, many states in this country still recognize a woman's right to choose.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 So if a woman lives in a state that restricts abortion, the Supreme Court's decision does not prevent her from traveling from her home state to the state that allows it, it does not prevent a doctor in that state from treating her. As the Attorney General has made clear, women must remain free to travel safely to another state to seek the care they need. My administration will defend that bedrock right. If any state or local official, high or low, tries to interfere with a woman's exercising her basic right to travel, I will do everything in my power to fight that deeply un-American attack. My administration will also protect a woman's access to medications that are approved by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 , the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , like contraception, which is essential for preventative health care.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone, which the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved 20 years ago, to safely end early pregnancies, and is commonly used to treat miscarriages. Some states are saying that they will try to ban or severely restrict access to these medications. But extremist governors and state legislators are looking to block the mail or search a person's medicine cabinet, or control a woman's actions by tracking data on her app she uses, are wrong and extreme and out of touch, with the majority of Americans. The \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 , the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , wrote to me and Vice President Harris stressing that these laws are not based on evidence, and asking us to protect access to care. They say by limiting access to these medicines, maternal mortality will climb in America.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN (UNITED STATES) (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 That's what they say. Today, I am directing the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  to take steps to ensure these critical medications are available to the fullest extent possible, and that politicians cannot interfere in the decisions that should be made between a woman and her doctor. My administration will remain vigilant as the implications of this decision play out. I've warned about how this decision risks the broader right to privacy for everyone. That is because Roe recognized the fundamental right to privacy that has served as a basis for so many more rights that have come to take, we've come to take for granted, that are ingrained in the fabric of this country.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The right to make the best decisions for your health. The right to use birth control, a married couple in the privacy of their bedroom, for God's sake. The right to marry the person you love. Justice Thomas said as much today, he explicitly called to reconsider the right of marriage equality, the right of couples to make their choices on contraception. This extreme and dangerous path the court is now taking us on. Let me close with two points. First, I call on everyone, no matter how deeply they care about this decision, to keep all protests peaceful, peaceful, peaceful, peaceful. No intimidation. Violence is never acceptable. Threats and intimidation are not speech.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We must stand against violence in any form, regardless of your rational. Second, I know so many of us are frustrated and disillusioned that the court has taken something away that is so fundamental. I know so many women are now going to face incredibly difficult situations. I hear you. I support you. I stand with you. The consequences and the consensus of the American people, core principles of equality, liberty, dignity, and the stability of the rule of law, demand that Roe should not have been overturned. With this decision, a conservative majority of the Supreme Court shows how extreme it is, how far removed they are from the majority of this country.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 They made the United States an outlier among developed nations in the world. But this decision must not be the final word. My administration will use all of its appropriate lawful powers, but Congress must act. With your vote, you can act. You can have the final word. This is not over. Thank you very much. More to say of this in weeks to come. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 REPORTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Mr. President. Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Biden at the White House, responding to history made on this day. The Supreme Court overturning Roe versus Wade, a woman's right to choose, and turning that decision back to the states to decide whether or not a woman can choose her own in the future. The President saying this is a fundamental right to privacy, that had broad national consensus, it had been the law of the land for 50 years, upheld by justices appointed by both the Democrats and Republican presidents. He pointed to three justices appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who he said were at the center of this decision after decades of precedent. He called it a tragic error, he said this decision looked back at legal theory from the 1800s.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He said literally taking America back more than hundred years to come up with this decision. He talked about how extreme it is, in his words, that women can be punished for making a choice on their own. He pivoted to the election, the midterms approaching, he said voters need to make their voices heard this fall, not only on the national level when it comes to the Senate, and elect more Democrats. He was making the case for in the Senate, if you want to protect this choice, to elect more state leaders who will protect this right. He said make no mistake, this fall, Roe is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Personal freedom is on the ballot, and he also talked about how this could go even beyond Roe versus Wade and a woman's right to choose. I want to bring in our chief White House correspondent, Cecilia Vega. The President making it very clear this will be a defining issue of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, he did. He said that, but, you know, I've got to point out, leading up to this in the recent weeks since this dropped opinion was leaked, there were emergency meetings at the White House with advocacy groups and lawyers and White House officials, he called on the Gender Policy Council, the White House Counsel's office, they've been gathering all together in these closed door meetings, trying to come up with a plan. There was never a consensus that the White House could executive order their way out of this, but there had been a lot of discussion about plans and potential things that they could do in some of these red states where women would be affected immediately.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Those 13 states where these trigger laws are now taking effect. But you didn't really hear the President talk about any of that. And I think a lot of progressives and a lot of pro-choice people in this country are certainly looking at this White House and to particularly this president for some bold executive action. Not that any of that would restore the rights of Roe, but certainly get a little closer to offer some protections that potentially they could do, and that did not come out of the President just now. He mentioned that he really wants Congress to codify Roe versus Wade. What that means is essentially Congress would guarantee abortion access nationwide.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look. It passed in the House, but it failed in the Senate just last month. They have basically moved past that. There is no chance that Congress is going to codify Roe versus Wade any time soon, so you scrap that. Then you heard the President say that perhaps DHS could issue, make abortion pills more readily available to women around the country. Certainly I think a lot of pro-choice people would feel like that is going to help in some situations, but you know, you also had advocates say that they wanted to hear this White House perhaps go beyond that, to see if funding can be made available in an emergency way to help women get transportation to these states outside of theirs, where access is no longer allowed.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) To perhaps use federal buildings to allow abortion providers to perform services in states where this is not allowed, but you didn't hear any of that from this president today, David, and I think a lot of folks on the progressive side of his party are gonna be wondering if he, in fact, is the one who is going to be able to lead this fight on abortion rights heading into the midterms in November.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Cecilia Vega, following this reaction from the President to the Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe versus Wade. Cecilia, thank you. You heard President Biden there speak of three justices appointed by the former president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he said who were at the center of this decision-making, who helped drive this in many so words, he said. We do have reaction former President Trump, not to President Biden, but to the ruling, former President Trump saying a short time before we came back on the air here, that when asked whether he feels he played a role in the reversal of Roe versus Wade, after having appointed three conservative justices to the high court, the former president, Trump, told Fox News, "God made the decision." So when asked if he played a role in this reversal, he said, "God made the decision."\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to bring in Rachel Scott. She's in front of the Supreme Court where she has been since this decision was handed down several hours ago. What is the status of the crowds there? We can see that they are still quite significant.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They are, David. DC is on high alert, as the crowds out here continue to grow. Just take a look behind me. We are seeing the response of this decision, minute by minute. We are seeing the crowds out in front of the Supreme Court here grow. And when the decision initially came out, we did see people on both sides of this issue gather out here outside of the Supreme Court, but within the last 30 minutes, we have really seen this start to change. Outrage is spilling over here. These people behind me say this feels like a gut punch to them. They have been out here for weeks.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I met people as far away as Florida, Ohio, who told me after that leaked draft opinion came out, they traveled here to Washington, DC, they have been protesting here every single day, trying to send a message to the justices on the bench. Now they say, where they are standing right now is extremely intentional, because not only are they in front of the Supreme Court, they are also in front of the United States Capitol. They say their message now turns to lawmakers. They want them to codify Roe versus Wade. But as Cecilia mentioned, there is absolutely no appetite for that among Republicans over on the Capitol, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Interesting you bring that point up after making it a federal law, Roe versus Wade. Rachel, stick with us for the follow-up here. Because you have covered this on the Hill repeatedly. You've questioned pro-choice Republican senators, including \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 . She is just out with a statement. And I want to get your reaction, Rachel. Susan Collins Republican from Maine, pro-choice, has said that "this decision is inconsistent with what Justices Kavanaugh and Gorsuch said in their testimony and in their meetings with me, where they both were insistent on the importance of supporting long standing precedence that the country has relied on." Seeming to imply that they indicated to her that Roe versus Wade was precedent and that it was important to support longstanding precedent in this country. Now we have seen that they have not. And she knows there is significant heat coming her way now.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Make no mistake about it. I am told that she is...\par \par \uc2 TECHNICAL\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott's live reporting freezing there, that is the way television goes on these live events. We'll go back to her as soon as we can. I want to being in Mary Bruce, live outside the White House. Mary, we can pick up that thought with you, actually. \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , you heart that statement she's just issued, one of the pro-choice Republicans who will feel heat, because she said publicly after having met with some of former president Trump's choices for the court, saying she was confident that they would uphold Roe versus Wade. They have not done so. She now says they were inconsistent. What their decision is, is inconsistent with what they had indicated to her.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She took them at their word that they were going to uphold this right and clearly that is not the case. And I think she will be getting a lot of political heat for this and she knows that. I think, you know, she was not pleased, obviously, when the draft opinion came out, and knows the amount of heat that she is going to face going forward. And David, I have to tell you, I was just in the room with the President. I cannot remember the last time I saw him this angry, frustrated and quite frankly, sad, that gut punch that Rachel was talking about, you can tell the President feels that as well in the room where many of his female staffers hugging each other, consoling each other, well aware of the fight ahead here.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, you heard the President outline some of the steps that he wants to take, but he is well aware of his limitations here. That anything he does won't have a huge sweeping impact. It certainly may have an impact, but it also will likely be caught up in a lot of litigation, and that is why the focus is so squarely on these elections. The President saying Roe is on the ballot. Urging Americans to make their vote count. Because if anything, this decision shows that votes do matter. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's drill down, Mary, the bottom line here then, as he turns and pivots to the midterms, saying Roe is on the ballot. The Democrats would need 60 votes in the Senate and they would need to hold on to their majority in the House in order to ever be able to make Roe versus Wade federal law.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And David, we know that that is a tall order. You know, Democrats are very concerned about how the midterms are shaping up for them and it is going to take a lot to get enough Democrats in office - to codify Roe into law which is what the President is calling for. Right now that will be the best chance to have any real lasting permanent impact on this. And the President was really blunt, right, in saying that he, never before has the court in his view fundamentally stripped away what he says is a constitutional right, and you heard us there at the end shouting, asking, is he still confident in the court? David, he wouldn't say.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) May Bruce, live at the White House, Mary, our thanks to you as always. I want to bring in Terry Moran, who has covered the Supreme Court for years for us here on ABC. Terry, we were on the air earlier, talking about these 13 states with trigger laws that go into effect almost immediately. We know Missouri, their ban has already gone into effect. We know in Alabama, the governor there says they will work to get that ban into effect immediately. And these 13 states, among 26 states, more than half the country, expected to move and move quickly on this, this historic decision from the court.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely, David. As of today, women in America occupy a different station in our society. In much of the country today, as you just outlined, and more to come, women will have a completely different relationship to the choices they make of their own bodies when they are pregnant. In other parts of the country, they will continue to have those choices. That is why the battleground is not necessarily in the Congress, although the President will try to lead the Democrats to a midterm victory, which right now seems far away. And if anything, if the Republicans take over, there will be Republican members of Congress who introduce bills to ban abortion by federal law nationwide. But the real battleground is going to be in the states. And there, as in the Republican party at large, there is a kind of ratchet toward more extreme laws, for a Republican to oppose a law, for example, that would outlaw abortion entirely from the beginning and criminalize women who choose it.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Those laws will be introduced. There's no question about it. And it will be difficult for many Republicans to vote against them.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) To vote against them. And so we'll be watching that at the state level. In the meantime, one last question for you, Terry, before we go here, because the bigger issue also coming out of this, and you heard the President mention this, the President says the court has now shown how extreme it is, and that it's far removed from the majority of the American people. And Terry, you well know the polling, just as recently as a couple of days ago from Quinnipiac, not long before that, from Pew, showing more than 60% of Americans believe that this is, should be a woman's choice, and the ABC News polling, which was even higher than that, saying this is a private choice between a woman and her doctor. The court not reflecting what we're seeing in the polling.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, the justices who found and overruled Roe, found this ruling and overruled Roe would say, well, you can exercise those opinions in your state legislatures and localities. What this court is demonstrating however is that when it comes to what are settled law, was thought to be settled law, as even Justice Kavanaugh and Gorsuch talked about Roe versus Wade, they are activist judges, these are judges who will reach out and reject decades of precedent, and of the reasoning involved in order to achieve what they think are the more pure, constitutional doctrines. Their reliance on the traditional conservative value of judicial restraint is very minimal, we are in a new era with a new activist court.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Terry Moran, Rachel Scott, Mary Bruce, and Cecilia Vega, my thanks to all of you. The President reacting to the Supreme Court overturning Roe versus Wade. Our coverage continues on ABC News Live, abcnews.com, I'll be back with the entire team a little later today for "World News Tonight." I'm David Muir in New York. Good day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS SPECIAL REPORT\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 This has been a special report from ABC News.\par \par \uc2 Document ABSR000020220628ei6o00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE FALL OF ROE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,272 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE FALL OF ROE\par \par \uc2 ROE, OVERTURNED, PROTESTS, MADI, HAWKINS, CATHEY, SIMPSON\par \par \uc2 ROE, ABORTION, PRAGER, MCCORVEY, GOODWIN, WALTON\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now, a look back at the woman behind the groundbreaking law that protected a woman's right to an abortion, and how it inspired the movement that led to today's historic decision.\par \par \uc2 NEWSCASTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court, today, ruled that abortion is completely a private matter to be decided by mother and doctor in the first three months of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Roe versus Wade was decided in January 22nd, 1973.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It was a 7-2 opinion. Five of those seven justices were Republican appointed.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JUSTICE BURGER (SUPREME COURT)\par \par \uc2 We'll hear arguments in number 18, Roe against Wade.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This was a case about a Texas woman, Jane Roe, a pseudonym for her real name, taking on Dallas County DA Henry Wade, to challenge Texas' abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 JOSH PRAGER (AUTHOR "THE FAMILY ROE")\par \par \uc2 Jane Roe was a woman named Norma McCorvey, and for years, she was totally anonymous.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When Norma McCorvey became pregnant, she'd actually already twice given birth, and both times given her babies up for adoption. And so she was vehement that this time she did not want to carry her pregnancy to term.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE GOODWIN (CHANCELLOR'S PROFESSOR \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA IRVINE\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 At the time, being pregnant and in a condition where you didn't have higher education and where you were economically fraught situation, it meant a life of continued poverty.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She ends up getting connected to these two young attorneys, Linda Coffey and Sarah Weddington.\par \par \uc2 JOSH PRAGER (AUTHOR "THE FAMILY ROE")\par \par \uc2 Eventually, they communicated to Norma that she was not herself going to be able to have an abortion, but she could help other women to do so if she became their plaintiff, if she decided to proceed.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JUSTICE BURGER (SUPREME COURT)\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Weddington, you may proceed whenever you're ready.\par \par \uc2 SARAH WEDDINGTON (ATTORNEY)\par \par \uc2 A pregnancy, to a woman, is perhaps one of the most determinative aspects of her life.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She did not initially set out to become a plaintiff in a case that would become one of the most important cases in the 20th century. While the Supreme Court, in Roe versus Wade, held that women had a constitutional right to access abortion, the decision came too late for Norma McCorvey to access her wanted abortion.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When the decision came down, there was opposition brewing, but we didn't really see the flashpoint and divisive political debate that we see today.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Let your baby live.\par \par \uc2 ABORTION ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 If a woman does not want to have a pregnancy, she will find a way to end it, whether it's legal or illegal.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The conservative movement in the '80s really latched on to abortion as an issue that they saw would give them energy in their base. And that's when they started to lay the groundwork for this movement that took off.\par \par \uc2 NEWSCASTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Within just the last few years, here in Washington and across the country, tens of thousands of people, many of whom had never been involved in anything more political than a PTA meeting, have gotten involved in the pro-life cause.\par \par \uc2 JOSH PRAGER (AUTHOR "THE FAMILY ROE")\par \par \uc2 It is Jerry Fawell who really says he makes abortion the sort of most important rallying cry in his organization. He founds the Moral Majority.\par \par \uc2 JERRY FAWELL (PASTOR)\par \par \uc2 And I look on the unborn as the last disenfranchised minority in this country that has absolutely no civil rights.\par \par \uc2 JOSH PRAGER (AUTHOR "THE FAMILY ROE")\par \par \uc2 And they have the perfect person entering the White House, at that point.\par \par \uc2 PETER JENNINGS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Today, the President announced some new regulations he would like to see in order to end the use of federal funds for abortion counseling and referrals.\par \par \uc2 FMR PRESIDENT RONALD REAGAN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We must not rest, and I pledge to you that I will not rest, until a human life amendment becomes a part of our Constitution.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA WALTERS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We have a very popular conservative president who has delivered on many of his promises. Do you think he can deliver on his promise to stop abortion?\par \par \uc2 FAYE WALTON (FORMER PRESIDENT PLANNED PARENTHOOD)\par \par \uc2 He cannot deliver on this promise any more than he could deliver on the promise that he has made since 1980 when he was elected. People simply do not accept his position on this issue.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It is a woman's choice. It is a woman's choice.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As the movement is gathering force, Norma McCorvey is nowhere to be found. She's living in anonymity. She had preferred to remain quiet. So, when she went public as Jane Roe, it was a celebration, really, among abortion rights advocates.\par \par \uc2 NORMA MCCORVEY (AKA JANE ROE)\par \par \uc2 Women should choose their own destiny. We should have the right to control our own bodies.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it was equally shocking when she flipped sides in 1995.\par \par \uc2 NORMA MCCORVEY (AKA JANE ROE)\par \par \uc2 Now I've changed my position on choice.\par \par \uc2 JOSH PRAGER (AUTHOR "THE FAMILY ROE")\par \par \uc2 Norma says, you know what I'm going to switch sides, and she does in a swimming pool in Texas, while the cameras are rolling.\par \par \uc2 PASTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Hallelujah. Here she is.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 She's baptized Christian and takes up the cause of the anti-abortion movement.\par \par \uc2 JOSH PRAGER (AUTHOR "THE FAMILY ROE")\par \par \uc2 What ends up happening then is that just as she was sort of a complicated advocate for the pro-choice on this side, now she's the complicated advocate for the pro-life on that side.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At the end of her life, Norma McCorvey gives an interview to this documentary, "AKA Jane Roe"...\par \par \uc2 NORMA MCCORVEY (AKA JANE ROE)\par \par \uc2 This is my deathbed confession.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...when she switches sides again. She says she felt used by the anti-abortion movement, and that she's always been in favor of a woman's right to choose.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Did they use you as a trophy?\par \par \uc2 NORMA MCCORVEY (AKA JANE ROE)\par \par \uc2 Of course. I was the big fish.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) McCorvey was a complex person and had a complex journey, with many different phases. And, in some ways, that's true about the history of this country and the legal debate around abortion. It has had twists and turns.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But it's really conservatives who build the most momentum, chipping away at Roe over those years.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Hey, hey, ho, ho, Roe v. Wade has got to go.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But I think one thing is clear, this is in no way the final chapter in the story of this country's debate over abortion.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE FALL OF ROE\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Be sure to stay with us for our continuing coverage of the monumental abortion ruling. That's "Nightline," for tonight. You can watch all of our full episodes on \uc2 Hulu\uc2 . And we'll see you right back here Monday. Thanks for staying up with us. Good night, America. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020220628ei6o0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE FALL OF ROE\b0\par\par\uc2 2,101 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE FALL OF ROE\par \par \uc2 ROE, OVERTURNED, PROTESTS, MADI, HAWKINS, CATHEY, SIMPSON\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thanks for joining us. Tonight, shockwaves reverberating around the country after an historic overturning of Roe v. Wade, the Supreme Court eliminating the constitutional right to an abortion. The impact likely immediate, leading to sweeping abortion bans in many states across the country.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Abortion. My right, my choice.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 In the streets.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A sweeping, deeply consequential decision from the nation's highest court, ruling to overturn Roe v. Wade, rocking the foundations of more than five decades of legal precedent in our country. The court taking away a constitutional right that women in America have had for almost 50 years. Women like Madi, a college student whose abortion journey "Nightline" followed last year.\par \par \uc2 MADI (TEXAS COLLEGE STUDENT)\par \par \uc2 It just breaks my heart, because the one thing I think about is all of these girls and these women that are going to have to face struggles.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We have abolished abortion.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today's decision reaffirming the views of anti-abortion rights advocates like Kristan Hawkins.\par \par \uc2 KRISTAN HAWKINS (PRESIDENT STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 We've been calling ourselves the pro-life generation. So, it's overwhelming to think now we are the first post-Roe generation.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The 6-3 decision by the court's conservative majority upheld Mississippi's ban on abortions after 15 weeks. Five of those Justices, \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett went even farther, voting to overturn Roe versus Wade itself.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) There's never been a case like this where settled law that has been affirmed and reaffirmed, granted an important constitutional protection, indeed, what has long been understood as a fundamental constitutional protection, and then is removed by the court.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This coming just weeks after a leaked draft of the opinion circulated, which nearly identically mirrored today's decision. The impact of today's ruling will be immediate. Thirteen states have trigger laws that automatically end access to abortion now that Roe is overturned.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Seven have already gone into effect. Among them, Arkansas, which outlawed abortion, today. Dr. Janet Cathey works at the state's only abortion clinic. She was already forced to turn people away.\par \par \uc2 DR. JANET CATHEY (PLANNED PARENTHOOD ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 We had patients in their car, on their way here. You know, we called patients, it's, like, I'm already on my way. And they thought, well, I'm already on my way. It'll be okay. And it's, like, no, it's immediate. It's immediate. I would say panic is the primary reaction.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some of those women now have to drive more than seven hours to states like Illinois.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Abortion access will look very, very different depending on where you live, and also depending on the resources you have access to, to travel elsewhere if you do live in a state that restricts or prohibits abortion.\par \par \uc2 MONICA SIMPSON (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR SISTERSONG)\par \par \uc2 However you feel about abortion, on a personal level, is up to you. It becomes problematic is when you take your personal beliefs and then try to move them into law.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Lack of access to abortion disproportionately impacts low-income women and women of color, who often don't have the financial means to travel to a state where abortion is legal.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What does this ruling mean for women of color across the country?\par \par \uc2 MONICA SIMPSON (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR SISTERSONG)\par \par \uc2 This is pushing this health care out of reach for women of color, for folks of color who need it, every single day. This has huge implications on how people manage their lives, every single day.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Public opinion polls have consistency shown that the majority of Americans support upholding Roe versus Wade. In a recent ABC News/"Washington Post" poll, seven in 10 people say the decision should be left to the woman and her doctor. Nearly one in every six adult women have had an abortion.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We won't go back. We trust women.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Demonstrators flooding the streets after today's decision. Young women in favor of abortion rights outraged.\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 All of this is going to do is exacerbate the amount of unwanted teen pregnancies that we are currently having in the community, as well as in the entire state of Texas.\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I was shocked, angry. But more than that, I was tired. Access to abortion care and abortion services should not depend on your zip code.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Others jubilant.\par \par \uc2 ANTI ABORTION ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm so excited, happy, overwhelmed, to be living now in a post-Roe America.\par \par \uc2 ANTI ABORTION ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I was shaking, and I was so happy, I couldn't even think about anything else. I was just shaking. I never thought that I would witness this moment.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President speaking from the White House, today, emphatic in his repudiation of the ruling.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It's a realization of an extreme ideology and a tragic error by the Supreme Court, in my view.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While Republican leaders are lauding the decision.\par \par \uc2 REP KEVIN MCCARTHY (REP) (MINORITY LEADER)\par \par \uc2 You know, today's Supreme Court decision in Dobbs is the most important pro-life ruling in American history. We now have a voice for all life.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Multiple state leaders vowing to keep access to abortion open for anyone seeking them in their state.\par \par \uc2 GOV KATE BROWN (DEM) (OREGON)\par \par \uc2 We will not stand on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 GOV \uc2 JAY INSLEE\uc2  (DEM) (WASHINGTON)\par \par \uc2 The West Coast of the United States is going to stand strong.\par \par \uc2 GOV GAVIN NEWSOM (DEM) (CALIFORNIA)\par \par \uc2 We'll fight like hell to protect your rights and your safety.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Texas, restrictions have already been in place since the fall, when the state legislature banned abortions as early as six weeks, a decision that has since affected countless women, including Madi, who found out she was pregnant just days after the law went into effect.\par \par \uc2 MADI (TEXAS COLLEGE STUDENT)\par \par \uc2 When she told me that I was measuring at 10 1/2 weeks, I just cried. I was heartbroken and terrified. I immediately knew that any chance I had of being able to have this procedure done in Texas was gone.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So, she was forced to travel 400 miles away, across state lines, to a clinic in Mississippi, Jackson Women's Health Organization, the very clinic at the heart of the Supreme Court case.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Do you feel secure in your decision today?\par \par \uc2 MADI (TEXAS COLLEGE STUDENT)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. My decision hasn't wavered. It's my body, and it's my choice. I don't think it's right for people to try and convince others when it's not their life that's about to change.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You said in our original pieces that your body is your property. You should have control over it. What does this ruling tell you about who has control of reproductive rights, of frankly, women's bodies?\par \par \uc2 MADI (TEXAS COLLEGE STUDENT)\par \par \uc2 It shows that I never had the right to my own body. It's clear now that it's up to Texas legislation.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Madi said she had the means and the support to fly to Mississippi for her procedure, but Mississippi is one of the states with a trigger ban. The clinic she went, the Pink House, announcing that it, too, will soon have to close its doors.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What's your reaction to hearing that news?\par \par \uc2 MADI (TEXAS COLLEGE STUDENT)\par \par \uc2 It breaks my heart, because it was the only clinic.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, for people like you who are having to travel out of Texas to get to a clinic, what does closing the Mississippi clinic and the 13 other states that have trigger laws do to women looking for abortion services?\par \par \uc2 MADI (TEXAS COLLEGE STUDENT)\par \par \uc2 It makes everything so much scarier, because the demand becomes higher. I couldn't go to Louisiana, because it was booked up three weeks. I didn't have three weeks to wait. These women are going to lose all choice that they have because of the little things that each state has done to make it difficult.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today's ruling a victory decades in the making for anti-abortion rights activists. Kristan Hawkins is the President of Students for Life of America. The group has had a vocal and vigorous presence at nearly every abortion event.\par \par \uc2 KRISTAN HAWKINS (PRESIDENT STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 Pro-life signs. Get your pro-life signs, guys.\par \par \uc2 KRISTAN HAWKINS (PRESIDENT STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 Today, I speak to you pregnant with my fourth child. My first daughter. We are the pro-life generation.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) "Nightline" has followed Hawkins to protests and rallies like the March for Life since 2015.\par \par \uc2 KRISTAN HAWKINS (PRESIDENT STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 At the end of my talk, I'm going to go, "for we are the," you guys need to start chanting it.\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Okay.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Hawkins' work gaining momentum with Donald Trump's Supreme Court picks. We were with her in 2018 as she lobbied for \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 's Supreme Court confirmation.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How has your mission changed, today?\par \par \uc2 KRISTAN HAWKINS (PRESIDENT STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 Our mission really hasn't changed. Reversing Roe, we've also considered that to be one of the first steps in our mission of making abortion unavailable and unthinkable in our nation.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Will you be going after those who are, the pregnant women themselves, now, given that it's the act of getting abortion that is now illegal?\par \par \uc2 KRISTAN HAWKINS (PRESIDENT STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 We do not believe in prosecuting or putting a woman who has, you know, solicited an abortion, had an abortion, into jail. Because we very much view her as the second victim of the abortion industry that has told her that she is not capable, that she is not strong enough.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are you fighting to stop abortions in the case of rape and incest, as well?\par \par \uc2 KRISTAN HAWKINS (PRESIDENT STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 We've been very clear that a child's circumstance of their conception doesn't negate their basic humanity and their right to be born.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) If a 14-year-old girl is raped by her father, would you try to deny her an abortion?\par \par \uc2 KRISTAN HAWKINS (PRESIDENT STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 I think it's almost laziness to say, oh, a woman's been raped, therefore we're going to protect her access to abortion, and that solves her problem. That actually doesn't solve her problem.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Hawkins says, in addition to getting abortion banned outright, her group will now work to raise awareness of existing support services for pregnant women and to advocate against medical abortion pills.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 My body, my choice\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, people across the country making their voices heard, like Madi, who says the fight continues.\par \par \uc2 MADI (TEXAS COLLEGE STUDENT)\par \par \uc2 This is a big deal. And I'm worried that the wakeup call we needed with Roe being overturned, and it's too late. Because now everything has become incredibly more difficult. And we are going to have to fight an incredibly hard and long uphill battle that women my age are not prepared for.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We won't go back. We trust women. We won't go back.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE FALL OF ROE\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Up next, the backstory of Roe in Roe v. Wade, and the Texas woman who made that landmark decision possible.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE FALL OF ROE\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 "Nightline," "The Fall of Roe," continues. Here, again, Juju Chang.\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020220628ei6o0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPREME COURT OVERTURNS ROE V. WADE 6-3 DECISION ENDS CONSTITUTIONAL PROTECTIONS FOR ABORTIONS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,541 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, TOBIAS, ABORTION, NORTHUP, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. And it's great to have you with us here on a Friday night.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT OVERTURNS ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we begin, tonight, with the historic and sweeping ruling from the Supreme Court. The conservative majority, by a 6-3 vote, overturning Roe versus Wade, the right to choose abortion that had stood for half a century in this country, a nearly 50-year precedent. The court now turning the issue over to the states. And tonight, 13 states have already had so-called trigger laws on the books already banning abortions, set to go into effect, immediately. Six states now banning abortion, Missouri and Oklahoma among them. And in all, 26 states plan to ban or severely restrict abortion now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, these images coming in from across the country, the demonstrations and protests. These images from New York City, Washington Square Park, at this hour. And outside the Supreme Court, crowds growing all day, one side declaring victory, the other side in disbelief that this is no longer a decision protected across this country between a woman and her doctor. Abortion rights opponents celebrating a victory 50 years in the making. Abortion rights supporters stunned that a 50-year precedent has been overturned, now vowing to fight this. A Mississippi law at the heart of this case, a ban on abortions after 15 weeks, the court upholding that law.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And five of the justices going further, overturning Roe. Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  writing for the majority, saying, "The Constitution does not confer a right to abortion under protections for liberty or privacy." Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  writing for the dissent, saying, "This is based on nothing more than the fact that three new justices appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  pass decisive votes to overturn Roe, and that these decision opens potential challenges now to other settled rights in this country, including contraception and same-sex marriage."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 26 STATES CERTAIN OR LIKELY TO BAN ABORTION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And tonight, the map, here, those 26 states that now plan to take action to restrict or to ban abortion, including those 13 states with immediate trigger laws.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And all of this playing out, tonight, despite the polling in this country that shows a majority of Americans believe abortion should be legal. A recent ABC News poll showing 70% of Americans believe this is a decision that should be between a woman and her doctor.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, the reaction swift. President Biden calling this a sad day for the court and the country, calling the decision a tragic error. Former President Trump calling it the biggest win for life in a generation, taking credit for overturning Roe. Speaker Pelosi saying this is a slap in the face for women. Women today have less freedom than their mother's did, she said. Tonight, we will hear from all sides. Rachel Scott, Mary Bruce, Deborah Roberts, all standing by. But we begin, tonight, with Terry Moran who has covered the Supreme Court for years and he's at the court, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 6-3 DECISION ENDS CONSTITUTIONAL PROTECTIONS FOR ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At the Supreme Court, today, an historic upheaval. In a sweeping ruling that overturned a half a century of precedents, five justices ended the right of American women to choose abortion under the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS UPHOLDS MISSISSIPPI'S 15-WEEK BAN 6-3\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The court upheld Mississippi's ban on abortions after 15 weeks by a 6-3 vote. And five of those justices went even farther voting to overturn Roe versus Wade itself.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS OVERTURNS ROE V. WADE 5-4\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today's opinion is nearly identical to the draft that leaked from the court last month. Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  rejecting the 1973 landmark ruling on Roe, scorning the idea that the Constitution protects a right to choose abortion. "Roe was on a collision course with the Constitution from the day it was decided," Alito wrote. "It is time to heed the Constitution and return the issue of abortion to the people's elected representatives." Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  joined the conservative majority in upholding the Mississippi law, but he insisted the court did not need to overturn Roe. Instead, he searched for some middle ground, pleading, "None of this, however, requires that we also take the dramatic step of altogether eliminating the abortion right first recognized in Roe."\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And from the liberal wing, a scathing, agonized dissent. Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  arguing that Alito's ruling is based on nothing more than the fact that three new justices appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , Brett Kavanagh, and Amy Coney-Barrett cast decisive votes to overturn Roe. "Neither law nor facts nor attitudes have provided any new reasons to reach a different result than Roe did. All that has changed is this court," Breyer wrote. And in the dissent's last line, Breyer, along with justices Elena Kagan and \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , struck a somber note. "With sorrow, for this court, but more, for the many millions of American women who have today lost a fundamental constitutional protection, we dissent."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 26 STATES SET TO RESTRICT ABORTION ACCESS AFTER DECISION\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The impact of today's ruling will be immediate. Thirteen states have trigger laws that end access to abortion now that Roe is overturned. Thirteen more states are now expected to move quickly to ban abortion. Abortion-rights groups are bracing for this new reality in America.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP\uc2  (PRESIDENT & CEO CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS)\par \par \uc2 We certainly are going to be doing everything we can to maintain abortion access in the states that we expect to see coming hard at trying to ban abortion to keep access to services as long as possible.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But for those opposed to abortion rights who have fought for this day for decades, there is victory and vindication.\par \par \uc2 CAROL TOBIAS (PRESIDENT NATIONAL RIGHT TO LIFE COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 We've been working on this for actually more than 50 years, before Roe v. Wade, working to protect unborn children. We have certainly, in the pro-life movement, a lot of support and programs, centers, pregnancy centers that will help women.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In a written statement, former President Trump calling today's ruling "the biggest win for life in a generation." And Trump takes credit for recent conservative Supreme Court decisions made possible, he claims, "because I delivered everything as promised, including nominating and getting three highly respected and strong constitutionalists confirmed to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 ." At the White House, President Biden warned of dire consequences as a result of this decision.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 With Roe gone, let's be very clear, the health and life of women in this nation are now at risk. It was three justices named by one president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who are the core of today's decision to upend the scales of justice and eliminate a fundamental right for women in this country.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  tweeting, "The Supreme Court not only reversed nearly 50 years of precedent, it relegated the most intensely personal decision someone can make to the whims of politicians and ideologues, attacking the essential freedoms of millions of Americans." And Speaker of the House \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  accused Republicans of trying to drag women into the past.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (DEM-CA) (SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE)\par \par \uc2 Because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , and the Republican Party, their supermajority in the Supreme Court, American women today have less freedom than their mothers. What this means to women is such an insult. It's a slap in the face to women about using their own judgment to make their own decisions about their reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Terry Moran joins us from the Supreme Court, tonight. And, Terry, we took note that Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , today, writing a concurring opinion, calling on his colleagues to use the same reasoning to now revisit other cases like contraception and same-sex marriage based on the legal reasoning used in this particular ruling?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He sure did, David. Justice Alito insists this opinion should only apply to abortion, but the reasoning the court used to overturn Roe versus Wade, that can be use against other cases. And Justice Thomas calls on his colleague to reconsider the cases that establish the right to gay marriage, to contraception, even to private sexual conduct. In dissent, Justice Breyer warning the country no one should be confident that this majority is done with its work. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We can see the protests on both sides, right there behind you. Terry Moran, leading us off, tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220627ei6o00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 POLITICAL FALLOUT TRUMP APPOINTED JUSTICES KEY TO OVERTURNING ROE V. WADE\b0\par\par\uc2 660 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, TOBIAS, ABORTION, NORTHUP, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, CATHEY, MCNICHOLS, BROWN, MANCINI, STEARMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RULING REACTION\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GORSUCH, BARRETT, CLINTON, TRUMP, KAVANAUGH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLITICAL FALLOUT\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now to the political battle, ahead. This Supreme Court decision overturning Roe versus Wade has been a fight in the works for nearly 50 years now, but many believe this was truly set into motion with the 2016 presidential election between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . And both parties, tonight, now recognize abortion will be front-and-center in the midterms later this year. Mary Bruce, on that part of the story, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP APPOINTED JUSTICES KEY TO OVERTURNING ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) No matter what side of the abortion issue you're on, the 2016 presidential election was pivotal. Then-candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was clear in his intentions.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 I am putting pro-life justices on the court. I will say this, it will go back to the states, and the states will then make a determination.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) His opponent, \uc2 Hilary Clinton\uc2 , vowing to defend the right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON (FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE)\par \par \uc2 I strongly support Roe v. Wade, which guarantees a constitutional right to a woman to make the most intimate, most difficult, in many cases, decisions about her health care that one can imagine.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Clinton went to win the popular vote, but Trump won the Electoral College and White House. As president, Trump got his chance, naming not just one, but three conservative justices to the court, all of them pressed at their confirmation hearings about whether they would overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE NEIL GORSUCH (\uc2 US SUPREME COURT\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 So, a good judge will consider as precedent of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  worthy as treatment of precedent, like any other.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2  (\uc2 US SUPREME COURT\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 And one of the important things to keep in mind about Roe v. Wade is that it has been reaffirmed many times over the past 45 years.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE AMY CONEY BARRETT (\uc2 US SUPREME COURT\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 As Richard Fallon from Harvard said, "Roe is not a super-precedent because calls for its overruling have never ceased, but that doesn't mean that Roe should be overruled."\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But even after answering that Roe was established precedent, today, all three Trump-appointed justices voted to overturn the right to an abortion. Republican Senator Susan Collins, who supports abortion rights and was the decisive vote to confirm Kavanaugh and critical to putting Gorsuch on the bench, says she was misled. Collins calling today's decision a sudden, radical jolt, saying it "is inconsistent with what Justices Gorsuch and Kavanaugh said in their testimony and their meetings with me, where they both were insistent on the importance of supporting longstanding precedents that the country has relied upon." Today's decision will forever change this nation, impacting generations of women. With the midterms just months away, the President blunt.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS COULD BE CRITICAL ISSUE IN MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 This fall, Roe is on the ballot. Personal freedoms are on the ballot. The right to privacy, liberty, equality, they're all on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, we know this, elections have consequences. And tonight, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is taking credit, while President Biden is trying to do what he can, taking actions to defend women who may travel to other states to get an abortion and to try to make sure that the abortion pill is still readily accessible. But he knows, real change here can only come from Congress. And that is why he's stressing the importance of the upcoming midterms. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Mary Bruce, live at the White House. Mary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220627ei6o00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE INDEX\b0\par\par\uc2 105 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, TOBIAS, ABORTION, NORTHUP, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, CATHEY, MCNICHOLS, BROWN, MANCINI, STEARMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RULING REACTION\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GORSUCH, BARRETT, CLINTON, TRUMP, KAVANAUGH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLITICAL FALLOUT\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DIVIDED OVER ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GUN REFORM, BILL, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONGRESS PASSES BIPARTISAN GUN BILL\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, INVASION, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE INDEX\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, Ukrainian forces retreating from the city of Sievierodonetsk, in the east.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , now, once step closer to claiming the Donbas region.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back, here, the \uc2 US Navy\uc2  destroyer discovered beneath the sea.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220627ei6o00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION RULING REACTION EMOTIONAL RESPONSES POURING IN FROM ACROSS THE COUNTRY\b0\par\par\uc2 1,006 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, TOBIAS, ABORTION, NORTHUP, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, CATHEY, MCNICHOLS, BROWN, MANCINI, STEARMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RULING REACTION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, tonight, the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe, of course, sparking emotional reactions on this issue, a deeply personal issue on both sides. ABC's Rachel Scott has been traveling the country for months to hear from women on all sides of this debate. Tonight, we hear from women outside the court and across the nation and from the clinics now forced, even today, to tell women who had appointments that they can no longer come, that they, in some cases, must travel seven to eight hours to get to another state. Here's Rachel Scott, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EMOTIONAL RESPONSES POURING IN FROM ACROSS THE COUNTRY\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 My body, my choice.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We won't go back.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This is the moment when the crowds gathered in front of the Supreme Court heard the news.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I can't believe it.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A joyful moment for Neydy Casillas (PH), who came with her husband.\par \par \uc2 ANTI-ABORTION SUPPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I was shaking, and I was so happy. I couldn't even think about anything else. I was just shaking.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For others, like 21-year-old Kaylan Tanner (PH), disappointment.\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS SUPPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It doesn't matter if you don't agree with abortion, but that doesn't mean you take the right away from somebody else. Period.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 No justice, no peace.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For Ivana Castillo (PH) this was deeply personal.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tell me why it was important for you to be out here, today.\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS SUPPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It's important, because a long time ago, I did have an abortion, myself. I was raped. So, for me, this moment in history is sad.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION TRIGGER LAWS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In parts of the country, the change was immediate. Abortion now illegal in at least six of those so-called trigger states, including Arkansas. Dr. Janet Cathey works at the only clinic in Arkansas providing abortions, already forced to turn people away.\par \par \uc2 DR JANET CATHEY (PLANNED PARENTHOOD ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 We had patients in their car, on their way here. And, you know, we called patients. It's like, I'm already on my way. And they thought, well, I'm already on my way, it will be okay. And it's, like, no, it's immediate. It's immediate. I would say panic is the primary reaction.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some of those women will now have to drive more than seven hours to states like Illinois. Dr. Colleen McNichols runs a Planned Parenthood clinic, there.\par \par \uc2 DR COLLEEN MCNICHOLS (PLANNED PARENTHOOD)\par \par \uc2 Within minutes we were receiving calls from clinics and other trigger-ban states to say, I have patients on the schedule, tomorrow. How many can you accommodate in the coming days?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) These women now facing a new reality.\par \par \uc2 DR JANET CATHEY (PLANNED PARENTHOOD ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 You're talking about spending the night somewhere, a day off work and finding childcare and gasoline and reliable transportation and knowing where to stay.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We did it.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Shortly after today's ruling, during ABC's live overage, David asking the president of March for Life what she would say to those women now being turned away, facing seven, eight-hour journeys to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What would you say to those women and about those women?\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI (PRESIDENT MARCH FOR LIFE)\par \par \uc2 What I would say is that, you've got this. You've got so many opportunities in front of you and that that is a growing life, an actual human being. The poorest of the poorest, the most vulnerable, the unborn that is within you. And we'd love to give you all sorts of resources to choose adoption to be able to support you to make the choice for life.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Over the last year, I've traveled the country to understand both sides of this. In Oklahoma, where abortion is now illegal, I spoke with Republican State Representative Wendy Stearman, herself a mother of six.\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE WENDY STEARMAN (REP) (OKLAHOMA)\par \par \uc2 I very much agree with the founding principles of our country, which are to defend life and liberty, and life is the first one.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you feel like the public support is behind you?\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE WENDY STEARMAN (REP) (OKLAHOMA)\par \par \uc2 In the state of Oklahoma, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But in states where Democrats are in power, a new push to protect abortion rights. Today, the governors of Oregon, California, and Washington banding together in what they call a West Coast offense.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATE BROWN (DEM) (OREGON)\par \par \uc2 We will continue to protect patients from any state who come to our states for abortion care.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GAVIN NEWSOM (DEM) (CALIFORNIA)\par \par \uc2 Defend doctors, nurses and medical professionals.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR JAY INSLEE (DEM) (WASHINGTON)\par \par \uc2 And we're going to expand access to abortion services for the people in need.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, in Jackson, Mississippi, the clinic at the center of the Supreme Court case is still performing abortions. By law, they have just another 10 days, workers escorting young women to and from appointments.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, I spoken to that Oklahoma lawmaker, Wendy Stearman. She told me she is thrilled by today's decision. She calls it long overdue. Planned Parenthood has been working for months to create a network of clinics so that women could still have access to abortions in states where it is illegal, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Rachel Scott at the Supreme Court, as well, for us. Rachel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220627ei6o00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NAVY DESTROYER DISCOVERED BENEATH THE SEA EXPLORERS FIND WWII NAVY DESTROYER\b0\par\par\uc2 107 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, TOBIAS, ABORTION, NORTHUP, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, CATHEY, MCNICHOLS, BROWN, MANCINI, STEARMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RULING REACTION\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GORSUCH, BARRETT, CLINTON, TRUMP, KAVANAUGH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLITICAL FALLOUT\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DIVIDED OVER ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GUN REFORM, BILL, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONGRESS PASSES BIPARTISAN GUN BILL\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, INVASION, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE INDEX\par \par \uc2 SAMMY B., SHIPWRECK, DISCOVERY\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A final image, a World War II \uc2 US Navy\uc2  destroyer, the Sammy B., 23,000 feet down into the Pacific, hit in October, 1944.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Eighty-nine Americans were lost on that ship.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220627ei6o00008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DIVIDED OVER ABORTION SUPREME COURT DECISION DIVIDING HOUSEHOLDS NATIONWIDE\b0\par\par\uc2 481 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, TOBIAS, ABORTION, NORTHUP, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, CATHEY, MCNICHOLS, BROWN, MANCINI, STEARMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RULING REACTION\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GORSUCH, BARRETT, CLINTON, TRUMP, KAVANAUGH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLITICAL FALLOUT\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DIVIDED OVER ABORTION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And one more note on this, tonight. The dividing line clear across this nation, sometimes opposing views within the same family, between mothers and daughters. ABC's Deborah Roberts, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT DECISION DIVIDING HOUSEHOLDS NATIONWIDE\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, across this country, the nation divided. Some considering today a victory 50 years in the making. Others outraged that a decision between a woman and her doctor is taken away. In some cases, the bitter divide within the same family. Nicole and Kathleen, a tight-knit mom and daughter from the Midwest. Mom, Nicole, considered aborting Kathleen, but didn't. Now, the grown daughter disagreeing with her mother, firmly believing to a woman's right to choose. They disagree, but it's complicated.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Your mom has equated abortion to murder, at one point, no?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 At a certain point, yes. If it's a viable child that could live outside the womb, I'd, yeah.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And why is this so important for you to try to change her mind?\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN (DAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 I have the right to control my own body, my own life, my own future, my own destiny.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The idea that now that there are so many states that are starting to enact laws that are prohibiting abortion, what was your reaction to those moves?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 I have to say, a little bit of fear.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Fear?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE (MOTHER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Because as much as I don't really agree with it, for me, it's a huge change. And any time you lose a freedom, even one that is controversial, that's frightening to me. Any freedom loss is a little frightening to me.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Despite her views, she's concerned about other women, she says, stepping into this new territory. This is a complicated and passionate conversation no doubt being had across dinner tables all across the country, tonight, with women like Kathleen, David, determined to convince their moms that they are entitled to the same right to choose, which their mothers had all those years ago when they faced tough decisions.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Dividing lines in the country, and sometimes within the same family. Thank you, Deborah. Really important, tonight. When we come back, here, the major headline from the war in Ukraine, tonight. Where the Ukrainians are now pulling out tonight. And later, the shipwreck discovered, the images of the \uc2 US Navy\uc2  destroyer coming in, now.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220627ei6o00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CONGRESS PASSES BIPARTISAN GUN BILL FIRST BIPARTISAN GUN SAFETY BILL IN 30 YEARS PASS THE HOUSE\b0\par\par\uc2 154 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, TOBIAS, ABORTION, NORTHUP, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, CATHEY, MCNICHOLS, BROWN, MANCINI, STEARMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RULING REACTION\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GORSUCH, BARRETT, CLINTON, TRUMP, KAVANAUGH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLITICAL FALLOUT\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DIVIDED OVER ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GUN REFORM, BILL, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONGRESS PASSES BIPARTISAN GUN BILL\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, the first bipartisan bill on gun safety in 30 years clearing the House, now, waiting for the President's signature, enhancing background checks and red flag laws and funding gun violence prevention and mental health.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But no action on assault weapons, AR-15 style rifles, or age limits like the issues brought up in Buffalo and Uvalde.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back, tonight, the latest coming in from Ukraine, where the Ukrainians are now pulling out, tonight.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220627ei6o00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WORLD NEWS TONIGHT WITH DAVID MUIR\b0\par\par\uc2 460 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, history made at the Supreme Court, the court overturning Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT OVERTURNS ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 A 6-3 vote, the conservative justices overturning Roe, a 50-year precedent, abortion rights protected across this country, until now. Tonight, deciding to uphold Mississippi's abortion law and overruling Roe versus Wade. Tonight, President Biden declaring it a sad day for the court and the country, calling it a tragic error, and saying the decision shows how extreme the court has become, the court turning abortion rights to the states to decide. Tonight, 13 states with so-called trigger laws, immediate laws that ban abortion now set to go into effect. Missouri and Oklahoma among the first states, tonight, with those bans already taking effect. Twenty-six states now preparing to ban or severely restrict abortion.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Demonstrator on both sides rallying at the Supreme Court and across the country. ABC's Terry Moran at the court, tonight, and ABC's Rachel Scott with women from all over this country on both sides of the debate. Some outraged that this is no longer a private decision between them and their doctor. Others who say they have fought for this moment for 50 years.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLITICAL FALLOUT\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The political battle to come. Former President Trump, who appointed three conservative justices to the court, tonight taking credit for the court overturning Roe. And tonight, Republican Senator Susan Collins, who supports abortion rights, now saying she was misled by justices Gorsuch and Kavanaugh. Tonight, what those justices said about Roe before being confirmed to the court. And Mary Bruce this evening on how both parties now believe this issue will be front be front-and-center in this year's midterms.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OPPOSING SIDES OF THE ISSUE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The dividing lines clear across this nation, sometimes opposing views within the same family. Mothers and daughters who disagree. Deborah Roberts, on that, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONGRESS PASSES BIPARTISAN GUN BILL\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The other major news this Friday night, the first major piece of federal gun reform in nearly 30 years. The bipartisan agreement from the Senate now passing the House, awaiting the President's signature.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UKRAINIAN FORCES RETREAT\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The major headline from Ukraine, tonight, where the Ukrainians are now pulling out. And the remarkable find, tonight, the world's deepest shipwreck. A \uc2 US Navy\uc2  destroyer, the underwater video, and where it's been found.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT WITH DAVID MUIR\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From ABC News world headquarters in New York, this is "World News Tonight with David Muir."\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220627ei6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 KEY SUPREME COURT DECISIONS LOOMING RULING ON GUNS & ABORTION LAW TAKES CENTER STAGE\b0\par\par\uc2 384 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, HEAT WAVE\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, CHILDREN, WALENSKY, GRUBER\par \par \uc2 GUN REFORM, BIDEN, COONS, CORNYN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE AGAINST TIME FOR GUN REFORM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LAWMAKERS WORK TO HAMMER OUT SPECIFICS ON PROPOSED BILL BEFORE SUMMER RECESS\par \par \uc2 GUN RIGHTS, ABORTION, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Turning now to a major Supreme Court ruling on gun rights that could come down within days.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEY SUPREME COURT DECISIONS LOOMING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RULING ON GUNS & ABORTION LAW TAKES CENTER STAGE\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ABC's Ike Ejiochi has more from the Supreme Court as gun rights and abortion are two of the biggest cases left on the High Court's calendar. Ike, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Janai. For weeks now, the nation's eyes have been on the Supreme Court awaiting some high-stakes decisions that could have far-reaching effects, including a decision on guns.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, the court will decide on the New York state case, questioning a person's fundamental right to carry a handgun in public for self-defense. A potentially sweeping impact on concealed carry nationwide at a time of soaring gun sales and several recent mass shootings. Also pending is the court's decision on abortion rights following the leaked draft opinion showing the court overturning Roe versus Wade. Now, it's one of most highly anticipated decisions, which could roll back nearly five decades of abortion rights precedent. A ruling that has the potential to transform abortion rights for tens of millions of women nationwide.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, that eight-foot-tall black non-scalable fencing still surrounds the nation's highest court as several protesters are expected to continue gathering outside the steps of the court as the justices prepare to release opinions on some very controversial topics. Now, this week, the court is expected to release two more rounds of opinions on Tuesday and Thursday. Now, other pending cases involve school vouchers, prayer in schools, immigration and the remain in Mexico policy from the Trump administration, and even a decision surrounding veterans' discrimination. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Ike Ejiochi, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220721ei6j0002x\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BREAST CANCER TREATMENT BREAKTHROUGH DRUG HELPS CERTAIN PATIENTS LIVE LONGER\b0\par\par\uc2 667 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 REYES, SHOOTING, INVESTIGATION, UVALDE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELEMENTARY SCHOOL SHOOTING INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT QUESTIONS MOUNT OVER POLICE RESPONSE\par \par \uc2 GUN REFORM, CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 GAS, PRICES, PRIMARY\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, INVASION, UKRAINE, MICHEL, TRAVEL BAN\par \par \uc2 GRINER, VIGIL, RUSSIA, REED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIGIL FOR BRITTNEY GRINER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TREVOR REED ATTENDS HOUSTON EVENT FOR DETAINED WNBA STAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 "TOP GUN: MAVERICK", LAWSUIT, PARAMOUNT, COUCH\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, INFLATION, SHRINKFLATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW REPORT ON "SHRINKFLATION"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW SOME COMPANIES ARE DOWNSIZING PRODUCTS TO KEEP PRICES SAME\par \par \uc2 SHARKS, SIGHTINGS, CLAVIN, SKOMAL\par \par \uc2 STANLEY CUP, FINALS, \uc2 COLORADO AVALANCHE\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 UVALDE, SHOOTING, REYES, TEACHER, WITNESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UVALDE TEACHER SPEAKS OUT FOR FIRST TIME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHOOTING SURVIVOR'S ACCOUNT FROM INSIDE CLASSROOM\par \par \uc2 GAS, PRICE, INFLATION, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GAS PRICES HIT NEW HIGH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PAIN AT THE PUMP WEIGHING ON VOTERS\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE, PRESS SECRETARY, POLITICS, GUNS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE WITH NEW WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KARINE JEAN-PIERRE ON BIDEN'S CALL FOR ACTION\par \par \uc2 TRAUMA, SHOOTING, CHILDREN, SURVIVORS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE CHILDREN OF SCHOOL SHOOTINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW YOUNG SURVIVORS GRAPPLE WITH LONG-TERM TRAUMA\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL, "SCREAM", ACTOR, HORROR, MOVIE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEVE CAMPBELL NOT RETURNING TO "SCREAM"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STAR SAYS PAY DISPUTE LED TO DIFFICULT DECISION\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 FRIEDLE, ROMANO, "KIM POSSIBLE", TV\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "KIM POSSIBLE" STARS LIVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHRISTY CARLSON ROMANO & WILL FRIEDLE\par \par \uc2 METASTATIC BREAST CANCER, DRUG, LONGEVITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER TREATMENT BREAKTHROUGH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DRUG HELPS CERTAIN PATIENTS LIVE LONGER\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're back now with a potential major medical breakthrough for women who will be diagnosed with a type of metastatic breast cancer this year and moving forward. Results from a new study found a treatment that may help some of these patients live a longer life. ABC News chief medical correspondent Dr. Jennifer Ashton is with us. Good morning, Jen.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Lara.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, this is not a new drug.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right. It's not a new drug, but it's used in a new subset of breast cancer patients. It's called Enhertu, so it's been approved for patients with HER2-positive breast cancer. This new study, just published in the New England Journal of Medicine, looked at 500 women who had what's called HER2 low, low levels of HER2 receptor, metastatic breast cancer, for whom there weren't a lot of treatment options available. This is an antibody chemotherapy combo cocktail, and what the study found in these 500 or so women is it got them more time.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They got an additional five months progression-free survival. That means without their cancer progressing and spreading further. And they got an additional six months survival. They lived six months longer. So it's about time for these women that really didn't have a lot of options.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You said it's not a cure, but that time, so precious, so precious.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, it's important, it's important.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, when will this drug be available for this use?\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That's the key. For this particular subset of people, we don't know. It's been granted what's called FDA-expedited breakthrough designation, which kind of speeds that timeline potentially. It does come with a big price tag. It's $14,000 a month.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I've read that.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it can have some serious side effects, but, again, when you hear this presented at the biggest cancer conference in the country, potentially it gives this group of patients some hope.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and hopefully more hopeful news coming our way.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thank you, Jen.\par \par \uc2 DR. JEN ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You bet.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220713ei670000n\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MENTAL HEALTH AND RACE BREAKING THE MENTAL HEALTH STIGMA FOR BLACK WOMEN\b0\par\par\uc2 273 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THIS MORNING, DEADLY TORNADO\par \par \uc2 LONG, TORNADO, MICHIGAN\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, SOUTH KOREA\par \par \uc2 BROWNSTEIN, MOGOR, BELICH, COVID, SURGE, BOOSTER, KIDS\par \par \uc2 SUTTON, COVID, BOOSTERS, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 BUFFALO, SUPERMARKET, SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, EMAILS, OVERTURN, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY, ANTON, BUCHA, MARIUPOL, FINLAND\par \par \uc2 ENTSMINGER, DONNARUMMA, PHILLIPS, WEST, FIRES\par \par \uc2 HOT, SNOW\par \par \uc2 OSAKA, TENNIS, FRENCH OPEN\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP OF "BIGGIE" PERFORMING\par \par \uc2 VAUGHT, NURSE\par \par \uc2 SNOW, COLORADO, HEAT, NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 THOMARIOS, GUILBAULT, KITCHENS, RETIREES, WORKFORCE\par \par \uc2 SHAW BROWN, SUMMER, TRAVEL, DEALS\par \par \uc2 MISHRA, 7-YEAR-OLD, NATIONAL ANTHEM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, BOOSTER, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 BABY FORMULA, SHORTAGE, OPERATION FLY FORMULA\par \par \uc2 AFRICA, HUMANITARIAN CRISIS, MONTHE, FAMINE\par \par \uc2 MENTAL HEALTH, WOMEN, RACE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Welcome back to "GMA" and May is Mental Health Awareness Month. And according to the \uc2 American Psychiatric Association\uc2 , only one in three black Americans who require mental health actually get the care that they need.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And Janai is breaking down the stigma in a very powerful roundtable conversation on mental health care for black women. Janai, you basically lay it all out there in this conversation.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, it was a really intimate and vulnerable conversation. It was an honor to get to do it and talk with those women about issues that impact millions of Americans. So, I think that so many people can benefit, have benefited as they've told me from watching it. And you can catch the entire conversation "Breaking the Mental Health Stigma for Black Women," it's on Hulu now.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, well done.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220617ei5l0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 OKLAHOMA PASSES NEAR TOTAL ABORTION BAN SUPREME COURT SET TO RULE ON ROE V WADE\b0\par\par\uc2 605 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID BOOSTER FOR KIDS\par \par \uc2 COVID, BOOSTERS, KIDS, CDC, AMIN, PARENTS\par \par \uc2 JHA, COVID, BOOSTERS, CASES, KIDS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ASIA, TENSIONS, CHINA, NORTH KOREA\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, BOMBINGS, US, AID, WAR CRIMES, FIGHTERS\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, TEMPS, HUMIDITY, WIND GUSTS\par \par \uc2 GASOLINE, PRICES, GOUGING, HOUSE\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BAN, WALES, STEARMAN, STITT\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Michael. We're gonna turn now to the battle over abortion rights. Oklahoma passing a near total ban outlawing the procedure from conception, and in nearly all cases. It is the most restrictive ban in the country, and it comes as the Supreme Court could soon overturn Roe V. Wade. Terry Moran is outside the court with the latest. Good morning, Terry.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OKLAHOMA PASSES NEAR TOTAL ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MOVE COMES WITH SUPREME COURT SET TO RULE ON ROE V WADE\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Good morning, Cecilia. This is a glimpse into the future for so many American women with the Supreme Court seemingly poised to overturn Roe Versus Wade. It's looking more and more like a race among the states to pass the most extreme law restricting abortion, and right now Oklahoma is winning.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, there's a new reality for women emerging in Oklahoma. The state legislature has passed the country's most restrictive ban on abortion, relying on private citizens to enforce it.\par \par \uc2 EMILY WALES (INTERIM PRESIDENT & CEO PLANNED PARENTHOOD GREAT PLAINS)\par \par \uc2 Once signed, abortion will be illegal in Oklahoma, full stop.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The new law bans abortions from the moment of fertilization and in nearly all cases, except to protect the life of the mother or in cases of incest and rape, but only if those crimes are reported to police. ABC's Rachel Scott noting how often crimes of rape and incest go unreported by victims, pressed the bill's author.\par \par \uc2 STATE REP WENDI STEARMAN (REP) (OKLAHOMA)\par \par \uc2 Well, the goal of this to protect the child, the unborn child. So I believe that putting in the exception as we have it is acceptable in this situation.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Oklahoma Governor \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2  has vowed to sign the bill into law, which would take effect immediately.\par \par \uc2 GOV \uc2 KEVIN STITT\uc2  (REP) (OKLAHOMA)\par \par \uc2 We want to outlaw abortion in the state of Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This law is enforced by empowering private citizens to sue anyone who is assists in an abortion, with a reward of at least $10,000, like a law passed in Texas last year. Abortion has been a constitutional right for nearly half a century now, but the new conservative supermajority on the Supreme Court could change that.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) According to that leaked Supreme Court draft opinion obtained by "Politico," five of the nine justices are ready to overturn Roe versus Wade and let states make their own abortion laws. Supporters of abortion rights, including Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , warn of a new repressive era if the Oklahoma law becomes the norm.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And it represents a threat, not just to women, but to all Americans.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That Supreme Court decision that could overturn Roe versus Wade could come as early as next week, but more likely at the end of June. And if the Supreme Court does go all the way and overturn Roe versus Wade, there are at least 26 states that have laws ready to go that would ban or severely restrict abortion. George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220616ei5k00008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 RESTRICTIVE ABORTION BILL OKLAHOMA LAWMAKERS PASS BILL BANNING NEARLY ALL ABORTIONS\b0\par\par\uc2 404 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 FORMULA SHORTAGE, MENDOZA, MEDRANO, DELAURO, CALIFF\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, GENDRON, PATTERSON, PERSONS, BRIDGES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUSPECT JEERED AS "A COWARD"\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINE, AMIN, \uc2 PFIZER\uc2 , TALBOT, PAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC OKS CHILD BOOSTERS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BAN, STITT, STEARMAN, HARRIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RESTRICTIVE ABORTION BILL\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the abortion battle, Oklahoma's legislature has now passed what would be the most restrictive abortion ban yet, outlawing almost all abortions after the moment of conception and, like Texas now, relying on lawsuits from private citizens to enforce it all. Here's Rachel Scott.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OKLAHOMA LAWMAKERS PASS BILL BANNING NEARLY ALL ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, in Oklahoma, a bill banning almost all abortions from the moment of conception heading to the governor, who has vowed to sign it.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KEVIN STITT (REP) (OKLAHOMA)\par \par \uc2 We want to outlaw abortion in the state of Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The bill includes narrow exceptions, to protect the life of the mother and for cases of rape and incest, but only if they're reported to law enforcement. I sat down with the bill's author, State Representative Wendi Stearman.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Critics say, why not make exceptions to cover all of those instances, especially when rape and incest are so underreported?\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE WENDI STEARMAN (REP) (OKLAHOMA)\par \par \uc2 Well, the goal of this is to protect the child, the unborn child. So, I believe that putting in the exception, as we have it, is acceptable, in this situation.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But today, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  calling it outrageous.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It's just the latest in a series of extreme laws around the country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OKLAHOMA BILL\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Oklahoma bill takes a page out of the Texas playbook, empowering private citizens to sue anyone who aids or abets an abortion. The reward, at least $10,000. And when it's signed, it will take effect immediately.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, all this as the Supreme Court is poised to overturn Roe versus Wade. If that happens, 26 states would either ban abortion or severely restrict access to it. And 13 states would do it almost immediately. David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Rachel Scott, up on the Hill for us, again, tonight. Thank you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220520ei5j00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WORLD NEWS TONIGHT WITH DAVID MUIR\b0\par\par\uc2 489 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, breaking news on the baby formula shortage and on COVID in children. Tonight, what the CDC is now saying about a new booster for children.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FDA: FORMULA CRISIS WILL EASE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 First, the FDA commissioner pressed on the formula shortage by lawmakers on both sides, insisting the supply shortage will begin to get better, quote, "within days," but acknowledging it will be a few weeks until we're back to normal. Ramping up production at home, and the Defense Department now using commercial planes to bring in formula from abroad.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BUFFALO SUSPECT CALLED "COWARD"\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 To Buffalo, and tonight, the suspect charged with killing 10 people in court, today, wearing an orange jumpsuit and shackles in front of grieving relatives of the victims. What was shouted at him in court. And will that beloved grocery store stay open in that community? The employee who hasn't taken off his nametag since that awful day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHILD BOOSTER SHOTS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, children and COVID in this country. And the CDC panel now recommending, tonight, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  booster shots for children 5 to 11. \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  now says it increases antibodies against the original Omicron variant 22-fold, just as new child COVID infections hit their highest point since February.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRICTEST ABORTION BAN IN U.S.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The abortion battle in this country. And, tonight, the Oklahoma legislature passing the most restrictive abortion ban in America, banning abortion beginning at conception, with few exceptions, and sending it to the governor, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN WELCOMES NEW NATO BIDS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 President Biden meeting with leaders of Finland and Sweden at the White House, today, supporting their bid to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 , defying threats from Vladimir Putin.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS EXCLUSIVE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The ABC News exclusive, tonight, Martha Raddatz onboard the USS Maine, one of 14 ballistic missile submarines in the \uc2 US Navy\uc2 , protecting the US from potential nuclear threats. And what they kept secret from Martha, even while she was on board.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MONKEYPOX INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The first case of monkeypox in the US this year. What we know about the patient in Massachusetts and the possible new case now being investigated in New York City.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FACTORY EXPLOSION AND FIRE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The explosion at a construction company. Emergency crews rushing to the scene, tonight. Several hurt, including firefighters.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 In London, tonight, 36 years after "Top Gun," Tom Cruise and the royal moment with Duchess Kate. You'll see the images. And "America Strong" here, tonight, the surprise letter at graduation. And that was just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT WITH DAVID MUIR\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From ABC News world headquarters in New York, this is "World News Tonight with David Muir."\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220520ei5j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 UNDERSTANDING PREGNANCY LOSS WHY DO THE CHANCES GO UP AS A WOMAN AGES?\b0\par\par\uc2 831 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPERMARKET TRAGEDY\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, SALTER, GRAMAGLIA, GARCIA, TOOKES\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, CHURCH, HALLOCK, PASTOR, PARISHIONERS\par \par \uc2 GUN VIOLENCE, AMERICA, EPIDEMIC, GUNMAN\par \par \uc2 STANFIELD, MCREYNOLDS, SHOOTING, TOPS, ESCAPE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, BUFFALO, SHOOTING, GUNS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 BABY FORMULA, SHORTAGE, PELOSI, MOMS, DOLAN\par \par \uc2 CALIFF, FDA, FORMULA, CONSTITUENTS, ABBOTT, SUPPLY\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, \uc2 NATO\uc2 , STOLTENBERG, SWEDEN, FINLAND\par \par \uc2 HEARD, DEPP, TRIAL, TESTIMONY, CROSS EXAMINATION, BARKIN\par \par \uc2 BARBEQUE, COMPLETION, CHEFS, JUDGES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE AND STORMS SET UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, GRAMAGLIA, MCREYNOLDS, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 BABY FORMULA, SHORTAGE, CALIFF, DOLAN, PELOSI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRITICAL BABY FORMULA SHORTAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LAWMAKERS VOW TO TAKE ACTION AS FAMILIES SCRAMBLE\par \par \uc2 JUDD, TRIBUTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CELEBRATING NAOMI JUDD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTRY MUSIC LEGEND HONORED AT PUBLIC MEMORIAL\par \par \uc2 SPEARS, MISCARRIAGE, ASGHARI, PREGNANCY\par \par \uc2 PREGNANCY LOSS, CAUSES, TIPS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNDERSTANDING PREGNANCY LOSS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHY DO THE CHANCES GO UP AS A WOMAN AGES?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And our chief medical correspondent Dr. Jen Ashton, a practicing OB/GYN, is here.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When you saw the post, you know, and she's 40 years old. Was that a factor at all?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, it is obstetrically. You know, we have this horrible term for women over the age of 35 in obstetrics. We call it advanced maternal age.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) I mean, it's unbelievable to think that 40 can be a risk factor now because that is so young. But in terms of miscarriage, pregnancy loss, obstetric health, it can be.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We don't understand why. A lot of pregnancy losses are unexplained, but there is a (inaudible) tend to go through. Chromosomal abnormalities that come from the egg or potentially the sperm obviously at the top of the list.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CAUSES OF PREGNANCY LOSS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Previous pregnancy loss, any kind of structural abnormality in the cervix or the uterus even caused by previous surgery, and then a whole list of hormonal disorders, including diabetes, thyroid disease, perimenopausal hormone imbalance. There's a long list, but it doesn't make it any easier to deal with.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, it does not, Jen. Physical, emotional. What do you tell your patients and their partners?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE EMOTIONAL IMPACT ON A WOMAN & HER PARTNER\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, first of all, include the partner because they're oftentimes forgotten. You know, no matter how common this is, 40% over the age of 40 pregnancies end prematurely.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This is a loss. It doesn't matter whether it occurs one week into pregnancy or eight-and-a-half months into pregnancy. And we're struggling with how we deal with that. And the first step, I think, which is a good one, is renaming this.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In obstetrics, the medical term is spontaneous abortion or miscarriage, the lay term. Now, we're shifting to a term that's less blame placing on the woman, which is pregnancy loss. And I think that's appropriate and I think it's important.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know. And that's why I was phrasing it like that when I saw the note come in.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I remembered people saying, I mean, just you put a stigma on it when you say it another way.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right. That's right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Is there anything, anything that a woman can do especially as she gets older?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, what I normally will tell women is that there are things under their control and things that are not under their control.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAVING A HEALTHY PREGNANCY OVER 35\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Before pregnancy, the things that can be under control, you know, cutting down on alcohol use, smoking. When you are pregnant, early prenatal care is the key.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW TO INCREASE THE CHANCES OF A SUCCESSFUL PREGNANCY\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And then, again, you want to loop in your midwife or your OB/GYN, taking prenatal vitamins with folic acid three months before conception if it's a planned pregnancy. But, Robin, remember, 40% to 50% of pregnancies in this country are unplanned.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That doesn't mean undesired. It means unplanned. So, some of these things we can control and some of these things we can't.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hope you'll talk about it more on "GMA3."\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We definitely will.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. All right. But George has a very special guest.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220609ei5g0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BREAKING THE MENTAL HEALTH STIGMA FOR BLACK WOMEN DISCUSSING THE MYTH OF THE "STRONG BLACK WOMAN"\b0\par\par\uc2 669 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, LEWIS, NICHOLS, PROBE, BROWN, GENDRON\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, FLYNN, BELONGIA, INVESTIGATION, GENDRON\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, GUN VIOLENCE, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEARLY 7,000 HAVE DIED FROM GUN VIOLENCE THIS YEAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ADMINISTRATION PROMISES $10 BILLION TO COMBAT VIOLENT CRIME\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, SALTER, INVESTIGATION, BROWN\par \par \uc2 BABY FORMULA, FLEMING, BRUNE, URBANSKI, JONES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAMILIES DESPERATE AMID BABY FORMULA SHORTAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MULTIPLE STATES AT LEAST 50% OUT OF STOCK\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, INVASION, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 SCHLITZ, LAW SCHOOL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, HATE CRIME, INVESTIGATION, GENDRON, ARREST\par \par \uc2 GENDRON, SHOOTING, INVESTIGATION, ARREST, HATE CRIME\par \par \uc2 MENTAL HEALTH, WILLIAMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING THE MENTAL HEALTH STIGMA FOR BLACK WOMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONVERSATION DISCUSSES THE MYTH OF THE "STRONG BLACK WOMAN"\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, May is Mental Health Awareness Month. It's a topic that remains taboo for many particularly in the black community. I sat down with three women trailblazing the field of mental health to discuss how racial and social issues intersect with mental health. And we discussed the myth of the strong black woman trope and shared how we can develop healthy practices to maintain a stable mental well-being in our ever-changing society.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What has it been like for you in your practice in the last two years?\par \par \uc2 DR TERESA TAYLOR WILLIAMS (PSYCHOTHERAPIST AND ACADEMIC SPECIALIST)\par \par \uc2 One of the things that I found in the last two years is that everyone, for a few minutes, bit of time, was in denial. The pandemic hasn't affected me. It hasn't changed my life. But as we got deeper and deeper into the pandemic, everything that was bothersome, everything that was a problem for them, whether it'd be work or family, relationships, all began to feel the effects of the pandemic, your health, and chip away at what they thought was strong foundations. Behind this pandemic, we have found that there are things that we thought were really strong foundations, things that I call, things that I absolutely know for sure. I'm married, my spouse loves me. My children are doing well. I'm healthy. I have a home, I have a job.\par \par \uc2 DR TERESA TAYLOR WILLIAMS (PSYCHOTHERAPIST AND ACADEMIC SPECIALIST)\par \par \uc2 All of those things were up for being impacted by the pandemic. And so, people who are coming to me now are trying to get back to who they were. And the one question that was common amongst most of all of my new clients were, tell me when I'm going to feel like myself again.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Probably, many of you have asked that question. During this conversation, these women brought up things like, the power of our imagination and shifting the narrative of the grind culture, overworking, to find a place of self-care and rest, and embracing and being satisfied with who we are right now. You can catch more of a "GMA" digital conversation "Breaking the Mental Health Stigma for Black Women" on "GMA" digital, "Nightline," ABC News Live, and \uc2 Hulu\uc2  starting this week. And, guys, we have all talked a lot about mental health.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) This was a really powerful and humbling way to bring it to the forefront.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And what she said about denial, you know, resonates across the board and our instinct to just tough it out and grind through, but there are resources available. It's a great conversation.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COOLING MARKET?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Well, coming up on "Good Morning America," signs that the red-hot housing market may be cooling off. What it means for buyers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EMERGENCY FILL-IN\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And then, still ahead here, stepping into a Broadway role with only an hour rehearsal. That's ahead in "Pop News."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220608ei5f00037\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TENS OF THOUSANDS PROTEST FOR ABORTION RIGHTS ANGER AND OUTRAGE AHEAD OF SUPREME COURT DECISION\b0\par\par\uc2 186 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, LEWIS, NICHOLS, PROBE, BROWN, GENDRON\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, FLYNN, BELONGIA, INVESTIGATION, GENDRON\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, GUN VIOLENCE, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEARLY 7,000 HAVE DIED FROM GUN VIOLENCE THIS YEAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ADMINISTRATION PROMISES $10 BILLION TO COMBAT VIOLENT CRIME\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, SALTER, INVESTIGATION, BROWN\par \par \uc2 BABY FORMULA, FLEMING, BRUNE, URBANSKI, JONES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAMILIES DESPERATE AMID BABY FORMULA SHORTAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MULTIPLE STATES AT LEAST 50% OUT OF STOCK\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ABORTION, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In the meantime, a national call to action yesterday with thousands of concerned citizens pouring into the streets protesting for abortion rights in cities across the country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TENS OF THOUSANDS PROTEST FOR ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ANGER AND OUTRAGE AHEAD OF EXPECTED SUPREME COURT DECISION\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Washington, abortion rights advocates marching from the National Mall to the steps of the Supreme Court. The protests, a direct response to that leaked draft opinion signaling that the High Court is positioned to overturn Roe v. Wade. Anti-abortion activists also present there making their voices heard as well.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220608ei5f0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 3,640 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 NY, SUPERMARKET, MASSACRE, RACISM, GENDRON\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL, BUFFALO, MASSACRE, HATE, SPEECH, CRIME\par \par \uc2 GRAMAGLIA, NY, SUSPECT, TOPS, MASSACRE, SALTER\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, GUNS, VIOLENCE, INFLATION, FORMULA, JAN 6\par \par \uc2 STEFANISHYNA, WAR, UKRAINE, \uc2 NATO\uc2 , CONTEST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SATURDAY\par \par \uc2 2022, MIDTERM, MONITOR, SENATE, MAP, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 BUFFALO, MASSACRE, MIDTERMS, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Abortion should be a human right in this country!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Hands off!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Our bodies!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Hands off!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Our bodies!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Keep your rosaries off my ovaries! Keep your rosaries off my ovaries!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thousands marching across the country in support of abortion rights, yesterday. Will it make a difference in the midterms? One of the topics on our Roundtable. We're joined by \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , Donna Brazile, the executive editor of the Associated Press, Julie Pace, and "Time" magazine national political correspondent, Molly Ball. And I do want to get to the midterms and abortion rights impact. But Donna, I, I have to begin with that horrific massacre in Buffalo overnight, a sick sign of the times. I want to pose the question that I posed to the Speaker, to Governor Hochul. Is there anything to be done about it?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We better do something about it, George. I mean, look, Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 , the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director warned us almost two years ago that this was a big concern, a threat to our domestic tranquility. And yet, what are we doing about it? This is basically a situation where we have to talk about it. We got to raise it. We got to talk about white supremacy. We got to talk about the radicalization of kids in this country. We cannot do a both-side argument on this one, here, because if we don't begin to address what is happening, it's going to continue to happen.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) El Paso, what we saw in Charlottesville, what we saw down in Charleston, we have to address it. We have to be vigilant, and we have to root out this type of extremism in our society.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Chris, you served as a governor and a prosecutor. Was this ever on your radar, then?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Not during my time as US Attorney, George. It did start to get on it a little bit during the near, the end of my second term as governor, where our law enforcement folks in New Jersey started to see more people willing to act out violently, seemingly without any type of heads-up, 'cause they're acting alone, and that's what makes it so difficult for law enforcement. Because even with good intelligence work, it's really hard if someone is a crazy person who is not doing things overtly publicly, it becomes harder. Now, I think with this guy, it'll be interesting to find out why we didn't see some of the stuff he was writing and some of the stuff he was posting and why he wasn't flagged for that.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But this is why \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2  has been saying the stuff he's been saying. He's been trying to alert state and local law enforcement. "Hey, we have to work together on this." The \uc2 FBI\uc2  can't do it by themselves, but state and local with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  can do this in a way that flags some of this stuff that's happening on social media, and keeps a watch on some of these folks who are going to act out violently like they did yesterday.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Julie, the amount of planning that went into this is just chilling.\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE EDITOR)\par \par \uc2 It really is. And I should say Buffalo is my hometown. Really just tragic to see what's happening there, but it's an incredibly resilient city that I now will come back from this. But, the amount of planning that seemed to happen in spaces where other people were, you know, these online communities right now are really robust, and it does feel like law enforcement is often behind the curve, I think. It's going to be really interesting to see where the debate goes in terms of what the responsibility of the tech companies will be here, and also other people that, that just exist online in these communities, what is their responsibility? We all get so used to flagging things when we see them in person, but this is sort of the next space I think where people need to be more alert.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Not just online, Molly Bald, the great replacement theory is a fixture on Fox News.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY BALL (TIME MAGAZINE NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 It's, it's really something that you see in the discourse in a way that is very disturbing, right? And, and I think, you know, I spend a lot of time just trying to talk to everyday voters and keep my finger on the pulse of the nation, and the number one thing people bring up is how mad we are at each other, how divided we are. And this is something that, you know, I think politicians see it as sort of just cheap, political rhetoric. Everybody says, "Oh, let's come together." But people are really taking this seriously, this sense of division and hatred and just all of the strife that, that we feel.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY BALL (TIME MAGAZINE NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 And I think particularly, you know, coming out of COVID, reemerging, trying to come back together as a society, there's so much we need to be doing just to try to knit people together and feel like a country again, feel like a community together.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It seems like that's going to be pretty difficult, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , heading into midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP'S MIDTERM ENDORSEMENTS DEEPENING GOP RIFT?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, look, it is. And I think midterms are particularly divisive, because you have lower turnout, and the people who come to vote in midterms typically true believers on both sides of the aisle, and that a lot of the folks in the middle typically are less likely to vote in midterms. So, as a result, what communications do politicians do? They want to communicate to the people who actually are going to show up and vote. And so, you're, I think, going to see in these midterms a lot more communication by the Democrats on what they consider to be hardcore progressive issues, and for the Republicans, to hardcore conservative base, as well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOTH PARTIES PREPARE FOR PA PRIMARIES THIS TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, I think you're right that you won't see a lot of that, but I do agree with Molly that I, I definitely sense from being out there and talking to voters, that as we turn the clock from '22 to '24, I think there's going to be a new desire among voters to say, I want to hear from people who have the ability and the record to be able to bring people together, rather than divide us. I think there is a desire for that in the country.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, but George, the problem with relying on - politicians to talk about these issues is that they, they speak in soundbites. They don't speak in the everyday, ordinary language that a child might hear and understand. They speak in soundbites. They're speaking to win. They're not speaking to open the hearts and minds of a man 18 years old. What drove this child to write a 110-page manifesto where he talks about his hatred of Jews? He talks about starting a war, Jews against non-Jews, and then he talks about Black people. I'm like, have you met one? Have you talked to anybody, unlike anything you've ever seen? No. I don't want politicians to control this conversation. Chris, you know, I...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MONMOUTH POLL: ECONOMY/ABORTION TOP MIDTERM CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...you and I can have a conversation. But politicians, this has to go to everyday, our teaching, our, our, our business leaders. Yes, and the media has to understand that they cannot be complicit in this conversation. We need to have a conversation that doesn't go about the next election. It's just like on abortion. People call me, "What do you think about Democrats and abortion?" I said, before I was a Democrat, I was a human being, and why not talk about that and not just the next election? No. We need more than politicians to have this conversation.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But Donna, I think part of the argument I'm trying to make here, though, is that I think that, you know, because you've worked for plenty of us, that politicians are geared towards winning, as you said. But I, I really believe, George, that there's going to be a different desire come '24 in terms of hearing something different from people who want to pursue high office because of the violence that we're seeing, because of the division that you're talking about. People are feeling it. They're feeling it at their dinner table. They're feeling it with their own children. They're feeling it at cocktail parties, at...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ..at sporting events. They don't want to see it.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...I, I don't disagree, I don't disagree with that, but Julie, it almost seems as if we're living in a parallel universes. There's this top line of people who are concerned about this, who want to bring people together, and then there's a whole layer of communication going online. And as the Governor of New York said, it's spreading like a virus.\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE EDITOR)\par \par \uc2 I think that's completely right. I think that for everybody who wants to have a conversation about bringing this country together, we have a lot of people who want to have conversations that are fueling those divisions, and they're happening, yes, online. They also are happening in public. And I, you know, I, it's great to, to think about the prospect of a 2024 campaign that is focused around unity in this country, but I think the reality is that we will have very powerful forces, potentially the former president, who doesn't really speak that language and has enormous support from within the Republican Party. So, I think there's a long way to go before we get to that place where we might really have that national conversation that is more focused on unity.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we do have these elections coming up this week. Molly Ball, as I talked about with Rick Klein, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  playing a big in these races, especially there in Pennsylvania, which has really turned into a dog fight, on the Republican side.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY BALL (TIME MAGAZINE NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 It is amazing, and it is, it has been amazing to watch. I mean, the sheer amount of money in this race. Last I checked, I think $50 million just between the top two candidates, both of whom, of course, are very wealthy. And what we've seen from voters is they've been turned off to both of them, and so now you have Kathy Barnett running up the middle. She's not running up the middle not in the centrist sense.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MOLLY BALL (TIME MAGAZINE NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 Not in the centrist sense, be in the sense that the, you know, the, the two men beating each other up, has created this opening for her to come in, and, and no one's attacked her until very, very recently. So, you see the Republican establishment getting very nervous about whether there is time to communicate to the electorate that they don't believe that she's electable. And then, are the other two, after they've been so damaged, so beat up? And so, when we get to the general election, whoever is in that seat is going to have been, you know, the subject of a ton of negative ads from their own party. And so then how is that going to play with, with the broader electorate?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Donna Brazile, it does seem like Pennsylvania, one of the few bright spots the Democrats are pinning their hopes on...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, we got a, we got a couple of others. I hope Ohio, after the Republicans spent over $60, $75 million, I think the, the tie up in Pennsylvania has gone up to $70 million, just beating each other up. So, that's an opening for Democrats. We have an opening, I think, in North Carolina, Pennsylvania, Ohio. These are seats where there are Republican retirements, but we also have to protect the home front, Georgia, Arizona, Nevada, New Hampshire. Democrats got to stop sleeping at the wheel, okay? I'm serious. Stop waiting sleeping at the wheel. Stop waiting for the last six weeks of the election. I know we're about to spend a ton of money on television. That's like just throwing water in the wind.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Democrats got to go back and create communities and remind people what the Democratic Party is doing to help them, help them at the gas station, help them in the grocery store, help them where it matters in their wallets, in, in, in their homes. But if Democrats are not able to do that and they continue to sleep at the wheel and just rely on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , forget it. But I, I, I believe, because I trust the woman who was sitting here just a few moments ago, and that's my leader. She's going to fight like hell. And she may not be able to turn the tide of history, which is always what we worry about, and you'd worry about if you had the White House.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But you know what? You can still put up a doggone fight, and if Democrats don't put up a fight, they're going to sleep at the wheel and Republicans and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and everything else is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  facing some blowback, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , for his endorsement of Mehmet Oz there in Pennsylvania. Has the season, as you look at it so far, been a net-positive or net-negative for Trump?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think, at this point, it's been a net-neutral. You know, he, he did well in the Ohio race for Senate, lost the Ohio race for governor. He lost the Nebraska governor's race, and, and won a West Virginia House primary. So, I think it's been a net-neutral. Look, the, the Oz endorsement was a risky one for him, and if it turns out that Barnett gets the nomination as a result of that...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...I think you're going to see a lot of recrimination against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 'Cause a lot of Republicans think she's simply unelectable...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I think a lot of Republicans, and I happen to agree. I don't think she's electable. And then he just weighed into the governor's race of Pennsylvania on Friday. He looked at the polls and saw that Mastriano is ahead, and decided to endorse him against Lou Barletta, who was probably one of the most loyal Trump advocates in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, why didn't he just endorse after the votes were counted? I mean, you know, this is one where he's looking at the field and trying to say, how am I going to balance off? But the thing that they didn't think about strategically, I don't think, in Trump world, was this, Mastriano has been spending most of his time in Pennsylvania, campaigning as a partner with Barnett.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, when you endorse Mastriano, you're undercutting your endorsement to Oz.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that's one of the reasons I think you'll see Barnett do even better.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you think he thought about that, at all?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, you know, I tend to think, George, that there's not a lot of strategic thinking going on down there in Mar-a-Lago, and a lot of emotional thinking. And I said this a few weeks ago, every one of these endorsements by the president is emotional until this past Friday. I bet you he couldn't pick Mastriano out of a lineup, but he saw that's Mastriano's ahead in the polls, and he decided, you know what? I might lose this Oz thing, and so I need to have something to balance it off. But what's not going to be balanced off is the week after that in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because Brian Kemp, I believe, is going to smoke David Perdue, against all predictions from a year ago of what was going to happen, and that is going to be the biggest loss...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC)(Inaudible)...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...he can have in this primary season.\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE EDITOR)\par \par \uc2 Well, and fascinating split between Pence...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I was just going to...\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE (ASSOCIATED PRESS EXECUTIVE EDITOR)\par \par \uc2 ...who's, who's going to be campaigning with the governor down there. Pence starting to find ways to distinguish himself from Trump, as he's also looking ahead to 2024.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Molly Ball, Democrats are hoping that some, that this reaction to the draft leak Supreme Court opinion on abortion, the decision likely coming in June or, or July is going to make a difference in these midterms.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY BALL (TIME MAGAZINE NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. And, you know, we're seeing the activation already. The marches across the country that we saw this weekend, there's clearly been an activation. And, and the Democrats have to hope that that continues, because, you know, to, with all due respect, people do not feel like the Democrats are helping them. People do not feel like the Government is doing anything to solve their problems, right now. They feel like there's a weak administration in the White House, and politicians in Washington are too busy arguing to get anything done. So, so the Democrats needed something to remind, especially their base, what they believe is at stake here.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY BALL (TIME MAGAZINE NATIONAL POLITICAL ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 And, and, and so, you know, you have a lot of voters saying, "All right, you know, I, I wasn't really checked in until now." And I think women, especially, you see literally coming off their couches and going down to these protests because they have been reminded of the stakes. And so, if that continues, it, but, but, you know, the, the other thing that I think is really important about this Roe conversation is that it is unprecedented. Right? I think there's lot of people who think that, you know, well, the politics of abortion are this sort of static thing, but we've never had this situation before. It has never been the case, and it's been 50 years since this was on the ballot this way.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Molly stole the question I was going to say to you. It has not made a difference in races in the past.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, it hasn't, because we've always figured the courts were over here and our lives depended on having people who were elected right here. So, I think we now can put the dots together. Yeah, it's the same Supreme Court that has weakened voting rights. And the Supreme Court, if that draft is true and they, and it's, it's a decision that will go down the way it is, 22 states will automatically, 23 states will revert to pre-Roe, and you're going to see a massive number of women and men and young people. What I've been - amazed at is the number of young people who have been mobilized by this issue.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION MARCHES TAKE PLACE ACROSS THE COUNTRY\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We will be watching it all year long. Thank you all for a thoughtful conversation. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is all for us, today. Thanks for sharing part of your Sunday with us. Check out "World News Tonight," and I'll see you tomorrow on "GMA."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK: DOWNLOAD THE APP: ABC NEWS: BREAKING NEWS EXCLUSIVES 24/7\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220519ei5f00008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BRACING FOR A CHANGE PREPARING FOR POSSIBLE CHANGE FOR ABORTION RIGHTS\b0\par\par\uc2 862 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHORT SUPPLY, BABY FORMULA CRISIS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, DELAURO, GATES, KNUTZEN, BABY FORMULA\par \par \uc2 PATEL, FORMULA, SHORTAGE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, O'LEARY CARMONA, HAWKINS, ROE, LEAK\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, NELIA, PENTAGON, AID, DELAY\par \par \uc2 GRINER, PRICE, BOUT, WHELAN, RUSSIAN, CUSTODY\par \par \uc2 DIXON, BIDEN, WHITE, GUN, VIOLENCE, SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 CURRY, \uc2 NBA\uc2 , PLAYOFFS, WARRIORS\par \par \uc2 FOG, CLOUDS\par \par \uc2 EVEREST, HENDERSON, CLIMB, BLACK\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP OF KALUSH ORCHESTRA REHEARSING\par \par \uc2 VAUGHT, MURPHEY, STRIANSE, NURSE, SENTENCE, PATIENT\par \par \uc2 LEWIS, JACKSON, KHAWAR, CRYPTO\par \par \uc2 COOK, FEANNY, COMMENCEMENT, GALLAUDET\par \par \uc2 COSMOPOLITAN, CASINO, EMPLOYEES, WINNERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 BABY FORMULA, SHORTAGE, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, RIGHTS, PROTESTS, THOMAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLARENCE THOMAS SPEAKS OUT AFTER DRAFT LEAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "WE USED TO BE DYSFUNCTIONAL FAMILY BUT WE WERE A FAMILY"\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRACING FOR A CHANGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFFICIALS AND ADVOCATES PREPARE FOR POSSIBLE CHANGE FOR ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But now to more on the abortion debate. Multiple states across the country are ramping up for an influx of abortion patients, including Oregon, where the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  predicts they could see a 234% increase in out-of-state patients. It's one of the states where activists are already saying that, despite all their efforts, they may have to turn some patients away. Zohreen Shah has the story.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, days after the Senate failed to pass a major vote protecting abortion access ahead of a likely Roe v. Wade reversal, multiple states bracing for a dramatically different landscape for reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible) it's a lie.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Oregon, several nonprofits started working years ago to expand abortion access.\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHT ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 It's going to pave a path towards a better future here in Oregon and that's incredibly powerful.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the first law of its kind, Oregon's lawmakers approving a war chest of $15 million for abortion, some to help women from other states, including next-door Idaho, where abortion could soon be completely banned. Idaho's lieutenant governor recently calling for a special session, which would in part eliminate rape and incest from abortion rights in this interview with NBC affiliate KTVB.\par \par \uc2 LIEUTENANT GENERAL JANICE MCGEACHIN (REP) (IDAHO)\par \par \uc2 We can, once and for all, prohibit all abortions and make Idaho be the sanctuary of life that we believe that life begins at the moment of conception.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Even though her words received backlash from her own party, abortion activists bracing for many scenarios, including potential criminalization for abortions.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are you guys ramping up for a legal fight in your organizations?\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHT ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely. We know that they're not going to stop at the constitutional right being overturned. We know that they're going to start trying to target providers and people in states like ours for continuing to provide that care.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And despite efforts to expand coverage, Oregon also bracing to turn away more women.\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHT ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I don't think we ever want to tell someone no, but we only have a number of appointments.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Portland, a quarter of the Lilith Clinic's patients are from out of state, 10% from Texas. It's the state's only option for abortions closer to 22 weeks.\par \par \uc2 HEALTH CARE WORKER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 And this is where patients are seen.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This center upping security recently, often seeing abortion protesters outside.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We're out here because we believe that we want to help women in their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And they only expect to see more protesters and patients.\par \par \uc2 HEALTH CARE WORKER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We already serve patients who are traveling hundreds or thousands of miles to get here for basic health care services. And we expect that number to only increase.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Two of the women on that panel were Native American and Latina. They fear their communities will be hit very hard by a potential Roe reversal. One saying this is just one thing in a long line of things that impacts tribal families. Guys?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Zohreen Shah for us there, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 3 MINUTES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SLEEPING IN SEPARATE ROOMS\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now to our "GMA Morning Menu." Could the secret to a healthy relationship be sleeping in separate rooms? That is the debate that is taking over TikTok.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 10 MINS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And leave it to the great Tory Johnson to make life just a little bit easier. "Deals & Steals" that are all clever solutions to everyday problems.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 8 MINUTES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNITED STATES OF BBQ\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Plus, my favorite part of the morning. Coming up here, fire up that grill. We're making a pit stop on the "GMA" United States of barbecue tour. Look at that. There he is, he is back. Flames are cooking. We'll be right back with more.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220608ei5e0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2  SPEAKS OUT AFTER DRAFT LEAK SUPREME COURT JUDGE'S STATEMENT\b0\par\par\uc2 456 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHORT SUPPLY, BABY FORMULA CRISIS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, DELAURO, GATES, KNUTZEN, BABY FORMULA\par \par \uc2 PATEL, FORMULA, SHORTAGE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, O'LEARY CARMONA, HAWKINS, ROE, LEAK\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, NELIA, PENTAGON, AID, DELAY\par \par \uc2 GRINER, PRICE, BOUT, WHELAN, RUSSIAN, CUSTODY\par \par \uc2 DIXON, BIDEN, WHITE, GUN, VIOLENCE, SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 CURRY, \uc2 NBA\uc2 , PLAYOFFS, WARRIORS\par \par \uc2 FOG, CLOUDS\par \par \uc2 EVEREST, HENDERSON, CLIMB, BLACK\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP OF KALUSH ORCHESTRA REHEARSING\par \par \uc2 VAUGHT, MURPHEY, STRIANSE, NURSE, SENTENCE, PATIENT\par \par \uc2 LEWIS, JACKSON, KHAWAR, CRYPTO\par \par \uc2 COOK, FEANNY, COMMENCEMENT, GALLAUDET\par \par \uc2 COSMOPOLITAN, CASINO, EMPLOYEES, WINNERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 BABY FORMULA, SHORTAGE, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, RIGHTS, PROTESTS, THOMAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2  SPEAKS OUT AFTER DRAFT LEAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "WE USED TO BE DYSFUNCTIONAL FAMILY BUT WE WERE A FAMILY"\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the latest overnight. Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  speaking out after that Roe v. Wade draft leak. This as thousands of people are taking to the streets across the country to make their voices heard on the issue of abortion. ABC's Ike Ejiochi is in Washington, DC, where one of those protests is planned. Good morning again, Ike.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, good morning, Eva. Overnight, Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  blasting the disclosure of that leaked draft opinion that would overturn the landmark Roe versus Wade decision.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, Justice Thomas, one of the justices voting in the majority of that leaked draft, says the confidence of the court has been shaken saying, quote, "It was beyond anyone's understanding or at least anyone's imagination that someone would do that."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONWIDE PROTESTS PLANNED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TENS OF THOUSANDS EXPECTED TO RALLY FOR AND AGAINST ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, the comments come on the heels of today's Bans Off Our Bodies Rally that's expected to draw around 20,000 abortion rights protesters to Washington, DC. It's one of more than 450 marches all across the country today, from Los Angeles to New York City where nearly 40,000 people have signed up to participate. Now, if overturned, 26 states would ban or severely restrict access to abortion, affecting more than half of the people of reproductive age in this country according to Planned Parenthood. Also, expect to see anti-abortion activists out in full force today.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, the protests are expected to swarm to around 20,000 people here on this lawn with everything beginning around noon. Now, as far as security, local law enforcement says several roads and streets around the area have been blocked off and there is a heightened police presence in and around the city. Janai?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Ike, thank you for the update there from DC.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220608ei5e0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2  SPEAKS OUT AFTER DRAFT LEAK COMMENTS ON SUPREME COURT\b0\par\par\uc2 630 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHORT SUPPLY, BABY FORMULA CRISIS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, DELAURO, GATES, KNUTZEN, BABY FORMULA\par \par \uc2 PATEL, FORMULA, SHORTAGE\par \par \uc2 THOMAS, O'LEARY CARMONA, HAWKINS, ROE, LEAK\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, overnight, Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  weighed in on that leak regarding Roe v. Wade. That as thousands of people are taking to the streets across the country to make their voices heard on the issue of abortion. ABC's Ike Ejiochi is in Washington, DC where one of those protests is planned for today. Ike, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Janai. In just a few hours, tens of thousands of people will be gathering right here on this lawn for the rally, calling for abortion rights. They'll then make their way to the steps of the Supreme Court. And this morning, new comments from one of the high court's justices.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  blasting the disclosure of the high court's leak draft opinion that would overturn the landmark Roe versus Wade decision. Justice Thomas, one of the justices voting in the majority in the leaked draft, saying the court's confidence has been shaken.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE CLARENCE THOMAS (SUPREME COURT)\par \par \uc2 You begin to look over your shoulder. It's like kind of an infidelity. It was beyond anyone's understanding, or at least anyone's imagination that someone would do that.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This as thousands are expected in the nation's capital and cities across the country in protest, demanding access to safe and legal abortions.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONWIDE PROTESTS PLANNED, TENS OF THOUSANDS EXPECTED TO RALLY FOR AND AGAINST ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL O'LEARY CARMONA (EXEC DIR WOMEN'S MARCH)\par \par \uc2 We want to make sure that folks have the opportunity to make their voices heard.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 450+ MARCHES ACROSS THE COUNTRY, FROM LOS ANGELES TO NEW YORK CITY, 40,000 PEOPLE EXPECTED TO PARTICIPATE\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Over 17,000 people expected to gather in Washington, DC. There'll be more than 450 marches all across the country today, from Los Angeles to New York City where nearly 40,000 people have signed up to participate. The call to action stemming from a leaked Supreme Court draft opinion overturning the landmark Roe versus Wade decision, guaranteeing a woman's right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BAN OR SEVERELY RESTRICT ABORTION\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If overturned, 26 states would ban or severely restrict access to abortion, affecting more than half the people of reproductive age in this country, according to Planned Parenthood. Not everyone gathering Saturday will be calling for abortion rights. Antiabortion activists will also be out in force in DC and elsewhere around the country.\par \par \uc2 KRISTAN HAWKINS (PRES STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 We've survived this abortion industry, and we, we consider it a great responsibility that we will become the first generation to live in a post-Roe America.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 160 YOUNG CELEBRITIES LIKE ARIANA GRANDE AND KENDALL JENNER USING THEIR PLATFORM TO CALL ATTENTION TO ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) One-hundred-and-sixty young artists and celebrities, like Ariana Grande and Kendall Jenner, using their platform to call attention to the issue, taking out an ad in the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," supporting abortion rights ahead of today's protests.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, the protest is expected to swarm to around 20,000 people here in the District today with everything beginning around noon. Now, as far as security, local law enforcement is saying they've closed off several roads and streets around the area, not to mention a heightened police presence in and around the city. Whit.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220608ei5e00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NATIONWIDE PROTESTS OVER ROE V. WADE SUPPORTERS, OPPONENTS OF ABORTION RIGHTS GATHER IN D.C., CITIES\b0\par\par\uc2 608 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, ARREST, BROWN, GRAMAGLIA, HOCHUL, PROBE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, ARREST, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY MASS SHOOTING IN SUPERMARKET\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, ARREST, INVESTIGATION, HATE CRIME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY MASS SHOOTING IN SUPERMARKET\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS, HOLTZMAN, DIMATTIA, THOMAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONWIDE PROTESTS OVER ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Another big story that we're following, tonight, thousands of protesters across the country marching in the streets over the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Large events from coast to coast, many outraged by the leaked Supreme Court draft opinion and the strong possibility that Roe v. Wade could be overturned. Anti-abortion rights advocates holding their own counter-demonstrations. And tonight, Supreme Court Justice Thomas, what he said about the leak, and what he fears it could mean for the court.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ABC's Ike Ejiochi, from Washington, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPPORTERS, OPPONENTS OF ABORTION RIGHTS GATHER IN D.C., CITIES\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Across the country, tonight, abortion rights advocates rallying in the streets, defiant against that leaked Supreme Court draft opinion overturning Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH HOLTZMAN (FORMER MEMBER US HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES)\par \par \uc2 This is a decision that treats women as objects, as less than full human beings. It puts us back. I've been fighting for our women's rights for 50 years, and I'm not going to give up.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We won't go back.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Abortion rights supporters descending on Washington, DC for the Bans Off Our Bodies March.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The march is now mobile. Just take a look. The people are making their way up Constitution Avenue, all headed for the steps of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Close to 400 protests held in cities all over the US, according to organizers. From Philadelphia...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Bans off our bodies.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...to Austin. Back in the nation's capital...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Abortion is murder.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Supporters facing off with anti-abortion rights activists.\par \par \uc2 DANA DIMATTIA (PROTESTER)\par \par \uc2 We're here to have the pro-life voice and the Christian perspective, here. This is hard work. There's not a lot of us. You see the responses that we get.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It all comes as Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  spoke out again, overnight, denouncing the draft's leak during a conference in Dallas.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE CLARENCE THOMAS (\uc2 US SUPREME COURT\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 What happened at the court is tremendously bad. I think it's, I wonder how long we're going to have these institutions, at the rate we're undermining them.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Justice Thomas, who voted in the majority in the draft, also saying trust within the High Court had been damaged.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE CLARENCE THOMAS (\uc2 US SUPREME COURT\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 You begin to look over your shoulder. It's like kind of an infidelity. It was beyond anyone's understanding, or at least anyone's imagination, that someone would do that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If Roe is overturned, at least 26 states are certain or likely to ban or severely restrict access to abortion, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit, other than a couple instances where people were escorted from the rally, the event was peaceful, with thousands of people participating. Now, the High Court is expected to publicly rule on the case in question by the end of June. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Ike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020220516ei5e00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS SHOWDOWN SENATE SET TO VOTE ON BILL\b0\par\par\uc2 297 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW INFLATION NUMBERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SAVE MONEY ON GAS\par \par \uc2 INFLATION, WALL STREET, RECESSION, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 GAS, PRICES, DISCOUNTS, SPEEDS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, INFLATION, PRICES, DEMOCRATS, REPUBLICANS\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, WHITE, CAPTURED, CONNOLLY, WEDDING\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, PUTIN, AID, HAINES, FILCHAKOV, PELOSI\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, SENATE, VOTE, ROE V WADE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Ian Pannell, thanks. The latest now on the debate over abortion rights in the face of a looming Supreme Court decision likely to overturn Roe V Wade. Senate Democrats set to vote on a bill to insure access nationwide. Rachel Scott is at the Supreme Court. And, Rachel, the problem for Democrats, they don't have the votes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE SET TO VOTE ON BILL IN WAKE OF LEAKED SUPREME COURT DECISION\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) George, good morning. That's right, they do not have the votes. But for Democrats this is about getting every single senator on the record and showing a contrast ahead of the critical midterm elections. And so in just a few hours, the Senate will take up legislation that would protect access to abortion. It would also put an end to state restrictions like mandatory waiting periods.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) This is expected to fail. Democrats do not have the Republican support they need to get it passed. In fact, only two Republican Senators, Lisa Murkowski and \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , support abortion rights. Both of those senators told me they are a no on this legislation. They call it too broad. Collins says that she has concerns about providers that might have religious objections. The majority of Americans support upholding Roe versus Wade, but the future of that landmark decision now rests with the Supreme Court. Michael.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220525ei5b0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION VOTE SHOWDOWN SENATE BILL TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS FAILS TO PASS\b0\par\par\uc2 572 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SAFETY, \uc2 UNITED AIRLINES\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS, MANCHIN, MURRAY, SCHUMER, MCCONNELL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION VOTE SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In the meantime, to Capitol Hill at this hour and the showdown across this country over abortion. Tonight, efforts by Democrats in the Senate to begin the process of protecting Roe versus Wade with a federal law failing, Republicans blocking them, along with a Democratic senator. The Democrats knew they didn't have the numbers, but they wanted every senator on the record, on this. Rachel Scott, on the Hill, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE BILL TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS FAILS TO PASS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, Democrats forcing a vote to safeguard abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (DEM-NY) (MAJORITY LEADER)\par \par \uc2 All of us will have to answer for this vote for the rest of our time in public office.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With the Supreme Court likely to overturn Roe versus Wade, Democrats say it's just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR PATTY MURRAY\uc2  (DEM) (WASHINGTON)\par \par \uc2 They're coming after your birth control. They are coming after Plan B and IUDs. And right here in the Senate, they are talking about a federal abortion ban, a federal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  recently said it is possible Republicans could pass a federal abortion ban if they win back Congress. Today, he called the Democrats' bill radical.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (REP-KY) (MINORITY LEADER)\par \par \uc2 This bill would take us to an even more extreme and darker place.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMEN'S HEALTH PROTECTION ACT\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The bill would make abortion rights outlined in Roe versus Wade federal law. It would also override state laws banning abortion before 24 weeks and abolish state restrictions like mandatory ultrasounds and waiting periods. Those provisions convincing Democratic Senator Joe Manchin to join every single Republican in voting no.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN (DEM) (WEST VIRGINIA)\par \par \uc2 It wipes 500, 500 state laws off the books. It expands abortion. And with that, that's not where we are today.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The motion is not agreed to.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  presiding, the Democrats bill, defeated.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's get to Rachel Scott, live on the Hill, tonight. Rachel, even with Democrat Joe Manchin joining Republicans today, I know Manchin did indicate that he might support a pared-down bill which would simply make the protection of Roe versus Wade federal law. You have reported here that Republican Senators Lisa Murkowski and \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  have proposed a bill with those basic protections, as well. So, for folks keeping track of this at home, is it possible this goes anywhere? Because even they have differences, don't they?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Exactly, David. Not quite, here. The two sides do still seem to be very far apart. Republican Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  wants protections for providers who may not want to perform abortions for religious reasons. Well, Senator Joe Manchin says he does not support that. The bottom line here is, even if they did get on the same page, they would still be far short of the votes that they need to get legislation passed, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Rachel Scott, live up on the Hill, again, tonight. Thank you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220512ei5b00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS FIGHT INTENSIFIES SECURITY FOR JUSTICES BEEFED UP\b0\par\par\uc2 567 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WHITE, BRYANT, SINGLETON, FUGITIVE\par \par \uc2 WHITE, ARREST, HUNT, KEELY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FUGITIVE COUPLE CAUGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: U.S. MARSHALS ON HOW THEY TRACKED DOWN MURDER SUSPECT & EX-ECTIONS OFFICER\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, INVASION, UKRAINE, KIRBY, BIDEN, ZELENSKYY\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, DOW JONES, S&P 500, RECESSION, STOCKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STOCKS SLIDE AS GAS PRICES SOAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONAL AVERAGE HITS NEW ALL-TIME RECORD\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS, ALITO, MURKOWSKI, MCCONNELL, PSAKI\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Michael, now we have the latest on the showdown over abortion rights after the leak of that draft Supreme Court decision that would overturn Roe v. Wade, leading to tensions across the country and on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS FIGHT INTENSIFIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SECURITY FOR JUSTICES BEEFED UP IN WAKE OF LEAKED DRAFT DECISION\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This while the security around the justices is being stepped up. Our congressional correspondent Rachel Scott is tracking all of this for us. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Robin, good morning. As the future of Roe v. Wade hangs in limbo, tensions are running high across the country. The barricades and the fencing still up here around the Supreme Court, and now increased level of security around the justices.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As the battle over abortion rights rages on, protesters gathering outside of the home of Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  overnight.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Alito is a coward.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) On the heels of protests outside Justice Kavanaugh and Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 ' homes.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We will not go back.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, the \uc2 US Marshals\uc2  stepping in to protect the members of the High Court and the Senate quickly moving to pass legislation that would extend security to family members. Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  accusing demonstrators of trying to intimidate the justices.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (REP-KY) (MINORITY LEADER)\par \par \uc2 It is an attempt to replace the rule of law with the rule of mobs.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This as McConnell also indicates overturning Roe could just be the beginning, saying it's possible Republicans could pursue a federal ban of abortion if they take control of Congress. The White House sounding the alarm.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  (WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 It's a serious risk. I heard, you noted \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and other Republicans in Congress are talking about a national ban on the woman's right to choose.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATS PUSH BILL IN WAKE OF SUPREME COURT DRAFT DECISION\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In just 24 hours, Democrats will force a vote in the Senate to protect access to abortion. It's all but certain to fail. Senator Lisa Murkowski, the Republican who said she would consider the legislation, now says she's a no.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR LISA MURKOWSKI (REP) (ALASKA)\par \par \uc2 Nothing's going to change. The vote's going to be the same.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILL BAN OR SEVERELY RESTRICT ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATES DRAW BATTLE LINES IN WAKE OF LEAKED SUPREME COURT DRAFT\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If Roe versus Wade is overturned, as that leaked draft suggested, 26 states would ban or severely restrict access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Democratic-run states like Connecticut and New York are moving to set up state funds to help out-of-state patients, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Rachel, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220525ei5a00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS FIGHT HEATS UP NEW PROTESTS NATIONWIDE AS DEMOCRATS PUSH BILL\b0\par\par\uc2 444 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PUTIN'S MASSIVE MILITARY PARADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST LADY'S SURPRISE VISIT\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, PARADE, UKRAINE, ASSAULT, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, BIDEN, \uc2 G7\uc2 , ALLIES, ZELENSKYY, SANCTIONS\par \par \uc2 GAS, PRICES, DIESEL, ECONOMY, TRUCKERS\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, EVACUATIONS, MORSE, WINDS, WRIGHT\par \par \uc2 FIRE, WARNINGS, DRY, HUMIDITY, WINDS, TEMPS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, SUPREME COURT, PROTESTS, DEMS, GOP\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Ginger, thanks very much. We're gonna get the latest now on the battle over abortion rights, after the leak of draft Supreme Court opinion that would overturn Roe V Wade. Congressional Democrats set to take action this week in an attempt to preserve abortion rights. And our congressional correspondent Rachel Scott is tracking it all. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS FIGHT HEATS UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW PROTESTS NATIONWIDE AS DEMOCRATS PUSH BILL ON CAPITOL HILL\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) George, good morning. This is shaping up to be a critical week in the battle over abortion rights. And what Democrats are calling the fight of a generation. Over the weekend, thousands of people took to the streets, rallying to send a message to the conservative leaning justices on the bench. Some gathering outside of the home of Justice Brent Kavanaugh, and Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Those demonstrators, part of the majority of Americans who believe Roe versus Wade should be upheld. But across the country if Roe is overturned, at least 26 states would either ban abortion or severely restrict access to it. And leaders in those states say it would be a long time coming, George.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS FIGHT HEATS UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATS PUSH BILL IN WAKE OF LEAKED SUPREME COURT DRAFT DECISION\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Rachel, given the divisions on Capitol Hill, this move by the Democrats to pass a national law to protect abortion rights is largely symbolic?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right, George. Senate Democrats in just 72 hours will force a vote on legislation that would protect access to abortion for millions of women in the country, but it is almost certain to fail. Republicans appear united against this. But this is about getting every single senator on the record on where they stand on this issue.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said that if Republicans do take control of Congress this midterm election, it is possible that his party could pursue a national ban on abortion. And now Democrats are hoping to use his words as a rallying cry this midterm election. George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott, thanks very much. Robin.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220524ei5900007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BATTLE OVER ABORTION MCCONNELL: NATIONAL BAN 'POSSIBLE' IF ROE V. WADE OVERTURNED\b0\par\par\uc2 673 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ARREST, SINGLETON, INVESTIGATION, WHITE, MANHUNT\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, WU, SHOVE, CASES, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RISING COVID INFECTIONS\par \par \uc2 BAHAMAS, PHILLIPS, INVESTIGATION, CHIARELLA, RAINBOLT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: U.S. TOURISTS DIE AT RESORT\par \par \uc2 INVASION, UKRAINE, RUSSIA, PUTIN, ZELENSKYY\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS, MCCONNELL, PSAKI, LIGHTFOOT, PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLE OVER ABORTION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back here at home, now, and to the showdown over abortion across this country. In fact, protesters outside the homes of Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 . And, tonight, what the Republican leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  in the Senate is now saying about the possibility of a federal abortion ban if Roe versus Wade is overturned. Here's our senior White House correspondent, Mary Bruce, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MCCONNELL: NATIONAL BAN 'POSSIBLE' IF ROE V. WADE OVERTURNED\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We will not go back. We will not go back.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) After a weekend when thousands took to the streets rallying for abortion rights, some gathering outside the homes of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . Tonight, security around the justices is stepping up.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 US Marshals\uc2  now involved in protecting the members of the court after that leaked draft opinion indicated they're preparing to overturn Roe v. Wade. It comes as the top Republican in the Senate, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , is indicating overturning Roe could be just the beginning, that if Republicans gain control of Congress, they could pass a federal ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXCERPT FROM SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL'S STATEMENT\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) McConnell saying, "If the leaked opinion became the final opinion, legislative bodies, not only at the state level but at federal level, certainly could legislate in that area." Adding, "I don't think it's much secret where Senate Republicans stand on that issue." Today, we took that to the White House.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How at-risk does the administration believe the US is to completely outlawing abortions?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  (WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 I think we're at serious risk. I heard, you noted \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and other Republicans in Congress are talking about a national ban on a woman's right to choose.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The White House warning Republicans could go try to go even further, possibly banning some forms of contraception.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CERTAIN OR LIKELY TO BAN ABORTION\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If Roe is overturned, 26 states would quickly move to severely restrict access to abortion, or ban it outright.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRIGGER LAWS BAN ABORTION IF ROE V. WADE OVERTURNED\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Thirteen have already passed so-called trigger laws that would ban the procedure the moment Roe is struck down. Now, a number of Democrat-led states like New York and Connecticut are exploring ways to assist women seeking help. And today, Chicago's mayor announced funding to help women from other states who cross into Illinois to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LORI LIGHTFOOT (DEM) (CHICAGO)\par \par \uc2 These dollars are going to support transportation, lodging, follow-up care and, if necessary, the safe and legal procedure of abortion.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Mary. Senate Democrats, I know, are scheduling this vote Wednesday on a bill to make a woman's right to an abortion a federal law. Obviously, they know the reality here that they don't have the votes. So, they want every senator on record on this issue, instead?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. This is purely symbolic, David. Tonight, the lone undecided Republican, Lisa Murkowski, just announced she is a no. Democrats know this vote isn't going anywhere, but what it will do is force every Republican to go on the record as Democrats and the President are preparing to make abortion rights a key issue in the upcoming midterm elections. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mary Bruce, live at the White House. Thank you, Mary.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020220510ei5900006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BRACING FOR POSSIBLE ROE V. WADE OVERTURN HOW STATES & LAWMAKERS ARE PREPARING\b0\par\par\uc2 586 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, INVASION, UKRAINE, BIDEN, BURNS, ZELENSKYY\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, G7 SUMMIT, UKRAINE, AID, RUSSIA, SANCTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS, SECURITY, PROTESTS, CHURCHES\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS, RUSSIA, UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's bring in ABC's Martha Raddatz who'll be hosting "This Week" later on this morning. Martha, good morning, always great to speak with you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRACING FOR POSSIBLE ROE V. WADE OVERTURN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW STATES & LAWMAKERS ARE PREPARING FOR WHAT'S TO COME\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, let's start there with that leaked draft opinion from the Supreme Court on Roe v. Wade. You have some big interviews on the show today, including Senator Amy Klobuchar and Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson. How are lawmakers and states preparing for what is expected to be an historic ruling?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit, in Arkansas, a ban will take place immediately if Roe is officially overturned. Arkansas is a trigger law state, one of 13 with no exceptions except for the life of the mother. No real plans in place for what happens if a woman needs an abortion there. And abortion providers in Arkansas could face 10 years in prison. And as for abortion rights supporters, they'll redouble efforts to get state laws passed that help women who wants an abortion and they will also concentrate on elections, on midterms and beyond to get money and support to abortion rights supporters.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All sides bracing for what's to come.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POSSIBLE PLANS FOR "VICTORY DAY"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PUTIN REPORTEDLY AIMS TO TAKE CONTROL AHEAD OF "VICTORY DAY"\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I do want to turn to the war in Ukraine. We noted earlier the heightened concerns ahead of Russia's Victory Day celebration. How worried are officials about what could happen in the next 48 hours?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit, I think they just don't know what Putin will do. He certainly wants to declare some kind of victory, but that will be pretty hard to do given what's going on in Ukraine. Or will he declare full-scale war or make claims on some part of Ukraine he doesn't currently occupy? Remember, Russians don't know the full extent of what is going on in Ukraine. And we don't know what exactly what Putin will do tomorrow or say to try to convince his people this fight is worth it, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Martha, thank you. Before I let you go, I do want to wish you a very happy Mother's Day to you as well.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) One of the many supermoms here we're blessed to work with at ABC News. Thank you. And be sure to tune into "This Week" later on this morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXCLUSIVE THIS MORNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM), MINNEOSTA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK WITH GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Again, Martha has exclusive interviews with Senator Amy Klobuchar and Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson and the future of abortion rights in the US.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (REP), ARKANSAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DR. ASHISH JHA, WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 RESPONSE COORDINATOR\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Plus, White House COVID response coordinator Dr. Ashish Jha discusses the grim forecast for 100 million new COVID cases later this year. Eva?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220523ei580005p\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HEIGHTENED SECURITY AT CATHOLIC CHURCHES ACROSS THE COUNTRY OFFICIALS ON ALERT AFTER CALLS FOR ABORTION RIGHTS PROTESTS\b0\par\par\uc2 584 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, INVASION, UKRAINE, BIDEN, BURNS, ZELENSKYY\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, G7 SUMMIT, UKRAINE, AID, RUSSIA, SANCTION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION RIGHTS, SECURITY, PROTESTS, CHURCHES\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the battle over abortion rights on this Mother's Day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEIGHTENED SECURITY AT CATHOLIC CHURCHES ACROSS THE COUNTRY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFFICIALS ON ALERT AFTER CALLS FOR MOTHER'S DAY ABORTION RIGHT PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Catholic churches in the US on heightened security alert due to calls of protests by abortion rights supporters. ABC's Faith Abubey joins us now from Washington. Faith, good morning to you. And all of this comes after that Supreme Court leak just days ago.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, good morning to you, Janai. And that leaked Supreme Court draft opinion has set off a firestorm among abortion rights advocates who are now concerned that the constitutional rights to an abortion could be overturned. And so, today, some of those activists are planning to take their fight directly in front or inside Catholic churches like this one.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But take a look at this video overnight, protesters gathered outside the homes of Justices Kavanaugh and John Roberts chanting pro-abortion messages.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRACING FOR POSSIBLE ROE V. WADE OVERTURN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROTESTORS TAKE TO THE STREETS OUTSIDE JUSTICE KAVANAUGH'S HOME\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Law enforcement agents across the country say they are aware and they're concerned about the calls for demonstrations from multiple groups. One of the groups explaining on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that they are calling for the Mother's Day abortion rights strike in and outside of Catholic churches because, quote, "six extremist Catholics set out to overturn Roe." Now, we've already seen some of the protests from other groups in a form of vandalism. A day after that bombshell Supreme Court draft opinion leaked, demonstrators defaced the walls, the doors and statues at Sacred Heart of Mary Church in Boulder, Colorado. The sheriff's office says they left graffiti with what he described as anti-church, pro-choice messaging and broke some glass there at that church.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In New York, police officers have been ordered to be on heightened awareness around Catholic churches. Some of the churches say they are working with police to protect themselves against any violence this weekend. But this morning, a Virginia attorney general is sending a clear warning to these protesters, reminding them that they could face legal consequences for obstructing, intimidating or even interfering with religious worship.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE THAN HALF OF U.S. CATHOLICS FAVOR LEGALIZED ABORTION\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, public opinion polling there's a disconnect between what people think Catholics believe and what many actually believe. A 2019 Pew survey reveals that even though Catholic teachings ban abortion or actually oppose abortion, more than half of US Catholics, 56% of them, said abortion should be legal in all or most cases.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, in the meantime, amid this protest, we're hearing from Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  for the first time. According to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," he told a group of lawyers and judges at a judicial conference that institutions cannot be bullied to give people the outcomes that they want. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Those comments getting a lot of attention. All right, Faith Abubey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220523ei580005o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 3,196 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 TOTENBERG, ABORTION, SCOTUS, DRAFT, TRIGGER, LAWS\par \par \uc2 ROE, ACTIVISM, DANNENFELSER, TIMMARAJU, MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR, ABORTION, DEMS, SCOTUS, ABC/WAPO, POLL\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON, ARKANSAS, ABORTION, TRIGGER, LAWS, BAN\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ZELENSKYY, UK, RU, WAR, INTEL, PUTIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 WAR, UK, RU, VICTORY DAY, PUTIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUSSIAN, UKRAINIAN FORCES CLASH BEFORE KEY HOLIDAY\par \par \uc2 JHA, COVID, FUNDING, BOOSTER\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BIDEN, HARRIS, MARRIAGE, TRUMP, \uc2 SCOTUS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 JD VANCE (REP-OH) (SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I have absolutely got to thank the 45th, the President of the United states, Donald J. Trump. This campaign I really think was a referendum on what kind of a Republican Party we want and what kind of a country we want.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 TIM RYAN\uc2  (DEM-OH) (SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I'm not going to win on Election Day and try to punish 50 percent of the people that are living in this state. We are here to come together, and I want Ohio to be the economic powerhouse of not just the United States, but of the world.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tuesday's Ohio Senate primary winners, Republican JD Vance and Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , are set to face off for Ohio's all-important seat, this fall. Here to discuss that and much more, the CEO of \uc2 Democracy for America\uc2 , Yvette Simpson, "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " national political correspondent, Alex Burns, and author of the new book "This Will Not Pass," ABC congressional correspondent Rachel Scott, and \uc2 Sarah Isgur\uc2 , a veteran of the Trump Justice Department, now an ABC News analyst. Welcome to all of you. And Rachel, I want to start with you, because you've been on the ground, this week, talking to women across the country on both sides of this issue. What, what are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, I think something that really struck out to me, Martha, was an abortion provider who told me people that are suddenly outraged by this haven't been paying attention. Because, as you have noted, 26 states will likely either outlaw abortion altogether, or severely restrict access to it. And what we have seen is that these bans won't necessarily stop abortion. Women will go through whatever hurdle necessary in order to have a choice. Our team was down in Wichita, Kansas, speaking with a woman that had to wait 37 days to get an appointment for an abortion. She was traveling from the state of Texas, which has now banned abortion as early as six weeks into a woman's pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oklahoma, a state that used to be a safe haven for women traveling from places like Texas, no longer will be, because abortions have now all but stopped in that state. That is the next domino to fall, and women in that state will now have to go elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Alex, Republicans in Congress haven't really said much about this yet, but there are all sorts of proposals at the state level, including in Louisiana, where Republicans have advanced a bill to make abortion a crime, homicide. Is, is there a risk for Republicans to really just be pushing this too far?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOUISIANA ADVANCES BILL MAKING ABORTION A HOMICIDE\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (NYT NAT'L POLITICAL /CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 I think there's a considerable risk for Republicans, Martha, particularly when you're looking at the kind of voters that the party has made inroads with over the last two years, ever since \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left office. You're looking at moderates. You're looking at a lot of suburban women who are upset about crime and inflation and the state of American education. These are not hardline anti-abortion voters. And, you know, one of the things that we write about in our book is the degree to which congressional Republicans have been kind of adrift, as far as what they actually stand for, that they're this party that is tethered to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and not a whole lot else, ideologically.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (NYT NAT'L POLITICAL /CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 If Roe is overturned, that is going to be a very, very significant test in the Republican Party in the states and in Washington. What do they actually believe federal policy ought to be as far as regulating a women's right to medically terminate her pregnancy?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Sarah, let's take that to you. You predicted Roe would be overturned after the oral arguments before the court, but you also predicted last year that the Republicans would experience a political backlash.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, it's fascinating. In the wake of the Texas SB-8 bill going into effect, I absolutely thought that Republicans would see political consequences to that, nationally, as well. And instead, that Texas bill, abortion as an issue, was really the dog that didn't bite. In Virginia, \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2 , the Democratic candidate, ran with abortion as a central issue in that campaign, and it didn't really seem to move the numbers at all, I think, in part, because with 50 years of this as a top-tier cultural issue, people have largely sorted themselves by party.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, the only way that this issue can make a difference is if people change their vote from one party to the other, I think that's already happened, largely, or turnout. Now, we are seeing young people fall away from the - from President Biden approval, numbers-wise. It's possible you'll see that. But when it comes to Latino and Black Democratic voters who you're also seeing that dip with, this is not an issue that Democrats want to campaign on with them. They tend to be to the right of the median Democratic white voter.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Yvette, so, so what do Democrats do?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, I think, first, we've got to bring it home. I think there's one thing between policy and there's a different thing about practice. So, you know, you don't like abortion, but when a woman has to be tracked, right, from one state to another, are we going to tell women that they can't cross state lines when they're pregnant, because they might be doing it to get an abortion, or do women have to report to their doctor as soon as they get pregnant, and does the government have the ability to regulate them from that point forward? We really have to bring it home and tell people how this will affect everyday women, child-bearing-aged women.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think about "The Handmaids Tale." I'm horrified at the idea that my daughters who live in different states might have different rules over their privacy and whether they want to get or not. The government might be able to decide whether they have to carry a child and be an incubator for a child. Forced labor? Are we going to actually require woman carry a child that they don't want to? So, we've got bring it, bring it, I think, to the forefront, because we've been talking about this theoretically, right? People don't like the idea of it, but practically in order to enforce it, states will have to do a lot of things that will make people very uncomfortable, especially women.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Rachel, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said the Senate will vote on Wednesday on a bill that would codify Roe. And there's just really no chance that that's going to happen. So, just - on that, what do you see Democrats doing? Does it motivate them? Does it bring people, Democrats forward who may not have voted in the past, as, as Sarah's talking about?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It doesn't seem like this issue and the leak is going to change any minds, here. We saw this same, exact piece of legislation try to come through the Floor of the Senate earlier this year, and it failed. I mean, the numbers have not changed. Democrats need 60 votes, the support of at least 10 Republicans. They don't even have 50, because Senator Manchin does not support it. Bob Casey, another Democrat, also does not support it. And so, this is going to be a massive hurdle, and it shows just how limited Democrats are, here. And so, you heard Senator Amy Klobuchar tell you that if it does not actually move forward, the next stop is the ballot box, and they're hoping that this is the rallying cry for their voters.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  hasn't really mentioned abortion at all until this week.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (NYT NAT'L POLITICAL /CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 Look, I think it's one of the, actually, significant challenges facing Democrats. And facing the abortion rights movement generally, is that, who is the singular standard bearer on the Democratic side, the most persuasive messenger talking about abortion rights? President Biden has had a pretty ambivalent with abortion rights over the course of his political career. His policy, you know, the official policy stance of his administration is unambiguously supportive. But he's a deeply, deeply religious Catholic, who, at other points in his career, has expressed real reservations about a woman's right to choose.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (NYT NAT'L POLITICAL /CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 So, I think Democrats are going to be looking for other people to carry that message in a more personal and a more authentic way. I think it's notable that Vice President Harris was out there over the last week. Because, you know, again, one of things that we depict in our book about this administration is she has had some real reservations about, you know, not wanting to be seen a vice president, a running mate, who speaks only to constituencies that look like her. Well, this is a really big issue for constituencies that look like her. And last week, it sure sounded like she was pretty comfortable carrying that message.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Yvette, do you think if Roe is reversed, the court could go further on taking back same-sex marriage?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think that...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Does this really open that up?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...I think they're signaling that, right? Like, in the, in the draft opinion, Alito specifically says he's not doing that, right? And he can't, right, 'cause the, the question before him is abortion. But he does open up in this question about the due process clause, that there are these previously given rights that are not deeply rooted or long held, like interracial marriage, like contraception, like gay marriage that could be right under the same analysis, if the court were to get that question. And so, that's another reason that Democrats need to be sounding the alarm.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because if you're not a woman and this what is not your issue, what is your issue and they may be coming for you next? We really need to make sure that we understand that in a broad spectrum, particularly the South, this is going to be an opportunity for them to tee that up, because we've already seen it with banning books, "Don't Say Gay," and other efforts to, I, I, think just roll back the clocks 50 years, giving states the ability to do this will make us, I think, more the Divided States of America than the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, Rachel, the, the person we have not heard from much about this is from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He's really been - uncharacteristically quiet. He did bring it up briefly at his rally the other night. What, what's going on, there?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP, GOP LARGELY IGNORE DRAFT ABORTION RULING\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. He had a rally in Pennsylvania. And look, the former president has never been shy to tout, or to take a victory...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And he got those justices in there.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly, never been shy to take a victory lap, but mum was the word, didn't really say much at all around this issue. A spokesperson says that he's waiting to see the final decision sort of come down and took sort of the same approach that we saw a lot of other Republicans take, which is that they're focusing less on the substance of the leak and more on the fact that it was leaked itself, that this document, this opinion actually came out and blasting that. But make no mistake about it, this will be Trump's legacy. This is part of his legacy, how he has cemented not only the federal benches across the country, but also the conservative-leaning Supreme Court for generations to come.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Sarah, you clerked on the 5th Circuit, you worked at the Justice Department. When you look at this leak, what would the motivation be to you, from, from someone on the left, from someone on the right? I know Nina Totenberg explained it, a bit. Is that what you agree with?\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, there's several theories of this. On the liberal side, it's the idea of warning the country, trying to see if you can intimidate justices into not wanting to destroy the institution. We talk about this as a six-three court, but it's really a three-three-three court, with the three conservatives in the middle differing from their three conservatives in the institution and wanting to protect that. We've seen Chief Justice Roberts break many times on, along that axis. On the conservative side, I think Nina Totenberg is right about that theory. I might differ a little bit.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think it's actually, it would be a concern that that draft opinion would not be the majority, that they're aren't four, a fifth vote on that, and that you would have a Gorsuch, or even Kavanaugh opinion, with a much narrower, still overturning Roe and Casey, but more focused on the, the state constitutional issues. But, of course, there are other options, here, a clerk who is dating someone who goes through their bag at night and the clerk doesn't even know that that's how opinion got leaked. There are other issues of how this leak happened. But the main issue is that it will so deeply change the judiciary branch, not just the Supreme Court, how clerks interact with their justices.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And what we've seen in the Legislative Branch and the Executive Branch, as things like this happen more and more, you end up isolating the decisionmaker from people with differing opinions based on trust, based on this likelihood, and it's bad decision-making that we get when a president can't talk to all his advisers, when a legislator is concerned about his staff. We don't want that happening at the judiciary. And that's why I'm so upset about this leak.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (NYT NAT'L POLITICAL /CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 (inaudible), I just wanted to...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So many people are upset. Please, please.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (NYT NAT'L POLITICAL /CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 ...I - I'm, I totally understand that, that perspective from sort of the legal angle. I, I do think that it's, it's sort of high time the American people had more visibility into how the judiciary actually works. And whatever you think of this sort of draft opinion, specifically whatever you think of the Roe issue, writ large, I, I think it's really hard for me, as a reporter, to sort of look at the back and forth this week of all the pearl clutching about the leak, and think that somehow the American people would be better off if they knew a whole lot less...\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Transparency...\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (NYT NAT'L POLITICAL /CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 ...about this institution that is extraordinarily powerful and hugely under scrutinized for the vast majority of Americans.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS LEAK INCREASES CALLS TO REFORM COURT\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...transparency in outcome is different than transparency in process. In our government, we need extreme transparency in outcome.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (NYT NAT'L POLITICAL /CO-AUTHOR 'THIS WILL NOT PASS')\par \par \uc2 I believe in both. I'm, I'm in favor of both.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But transparency in process has broken our Legislative Branch. It has hurt our Executive Branch, and now we're going to see what happens in the judiciary.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and I'm going to turn here to Rachel, because we want to get in on the midterms, a little bit, here, and what's going on. You were in Ohio. JD Vance, what happened there? What's happening next? It really does show the power of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , as well. Every candidate he endorsed won.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JD VANCE, TIM RYAN TO FACE OFF FOR OHIO SENATE SEAT\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. The first major test of Trump as a sort of kingmaker, right? I mean, this is, JD Vance, he was down in the polls weeks before the primary. He called Trump an idiot, reprehensible. He not only got his endorsement. He actually ended up sealing the deal. He actually ended up winning. You had several voters that I talked to on the ground that were swayed by that endorsement. They said, well, if Trump can forgive, well, well, so can I. But I think more so than anything, this shows the impact that Trump is having on the Republican Party. This was a primary race that was very crowded. Everybody was working to out-Trump one another. And only one candidate in that crowded field said it was time for their party to move on from the false claims about the 2020 election. That shows something.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He is not going away. But we got to go away, right now. Sorry about that. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that's all for us, today. Thanks for sharing part of your Sunday with us. Check out "World News Tonight," and have a happy Mother's Day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK: DOWNLOAD THE APP: ABC NEWS: BREAKING NEWS EXCLUSIVES 24/7\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220511ei5800009\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON ARK 'TRIGGER LAW' WOULD BAN NEARLY ALL ABORTIONS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,034 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 TOTENBERG, ABORTION, SCOTUS, DRAFT, TRIGGER, LAWS\par \par \uc2 ROE, ACTIVISM, DANNENFELSER, TIMMARAJU, MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR, ABORTION, DEMS, SCOTUS, ABC/WAPO, POLL\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON, ARKANSAS, ABORTION, TRIGGER, LAWS, BAN\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's bring in Arkansas' Republican governor, \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 , chair of the \uc2 National Governors Association\uc2 . Good to be with you this morning, Governor. If, as expected, Roe is overturned, the bill you signed last year banning abortion in your state would go into effect immediately, as soon as your state attorney general signs it. The only exception in that bill is the life of the mother. What would you tell those women in your state who cannot afford to travel to get an abortion, who cannot afford to raise a child, or those who have been raped, or the victims of incest?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARKANSAS 'TRIGGER LAW' WOULD BAN NEARLY ALL ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON (REP-AR) (CHAIR NATIONAL GOVERNORS ASSOCIATION)\par \par \uc2 Well, thank you, Martha, and Happy Mother's Day to everyone. And in terms of Arkansas law, our law simply expresses the will of the people of Arkansas. Senator Klobuchar really didn't address that, and the fact is that each state has differing views on the, where should we have on abortion restrictions. In Arkansas, it's a policy of Arkansas that we protect the life of the unborn. And so, yes, if Roe versus Wade is reversed, then we would have the trigger law in place to protect the life of the unborn. This is important to - go after and discuss this issue with conviction. And it's divided our country, and that division has been going on for decades, and it will continue after whatever decision the Supreme Court renders.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Governor, I, I want to go back to my question about those women. What would you say to those women who seek an abortion, who don't have the money to travel, who don't have the money to raise a child? What would you say to them?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON (REP-AR) (CHAIR NATIONAL GOVERNORS ASSOCIATION)\par \par \uc2 Well, first of all, again, that's where your heart goes out to them. I've had to deal with those very difficult circumstances of, of rape and incest, as governor, and it's difficult. And so, you have to understand that. You have provide services, and I believe we want to increase the services for maternal health, to increase the services for adoption services, as well. And so, we want to invest in those areas that will help those women with very difficult circumstances of the pregnancy. But secondly, I think to your point, the rape and incest exceptions will continue to be a part of the debate. Right now, we do not have rape and incest as exceptions under the Arkansas trigger law, but there's, I think that will be a part of the debate. I've always expressed that those exceptions are important.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Would you like to see those exceptions?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON (REP-AR) (CHAIR NATIONAL GOVERNORS ASSOCIATION)\par \par \uc2 Yes, I expressed, whenever I signed the law that I would prefer the rape and incest exceptions to be in there. And even though we have the trigger law, I expect those exceptions to be a significant part of the debate in the future, even though we're going to immediately go to restrict abortions in the exceptions, with exception of the life of the mother in danger.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why do you support those exceptions?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IAN PANNELL ON MARIUPOL'S LAST HOLDOUT AGAINST RUSSIAN OCCUPATION AND THE US INTELLIGENCE ASSISTANCE TO UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON (REP-AR) (CHAIR NATIONAL GOVERNORS ASSOCIATION)\par \par \uc2 Well, it's, it's, it's been a, even though we believe life begins at conception, that we try to save lives, the life of the unborn. And to do that and to gain public acceptance, those exceptions are what generally the public has insisted upon as being reasonable exceptions to abortion limitations. And, and so, I, you know, there's a, the principle involved of life begins at - of, at conception, difficult circumstances, but those are accommodations that are made to get public acceptance of the limitations and saving more - unborn lives, and that's the objective of it.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and governor, you tweeted that you hope the court returns authority to the states. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  told USA Today it's possible Republicans would pursue a national ban. Would you oppose that?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOME IN GOP PUSH FOR NATIONAL ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON (REP-AR) (CHAIR NATIONAL GOVERNORS ASSOCIATION)\par \par \uc2 I think that's inconsistent with what we've been fighting for, for four decades, which is that we wanted the Roe versus Wade reversed and the authority return to the states. And I, and so, as a matter or principle, that's where it should be, if you look at a constitutional or a national standard that goes against the, that thrust of the states having prerogative. And secondly, I think there's some constitutional issues of a national standard, as well, as to what is the authority under the Constitution to enact that.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON (REP-AR) (CHAIR NATIONAL GOVERNORS ASSOCIATION)\par \par \uc2 And Martha, there's a fundamental point, because everybody describes this as right. If the court reverses Roe versus Wade, they're saying that the Constitution does not provide that, which returns it to the states, and to me, that makes sense under the Constitution. And that's where the vigorous debate is going to be. That is where we're going to face a lot of concerns on the compassion side, and states are going to make different determinations of it. But the people are going to express through their representatives exactly the direction they want to go, and to me, that makes sense, and I think it makes sense under our Constitution.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Thanks so much for joining us this morning, governor. We'll be back, in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220511ei5800005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FIRST LADY J. BIDEN MAKES UNANNOUNCED VISIT TO UK US ACKNOWLEDGES PASSING MILITARY INTEL TO UKRAINE\b0\par\par\uc2 919 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 TOTENBERG, ABORTION, SCOTUS, DRAFT, TRIGGER, LAWS\par \par \uc2 ROE, ACTIVISM, DANNENFELSER, TIMMARAJU, MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR, ABORTION, DEMS, SCOTUS, ABC/WAPO, POLL\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON, ARKANSAS, ABORTION, TRIGGER, LAWS, BAN\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ZELENSKYY, UK, RU, WAR, INTEL, PUTIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY (PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 The United States provides battlefield intelligence to help Ukrainians defend their country. We do not provide intelligence on the location of senior military leaders on the battlefield or participate in the targeting decisions of the Ukrainian military. Ukraine combines information that we and other partners provide with the intelligence that they themselves are gathering on the battlefield, and then they make their own decisions and they take their own actions.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Pentagon Press Secretary John Kirby after several leaks this week about the US sharing intelligence with Ukraine, which may have helped the Ukrainian military kill Russian generals and sink the flagship Moskva last month. It comes amid fears that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  could use tomorrow's Victory Day holiday to expand Russia's offensive. Our senior foreign correspondent, Ian Pannell, is just outside Kharkiv in eastern Ukraine, tracking the very latest. Good morning, Ian.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAP OF UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST LADY JILL BIDEN MAKES UNANNOUNCED VISIT TO UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Martha, good morning from the small village of Mala Rogan. We're only about 20 miles from the Russian border. We're going to hear the sounds of the battle still going in the distance. But the Ukrainians have retaken this land, which was occupied by Russian forces. You can see, this is a Russian tank behind me. The letter Z is clearly sprayed on the side. We're out with war crimes investigators. They're not just investigating what happened here, but elsewhere in the country, as we're getting news of that school that was sheltering up to 90 people being directly bombed again by the Russians. And if that's true, that again, of course, would be a war crime.\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, in a surprise and highly secretive move, the First Lady, Jill Biden, crossing over the border into Ukraine, meeting with the First Lady, Olena Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 FIRST LADY JILL BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 That is important to hear the Ukrainian people. People of the United States stand with the people of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WAR IN UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: US ACKNOWLEDGES PASSING MILITARY INTEL TO UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This week, emboldened with weapons and intelligence from the West, a show of force and resistance from Ukraine, attempting to thwart any success by Russia. For the first time this week, the US acknowledging the role its intelligence has played in the war, calling it legitimate and lawful.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY (PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 We provide them what we believe to be relevant and timely information about Russian units that will allow them to adjust and execute their - their self-defense, to the best of their ability.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUSSIAN FORCES SURROUND MARIUPOL STEEL MILL\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It also emerged this week that it helped identify Russian mobile command centers, the Ukrainians combing it with their own intelligence to kill as many as 12 Russian generals, but the publicity now causing concerns with the administration. Biden telling his intelligent heads to plug up the leaks, worried that it could lead to a direct conflict with Russia. With Victory Day looming in Russia, the Kremlin desperate to demonstrate success on the battlefield, the fight for Mariupol now at a climax. Video circulating online showed Russian forces surrounding the city's last holdout, pummeling the Azovstal steel mill in an effort to capture the strategic port. With its land invasion repeatedly stalling, the Kremlin escalated aerial attacks, this week.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUSSIAN STRIKES POUND CIVILIAN AREAS IN EASTERN UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Online videos like these show Russia targeting railroad stations, bridges and other supply routes. But Russia's imprecise targeting and disregard for the rules of war mean many of Russia's so-called legitimate targets this week have again been civilian areas, a devastating strike at a bus stop in Donetsk and the destruction of an amusement park here in Kharkiv.\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) There was a beer garden, a photo booth, even a child's playground. Luckily, there was no one playing here at the time, but it just tells you everything you need to know about whether or not the Russians are actually targeting anything or not. These are civilian sites that are being repeatedly hit across this city.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: G-7 LEADERS TO MEET WITH ZELENSKYY IN SHOW OF UNITY\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) President Biden's set to meet virtually with President Zelenskyy during a meeting of \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders ahead of tomorrow's Russian Victory Day holiday, seen as a key date for Putin, as he desperately looks to showcase success in the war he's struggling to win.\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Martha, tomorrow is May the 9th, which is Victory Day in Russia. It commemorates the 77th anniversary of the victory of the Soviet forces against the Nazis, and there's a real sense of dread, here, about what could happen in the country in terms of bombardments, and also what \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  may well announce in terms of doubling down on this war. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Ian Pannell.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220511ei5800006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA WHITE HOUSE WARNS OF FALL, WINTER COVID SURGE\b0\par\par\uc2 907 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 TOTENBERG, ABORTION, SCOTUS, DRAFT, TRIGGER, LAWS\par \par \uc2 ROE, ACTIVISM, DANNENFELSER, TIMMARAJU, MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR, ABORTION, DEMS, SCOTUS, ABC/WAPO, POLL\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON, ARKANSAS, ABORTION, TRIGGER, LAWS, BAN\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ZELENSKYY, UK, RU, WAR, INTEL, PUTIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 WAR, UK, RU, VICTORY DAY, PUTIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUSSIAN, UKRAINIAN FORCES CLASH BEFORE KEY HOLIDAY\par \par \uc2 JHA, COVID, FUNDING, BOOSTER\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The White House is sounding the alarm about a fall and winter surge. Here to discuss is the White House COVID-19 response coordinator, Dr. \uc2 Ashish Jha\uc2 . Pardon my allergies there, Dr. Jha. I want to start out by talking about this possible surge. You say as many as a hundred million people could be affected.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID-19: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHITE HOUSE WARNS OF FALL, WINTER COVID SURGE\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR ASHISH JHA (WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 RESPONSE COORDINATOR)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, good morning, Martha. Thanks for having me, here. You know, if you take a step back and look at the last two winters, we've had relatively large surges of infections. What - we're looking at a range of models, both internal and external models, and what they're predicting is that if we don't get ahead of this thing, we're going to have a lot of waning immunity, this virus continues to evolve, and we may see a pretty sizable wave of infections, hospitalizations and deaths this fall and winter. Whether that happens or not is largely up to us, as a country.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR ASHISH JHA (WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 RESPONSE COORDINATOR)\par \par \uc2 If we can prepare, and if we can act, we can prevent that. But we're going to need Congress' help, that's one of the key messages, here, is we need the resources to fight that battle so we don't have that kind of a fall or winter.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW COVID FUNDING FACES IMPASSE IN CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, of course, this is coming when you're asking for an additional $22.5 billion in emergency aid with Republicans wanting a much lower number, about $10 billion. What difference will that make?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR ASHISH JHA (WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 RESPONSE COORDINATOR)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, it's going to make an enormous difference, actually. So, let's talk about what happens if we don't get this. There is a new generation of vaccines that's almost surely coming. \uc2 FDA\uc2 's making final decisions on that. Americans, we will not have enough of those vaccines for all Americans. We're going to run out of treatments. We're going to run out of testing. So, if Congress doesn't step up and fund these, I think, urgent, emergent priorities, and they have to do it now. We can't wait till the fall. It'll be too late. If, if Congress does not do that now, we will go into this fall and winter with none of the capabilities that we have developed over the last two years.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and you talk about hospitalizations and deaths, but, but do you expect, if this happens, do you expect it will be much like it is today, those who have been vaccinated and boosted will not get extremely sick? Or are you waiting for some sort of new variant?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID CASES RISE IN 42 STATES, HOSPITALIZATIONS UP IN 37\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR ASHISH JHA (WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 RESPONSE COORDINATOR)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, all of the models we're looking at assume no major new variant, right? We assume that this virus will continue to evolve, as we have seen it evolve, and we're basing it on that. That said, what we know is, for instance, that we've seen a surge of infections here in the Northeast, we've not seen a huge spike in deaths because the population is well so vaccinated and boosted. That's not true for the whole country. So, we've got to continue plugging away at getting more people vaccinated and boosted. We've got to get more therapeutics in place. If we have the resources to do all of that, I do think we can get through this winter without a lot of suffering and death.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and right now, only Americans over 50 and those at high risk are approved to get the second booster. Do you see that changing any time soon?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHEN WILL SECOND BOOSTER BE AVAILABLE FOR ALL ADULTS?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR ASHISH JHA (WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 RESPONSE COORDINATOR)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, well, that decision made by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and CDC was based on the available data. And the available data, largely from Israel, suggest that people over 60 benefit enormously from that second booster as long as they're five months out from their first booster. Fifty to fifty-nine, the data is not as clear, and that's why they recommended, \uc2 FDA\uc2  recommended that people who are in higher-risk group talk with their doctor and get it. People under 50, just don't have the data yet. So, I think we always want to be driven by evidence. We'll see what the data shows.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we will stand by. Thank you for joining us this morning, Dr. Jha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Roundtable's next. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220511ei5800008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NINA TOTENBERG DRAFT SCOTUS OPINION SIGNALS ROLLBACK OF ABORTION\b0\par\par\uc2 1,356 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 TOTENBERG, ABORTION, SCOTUS, DRAFT, TRIGGER, LAWS\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Our legal team is standing by to analyze all the fallout. Senior national correspondent Terry Moran leads us off. And, Terry, this may be just a draft opinion, but there seems little doubt that Roe will be overturned, and massive implications across America.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT BOMBSHELL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DRAFT SCOTUS OPINION SIGNALS ROLLBACK OF ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It's almost a certainty, Martha, is, this is a draft. It's the draft opinion conservatives have been dreaming about and working toward half a century, and sometimes votes can switch between draft opinions and the final result. But it's clear that the five conservative justices on this draft opinion, Thomas, Gorsuch, Alito, Barrett, and Kavanaugh and have decided to go for it, and, and do what conservatives have been trying to do for all these years, which is overturn Roe versus Wade, and the impact, impact is going to be incalculable on the lives of women.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT'S NEXT FOR ABORTION LAWS IF SCOTUS OVERTURNS ROE?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Women will occupy a different place in American society. We'll have two regimes in the country where women do have the right of bodily autonomy under the old way and can choose to have abortion, and where they're, where the process is criminalized. And that will change the country and it's changed the court. The court has never done this, rollback a previously granted individual constitutional right. It, it shows an eagerness, really, to crown a conservative political moment, movement with a judicial triumph.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Terry, you talk about divide, the nation is already politically divided. This divides it further.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COURT DECISION COULD DEEPEN AMERICA'S POLITICAL DIVIDE\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It does. And I think it's, the leak itself is not a great sign. It shows that, look, everybody always knew there were politics in the Supreme Court, but that tradition of secrecy, that of confidentiality, of mutual trust, no matter how badly they fought, it served to take the court out of the muck and mire of our politics. And this shows that people inside the institution are willing to drag it into our politics and use it. And I think it will, it will damage the court's reputation, certainly worse than Bush versus Gore, which, which did take it down a significant notch. I - think it's going to divide the country, because this is an issue that we haven't stopped fighting about. And, and as I say, it, it will end up with two Americas for women in - this country.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And - thanks to you, Terry. Let's turn to our chief legal analyst, Dan Abrams. And Dan, when the court has overturned previous rulings, hasn't it largely been to correct the lack of rights? Terry talked about that, but that's not what you would have here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOMBSHELL DRAFT OPINION LEAK ROCKS SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 DAN ABRAMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right. It's typically been to prevent a state from enacting a law that violates the United States Constitution, and here you have just the opposite, which is to overturn a previous decision, to say that, yes, the states can do that. But when you look at precedent, I think that the most important question moving forward after this case is going to be, what could be next? And I think that certainly the gay marriage ruling is going to be on the table. Why? Because Justice Alito wrote a dissent in that case, which used almost the exact same reasoning as he's using in this case. And so, I think it is entirely fair to ask, okay, could that be next? Could he have five votes for something like that? And, of course, you would also need states to outlaw it, as well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION DRAFT RAISES CONCERNS FOR SAME-SEX MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Dan, let's turn back to states. Talk about the effect at the state level, if Roe is overturned. I know there are these so-called trigger laws. What else?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRIGGER LAWS' IN 13 STATES WOULD BAN ABORTION IMMEDIATELY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CERTAIN OR LIKELY TO BAN ABORTION\par \par \uc2 DAN ABRAMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, yeah, the trigger laws, as you correctly point out, would mean that as soon as Roe versus Wade is overturned, those abortion bans go onto the books. There are other statutes that are just sitting there, that had been invalidated as a result of Roe versus Wade that would likely get kicked back into gear. You're talking about least 20 states, or so. And then, there's going to be the question in the other states, which haven't addressed this directly in the wake of Roe versus Wade as much, where they're going to have to talk about what restrictions, do we increase restrictions particularly in purple states?\par \par \uc2 DAN ABRAMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, yes, they're going to be that states that immediately ban abortion, but there are also very important debates going on in a lot of the more purple states about where the line will be drawn now that the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  isn't dictating where that line ought to be.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WITHOUT ROE, 26 STATES CERTAIN OR LIKELY TO BAN ABORTION\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Thanks, Dan.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's bring in NPR's legal affairs correspondent, Nina Totenberg. And Nina, you've been covering the courts since the early days of Roe. How much has this leak rattled the institution and, and the way it's viewed?\par \par \uc2 NINA TOTENBERG (NPR LEGAL AFFAIRS ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 Well, the way it's viewed it's, it's an earthquake, and I think within the court itself it's an earthquake. There has never been a leak like this. There have been minor, little springs that have emerged from the court, reports of a, a tentative vote or misbehavior by one justice back in the '50s, '60s, '70s. There were leaks, those kinds of leaks, but never an entire draft of a majority opinion. That has never, ever occurred before. And it can only, in all likelihood, have come from a justice that I think that is less likely, perhaps one of the clerks, and the leading, the leading theory is a conservative clerk who was afraid that one of the conservatives might be persuaded by Chief Justice Roberts and to, to join a - much more moderate opinion.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS ORDERS INVESTIGATION INTO LEAK\par \par \uc2 NINA TOTENBERG (NPR LEGAL AFFAIRS ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 And then there's another theory that it was an outraged liberal clerk. But I think the only one that makes sense is that it came from somebody who was afraid that this majority might not hold, that Chief Justice Roberts might persuade one of the conservatives to come over to him in a much more moderate opinion.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Nina, how likely is it that they'll discover who did leak this?\par \par \uc2 NINA TOTENBERG (NPR LEGAL AFFAIRS ESPONDENT)\par \par \uc2 I think it's very unlikely. I mean, the marshal of the court is in charge of protecting the court, and that's another thing you're going to see. The court will look like it is literally hunkered down. The, all the, the security walls and all those kinds of things will go into place, at least until the end of June or later, and it's going to look like it's under siege the way, in some ways, this is emotionally, put the court under siege in the same way that the Capitol across this very street was put under literal siege.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Thanks so much, Nina. We'll be watching all of this. Thanks to you, Terry, and thanks to Dan.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220511ei5800002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THIS WEEK WITH GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS\b0\par\par\uc2 523 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 "This Week with George Stephanopoulos" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 High court, high stakes.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 My body, my choice.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court preparing to overturn Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 How dare they tell a woman what she can do and cannot do with her own body.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 And I do encourage you once more to just pray and send Roe versus Wade to the ash heap of history.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TERRY MORAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DAN ABRAMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NINA TOTENBERG\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 A major shift after 50 years of abortion access. This morning, our special look at what this ruling means for the rights of all Americans. Terry Moran on the bombshell draft opinion, chief legal analyst Dan Abrams on the battles playing out in the states, and veteran court reporter Nina Totenberg on the fallout from the leak. Plus, exclusive interviews with Minnesota Senator Amy Klobuchar and Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson. And the Powerhouse Roundtable on the new outlook for the midterms.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Doubling down...\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  (WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 We want Ukraine to win. We're going to do everything we can to help Ukraine defend its sovereignty.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ...as \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  prepares for Russia's annual Victory Day, intelligence leaks prompt new fears that the US could be closer to direct conflict with Russia. Ian Pannell is live from the warzone. And...\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR ASHISH JHA (WHITE HOUSE CORONAVIRUS RESPONSE COORDINATOR)\par \par \uc2 With waning immunity, we may very well get a large surge of infections.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DR. ASHISH JHA\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ...the White House warns that 100 million could be infected by the fall. Dr. Ashish Jha joins us, live.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From ABC News, it's "This Week." Here, now, co-anchor Martha Raddatz.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OVERTURNING ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning and welcome to "This Week." As we come on the air this Mother's Day, millions of women across America are bracing for the rollback of abortion rights after the bombshell leak revealing a majority of the Supreme Court supports overturning Roe v. Wade. Since the Court established that the Constitution protects a woman's right to an abortion, no issue has animated and divided our political system more than the landmark precedent.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Across five decades, 10 presidential administrations, 23 Supreme Court justices and countless legal battles, this nation has fiercely debated the promise and the limits of that constitutional right. But as conservatives have solidified their hold on the Supreme Court after years of heated confirmation battles, the fervent activism by the anti-abortion rights movement appears on the cusp of a major victory. And while the source of the leak remains unclear, it's all but certain if the ruling holds, many states will immediately ban the procedure. The intense debates will grow stronger, and the nation's divisions ever wider.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220511ei5800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR SENATE DEMS PUSH TO CODIFY ROE V WADE INTO LAW\b0\par\par\uc2 1,193 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 TOTENBERG, ABORTION, SCOTUS, DRAFT, TRIGGER, LAWS\par \par \uc2 ROE, ACTIVISM, DANNENFELSER, TIMMARAJU, MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR, ABORTION, DEMS, SCOTUS, ABC/WAPO, POLL\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And joining us now is Senator Amy Klobuchar, a member of the Judiciary Committee. Senator Klobuchar, great to see you this morning. Let's, let's start right there, do you believe...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...do, do you believe there should be a litmus test? The Democrats have several candidates who do not support abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK: EXCLUSIVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE DEMOCRATS PUSH TO CODIFY ROE V WADE INTO LAW\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (JUDICIARY COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, first of all, you have people who are personally, personally pro-life, but yet believe that that decision should be a woman's personal choice, even if they may not agree with them. So, I think it's important to note that we have people in our party that vote to uphold Roe v. Wade that may have personal opinions that are different. That is a really important distinction, Martha. And also, I think what you see in our party, is a party that is clearly pro-choice. It believes that a woman should have a right to make her own reproductive health decisions when it comes to abortion. That is the position of our party, and I think you see, in primary after primary, that matters to our voters, certainly now more than ever.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Senate Democrats plan to hold a vote this week to codify abortion rights, but it's most certainly going to be blocked, not go through. So, if the court does overturn Roe, which seems an almost certainty, what options do abortion rights advocates have, at this point, and Democrats?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (JUDICIARY COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, let's be clear about what's going on, here. With this leaked opinion, the court is looking at reversing 50 years of women's rights, and the fall will be swift. Over 20 states have laws in place already, Martha, and I think the question that voters are going to be asking, when 75 percent of people are with us on this, is who should make this decision? Should it be a woman and her doctor, or a politician? Should it be Ted Cruz making this decision or a woman and her family? Where are women's equal rights?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (JUDICIARY COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 So, my answer to you is, if we are not successful in passing this in the Senate, and we'll do everything to do so, and I'll note it's already passed in the House where not one Republican voted for it, to codify Roe v. Wade. If we are not successful, then we go to ballot box. We march straight to the ballot box. And the women of this country and the men who stand with them will vote like they've never voted before. Because this is 50 years of rights in a leaked opinion where Justice Alito is literally not just taking us back to the 1950s, he's taking us back to the 1850s. He actually cites the fact that abortion was criminalized back when the 14th Amendment was adopted.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Senator, should this be the principal issue...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (JUDICIARY COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 And so, this is really extreme thought...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...should this be the principal issue for Democrats at the - at the ballot box, abortion rights?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (JUDICIARY COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 It will not be only the issue, Martha. I think you know that people right now are focused on the economy. They've seen the leadership of the President when it comes to Ukraine and the importance of preserving our democracy. They've seen the extremity of what we're seeing across the country, extreme Republicans who literally have been supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's assault on our democracy and refused to admit that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  actually won the election. All of this is going to be on the ballot. But clearly, when you look at especially a new generation of women, are looking at this and saying, wait a minute, my mom and my grandma are going to have more rights than I'm going forward.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (JUDICIARY COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 I'm going to have to look at a patchwork of states laws with 15 of them all ready looking to ban medication abortion, which is what people do online and things like that, they're going to look at this and say, what world do I live in? And so, as you look at these attempts to limit people's rights, limit people's voting rights, that's just not where America is, Martha. So, of course, it's going to be a major issue going into the fall.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC/WAPO POLL: MAJORITY SUPPORTS SCOTUS UPHOLDING ROE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, but, but on that, on that point, Senator, public polls show a majority of Americans support the right to an abortion, in most cases. But in the states that would almost immediately ban abortion if Roe is overturned, a majority of adults believe abortions should be illegal in all or most cases according to a "New York Times" analysis. So, wouldn't these laws simply reflect a majority of people in those states?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (JUDICIARY COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 I am someone that believes, and I think when your viewers think of this and they talk to their daughters and they talk to their sisters and mothers, I say this, why should a woman in Texas have different rights and a different future and a different ability to make decisions about her body and her reproductive choices than a woman in Minnesota? How can that be, in this country, that we'd have a patchwork of laws? And it, one of the things, it isn't always easy to do, is put yourself in someone else's shoes, a waitress in the middle of Texas who has to make a decision about, she's going to quit her job to be able to get on a bus and go 250 miles.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (JUDICIARY COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 Those are the kinds of things that are going to be happening. It's especially going to fall on the backs of poor women, women of color. This is just wrong. And that is part why Justice Blackmun, who is a Republican-appointed justice, no less, made that thoughtful decision, looked at the Constitution and said the right to privacy includes the right for women to make a choice like this.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. We're going to have to leave it there, but we thank you for your time, this morning. Thank you so much, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM-MN) (JUDICIARY COMMITTEE)\par \par \uc2 It was great to be on, Martha. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220511ei5800004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ACTIVISTS MOBILIZE, SCOTUS WEIGHS OVERTURNING ROE HOW WILL SCOTUS ABORTION RULING IMPACT MIDTERMS?\b0\par\par\uc2 778 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 TOTENBERG, ABORTION, SCOTUS, DRAFT, TRIGGER, LAWS\par \par \uc2 ROE, ACTIVISM, DANNENFELSER, TIMMARAJU, MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In the wake of the leaked draft, activists on both sides of the debate immediately began mobilizing for a drastic shift in America's abortion laws and the rights afforded to millions of American women. We spoke with leaders of two groups who are already preparing for America's next abortion battle, as the Supreme Court readies its ruling.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTIVISTS MOBILIZE AS SCOTUS WEIGHS OVERTURNING ROE\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 My body, my choice!\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The reaction to this week's history-altering news was swift.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Hey, hey! Hoe, hoe! Roe v. Wade is going to go!\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Proponents and opponents swarming the steps of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Abortion is just a basic human right.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 You won't find abortion written in invisible ink in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 's draft opinion a highly contentious and deeply personal issue.\par \par \uc2 JEANNE MANCINI (PRESIDENT MARCH FOR LIFE)\par \par \uc2 Pro-abortion advocates thought we would become desensitized to abortion, but nothing could be further from the truth.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 We have reached the culmination of what Republicans have been fighting for, angling for, for decades, now, and we are going to fight back.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Hell, yeah!\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Despite some polls saying 70 percent of Americans believe the choice should be left up to a woman and her doctor, America is awakening to a generational shift on abortion, as a wave of state legislation has restricted access across the country.\par \par \uc2 MINI TIMMARAJU (PRESIDENT NARAL PRO-CHOICE AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 As a movement, this has been probably the most devastating year since pre-1973.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Mini Timmaraju is the president of \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 , one of three reproductive rights groups planning to spend more than $150 million to elect abortion rights advocates this year.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you think this will be the issue in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW WILL SCOTUS ABORTION RULING IMPACT MIDTERMS?\par \par \uc2 MINI TIMMARAJU (PRESIDENT NARAL PRO-CHOICE AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 This issue is a major motivator, not only for Democratic base voters, not only for women, but for Americans, overall.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) President Biden warning this could just be the beginning of a wave of rollbacks.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH BIDEN (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 If it becomes a law and if what is written is what remains, it goes far beyond the concern of whether or not there is the right to choose. It goes to other basic rights.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Margorie Dannenfelser is the founder of the Susan B. Anthony List, a political organization helping elect anti-abortion rights activists to office.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER (PRESIDENT SUSAN B ANTHONY LIST)\par \par \uc2 I am absolutely for every unborn child, for saving the life of every unborn child.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION "TRIGGER LAWS" IN PLACE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If the final ruling does overturn Roe, 26 states are certain or likely to ban abortion, including 13 states with trigger laws that would ban it immediately.\par \par \uc2 MINI TIMMARAJU (PRESIDENT NARAL PRO-CHOICE AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 We have to explain to the American people that we can't rely on the court anymore to protect our constitutional rights. We have go to Congress, governors races, attorneys general who enforce these laws.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) States aren't waiting until a final ruling or the fall to take action. This week, Oklahoma Governor Kevin Stitt signed a six-week abortion ban into law, modeled after the Texas SB-8 bill that went into effect last summer. Blue states like Connecticut passed new legislation protecting abortion providers from out-of-state lawsuits, and California is aiming to codify reproductive rights into law.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GAVIN NEWSOM (DEM) (CALIFORNIA)\par \par \uc2 Wake up, America. Wake up to who you're electing.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) These opponents and defenders of abortion rights do agree on one thing. It should be a defining issue for any political candidate.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER (PRESIDENT SUSAN B ANTHONY LIST)\par \par \uc2 We put elections first, because we need to elect candidates who will pass laws that will save lives. We got to know what they're going to do once they're elected.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you think this should be a litmus test for Democratic candidates?\par \par \uc2 MINI TIMMARAJU (PRESIDENT NARAL PRO-CHOICE AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 We don't think that we should accept any more anti-choice Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020220511ei5800003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPREME COURT ABORTION BATTLE PROTESTS OUTSIDE HOMES OF JUSTICES AFTER DRAFT OPINION LEAK\b0\par\par\uc2 607 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, ZELENSKA, \uc2 G7\uc2 , PUTIN, ZELENSKYY, ATTACKS, SAMOILENKO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST LADY'S SURPRISE TO UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 SANCTIONS, \uc2 G7\uc2 , UKRAINE, PUTIN, OIL\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, EVACUATIONS, DROUGHT, MORSE, RESIDENTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXTREME FIRE DANGER\par \par \uc2 FIRES, WINDS, DRY, TEMPERATURES\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, PROTESTS, ABORTION, MCMORRIN, PELOSI, GRAHAM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Many on the East Coast will welcome those warmer temperatures. Cheryl, thank you. And we turn now to the battle over abortion, in the wake of that leaked Supreme Court draft opinion. This weekend, abortion rights protesters targeted the private homes of two high court justices. ABC's Faith Abubey is at the Supreme Court for us, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROTESTS OUTSIDE HOMES OF JUSTICES AFTER DRAFT OPINION LEAK\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, protestors back on the streets and in front of the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  after last week's bombshell leak of the Supreme Court draft opinion. From California...\par \par \uc2 YASMINE-IMANI MCMORRIN (CULVER CITY COUNCILMEMBER)\par \par \uc2 Abortion is health care, and it's 2022. I can't believe we're having this conversation, but here we are.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...to Texas.\par \par \uc2 OWEN SHROYER (PROTESTER)\par \par \uc2 I do believe in the right to life, and I believe this right to life is being denied.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Demonstrators showing the abortion debate is far from over.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Keep abortion safe and legal.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And in the DC area, dozens protesting outside the homes of conservative Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . In a statement last week, the court saying the draft opinion, which suggests the conservative majority on the court plans to overturn the landmark 1973 Roe versus Wade ruling, quote, "Does not represent a decision by the court or the final position of any member on the issues in the case." But House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , today, saying the draft is still a slap in the face.\par \par \uc2 REP NANCY PELOSI (DEM) (SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE)\par \par \uc2 Here we are on Mother's Day, a week where this court has slapped women in the face in terms of disrespect for their judgments about the size and timing of their families. So, the fact is, let's keep our eye on the ball, and the ball is in the court of those justices.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And there are renewed calls for Congress to take action. The Women's Health Protection Act, which would codify federal abortion protections, passed the House back in September, but has stalled in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN LINDSAY GRAHAM (REP) (SOUTH CAROLINA)\par \par \uc2 My Democratic friends are trying to pass a law Wednesday that will allow abortion right up to the day before the baby is delivered. Different states will take different approaches. So, the abortion debate will not go away in the country. It will be decided by the people, not a handful of judges.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Senator \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  telling ABC News that if Wednesday's scheduled vote fails, Democrats are ready to take the issue directly to voters.\par \par \uc2 SEN AMY KLOBUCHAR (DEM) (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 We march straight to the ballot box, and the women of this country and the men who stand with them will vote like they've never voted before.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Linsey, the reality is Democrats need at least 60 votes in the Senate in order to pass this measure. However, at this time, they just don't have the support. That vote Wednesday is expected to fail. Linsey.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020220509ei5800006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HOT PINK HOT TAKE SIZZLING MONOCHROMATIC TREND\b0\par\par\uc2 997 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT, DEADLY STORMS\par \par \uc2 HAIL, FLOODING, WINDS\par \par \uc2 COVID, CASES, NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 BROWNSTEIN, JILLIAN, CHILDREN, HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 MARQUEZ, MARIUPOL, STEEL PLANT, ESCAPE\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, SUSPECT, OFFICER, WHITE\par \par \uc2 DALTON, HICKS, KELLER, ROSE, WALENSKY, FANIO, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JOBS, INFLATION, RECESSION\par \par \uc2 PLANE, BACKYARD, SAFE\par \par \uc2 CUBA, HOTEL, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FIRES, HEAT\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP OF KENTUCKY DERBY\par \par \uc2 SHAW, VIRNARAJAH, BARKOW, GERGIS, ABORTION, LEAK\par \par \uc2 RAIN\par \par \uc2 MURPHY, HARRY, MEGHAN, QUEEN, JUBILEE\par \par \uc2 QUEEN, JUBILEE, HARRY, MEGHAN, FAMILY\par \par \uc2 HORNER, DOMENICALI, GARFINKEL, NORRIS, FORMULA 1, GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 DUCKS, ESCORTED, ELDERLY, SPAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, MARQUEZ, INVASION, EVACUATION, KIRBY\par \par \uc2 HEPATITIS, CHILDREN, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC INVESTIGATES MYSTERIOUS ILLNESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIVE CHILD DEATHS NOW BLAMED ON SEVERE HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, DALTON, KELLER, FANIO, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRACING FOR POSSIBLE ROE V. WADE OVERTURN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALIFORNIA AMONG STATES PUSHING TO EXPAND ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATION, ATTACK, CHAPPELLE, LEE\par \par \uc2 F1 MIAMI GRAND PRIX, SENNA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "LIGHTS OUT & AWAY WE GO"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA TAKES A RIDE ON THE FAST LANE AT THE MIAMI F1 GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 ADELE, SWIFT, STORYCORPS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 AHRENS, MAYER, RAE, HARRINGTON, "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS"\par \par \uc2 MOTHER'S DAY, GAYLE\par \par \uc2 NIMBLE, CORAVIN, BLANQUIL, VEHO, POURRI, BUTTER LONDON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 HOT PINK, SANDS, FASHION\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) On Saturdays, we talk about hot pink. The color popped on the red carpet earlier this week at the Met Gala and seems to be a blooming trend. Fashion expert Katie Sands joins us now for this breaking news and ways you can use it. Katie, so glad to have you here in hot pink and the shoes are to die for. Okay, it was all heart eyes Monday night when Sebastian Stan and Glenn Close also hit the Met Gala carpet. Lots of hot pink. Tell us about it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOT PINK HOT TAKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SIZZLING MONOCHROMATIC TREND THAT TOOK OVER THE MET GALA\par \par \uc2 KATIE SANDS (FASHION EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 Lots of hot pink. So, first of all, I have heart eyes for Sebastian Stan whether he's in pink or not hot pink. We can, I think if my husband's watching, he probably knows that already. But pink is the hottest trend. We're told by Pantone it was going to be the year of periwinkle. But, no, people wanted bigger, brighter and bolder. They wanted something more fun to lift their spirits as we started to leave the house after the pandemic. So, pink is really it. You can see it on the fashion week here. Valentino really brought this vibrant trend to the forefront. However, we're seeing it on the red carpet now. We are seeing it on street styles. We're seeing it in fast fashion and sustainable fashion. The color is everywhere.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're seeing it on women and men getting in the mix as well. Colman Domingo at the Oscars, Lil Nas X at the Grammys, so many more. Tell us about how these guys were rocking the style.\par \par \uc2 KATIE SANDS (FASHION EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 So, pink is not just a Barbie color anymore. It's really for all genres. We're seeing it on men like we saw at the Grammy's. We saw it, of course, at the Met Gala. We are talking about Sebastian Stan and his monochromatic look. It's for everyone. And it's no secret that \uc2 Mattel\uc2  is coming out with a new Barbie movie this year because it really is the theme. But it did start I have to say with Marilyn Monroe with in "Diamonds Are a Girl's Best Friend" years ago. So, it's funny to see her right here and see that the color is now coming back in full throttle on the red carpet.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, what are some ways people can start incorporating pink into their wardrobe? Because it's a loud color.\par \par \uc2 KATIE SANDS (FASHION EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 Oh, it's a loud color. I mean, I'm wearing it today. Walked into the studio in this hot pink. Like, people were like, okay, we see you. You can't miss the color, right? But you can incorporate it in so many ways. You can incorporate it with linen blazers like I'm wearing here. You can use a little accessory. My shoes are, of course, a little extra because they're a little shiny and crystal colorful, but you can also incorporate it with a cross body bag. You can incorporate like Justin Bieber did like we saw in the picture before with his beanie. You can have subtle little hints of pink, but can also have brighter hints because it's a happy color and that's what we're looking for this spring and summer.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It absolutely is. Cheryl Scott is in with us and walked in in a hot pink coat. And I said, girl, where is that? Love the hot pink. Katie, thank you so much for being here with us.\par \par \uc2 KATIE SANDS (FASHION EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we will be right back after this.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Thanks for pulling off a surprise, by the way.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 Nicely done. I am surprised.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Ms. Kim, we've been admiring the shoes as well, coming with like the leopard print and the bling, the sparkle.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Fashion forward and all the shine.\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 It's in this color now.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 I love it. Collect the whole set.\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We love having you.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Guys, happy Mother's Day to all the moms. We'll see you back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 Happy Mother's Day.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220523ei570003o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ON-STAGE AFTERMATH SUSPECT IN DAVE CHAPPELLE ATTACK ON $30K BAIL\b0\par\par\uc2 472 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT, DEADLY STORMS\par \par \uc2 HAIL, FLOODING, WINDS\par \par \uc2 COVID, CASES, NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 BROWNSTEIN, JILLIAN, CHILDREN, HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 MARQUEZ, MARIUPOL, STEEL PLANT, ESCAPE\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, SUSPECT, OFFICER, WHITE\par \par \uc2 DALTON, HICKS, KELLER, ROSE, WALENSKY, FANIO, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JOBS, INFLATION, RECESSION\par \par \uc2 PLANE, BACKYARD, SAFE\par \par \uc2 CUBA, HOTEL, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FIRES, HEAT\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP OF KENTUCKY DERBY\par \par \uc2 SHAW, VIRNARAJAH, BARKOW, GERGIS, ABORTION, LEAK\par \par \uc2 RAIN\par \par \uc2 MURPHY, HARRY, MEGHAN, QUEEN, JUBILEE\par \par \uc2 QUEEN, JUBILEE, HARRY, MEGHAN, FAMILY\par \par \uc2 HORNER, DOMENICALI, GARFINKEL, NORRIS, FORMULA 1, GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 DUCKS, ESCORTED, ELDERLY, SPAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, MARQUEZ, INVASION, EVACUATION, KIRBY\par \par \uc2 HEPATITIS, CHILDREN, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC INVESTIGATES MYSTERIOUS ILLNESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIVE CHILD DEATHS NOW BLAMED ON SEVERE HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, DALTON, KELLER, FANIO, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRACING FOR POSSIBLE ROE V. WADE OVERTURN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALIFORNIA AMONG STATES PUSHING TO EXPAND ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATION, ATTACK, CHAPPELLE, LEE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) First, though, we do want to get to our "GMA" cover story, the latest developments in the case of that alarming on-stage attack of comedian Dave Chappelle. Phil Lipof is joining us now with more. Phil, good morning.\par \par \uc2 PHIL LIPOF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Whit. You know the story is still developing.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON-STAGE AFTERMATH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUSPECT IN DAVE CHAPPELLE ATTACK ON $30K BAIL\par \par \uc2 PHIL LIPOF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Yesterday, prosecutors charged 23-year-old Isaiah Lee, who was in court for the first time with misdemeanors for alleged rushing and tackling comedian Dave Chappelle as he performed at the Hollywood Bowl on Tuesday. Lee pleaded not guilty and the Los Angeles judge ordered Lee to stay at least 100 yards away from Chappelle as well as from the venue where the attack occurred. Bail set at $30,000. Now, though, Chappelle himself is speaking out about the incident during another stand-up performance at the Comedy Store. The comedian told the audience, he actually spoke to his alleged assailant, asking him what prompted the attack.\par \par \uc2 PHIL LIPOF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) He said he found the answer to be a bit odd. Chappelle told the audience that Lee said, the attack was to draw attention to the plight of his grandmother who had been forced out of her Brooklyn neighborhood by gentrification. Fellow comedian Kevin Hart also weighed in during an appearance on "The \uc2 Jimmy Kimmel\uc2  Show" saying, "I think it's time to get back to a place of respect for your live entertainer and understand that you're coming to a show." Right now, Lee faces four misdemeanor charges.\par \par \uc2 PHIL LIPOF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) If convicted on all counts, he faces up to 18 months in jail and up to a $4,000 fine. Guys?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Dave Chappelle certainly not the only one finding that reasoning odd. Thank you, Phil.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220523ei570003f\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CELEBRATING MOMS EVA'S MOM IN THE STUDIO\b0\par\par\uc2 828 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT, DEADLY STORMS\par \par \uc2 HAIL, FLOODING, WINDS\par \par \uc2 COVID, CASES, NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 BROWNSTEIN, JILLIAN, CHILDREN, HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 MARQUEZ, MARIUPOL, STEEL PLANT, ESCAPE\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, SUSPECT, OFFICER, WHITE\par \par \uc2 DALTON, HICKS, KELLER, ROSE, WALENSKY, FANIO, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JOBS, INFLATION, RECESSION\par \par \uc2 PLANE, BACKYARD, SAFE\par \par \uc2 CUBA, HOTEL, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FIRES, HEAT\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP OF KENTUCKY DERBY\par \par \uc2 SHAW, VIRNARAJAH, BARKOW, GERGIS, ABORTION, LEAK\par \par \uc2 RAIN\par \par \uc2 MURPHY, HARRY, MEGHAN, QUEEN, JUBILEE\par \par \uc2 QUEEN, JUBILEE, HARRY, MEGHAN, FAMILY\par \par \uc2 HORNER, DOMENICALI, GARFINKEL, NORRIS, FORMULA 1, GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 DUCKS, ESCORTED, ELDERLY, SPAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, MARQUEZ, INVASION, EVACUATION, KIRBY\par \par \uc2 HEPATITIS, CHILDREN, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC INVESTIGATES MYSTERIOUS ILLNESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIVE CHILD DEATHS NOW BLAMED ON SEVERE HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, DALTON, KELLER, FANIO, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRACING FOR POSSIBLE ROE V. WADE OVERTURN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALIFORNIA AMONG STATES PUSHING TO EXPAND ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATION, ATTACK, CHAPPELLE, LEE\par \par \uc2 F1 MIAMI GRAND PRIX, SENNA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "LIGHTS OUT & AWAY WE GO"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA TAKES A RIDE ON THE FAST LANE AT THE MIAMI F1 GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 ADELE, SWIFT, STORYCORPS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 AHRENS, MAYER, RAE, HARRINGTON, "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS"\par \par \uc2 MOTHER'S DAY, GAYLE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, we are back on "GMA." We've been celebrating our moms all morning long here. We decided we need to take a moment or a mom-ent to surprise someone special.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, earlier this week, Eva's mom showed America how to make her Korean short ribs. And now, we think it's time to share something with her.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mom. She's over here. She, I brought her here under the guise that I needed her to bring Ella for me.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Miss Kim, we've got a chair for you right here.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I don't think she was expecting it.\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She wasn't expecting to be on TV this morning.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There we go.\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 She didn't tell me that, yeah. She said, make sure you put the makeup on.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, now you've been on national TV twice in one week.\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 How about that? I mean, debut on the TV.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, can I ask you a question? So, Eva's been telling us for years that she was this goody two-shoes, she followed the rules.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Never got in trouble.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Never got in trouble. What's the true story of Eva Pilgrim the little girl?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Give us the tea.\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 She actually very good.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) See?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Really?\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We got verification from mom.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 Look. Because if she had been somewhere without I approved, I go over there and get her.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thank you for your honesty.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She stayed in line because you kept her in line.\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 I put her on tiptoes.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, you have two grandsons we know, but Ella is your first granddaughter. This is your first Mother's Day with Eva as a mom. What is this like for you?\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 Well, I'm so proud of her and she's first-time mom and she was such a good mom and she come so far.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Aww.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We got you breakfast, mom.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Big show of gratitude.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look how many pancakes there are. Janai wants one of them.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I saw those pancakes and I said, pancakes.\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 I can share.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Happy Mother's Day, too.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible) Yeah. There's breakfast in bed. We got you breakfast at the desk.\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 How about that? That's pretty good. I'll take it.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thanks, mom. I love you.\par \par \uc2 INSOOK KIM GAYLE (MOTHER OF EVA PILGRIM)\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much. This is a treat.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You haven't even started with the food yet, a real treat coming.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220523ei570003l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 POP NEWS\b0\par\par\uc2 932 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT, DEADLY STORMS\par \par \uc2 HAIL, FLOODING, WINDS\par \par \uc2 COVID, CASES, NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 BROWNSTEIN, JILLIAN, CHILDREN, HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 MARQUEZ, MARIUPOL, STEEL PLANT, ESCAPE\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, SUSPECT, OFFICER, WHITE\par \par \uc2 DALTON, HICKS, KELLER, ROSE, WALENSKY, FANIO, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JOBS, INFLATION, RECESSION\par \par \uc2 PLANE, BACKYARD, SAFE\par \par \uc2 CUBA, HOTEL, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FIRES, HEAT\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP OF KENTUCKY DERBY\par \par \uc2 SHAW, VIRNARAJAH, BARKOW, GERGIS, ABORTION, LEAK\par \par \uc2 RAIN\par \par \uc2 MURPHY, HARRY, MEGHAN, QUEEN, JUBILEE\par \par \uc2 QUEEN, JUBILEE, HARRY, MEGHAN, FAMILY\par \par \uc2 HORNER, DOMENICALI, GARFINKEL, NORRIS, FORMULA 1, GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 DUCKS, ESCORTED, ELDERLY, SPAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, MARQUEZ, INVASION, EVACUATION, KIRBY\par \par \uc2 HEPATITIS, CHILDREN, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC INVESTIGATES MYSTERIOUS ILLNESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIVE CHILD DEATHS NOW BLAMED ON SEVERE HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, DALTON, KELLER, FANIO, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRACING FOR POSSIBLE ROE V. WADE OVERTURN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALIFORNIA AMONG STATES PUSHING TO EXPAND ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATION, ATTACK, CHAPPELLE, LEE\par \par \uc2 F1 MIAMI GRAND PRIX, SENNA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "LIGHTS OUT & AWAY WE GO"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA TAKES A RIDE ON THE FAST LANE AT THE MIAMI F1 GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 ADELE, SWIFT, STORYCORPS\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, and now it's time for "Pop News" with Megan Ryte. And you are sitting in a chair today.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I am. I love this chair. I love it. I love it. Let's begin with Adele.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ADELE CELEBRATES HER 34TH BIRTHDAY\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The superstar and best friend in my head celebrated her 34th birthday on Thursday and marked the occasion by sharing two new photos with fans looking happy and glamorous in custom Carolina Herrera dress. In the caption, the singer reflected on the past year saying, quote, "What a difference a year makes. If time keeps healing and smoothing out all the creases in my life like it does as the years fly by, then I can't wait to be 60. I've never been happier." And what better gift is that? Happy birthday, Adele.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are we all going to the concert in Vegas?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Road trip.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Can you get us tickets?\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I can try. I can try.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, then the answer is yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We can take the show on the road.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That's a great idea. Let's put it in the universe.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TAYLOR SWIFT DROPS 'THIS LOVE (TAYLOR VERSION)'\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And from one music superstar to the next, Taylor Swift.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The singer dropped her last rerecord on Friday releasing "This Love (Taylor's version)." Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TAYLOR SWIFT'S MUSIC VIDEO, "THIS LOVE (TAYLOR'S VERSION)"\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The track originally appeared on her hit pop album "1989." And now, eight years later, what's old is new again. Swift saying in \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , quote, "'This Love (My Version)' is out and I'm currently reliving the '1989' tour in my head and spiraling. It's fine." The song has fans are wondering if she'll drop the full "1989 (Taylor's version)" but no official word.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As always, Swift is keeping us guessing.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She kept me guessing. I was waiting for someone to walk across that wooden bridge. It was just there. I was like, somebody has to walk at any moment, and they never did.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It was just there. It was just there.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And finally, tomorrow is Mother's Day. And what better way to celebrate than with real stories of motherhood.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORYCORPS CELEBRATES MOTHER'S DAY\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The nonprofit StoryCorps has released three new animations for their season, Listening to Mom. Take a look at a clip from the episode titled "Joyce's Mom."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TV SERIES, "JOYCE'S MOM"\par \par \uc2 ANIMATED CHARACTER VOICEOVER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 You got more than enough love to spread and share with people.\par \par \uc2 ANIMATED CHARACTER VOICEOVER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 And what I always said about you was, I wanted you to be happy and I wanted you to be a good person.\par \par \uc2 ANIMATED CHARACTER VOICEOVER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Yes, you did. I want to say that that would be in my nature but I was raised that way. I learned that from you.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) You can check out all three episodes of the season on the StoryCorps website. My best friend's name is Joyce, so I sent that to her to play. You've got to send this to your mom.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, I love that.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That was really sweet. There's something about being a momma on Mother's Day.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And how much you love your mom now that you're a mom.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, yeah, yeah, so appreciative.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And happy early Mother's Day to the mothers on set.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, of course. Of course. Yes, indeed. All right, coming up here, we are, and thank you, by the way, Megan Ryte as always.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Coming up here on "GMA," we're feeding you a healthy dose of nostalgia and celebrating 40 years of "Little Shop of Horrors."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 STORY SYNOPSES\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220523ei570003i\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BRACING FOR POSSIBLE ROE V. WADE OVERTURN CALIFORNIA AMONG STATES PUSHING TO EXPAND RIGHTS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,089 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT, DEADLY STORMS\par \par \uc2 HAIL, FLOODING, WINDS\par \par \uc2 COVID, CASES, NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 BROWNSTEIN, JILLIAN, CHILDREN, HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 MARQUEZ, MARIUPOL, STEEL PLANT, ESCAPE\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, SUSPECT, OFFICER, WHITE\par \par \uc2 DALTON, HICKS, KELLER, ROSE, WALENSKY, FANIO, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JOBS, INFLATION, RECESSION\par \par \uc2 PLANE, BACKYARD, SAFE\par \par \uc2 CUBA, HOTEL, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FIRES, HEAT\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP OF KENTUCKY DERBY\par \par \uc2 SHAW, VIRNARAJAH, BARKOW, GERGIS, ABORTION, LEAK\par \par \uc2 RAIN\par \par \uc2 MURPHY, HARRY, MEGHAN, QUEEN, JUBILEE\par \par \uc2 QUEEN, JUBILEE, HARRY, MEGHAN, FAMILY\par \par \uc2 HORNER, DOMENICALI, GARFINKEL, NORRIS, FORMULA 1, GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 DUCKS, ESCORTED, ELDERLY, SPAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, MARQUEZ, INVASION, EVACUATION, KIRBY\par \par \uc2 HEPATITIS, CHILDREN, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC INVESTIGATES MYSTERIOUS ILLNESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIVE CHILD DEATHS NOW BLAMED ON SEVERE HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, DALTON, KELLER, FANIO, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRACING FOR POSSIBLE ROE V. WADE OVERTURN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALIFORNIA AMONG STATES PUSHING TO EXPAND ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, now to more fallout from the leaked Supreme Court draft decision that could overturn Roe v. Wade. ABC's Zohreen Shah is back with more on this story looking at what states are doing now to try to protect abortion rights. Zohreen, good morning again to you.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Janai. The White House is launching a mental health hotline for pregnant women and for new moms who might need it. There's also an effort to protect abortion access, but those are all likely to fail. So, now, many states are urgently trying to take action.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, many states bracing for potential Roe v. Wade reversal...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible)\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INDIVIDUAL STATES\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \u8230\'20\'26leaving abortion up to individual states. Nearly half likely are certain to ban abortion in most cases. The \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a reproductive rights research organization, estimating there could be nearly a 3,000% increase of out-of-state women whose closest abortion clinic will be in California. Ever since September when Texas's restrictive ban took effect, providers have been seeing surges.\par \par \uc2 DR LAURA DALTON (CHIEF MED OFFICER PLANNED PARENTHOOD MAR MONTE)\par \par \uc2 We've seen a doubling of the number of patients coming from outside of California and we expect that to only increase.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Laura Dalton has been anticipating more out-of-state women at the country's largest Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 DR LAURA DALTON (CHIEF MED OFFICER PLANNED PARENTHOOD MAR MONTE)\par \par \uc2 We are able to increase capacity by 250 to 500 patients a week.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) California already has laws not requiring patient disclosure even if they are out of state. But lawmakers are now trying to take greater steps.\par \par \uc2 POLITICIAN (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Our message to women across the country is California will be a place where you can get answers. It will be a place where we will embrace you and help you with all of the information you need, and if it means providing the ability to help you get here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AFTER TEXAS BAN\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Thirteen state bills were proposed after Texas's ban, everything ranging from paying for travel to preventing criminalization. But already, some Californians voicing opposition.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KELLER (PRESIDENT CALIFORNIA FAMILY COUNCIL)\par \par \uc2 Part of our huge concern with so-called abortion tourism is that, the state of California would use taxpayer dollars that, in our opinion, should be going to mothers and families here in the Golden State and would instead be incentivizing people to fly across the country here to California to receive free abortions.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOME STATES MOVE TO RESTRICT ABORTIONS, OTHERS MOVE TO PROTECT RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) California resident Abby Fanio was six weeks in when she discovered she was pregnant. It took another month to get an abortion, but she says she at least had the option.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What is your reaction hearing that there's going to be a likely Roe reversal and some women are going to have to travel thousands of miles to get the care that you got?\par \par \uc2 ABBY FANIO (ABORTION PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 Really scared, like I can still sort of feel the shot of adrenaline that went through me when I saw that positive pregnancy test. And the only way I calmed myself down in that moment was knowing that I had a choice to get a safe abortion.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And CDC director Rochelle Walensky says, her fear is for women who don't have the resources to travel. She says, they might take matters into their own hands and, she says, lives could be at stake. Guys?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Zohreen Shah for us there, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IN 4 MINUTES, DAVE CHAPPELLE LATEST\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Coming up, more on that attack on Dave Chappelle at the Hollywood Bowl. The latest on the suspect as he faces a judge.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE F1 MIAMI GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And we are in Miami for qualifying day on the F1 Miami Grand Prix. We'll take you behind the scenes of the race.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW MUSIC FROM TAYLOR SWIFT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Plus, Taylor Swift releasing her version of "This Love," bringing us one step closer to the rerelease of her album "1989."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Welcome back to "GMA" on this Saturday morning. Thanks for joining us. We are only one day away from Mother's Day. Are you guys ready?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, yep.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You got your presents?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A few more things to sort out but, you know, we will be ready.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Put you on the spot. Good. And we want to celebrate our moms this morning. I think we've got a picture of me and my momma. Look at that. Her wedding, my wedding, Miss Tammy. Love you, Mom. She'll be watching.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Is that little Janai? Right there.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is little Janai. And that is me and my paternal grandmother. That is Miss Sanora Norman. Happy Mother's Day early to both of them.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I love it.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I love that. Celebrating all the moms throughout the show here today and tomorrow, of course.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220523ei570003e\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WEATHER\b0\par\par\uc2 429 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT, DEADLY STORMS\par \par \uc2 HAIL, FLOODING, WINDS\par \par \uc2 COVID, CASES, NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 BROWNSTEIN, JILLIAN, CHILDREN, HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 MARQUEZ, MARIUPOL, STEEL PLANT, ESCAPE\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, SUSPECT, OFFICER, WHITE\par \par \uc2 DALTON, HICKS, KELLER, ROSE, WALENSKY, FANIO, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JOBS, INFLATION, RECESSION\par \par \uc2 PLANE, BACKYARD, SAFE\par \par \uc2 CUBA, HOTEL, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FIRES, HEAT\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP OF KENTUCKY DERBY\par \par \uc2 SHAW, VIRNARAJAH, BARKOW, GERGIS, ABORTION, LEAK\par \par \uc2 RAIN\par \par \uc2 MURPHY, HARRY, MEGHAN, QUEEN, JUBILEE\par \par \uc2 QUEEN, JUBILEE, HARRY, MEGHAN, FAMILY\par \par \uc2 HORNER, DOMENICALI, GARFINKEL, NORRIS, FORMULA 1, GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 DUCKS, ESCORTED, ELDERLY, SPAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, MARQUEZ, INVASION, EVACUATION, KIRBY\par \par \uc2 HEPATITIS, CHILDREN, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC INVESTIGATES MYSTERIOUS ILLNESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIVE CHILD DEATHS NOW BLAMED ON SEVERE HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, DALTON, KELLER, FANIO, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRACING FOR POSSIBLE ROE V. WADE OVERTURN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALIFORNIA AMONG STATES PUSHING TO EXPAND ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATION, ATTACK, CHAPPELLE, LEE\par \par \uc2 F1 MIAMI GRAND PRIX, SENNA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "LIGHTS OUT & AWAY WE GO"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA TAKES A RIDE ON THE FAST LANE AT THE MIAMI F1 GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 ADELE, SWIFT, STORYCORPS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 AHRENS, MAYER, RAE, HARRINGTON, "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS"\par \par \uc2 MOTHER'S DAY, GAYLE\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the weather is supposedly getting better. Let's go and get a check of our weather with Cheryl Scott. Mom, don't go anywhere because you actually have to help with the next segment.\par \par \uc2 CHERYL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What? All right, while, the weather is getting a little bit better for the second half of the weekend here on the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST HEAT WAVE\par \par \uc2 CHERYL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But another big story is the heat that is building here in the south. We're talking record heat, triple-digit heat on the way for some of these cities and Texas. San Antonio forecasted high today, 102, the record for you is 100 today. So, we'll likely beat that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MOTHER'S DAY CITIES\par \par \uc2 CHERYL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And then, since it is such a special weekend, happy mom's day to my mom, all the moms out there, let's focus on our key cities. Mothers Hill, Colorado, 91. Strong winds, Mothers Arm, sunny 88, Flowers, Mississippi, sunny at 91 degrees. That's a check here. Now, a check of your local forecast.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONAL MAP\par \par \uc2 CHERYL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And some warm weather also on the way for the Midwest this week. You've been dealing with unseasonably cool weather, so I know you will like the sound of that. We're going to send it over to Eva and mom. Happy mom's day.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220523ei570003m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUDDENLY CELEBRATING CULT-CLASSIC MUSICAL "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS" TURNS 40\b0\par\par\uc2 969 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT, DEADLY STORMS\par \par \uc2 HAIL, FLOODING, WINDS\par \par \uc2 COVID, CASES, NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 BROWNSTEIN, JILLIAN, CHILDREN, HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 MARQUEZ, MARIUPOL, STEEL PLANT, ESCAPE\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, SUSPECT, OFFICER, WHITE\par \par \uc2 DALTON, HICKS, KELLER, ROSE, WALENSKY, FANIO, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JOBS, INFLATION, RECESSION\par \par \uc2 PLANE, BACKYARD, SAFE\par \par \uc2 CUBA, HOTEL, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FIRES, HEAT\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP OF KENTUCKY DERBY\par \par \uc2 SHAW, VIRNARAJAH, BARKOW, GERGIS, ABORTION, LEAK\par \par \uc2 RAIN\par \par \uc2 MURPHY, HARRY, MEGHAN, QUEEN, JUBILEE\par \par \uc2 QUEEN, JUBILEE, HARRY, MEGHAN, FAMILY\par \par \uc2 HORNER, DOMENICALI, GARFINKEL, NORRIS, FORMULA 1, GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 DUCKS, ESCORTED, ELDERLY, SPAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, MARQUEZ, INVASION, EVACUATION, KIRBY\par \par \uc2 HEPATITIS, CHILDREN, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC INVESTIGATES MYSTERIOUS ILLNESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIVE CHILD DEATHS NOW BLAMED ON SEVERE HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, DALTON, KELLER, FANIO, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRACING FOR POSSIBLE ROE V. WADE OVERTURN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALIFORNIA AMONG STATES PUSHING TO EXPAND ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATION, ATTACK, CHAPPELLE, LEE\par \par \uc2 F1 MIAMI GRAND PRIX, SENNA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "LIGHTS OUT & AWAY WE GO"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA TAKES A RIDE ON THE FAST LANE AT THE MIAMI F1 GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 ADELE, SWIFT, STORYCORPS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 AHRENS, MAYER, RAE, HARRINGTON, "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS"\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Another story that we're following this morning. Here we are celebrating 40 years of the cult classic "Little Shop of Horrors."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUDDENLY CELEBRATING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CULT-CLASSIC MUSICAL "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS" TURNS 40\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The little show that could first debuted back in 1982. And this weekend, the cast and crew are celebrating everything that makes it a piece of pop culture history.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Zachary Kiesch has more.\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY KIESCH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's the cult classic that comes with a warning, don't feed the plants. "Little Shop of Horrors" is the story of Seymour, a down-on-his-luck florist who discovers a one-of-a-kind plant that could be his ticket out of Skid Row. For four decades, the original score by composer Alan Menken and the late lyricist Howard Ashman, has had audiences tapping their toes from Broadway stages to countless community theaters across the country.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT AHRENS (PRODUCER "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS")\par \par \uc2 If a show is true to itself, it can embark on something a little bit ridiculous but there's still an honesty to it. And I think that honesty that Howard created and Michael re-created is evident to the audiences.\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY KIESCH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What was it about him and his approach that really made this such an innovative show?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MAYER (DIRECTOR "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS")\par \par \uc2 As a director, I respond so much to what he did. I saw the original production. I was blown away by it and I just loved how clever he was and how much ground gets covered in such a short amount of time. When you watch the show, you can't believe it's over so fast and you've had such an incredible meal.\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY KIESCH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This week marks the 40th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MAYER (DIRECTOR "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS")\par \par \uc2 It's very rare that you get to have this kind of experience as a performer, as a designer, director, musical director, choreographer, where you can be really right in the lap of the audience and they can have that exchange that's so vital and so immediate. So, I think that's part of what makes it.\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY KIESCH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The word that I keep on hearing is, is that this place is intimate.\par \par \uc2 AARON ARNELL HARRINGTON (ACTOR "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS")\par \par \uc2 It's a different experience. I think what even makes this show work is that it's intimate. They can feel and sense when things are getting scary or that they can feel or sense when, you know, good things are happening and everyone happens to be in a good place and you're rooting for the hero of the story.\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY KIESCH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Opening night featured familiar faces including Jonathan Groff and Christian Borle. Now, the show welcomes newcomers Christina Rae along with Aaron Arnell Harrington.\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY KIESCH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Is this the first off-Broadway show for both of you guys?\par \par \uc2 AARON ARNELL HARRINGTON (ACTOR "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS")\par \par \uc2 It's been, I mean, like a dream come true for this to be the first, you know, off-Broadway show for me and I guess I can say the same for you.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RAE (ACTOR "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS")\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 AARON ARNELL HARRINGTON (ACTOR "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS")\par \par \uc2 It's been nothing short of a blessing.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RAE (ACTOR "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS")\par \par \uc2 I love being here because we get to sing music that has some soul to it.\par \par \uc2 AARON ARNELL HARRINGTON (ACTOR "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS")\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RAE (ACTOR "LITTLE SHOP OF HORRORS")\par \par \uc2 Like, I'm for the funk. So, when it comes to dancing and everything, we get to put our little attitudes into it. I'm just grateful to be here.\par \par \uc2 ZACHARY KIESCH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Zachary Kiesch, ABC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A big thanks to Zachary.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And coming up, two words, free shipping. That's coming up next in today's "Deals & Steals."\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But first, though, we're giving shout outs to the moms in our lives. And it's my turn now. We got some pictures here. There's my mom Sharon. There we are in Hawaii and, of course, my wife Andrea as well. And a shoutout to my stepmom Leslie. These women have shaped me and saved me time and time again. I cherish all that you do and love you so much. Happy Mother's Day. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220523ei570003k\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BRACING FOR POSSIBLE ROE V. WADE OVERTURN CALIFORNIA AMONG STATES PUSHING TO EXPAND RIGHTS\b0\par\par\uc2 778 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT, DEADLY STORMS\par \par \uc2 HAIL, FLOODING, WINDS\par \par \uc2 COVID, CASES, NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 BROWNSTEIN, JILLIAN, CHILDREN, HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 MARQUEZ, MARIUPOL, STEEL PLANT, ESCAPE\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, SUSPECT, OFFICER, WHITE\par \par \uc2 DALTON, HICKS, KELLER, ROSE, WALENSKY, FANIO, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Elwyn. Thank you for the latest on that. Turning now to the fallout from that leaked Supreme Court draft decision that could overturn Roe v. Wade. ABC's Zoreen Shah is in Los Angeles with a look at what states are trying to do now to protect abortion rights. Zohreen, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Janai. The White House is launching a mental health hotline for new moms and pregnant women who might need it. There are also efforts to protect abortion access, but those are all likely to fail. So, now, many states are trying to urgently take action.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, many states bracing for a potential Roe v. Wade reversal, leaving abortion up to individual states. Nearly half likely are certain to ban abortion in most cases.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GUTTMACHER INSTITUTE'S STATEMENT\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a reproductive rights research organization, estimating there could be a nearly 3,000 percent increase of out-of-state women whose closest abortion clinic will be in California. Ever since September when the Texas restrictive ban took effect, providers in California have been seeing surges.\par \par \uc2 DR LAURA DALTON (CHIEF MED OFFICER PLANNED PARENTHOOD MAR MONTE)\par \par \uc2 We've seen a doubling of the number of patients coming from outside of California and we expect that to only increase.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Doctor Laura Dalton has been anticipating more out-of-state women at the country's largest Planned Parenthood affiliate.\par \par \uc2 DR LAURA DALTON (CHIEF MED OFFICER PLANNED PARENTHOOD MAR MONTE)\par \par \uc2 We're able to increase capacity by 250 to 500 patients a week.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTION AFTER TEXAS BAN\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Thirteen state bills were proposed soon after Texas's ban. Everything ranging from paying for travel to preventing criminalization.\par \par \uc2 JODI HICKS (CEO PLANNED PARENTHOOD AFFILIATES OF CALIFORNIA)\par \par \uc2 We will enforce civil judgments that other states impose because of their laws. We're protecting patients and providers should they seek care here in our state.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But already some Californians voicing opposition.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KELLER (PRESIDENT CALIFORNIA FAMILY COUNCIL)\par \par \uc2 Part of our huge concern with so-called abortion tourism is that the state of California would be using taxpayer dollars that, in our opinion, should be going to mothers and families here in the Golden State, and would instead be incentivizing people to fly from across the country here to California to receive free abortions.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Lila Rose is the founder of Live Action, an organization that advocates against abortion. She thinks the expected Supreme Court decision about abortion does not go far enough.\par \par \uc2 LILA ROSE (PRESIDENT & FOUNDER LIVE ACTION)\par \par \uc2 It's not full justice, though, because whether you're conceived in California or Texas, you, as a human being, deserve the right to live.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But some, like CDC Director Doctor \uc2 Rochelle Walensky\uc2 , saying her fear is the women who won't have the means to travel.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR \uc2 ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2  (DIR CDC)\par \par \uc2 Those who don't may take matters into their own hands. I do think that lives could be at stake in that situation.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC DIRECTOR ON ROE V. WADE DEBATE, DOCTOR WALENSKY: I DO BELIEVE LIVES COULD BE AT STAKE\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And even some California residents say they struggle getting care. Abby Fanio was six weeks in when she discovered she was pregnant. It took another month to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Why have you decided to speak out so strongly for women who do have to travel?\par \par \uc2 ABBY FANIO (ABORTION PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I think that if we want California to be a state where people really have the right to choose, we need to make sure that people can - actually access abortions. And I was just surprised by how hard it was for me to get one in a timely manner and without a lot of stress.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're now also learning that technology might be used against women who are looking to get an abortion in states where it may not be legal. There are a lot of apps that keep track of data that may reveal if a woman has gotten an abortion, and that data could be used as evidence. Whit.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220523ei570002z\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 09:30 HALF-HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 533 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT, DEADLY STORMS\par \par \uc2 HAIL, FLOODING, WINDS\par \par \uc2 COVID, CASES, NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 BROWNSTEIN, JILLIAN, CHILDREN, HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 MARQUEZ, MARIUPOL, STEEL PLANT, ESCAPE\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, SUSPECT, OFFICER, WHITE\par \par \uc2 DALTON, HICKS, KELLER, ROSE, WALENSKY, FANIO, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JOBS, INFLATION, RECESSION\par \par \uc2 PLANE, BACKYARD, SAFE\par \par \uc2 CUBA, HOTEL, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FIRES, HEAT\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP OF KENTUCKY DERBY\par \par \uc2 SHAW, VIRNARAJAH, BARKOW, GERGIS, ABORTION, LEAK\par \par \uc2 RAIN\par \par \uc2 MURPHY, HARRY, MEGHAN, QUEEN, JUBILEE\par \par \uc2 QUEEN, JUBILEE, HARRY, MEGHAN, FAMILY\par \par \uc2 HORNER, DOMENICALI, GARFINKEL, NORRIS, FORMULA 1, GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 DUCKS, ESCORTED, ELDERLY, SPAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, MARQUEZ, INVASION, EVACUATION, KIRBY\par \par \uc2 HEPATITIS, CHILDREN, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC INVESTIGATES MYSTERIOUS ILLNESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIVE CHILD DEATHS NOW BLAMED ON SEVERE HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, DALTON, KELLER, FANIO, ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRACING FOR POSSIBLE ROE V. WADE OVERTURN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALIFORNIA AMONG STATES PUSHING TO EXPAND ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATION, ATTACK, CHAPPELLE, LEE\par \par \uc2 F1 MIAMI GRAND PRIX, SENNA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "LIGHTS OUT & AWAY WE GO"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA TAKES A RIDE ON THE FAST LANE AT THE MIAMI F1 GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 ADELE, SWIFT, STORYCORPS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 ACTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning right back. I was a little off beat. I know. I know. Stop it. Welcome back to "GMA." So, those are the stars of the off-Broadway musical, "Little Shop of Horrors." This week, they are celebrating the show's 40th anniversary. And coming up, we sat down with the cast and the minds behind the show who gave us a behind-the-scenes look at the cult classic's history.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 But first here, the stories we're following right now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NORTH KOREA MISSILE\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 This morning, South Korea is reporting that North Korea has apparently launched a short-range ballistic missile into the sea. The suspected launch believed to most likely come from a submarine. If confirmed, this will be North Korea's 15th missile launch this year.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY PLANE CRASH\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  is investigating a deadly plane crash near the Golden Gate Bridge. Authorities say the single engine plane went down just before 3:00 PM on Friday, two people on board were pronounced dead at the scene. It's unclear if weather was a factor in the crash.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ESSAY FOR MOM\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And Ashley Judd sharing a heart-wrenching essay about her mother and the value of motherhood one week after the death of her mom, Naomi. The actress writing, "It wasn't supposed to be this way," saying, "she died just days before my sister and I could show her again how much we love and honor her." Judd went on to write about motherhood and women's rights in light of that leaked Supreme Court draft decision that could overturn Roe v. Wade. I want to touch on how she ended that essay. She said, "If you're lucky enough to still have your mother, honor more than her love and sacrifice. Honor her by demanding a world where motherhood everywhere is safe, healthy and chosen."\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely. Still so many shocked by that loss.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220523ei570003j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DEALS & STEALS CASH IN ON SATURDAY SAVINGS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,181 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT, DEADLY STORMS\par \par \uc2 HAIL, FLOODING, WINDS\par \par \uc2 COVID, CASES, NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 BROWNSTEIN, JILLIAN, CHILDREN, HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 MARQUEZ, MARIUPOL, STEEL PLANT, ESCAPE\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, SUSPECT, OFFICER, WHITE\par \par \uc2 DALTON, HICKS, KELLER, ROSE, WALENSKY, FANIO, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JOBS, INFLATION, RECESSION\par \par \uc2 PLANE, BACKYARD, SAFE\par \par \uc2 CUBA, HOTEL, EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 FIRES, HEAT\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP OF KENTUCKY DERBY\par \par \uc2 SHAW, VIRNARAJAH, BARKOW, GERGIS, ABORTION, LEAK\par \par \uc2 RAIN\par \par \uc2 MURPHY, HARRY, MEGHAN, QUEEN, JUBILEE\par \par \uc2 QUEEN, JUBILEE, HARRY, MEGHAN, FAMILY\par \par \uc2 HORNER, DOMENICALI, GARFINKEL, NORRIS, FORMULA 1, GRAND PRIX\par \par \uc2 DUCKS, ESCORTED, ELDERLY, SPAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, MARQUEZ, INVASION, EVACUATION, KIRBY\par \par \uc2 HEPATITIS, CHILDREN, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC INVESTIGATES MYSTERIOUS ILLNESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIVE CHILD DEATHS NOW BLAMED ON SEVERE HEPATITIS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, DALTON, KELLER, FANIO, ROE V. 